\par
\emph{Introduction}In the past decades the demand for health care has increasingly surpassed the supply.
\par
This is due, in part, to continuing technological developments in the field of medicine,
\par
as well as to other factors such as the increasing number of elderly people in western
\par
societies. Consequently, health care budgets are currently under pressure, which raises
\par
the question as to whether the health benefits of a new technology can justifY its costs.
\par
This question is typically addressed through an economic evaluation of the new
\par
technology, where it is compared with its best alternative. Unfortunately, such
\par
economic evaluation does not always provide a definite answer, partly because the
\par
outcome of the evaluation will always be surrounded by some degree of uncertainty.
\par
Several sources of uncertainty can be distinguished (see Manning et al., 1996;
\par
Drummond et al., 1997; Briggs tt Sculpher, 1995). Manning and colleagues
\par
differentiate between two main types of uncertainty: parameter uncertainty and
\par
modelling uncertainty.
\par
Parameter uncertainty is the uncertainty about the true value of the parameters used
\par
\textbf{as input and can arise in several ways. For instance, the value of a parameter may be}
\par
estimated from a study sample or may be estimated on the basis of opinion, e.g. from
\par
an expert panel. Furthermore, parameter uncertainty may be introduced when fairly
\par
certain outcomes are generalised to a larger population or another setting, or
\par
extrapolated beyond the observation period or from an intermediate to a final endpoint
\par
(e.g. from bone mineral density to quality-adjusted life years). Parameter uncertainty
\par
also occurs when there is disagreement about the appropriate value, as might be the
\par
case with the discount rate or when a choice is made for the valuation of quality of life
\par
through either time trade-off or standard gamble.
\par
Modelling uncertainty arises when the analyst is uncertain about the mathematical
\par
equations of the model, regarding both the inclusion of certain variables and the
\par
functional form by which parameters should be combined. For example, does coronary
\par
heart disease multiply the age-sex-specific mortality rates by a constant amount or
\par
does it add a constant amount to the mortality rate. Such choices are often made in
\par
the absence of clear evidence that one or other of the functions is the appropriate one
\par
\textbf{to use.}
\par
\textbf{In summary, there are many sources of uncertainty and it is unrealistic to assume}
\par
that all uncertainty can be eliminated. Instead, methods are developed to deal with
\par
uncertainty in the best possible way. A first step in dealing with uncertainty is to
\par
establish how uncertain we are; for example, a well-known way of describing
\par
uncertainty is through use of a 95\% confidence interval. Next, methods are sought to
\par
decrease uncertainty as much as is feasible. This may be achieved, for example, by
\par
specifying before a study is conducted which level of uncertainty is acceptable and
\par
then calculating the corresponding sample size. Or, after the results of a study become
\par
available, by combining these results with results of previous studies, for instance
\par
through meta-analysis. Finally, we should strive to help decision makers to deal with
\par
\textbf{uncertainty in the decision-making process.}
\par
\textbf{(HRPTER 1 }10\textbf{Overview of developments}
\par
In the early days of cost-effectiveness analysis in health care, most studies used a
\par
modelling approach, since data on costs and effects at the patient level were scarce.
\par
Decision trees, a frequently used type of model, had been used for years in operations
\par
research and medical decision making. Decision trees allow a systematic assessment of
\par
all possible outcomes of a process given a set of decisions on, for example,
\par
interventions and diagnostic procedures. A sensitivity analysis has always been part of
\par
such analysis: probabilities in the model are varied to see if and how the decision
\par
changes. It seems reasonable to assume that sensitivity analysis would also be an
\par
integrated part of modelling studies in economic evaluations of medical technologies.
\par
However, this proved to be less obvious than expected. In 1995, Briggs Et Sculpher
\par
reviewed economic evaluations published in 1992. A total of 93 studies (modelling,
\par
prospective and retrospective) were examined. Of these, 24\% failed to consider
\par
uncertainty at all and 38\% of the studies had attempted a sensitivity analysis that was
\par
judged too limited to provide an adequate representation of uncertainty. Of the
\par
remaining studies, 14\% was judged good and 25\% adequate in representing
\par
uncertainty.
\par
Agro et a!. (1997) published a similar review; they studied 90 articles published
\par
between January 1989 and December 1993, equally divided among health economic
\par
journals, medical journals and pharmacy journals. They concluded that only 59\% of
\par
the studies used sensitivity analysis. However, there was a large difference between the
\par
types of journals: 80\% of the articles in the health economics journals and 77\% of the
\par
articles in the medical journals had conducted sensitivity analysis, compared with only
\par
20\% of the articles in the pharmacy journals.
\par
In those studies that did include a sensitivity analysis, these analyses were mainly
\par
one-way, multi-way or extreme case sensitivity analyses. In a one-way or multi-way
\par
analysis one or more (usually not more than three) parameters are varied between
\par
\textbf{certain limits. However, no attention is given to the fact that the limits are often less}
\par
likely to occur than the baseline estimate. Consequently, in the last few years some
\par
studies also include a probabilistic sensitivity analysis, in which all input variables are
\par
varied simultaneously, according to probability distributions (e.g. chapter 5; Michel et
\par
a!., 1996; Doubilet et al., 1985). Such an analysis presents information on all possible
\par
outcomes, as well as on the likelihood of these outcomes.
\par
\textbf{Situations may occur where parameters themselves are not associated with}
\par
\textbf{uncertainty because the parameter is a decision parameter. However, when no final}
\par
decision has yet been made, it may be unclear how these parameters influence the
\par
\textbf{outcome. For instance, in a cervix cancer screening program, a decision has to be made}
\par
about the screening frequency. This could be once every 3 years or once every 5 years
\par
and it may not be clear how this choice influences the cost-effectiveness ratio. In such
\par
a situation a scenario analysis can be used to calculate the outcome for several options
\par
of the decision parameter. The scenario analysis can also be used to see what happens
\par
\textbf{to the outcome when decision makers influence certain parameters, i.e. when there are}
\par
\textbf{external influences on a parameter. This is, for instance, the case for transplantation}
\par
programs: while it is possible to estimate the number of donor organs available fromobservations, this number can be influenced by e.g. new laws or protocols.
\par
In recent years, data on costs and effectiveness at the patient level are often
\par
collected alongside clinical trials. The analysis of such data requires statistical methods
\par
describing the uncertainty due to sampling. For a large part, the same methods used
\par
for the analysis of clinical data were used for the economic and outcomes data. The
\par
analysis of cost data, however, raised some imported issues, due to the skewness of the
\par
cost distribution. This skewness is easily explained by the fact that, for many diseases
\par
and their treatments, most patients will incur small to moderate costs, while a small
\par
number of patients will incur very high costs due to medical complications or extended
\par
hospital stay. In biostatistics, skewed data is usually log transformed and all statistical
\par
inference is then performed on this transformed data. Testing the mean of log
\par
transformed data is equivalent to testing the geometric mean on the original (cost)
\par
scale. For skewed data, the geometric mean costs will always be smaller than the
\par
\textbf{average costs. However, for economic evaluations the measure of interest is the average}
\par
costs per patient, as the purpose of the analysis is to inform decision makers on the
\par
budget needed to treat patients, which can be obtained by multiplying average costs
\par
by the total number of patients. Thus, inference based on the geometric mean will
\par
provide irrelevant conclusions. Clearly, the analysis of skewed cost data is not
\par
straightforward and consequently this subject has recently received much attention (see
\par
e.g. Briggs a Gray, 1998a; Thompson a Barber, 2000; O'Hagan a Stevens, 2001b).
\par
Another feature of economic evaluations that attracted attention is the incremental
\par
cost-effectiveness ratio (ICER), as a ratio of two stochastic variables. The ratio of two
\par
normally distributed variables will rarely have a well-behaved distribution. The
\par
distribution may be skewed, and may also have heavy tails, which implies that,
\par
theoretically, mean and variance do not exist. In 1994, O'Brien et al. proposed an
\par
analytical method for the calculation of a confidence interval around the ICER. This
\par
report was the first of numerous papers on this issue, one of which is presented in
\par
chapter 2. Both analytical and bootstrap (i.e. simulation) methods have been proposed
\par
and compared (see e.g. O'Brien et al., 1994; van Hout et al., 1994; Wakker a Klaassen,
\par
1995; Sacristan et al., 1995; Chaudhary a Stearns, 1996; Willan a O'Brien, 1996;
\par
Briggs et al., 1997; Polsky et al., 1997).
\par
When confidence intervals for the ICERs are calculated, it becomes clear that these
\par
intervals are usually very wide, as a direct result of the fact that the sample size of
\par
clinical trials is usually based on the primary clinical endpoint of the study. As the
\par
variation in costs is often much larger than in the primary (clinical) endpoint, cost-
\par
effectiveness studies usually have little power to detect whether a new intervention is
\par
cost-effective compared to the alternative. In cost-effectiveness studies, we study the
\par
ratio of two variables, which may be highly correlated. This means that the methods
\par
for sample size calculation used for the primary endpoint cannot easily be translated
\par
to methods for the ICER. Consequently, specific methods for sample size calculation in
\par
economic evaluation have been developed. At first a simulation method was proposed
\par
(see chapter 3) and recently several papers have described analytical methods (see e.g.
\par
Briggs a Gray, 1998b; Laska et al., 1999; Willan a O'Brien, 1999).
\par
Theoretically, it would be possible to include economic parameters in a sample size
\par
CH\textasciitilde PTER \textbf{1 }12calculation for randomised clinical trials. However, in studies where the primary goal
\par
is to show improved efficacy or effectiveness, with proof of cost-effectiveness as
\par
secondary goal, the question arises whether it would be ethical to include economic
\par
parameters in the sample size calculation. Surely an important purpose of sample size
\par
calculation is to assure that enough patients enter a randomised clinical trial to allow
\par
for statistically significant results while ensuring that not too many patients are
\par
exposed to the risks that usually accompany such a trial. When the primary goal of a
\par
study is to prove cost-effectiveness, while superior efficacy has already been
\par
demonstrated, it may be considered unethical to assign patients to a control group with
\par
the lower efficacy. Besides ethical issues, practical reasons may preclude basing sample
\par
size on economic parameters; for example, the period of enrolment may become too
\par
long, or the costs of the clinical trial might become too high.
\par
Clearly it is unreasonable to expect that the sample size of future studies should be
\par
increased in order to reduce uncertainty around the ICER. Therefore it is important to
\par
consider other available options. One important option is to use Bayesian statistics. In
\par
1763 Bayes{$\cdot$} paper "Essay Towards Solving a Problem in the Doctrine of Chances" was
\par
published posthumously and this paper became the basis of what is now called
\par
Bayesian statistics. In Bayesian statistics, a prior probability distribution for the
\par
parameter of interest is specified first. Sample information is then obtained and
\par
combined using Bayes' theorem, resulting in a posterior probability distribution for the
\par
parameter. This posterior distribution can then be used for statistical inferences about
\par
that parameter. The key, and somewhat controversial, feature of Bayesian methods is
\par
the notion of a probability distribution for a population parameter. According to
\par
frequentist statistics, parameters are constants and cannot be represented as random
\par
variables. Bayesian proponents argue that, if a parameter value is unknown, it is logical
\par
to specifY a probability distribution that describes the possible values for the parameter
\par
as well as their likelihood. In general, we can state that Bayesians deal with the
\par
probabilities of hypotheses, given a data set, whereas frequentists deal with the
\par
probability of data sets, given a hypothesis.
\par
The use of Bayesian methods in health care has been proposed in an attempt to
\par
make full use of other information already available outside the clinical trial (see for
\par
instance Eddy et al., 1990; Jones, 1996). Since the Bayesian approach deals with
\par
uncertainty by treating data collection as an ongoing process whereby current beliefs
\par
are modified in the light of new information, the uncertainty about the parameter
\par
values is reduced but is never completely removed. One of the main challenges of using
\par
a Bayesian approach in health economics is describing these current beliefs through a
\par
prior distribution (see also chapter 4).
\par
Besides being able to reduce uncertainty by combining sources of information, the
\par
use of Bayesian methods may offer other benefits (Briggs, 1999; Hei1jan et al., 1999).
\par
One such benefit is that Bayesian inferences are easier to understand in the sense that,
\par
for instance, Bayesian confidence intervals have a far more intuitive interpretation
\par
than classical confidence intervals. A frequentist 950fo confidence interval tells us that
\par
if the trial was repeated often enough and if a confidence interval was calculated each
\par
time, 950fo of those intervals would contain the true difference. Most people, however,are inclined to interpret the interval as having a 95\% chance that the true difference
\par
lies within the interval. Within the frequentist framework this interpretation is not
\par
allowed, whereas it is within the Bayesian framework. Another benefit is that some
\par
problems that cannot be easily handled with classical methods are rather
\par
straightforward using Bayesian analysis. A well-known example is the testing for
\par
equality of means with unequal variances (see Box \& Tiao, 1973).
\par
\textbf{The recent interest in the use of Bayesian analysis in economic evaluations has led}
\par
to the "Bayesian Initiative in Health Economics \& Outcomes Research". This initiative
\par
was established in 1997 by MEDTAP International to explore the value of the Bayesian
\par
approach to health econm:nics and outcomes research. Their premise is that the unique
\par
decision-oriented perspective of the Bayesian approach will produce information that
\par
\textbf{is more interesting and relevant to decision makers (see also \emph{www.bayesian-}}
\par
\emph{initiative.com). }This idea is, for instance, also supported by Claxton, who describes a
\par
Bayesian approach to the value of information and its implication for the regulation of
\par
new pharmaceuticals (Claxton, 1999a) and optimal trial design (Claxton, !999b).
\par
\textbf{Thus, there is a growing trend in health economic literature towards discussions on}
\par
how the outcomes of health economic evaluations are used and may be used in aiding
\par
decision making. Results of these evaluations may be used for decisions on
\par
reimbursement for one specific technology, or for the allocation of a budget over a
\par
large number of technologies. Furthermore, outcomes may be used to decide on further
\par
data collection. Some methodological issues regarding the possible role of uncertainty
\par
\textbf{in the decision-making process are discussed in this thesis.}
\par
\textbf{Outline of this thesis}
\par
Chapter 2 presents the 'C/E acceptability curve{$\cdot$} as a method for statistical inference
\par
regarding the ICER. This method can be used in economic evaluations that are
\par
performed alongside randomised clinical trials, in which economic data, such as safety
\par
and efficacy data, are collected at the patient level. The acceptability curve measures
\par
the probability that the ICER is acceptable in comparison to a predefined limit R (which
\par
reflects the critical cost-effectiveness ratio society has decided on) for all positive
\par
values of R. The problems and proposed methodology are illustrated by using, as an
\par
example, data from a phase II trial in which the costs and effects of anakinra (a poten-
\par
tial therapeutic agent for the treatment of sepsis syndrome) were investigated.
\par
In chapter 3 uncertainty in the design phase of a cost-effectiveness study is
\par
addressed. A simulation method is presented for sample size calculation in such studies
\par
\textbf{and an assessment made as to how sensitive the estimated sample size is to changes in}
\par
assumptions about the expected costs, effectiveness and the correlation between them.
\par
Data from two trials are used to illustrate this; one comparing primary coronary
\par
angioplasty and streptokinase in the treatment of acute myocardial infarction and the
\par
other comparing the efficacy and safety of lansoprazole with omeprazole in the
\par
treatment of reflux oesophagitis.
\par
\textbf{Chapter 4 summarises some first experiences with a Bayesian economic analysis}
\par
based on data from a randomised clinical trial using informative priors. First, a
\par
\textbf{CHAPTER 1 }14univariate approach is presented for the analysis of the differences in costs and effects.
\par
Second, some initial ideas about a multivariate approach for the analysis of the ICER
\par
are presented. Subsequently, the methodology is applied using data from two trials. For
\par
both trials, the balance was assessed between the costs and effects of stent implantation
\par
compared with balloon angioplasty in patients with angina. Several prior distributions
\par
are used for the analysis of the second trial to see how the conclusions differ after
\par
combining data with these priors.
\par
In chapter 5 an example is given of a modelling study that uses sensitivity analysis
\par
to address uncertainty. The objective of this study was to compare the costs and effects
\par
of a fixed dose combination diclofenac/rnisoprostol and diclofenac alone in the
\par
treatment of patients with rheumatoid arthritis. The sensitivity of the results for
\par
variation in the input variables was studied both univariately and multivariately (i.e.
\par
probabilistic). The univariate analysis shows which variables markedly influence the
\par
outcome, whereas the multivariate analysis yields the most probable outcome, the
\par
'worst' and the 'best' possible outcome and also the distribution of possible outcomes.
\par
Chapter 6 presents an example of a scenario analysis, as it was performed in the
\par
economic evaluation of the Dutch lung transplantation program. The scenario analysis
\par
describes future transplantation programs, operational for 15 years. This simulation
\par
period is necessal)' to reach a stable number of patients on the waiting list, which has
\par
not yet been established during the observation period. Societal costs, survival and
\par
quality of life are followed for up to 40 years, comparing the situation with and
\par
\textbf{without a lung transplantation program. Besides a baseline scenario (i.e. a prolongation}
\par
of the current program), two alternative scenarios will be presented: a policy scenario,
\par
restricting the inflow of patients on the waiting list and a donor scenario assuming a
\par
larger supply of donor lungs. These scenarios address the uncertainty arising from
\par
possible external influences on a parameter.
\par
In chapter 7, models for budget allocation are explored. In most textbooks and
\par
articles on the foundation of cost-effectiveness analysis, one of the basic premises is
\par
that the goal is to maximise health gains given a fixed budget, However, this
\par
optimisation problem does not allow for decreasing marginal value and uncertainty.
\par
An alternative model is proposed with decision makers maximizing a value function
\par
with decreasing marginal returns over health effects and the budget available for
\par
purposes competing with health care spendings. The question is raised whether
\par
different models lead to differences in the decision as to whether a new program should
\par
be implemented, and in the effect of the introduction of a new program on current
\par
programs. Next, attention is given to the fact that, in practice, the estimates of costs
\par
and effects will be surrounded by uncertainty. Clearly, if costs are uncertain, it is
\par
impossible to stay within the fixed budget with certainty. In this chapter an
\par
optimisation problem is formulated that allows for uncertainty, based on expected
\par
utility. The question may now be asked whether accounting for uncertainty has any
\par
influence on the decision which programs should be funded.
\par
Finally, chapter 8 presents the conclusions of the previous chapters and discusses
\par
recent developments and areas for future research.\textbf{CHAPTER 2}
