\textbf{Summary}
\par
\emph{A general approach is discussed ta assess the uncertainty surrounding the incremental cast-}
\par
\emph{effectiveness ratio \textbraceleft ICER) estimated an the basis of data from a randamised clinical trio/. The}
\par
\emph{approach includes the calculation of a 95\% probability ellipse and introduces the concept af a}
\par
\emph{so called C/E-acceptability cuNe. This latter cuNe defines for each predefined !CER the}
\par
\emph{probability that the ICER found in the study is acceptable. The approach is illustrated by}
\par
\emph{estimates afcosts per life saved and costs per patient discharged alive on the basis of data from}
\par
\emph{a phase }II \emph{trial addressing the value ofanakinra in treating sepsis syndrome.}\textbf{Introduction}
\par
Before new medical procedures are introduced, they are traditionally assessed in terms
\par
of safety and efficacy. Such assessments are obligatory in the case of the registration
\par
of pharmaceutical products and there are strict rules on how such assessments should
\par
take place.
\par
While safety and efficacy may be the primary parameters for the registration of
\par
pharmaceutical products, it is increasingly recognised that additional parameters, such
\par
as costs, need to be considered when decisions have to be made about reimbursement.
\par
Since policy makers have encountered large increases in health expenditures because
\par
\textbf{of the introduction of new medical procedures, there is a growing interest in}
\par
information on the additional costs and benefits of these procedures. This need for
\par
information has been formalised in the guidelines concerning pharmaceutical products
\par
in Australia (see Commonwealth of Australia, 1992), in the proposed guidelines in
\par
Canada (see Schubert, 1993), and in the requirement for information about costs and
\par
benefits when financing new health care programmes in The Netherlands (see Rutten
\par
a Bonsel, 1992).
\par
Among the techniques to assess safety and efficacy, the randomised clinical trial
\par
(RCT) is, despite its limitations, respected as the golden standard. The results of an RCT
\par
may not always reflect common practice and the comparator may not always be the
\par
appropriate one. However, the experimental design minimises potential biases in the
\par
measurement of differences (e.g. Pocock, 1983). Additionally, the RCT offers the
\par
possibility to test, by use of statistical procedures, whether the observed differences are
\par
\textbf{due to coincidence or not. P-values and confidence limits are common concepts within}
\par
clinical trials.
\par
For similar reasons as with respect to safety and efficacy one may state that
\par
economic data should also be gathered in an RCT. This could permit the use of a
\par
statistical paradigm similar to that used for safety and efficacy. However, while there
\par
are a number of well developed statistical procedures, for example concerning survival,
\par
\textbf{such procedures are not available for the principal outcome of an economic evaluation:}
\par
the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio (ICER).
\par
This chapter sets out to solve some of the problems in the statistical assessment of
\par
a ICER. The problems and methodology are illustrated using, as an example, data from
\par
a phase II trial in which the costs and effects of anakinra, a potential therapeutic agent
\par
for the treatment of sepsis syndrome, were investigated. We look at the possibilities for
\par
statistical inference regarding the costs and effects calculating a 95\% probability ellipse
\par
and we introduce a new concept based on some of the ideas of O'Brien et al. (1994):
\par
the so-called 'C/E-acceptability curve: This curve measures the probability that the
\par
ICER -resulting from a trial- is acceptable in comparison to a predefined ICER R for all
\par
positive values of R. The basic idea behind this concept is generally applicable to a
\par
large number of situations and its use is illustrated using the data from the
\par
anakinra-trial.
\par
\textbf{CHAPTER 2 }18\textbf{Anakinra versus placebo}
\par
Anakinra is a therapeutic agent developed for the treatment of patients with sepsis
\par
syndrome. Sepsis syndrome is caused by a bacterial infection and may lead to multiple
\par
organ failure. shock and death. During the last ten years aggressive antibiotics and
\par
cardiovascular support have been the standard management, but 4-week mortality has
\par
remained at 20 to 60\% (see Parrilo et a!., I 990). One of the natural body responses to
\par
infection is the production of interleukin I (IL- I) which, when over-produced, may
\par
help lead to sepsis syndrome. In response, the body produces a natural IL- I receptor
\par
antagonist (!L-Ira) which counteracts the effects of IL-L !L-Ira can now also be
\par
produced by recombinant techniques (recombinant methionyl human interleukin I
\par
receptor antagonist or rh!L- Ira) and is being tested in patients with sepsis syndrome.
\par
Here the results will be used of the phase II study. In this study data about costs and
\par
effects were gathered regarding 99 patients with sepsis syndrome (see Gordon et al.,
\par
1992; Fisher et al., 1994). Patients with underlying disease or injury anticipated to be
\par
rapidly fatal were excluded. In addition, patients with neutropenia due to cancer
\par
chemotherapy or AIDS were not included. Patients were randomised to receive a 100
\par
mg bolus of placebo or anakinra followed by a 72 hours continuous infusion of placebo
\par
or anakinra at I 7, 67 or 133 mg/hr; 25 patients were treated with placebo, 25 with low
\par
dose anakinra, 24 with medium dose and 25 patients received a high dose. Here,
\par
efficacy is determined by evaluating 28 day all cause mortality and the proportion of
\par
patients discharged alive. Additionally, costs are analysed by using length of stay data
\par
during the 28 day study period distinguishing between normal and intensive care.
\par
Sepsis is an acute illness which usually results in either a cure or death within 5-10
\par
days. Hence, in order to capture the effects on survival clinical trials such as those for
\par
HA- !A, another experimental agent (see Ziegler et aL, 1991), and anakinra have limited
\par
their follow up period to 28 days. It is clear that 28 days is not enough for an estimate
\par
of costs per life year gained. For that purpose one needs long term data. When this is
\par
lacking, one tends to make assumptions, gather information from other sources and use
\par
epidemiological knowledge (e.g. Schulman et a!., 1991), but this may produce
\par
\textbf{unreliable estimates. Here, we limit ourselves to some short term parameters for}
\par
\textbf{effectiveness and we will concentrate on costs per additional survivor and costs per}
\par
additional patient discharged alive. Additionally, as we are only using the data for
\par
illustrative purposes, we limit ourselves to the comparison between the high dose
\par
anakinra group and the placebo group. Figure 2. I presents product-limit estimates of
\par
the duration distributions until death and discharge.\textbf{fig 2.1 3 }1.0
