30\textbf{Discussion}
\par
In this chapter we have presented a methodology that is meant to give an idea about
\par
the uncertainty surrounding the estimate of the ICER. Additionally, the methodology
\par
may be useful in following the lines of thought as presented in earlier drafts of the
\par
guidelines for economic appraisal of pharmaceuticals in Ontario (see Laupacis et a!.,
\par
1992). Here it was proposed to duster various technologies according to their ICER and
\par
the C/E-acceptability curve can be used to indicate whether the ICER is within a certain
\par
duster. As such the approach can be seen as an addition to common statistical analyses
\par
and therefore it is to be preferred that the relevant dusters and their boundaries are
\par
defined in advance together with the clinical hypotheses. This also prevents the use of
\par
the C/E-acceptability curve as a post-hoc instrument to safely indicate which limit has
\par
not been exceeded, an approach that might be seen as of questionable value.
\par
The methodology has been illustrated using the results from a randomised clinical
\par
trial concerning anakinra. This is a typical example of a trial in which the number of
\par
observations is small, where the ICER looks relatively small in comparison to the
\par
average costs, but where there is considerable uncertainty about the reliability of the
\par
estimates. It is noted that the price of anakinra has not been included in the
\par
calculations and therefore it is emphasised that our example should be interpreted
\par
solely as an illustration.
\par
With respect to the underlying assumptions, it is emphasised that the approach does
\par
not assume normally distributed costs and effects but it does assume that \emph{average }costs
\par
and \emph{average }effects are normally distributed. This assumption is based on the Central
\par
\textbf{Limit Theorem and as such it is an approximation. Of course, the more observations,}
\par
the better the approximation. Ideally, testing for normality would be possible by
\par
\textbf{splitting the observations in a number of groups each with its own group mean, and}
\par
testing whether these means are normally distributed. Such an approach would ask for
\par
many observations. When these are not available, like in the case of anakinra, one
\par
should be aware of the underlying assumption of normality, an assumption that is not
\par
formally tested.
\par
A final remark concerns the usage of the methodology presented here for the
\par
outcome which is more commonly used: costs per life-year gained. For an unbiased
\par
estimate of the covariance between life-years gained and costs, complete patient
\par
specific observations are needed and the use of censored data may bias the estimates.
\par
Such complete observations are rarely gathered. Moreover, one often needs modelling
\par
techniques to estimate life-years gained and costs in later years. For these analyses
\par
sensitivity analysis will remain vel)' useful and it may be expected that it will only be
\par
replaced by a formal procedure like the one presented here in some ideal cases.
