\textbf{\emph{Sample size calculation}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{in economic evaluations}}
\par
\textbf{Summary}
\par
\emph{A simulation method is presented for sample size calculation in economic evaluations. As input}
\par
\emph{the method requires: the expected difference and variance of costs and effects, their}
\par
\emph{correlation, the significance level and the power of the testing method and the maximum}
\par
\emph{acceptable incremental cost-effectiveness ratio. The method is illustrated with data from two}
\par
\emph{trials. The first compares primary coronary angioplasty with streptokinase in the treatment of}
\par
\emph{acute myocardial infarction, in the second trial, lansoprazole is compared with omeprazole in}
\par
\emph{the treatment of reflux oesophagitis. These case studies show how the various parameters}
\par
\emph{influence the sample size.}
\par
\emph{Given the large number of parameters that have to be specified in advance, the lack of}
\par
\emph{knowledge about costs and their standard deviation, and the difficulty of specifying the}
\par
\emph{maximum acceptable cost-effectiveness ratio, the conclusion of the study is that from a}
\par
\emph{technical point of view it is possible to perform a sample size calculation for an economic}
\par
\emph{evaluation, but one should wonder how useful it is.}\textbf{introduction}
\par
In general, randomised double-blind controlled clinical trials are seen as the optimal
\par
method to evaluate the efficacy and safety of a new medical technology. These trials
\par
may be either designed to prove superior efficacy or equivalence of the new treatment,
\par
An important part in the design of a clinical trial is the calculation of the sample size
\par
required to demonstrate, with high statistical significance, that the new treatment is
\par
\textbf{equivalent or superior if indeed it is.}
\par
Nowadays, more and more decision makers are not only interested in safety and
\par
efficacy, but also in the balance between costs en effectiveness (e.g. life years or
\par
quality-adjusted life-years), and a goal of a study may be to prove that the new
\par
treatment is cost-effective, i.e. that the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio (ICER) is
\par
below a certain limit, \emph{As }a result, in the design of a study increasingly the question is
\par
asked what the sample size should be to be able to prove cost-effectiveness. One may
\par
expect the required sample size to be larger compared to efficacy trials, since variation
\par
in costs is usually larger than the variation in treatment efficacy.
\par
For efficacy trials we know that the required sample size is depended on the
\par
assumptions made about efficacy in both treatment groups. If efficacy is measured as
\par
a proportion, assumptions have to be made about only two parameters. Even with this
\par
small number, the influence of small changes in the assumptions on the required
\par
sample size can be large. This problem will only be exacerbated by assessing cost-
\par
\textbf{effectiveness, since assumptions have to be made about many more variables.}
\par
The purpose of this study is twofold. First, we will show how, using a formula for
\par
the confidence interval of the ICER previously published (see Sacristan et al., 1995;
\par
Laska et al., 1997), the required sample size may be estimated. Second, we will assess
\par
\textbf{how sensitive this estimate is for changes in assumptions about the expected costs,}
\par
effectiveness and the correlation between them.
\par
Data from two trials will be used to illustrate this: one comparing primary coronary
\par
angioplasty and streptokinase in the treatment of acute myocardial infarction and the
\par
other comparing the efficacy and safety of lansoprazole with omeprazole in the
\par
treatment of reflux oesophagi tis.
\par
\textbf{CHHPH.R '3 }34\textbf{Theory}
\par
Before sample size calculations can be performed, the hypothesis to be tested, the
\par
method of testing, and the acceptable errors should be known. In testing efficacy the
\par
null-hypothesis usually is E, - Eo \textasciitilde{} 0, with E. as the expected effects in the treatment
\par
group, and Eo representing the expected effects in the comparator group. If the efficacy
\par
parameter is a continuous variable a t-test will be performed to test the hypothesis, if
\par
it is a dichotomous variable, for instance cure, a chi square test might be used. In
\par
testing, the probability that an observed (or more extreme) difference could have been
\par
obtained if the null hypothesis were true is calculated. If this probability is {$<$} \emph{a, }the
\par
null hypothesis is rejected. Often 50/o is chosen for \emph{a. }Thus, the probability of falsely
\par
rejecting the null hypothesis [type I error), is less than 5\%. However, there is also a
\par
chance that one might accept the null hypothesis, when the alternative is true (type II
\par
error). The probability that this happens is usually denoted by \textasciitilde{$\cdot$} The power of a study
\par
to detect an effect of a specified size then is I -
\par
\textasciitilde{$\cdot$} If \emph{a, }the sample size and the effect
\par
one wants to detect are specified, the power may be calculated. On the other hand, it
\par
is also possible to fix \textasciitilde{} in advance and to calculate the required sample size so that
\par
the study will have a high probability of finding a true effect of a given magnitude.
\par
Much literature has already been devoted to sample size calculations for efficacy trials
\par
(e.g. Lwanga 8: Lemeshow, 1991; Cohen, !997).
\par
But imagine that instead of efficacy cost-effectiveness has to be proven. What
\par
should be the null hypothesis, how should it be tested, and how can the required
\par
sample size be calculated? In economic evaluations the variable of interest is the ICER:
\par
