\par
\textbf{comparator group, respectively, and ET and Ec here represent effectiveness rather than}
\par
efficacy. This ratio may be estimated byOne may assume that society will put a limit R on the additional costs that will be
\par
accepted to obtain additional effects. Now, the null hypothesis should be that \emph{/',.C/M. }is
\par
unacceptable, i.e. that it is above the R-line (above the R-line means \emph{/',.C/M. }{$<$} R if \emph{M.{$<$}O}
\par
and \emph{/',.C/M. }{$>$} R if \emph{M.{$>$}O, }see figure 3.1). To test this hypothesis, a confidence interval
