\par
significance level, no limits will be found. This means that at that significance level,
\par
the data are consistent with all possible hypotheses concerning the value of \emph{t.C/M, }and
\par
thus the null-hypothesis can not be rejected.
\par
Fieller's theorem, on which the confidence interval is based, assumes that the
\par
underlying distributions are normal. When working with costs, this will almost
\par
certainly not be the case. However, we are interested in the distribution of the average
\par
costs and the average effects, and the Central Limit Theorem tells that the distribution
\par
of those will approximate a normal distribution if the sample size is large enough. If
\par
our proposed sample size estimation method shows that the required sample size is
\par
small, one might suggest the use of a bootstrap method (see e.g. Chaudhary a Steams,
\par
1996; Briggs et al., 1997; Polsky et al., 1997) for the calculation of the confidence
\par
interval. However, performing a bootstrap simulation within a simulation will increase
\par
computation time considerably.
\par
Since the hypothesis to be tested is one-sided, we seek a 95\% one-sided confidence
\par
interval, which is equivalent to calculating a 90\% two-sided confidence interval (of
\par
which one limit is disregarded). If the confidence interval is now below the R-line, we
\par
can reject the null hypothesis that the ICER is unacceptable, i.e. that it is above the R-
\par
\textbf{line, at the 50/o significance level.}
\par
Having answered the questions what the null hypothesis should be, and how it
\par
should be tested, the next section will present a simulation method to calculate the
\par
\textbf{sample size for a given power and a.}
