\par
If a trial that aims at demonstrating that the lCER is below a certain limit (say,
\par
arbitrarily, NLG 40,000 per additional survivor) with \emph{a. }= 5\% and a power of 80\% is set
\par
up, the required sample size can be calculated by simulation. For this simulation,
\par
estimations of the average costs (!lcr, !lee) and effects (!lrr, !lee) in both groups, the
\par
standard deviation of the costs (crcr, creel and effects (O'ET, O'Ec) for both groups and the
\par
correlation between costs and effects (p) are required. Furthermore, an assumption
\par
should be made about the distribution of the costs and effects in the patient groups.
\par
For the costs, often a log normal distribution will be a realistic assumption, if the effect
\par
measure is dichotomous a binomial distribution is appropriate. It is assumed that the
\par
sample size should be equal in both groups, so \emph{m }= nc = n.
\par
Now the estimations are assumed to represent the true distribution of costs and
\par
effects, and thus we can also calculate the expected ICER, which could be for example
\par
NLG 20,000. Since this ICER is indeed below R = NLG 40,000, we expect that the trial
\par
outcome will lead to rejection of the null hypothesis that the ICER is above the R-line.
\par
The next step is to simulate trial outcomes by drawing random samples from the
\par
specified cost and effect distributions. First this is done for a trial with the arbitrary
\par
sample size n=25. For this sample, it can be tested by means of the method explainedin the TheOiy section, whether the ICER is above the R-line. If the confidence interval
\par
includes R, the ICER is unacceptable, and thus the null hypothesis will be accepted.
\par
Remember that the distributions of costs and effects are specified in such a way that
\par
we know that the true outcome (NLG 20,000) lies below the line R = NLG 40,000. So,
\par
by accepting the null hypothesis we will make an error.
\par
If 1,000 samples of size 25 are drawn, sometimes the null hypothesis is rejected, in
\par
the other samples it will be accepted. By counting how often the null hypothesis is
\par
rightly rejected, the power may be estimated. In this example, if for 400 of the 1000
\par
trials with a sample size of 25 the right decision is made, the power will be 40\%. Since
\par
this power is too low, the procedure can be repeated, now with a sample size of n=50.
\par
By subsequently increasing the sample size, the required sample size for a power of
\par
80\% can be found. It should be emphasised that the limit R should be specified in
\par
advance, since this limit has a large influence on the power of the study.
\par
To summarise, a backward procedure is used to calculate the required sample size
\par
i,e. for a given n, the power is calculated. \emph{As }long as the power does not equal the value
\par
predefined, the sample size is changed and the new power is calculated. This is repeated
\par
until the power has reached the required value. This simulation method is easily
\par
programmed, for this study Pascal was used.
\par
Below, the proposed method will be illustrated with data from two clinical trials. In
\par
the first, both additional costs and additional effects occur, in the second, effects are
\par
equal for both treatment groups, and the additional costs are mainly dependent on the
\par
price difference between the two drugs considered.
\par
