\textbf{sample size.Discussion}
\par
Analogous to the calculation of sample size in efficacy or equivalence trials, one would
\par
like to calculate sample size for economic evaluations. This chapter presents a
\par
simulation method that enables the calculation of sample sizes for economic
\par
evaluations, by using the confidence interval for ICERs that has been published by
\par
several authors (e.g. Sacristan et al., 1995; Laska et al., 1997).
\par
\textbf{It is important to realise that for sample size calculation in economic evaluations,}
\par
more prior information is required compared to efficacy or equivalence trials. In the
\par
illustrations presented in this chapter only dichotomous effect measures have been
\par
used. If instead life-years gained or quality-adjusted life-years (QALYs) gained are used,
\par
not only the expected outcome should be estimated, but the standard deviation as well.
\par
Furthermore, the required sample size is influenced by the expected costs and their
\par
standard deviations and the correlation between costs and effects. In practice it may be
\par
difficult to find a reasonable estimate for the expected costs, and it will be even more
\par
difficult to find an estimate for the standard deviation of the costs. Moreover, the
\par
variation in costs will depend on how detailed resource utilisation is measured. For
\par
instance, if it is expected that some patients in a study may need a PICA, one might
\par
decide to assign a fixed price to every PICA performed, but one may also record length
\par
of hospital stay, all procedures, medication etc. per patient, to find the costs of the
\par
PICA. The second method will lead to a larger standard deviation of the costs, and thus
\par
to a larger required sample size.
\par
Besides the above mentioned variables the significance level and the power of the
\par
testing method should be specified in advance, but, moreover, the hypothesis to be
\par
tested should also be clearly defined, that is, it should be decided in advance which
\par
ICER is the maximum acceptable ratio. Defining this maximum acceptable ratio will
\par
not be easy in practice. If QALYs are used as a measure of effectiveness, one may look
\par
at the ICER of other technologies that have already been approved. Here, a distinction
\par
may have to be made between prophylactic treatment policies (e.g. breast cancer
\par
screening), common therapies (e.g. treatment of heart failure with ACE-inhibitors) and
\par
'high tech' procedures (e.g. heart transplant). Unfortunately, many clinical trials do not
\par
use QALYs or life years as a measure of effectiveness, but intermediate (efficacy)
\par
measures, like disease-free period, or percentage cured. Such effectiveness measures
\par
make it difficult to compare technologies, and to decide what is acceptable. In our case
\par
studies, this problem clearly arises in the comparison of lansoprazole versus
\par
omeprazole. How does one determine how much society is willing to pay to cure one
\par
extra patient with reflux oesophagitis?
\par
From the above it will be clear that it will not be difficult to manipulate the outcome
\par
of a sample size calculation. If one prefers a sample size of 100 for practical reasons,
\par
it is possible to specifY parameters in such a way that the calculations result in a sample
\par
size of 100. So, one may conclude that from a technical point of view it is possible to
\par
perform a sample size calculation for an economic evaluation, but one should wonder
\par
how useful it is.
\par
Regarding the technicalities of sample size calculation a remark should be made.
\par
\textbf{CHAPTER. 3 }44Stinnett (1996) has shown that the estimator for the ICER, as presented in the theory
\par
section, is biased but consistent. Consistent estimators are asymptotically unbiased, and
\par
therefore the bias may be negligible in studies with large sample sizes but is potentially
\par
important when sample sizes are small. This bias does not only influence the point
\par
estimate of the ICER, but also its confidence interval. Both Chaudhary a Steams (1996)
\par
and Briggs et a!. (1997) suggest that the so-called bias-corrected and accelerated
\par
bootstrap interval, which adjusts for bias of the estimator, may be used. For large
\par
sample sizes, the Fieller confidence interval and this bootstrap interval seem
\par
comparable. For smaller sample sizes, it might be better to use the bootstrap confidence
\par
interval, both because of the bias and because the parametric assumptions necessary to
\par
use Fieller may not be valid for small sample sizes. One should realise, however, that
\par
performing a bootstrap simulation within the sample size simulation will lead to a large
\par
\textbf{increase of computation time.}
\par
Finally, one ought to be aware of the fact that the uncertainties in health care are
\par
not only of a statistical nature. An example might be the price difference between
\par
lansoprazole and omeprazole. Given the price difference one may state quite firmly
\par
that lansoprazole is very cost-effective. However, if the cost difference between both
\par
agents decreases, for example due to a price reduction of omeprazole, treatment with
\par
lansoprazole will be less or not cost-effective. One should take into account that just
\par
as with clinical trials research in health care is a snapshot in time and extrapolation to
\par
the future or to other countries may be difficult. That of course makes it even more
\par
important that statistical procedures are available to address those uncertainties in
\par
\textbf{economic evaluations that \emph{are }of a statistical nature.}
