Summary
\par
\emph{New results about the costs and effects of a new therapy may be weighted with prior}
\par
\emph{information. As such, classical confidence intervals surrounding the costs and effects of a}
\par
\emph{therapy may not reflect the real uncertainties. Bayesian techniques may improve this by}
\par
\emph{forma/ising the way that prior information is taken into account in assessing the new evidence.}
\par
\emph{Costs and effects can be analysed separately, but also, when considering the balance between}
\par
\emph{costs and effects, they can be analysed simultaneously. Here, an example is given using data}
\par
\emph{from two trials that compared costs and effectiveness of stent implantation versus balloon}
\par
\emph{angioplasty. The Bayesian results make it clear that different prior distributions may lead to}
\par
\emph{different decisions and it is concluded that even Bayesian analysis may not always reflect the}
\par
\emph{process of capturing the remaining uncertainties.}\textbf{Introduction}
\par
Economic evaluations assessing the balance between the costs and effects of a therapy
\par
may not only summarise the results in terms of single point estimates for the
\par
incremental costs, effects and cost-effectiveness ratio [ICER), but they may also indicate
\par
what the uncertainties are surrounding these estimates. Even when the uncertainty
\par
around the difference in efficacy is reasonable or small, the uncertainty around the
\par
difference in costs and the lCER can be quite large. However, the real uncertainties that
\par
remain after a study has been completed may vmy from what is suggested by the data
\par
alone. There may be prior information or prior ideas and the results will often be valued
\par
with this prior knowledge in mind. Bayesian analysis offers a method to take explicit
\par
account of this, potentially reducing the uncertainty surrounding the cost-effectiveness
\par
\textbf{ratio.}
\par
An additional advantage of using a Bayesian approach is that the interpretation of
\par
the results is more appealing. In the classical frequentist way of analysing data, results
\par
are often dichotomised: a difference is either significant or not. And although some
\par
journals now propagate the use of confidence intervals instead of P-values, the
\par
interpretation of the confidence interval within a frequentist framework is hardly
\par
appealing. A frequentist 95\% confidence interval tells us that if the trial was repeated
\par
often enough and if a confidence interval was calculated each time, 95\% of those
\par
intervals would contain the true difference. Most people, however, are inclined to
\par
interpret the interval as having a 95\% chance that the true difference lies within the
\par
interval. Within the frequentist framework this interpretation is not allowed, whereas
\par
it is within the Bayesian framework.
\par
Many articles have been published propagating the use of Bayesian analysis in
\par
biostatistics (e.g. Breslow, 1989: Spiegelhalter et al., 1993; Kadane, 1995: Brophy \&
\par
Joseph, 1995; Lilford \& Braunholtz, 1996) and pointing out the potential advantages
\par
of using Bayesian analysis in economic evaluations (e.g. Eddy et al., 1990; Manning et
\par
a!., 1996; Jones, 1996). Moreover, Manning et al. (1996) state that a major goal of
\par
methodologists in cost-effectiveness analysis should be to further explore methods like
\par
Bayesian analysis. And while some first steps were taken by Eddy et al. (1990) about
\par
10 years ago, concentrating on univariate analyses estimating single parameters, it was
\par
only recently that Heiijan et a!. (1999) .addressed Bayesian estimation of a cost-
\par
\textbf{effectiveness ratio, using non-informative priors.}
\par
\textbf{CHAPiER 4 }48\textbf{In this chapter, we summarise our first Bayesian experiences of an economic}
\par
analysis based on data from a randomised clinical trial also using informative priors.
\par
First. a univariate approach for the analysis of the differences in costs and effects is
\par
presented. Second, some initial ideas about a multivariate approach for the analysis of
\par
the ICER are presented. Subsequently, the methodology is applied using data from the
\par
Benestent I and II trials (see Serruys et a!.. 1994; Serruys et a!., 1996; Serruys et a!.,
\par
1998). For both trials, the balance was assessed between the costs and effects of stent
\par
implantation in comparison with balloon angioplasty. Finally, the results are reviewed
\par
by asking how the Bayesian results compare with the classical ones in reflecting our
\par
\textbf{uncertainties.Background}
\par
\textbf{Currently, there are a number of ways to assess the uncertainties surrounding estimates}
\par
of costs, effects and the ICER. Within the recent literature, the assessment of the ratio
\par
has drawn most attention, and currently there are at least two methods that are
\par
commonly used to find a confidence interval surrounding this ratio: Fieller's method
\par
(Fieller, 1954) and bootstrapping (see e.g. Chaudhal)' tt Steams. 1996). Both methods
\par
do not use any prior information and inference is solely based on the data from the
\par
trial. Usually no assumption is made about the underlying distribution of costs and
\par
effects. In practice it is only assumed that the average costs and effects follow a normal
\par
distribution which is reasonable for sample sizes large enough. Which sample size is
\par
large enough depends on the underlying distributions (see Briggs tt Gray, 1998a), for
\par
binomial distributions, for example, a rule of thumb is np{$>$}5 and n(1-p){$>$}5. When the
\par
number of observations is large, both methods lead to similar results concerning the
\par
distribution surrounding the ICER.
\par
In order to carry out a Bayesian analysis, it is necessal)' to assume parametric
\par
probability distributions for both costs and effects and their simultaneous distribution.
\par
When such a probability distribution is seen as a function of its parameters, for fixed
\par
observations, it is called the likelihood function. Additionally, prior distributions need
\par
to be specified for the parameters of both distributions. Subsequently, posterior
\par
distributions are obtained after combining the data from the experiment with the
\par
knowledge reflected by the prior distributions. Thus, the posterior distributions reflect
\par
all uncertainties after having carried out the study.
\par
A variety of likelihood functions can be chosen for costs and effects and an
\par
additional variety of distributions can be chosen for the priors. Generally, both the
\par
likelihood function and the prior distribution may be extremely flexible, such as a
\par
mixture of normals. Most combinations of prior distribution and likelihood will result
\par
in posteriors that do not follow any standard parametric form. Subsequently,
\par
evaluation of such posteriors may require extensive simulation methods, like Markov
\par
Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) methods (see e.g. Gelman et al., 1995; Carlin tt Louis,
\par
1996). If one does not want to use these simulation methods, the potential choices of
\par
distributions are rather limited. Here, we will avoid MCMC simulation by choosing
\par
convenient priors for the parameters of the distributions and we will concentrate on
\par
the situation where costs may be characterised by a log normal distribution and where
\par
effectiveness is described by a dichotomous variable.
\par
If one has no prior ideas about the distribution of the parameters being studied, one
\par
may choose a non-informative prior. For the mean of a normal distribution, a non-
\par
\textbf{CHAPTER 4 }50informative prior would be a distribution giving equal weight to all values from -= to
\par
= (e.g. Carlin 8: Louis, !996). However, a prior distribution may also be more
\par
\textbf{informative, for example based on the ideas of one or more experts. In this case, it is}
\par
advisable to explore a number of different priors and see how the conclusions after
\par
combining data with these priors differ.
\par
Another possibility is to use data from previously completed studies. This approach
\par
is followed by Eddy et al. (1990): updating the distribution of the parameter as data
\par
comes along. Here, if a technology is studied for the first time, a non-informative prior
\par
is used. Subsequently, when a second study is conducted, the posterior from the first
\par
analysis is used as a prior for the analysis of the second study, and so on. In practice,
\par
such an approach will lead to similar results as a 'classical' fixed-effect meta-analysis.
\par
In the example discussed here, the last approach will be followed. Additionally,
\par
when addressing the multivariate model, we will also follow the idea of Brophy 8:
\par
Joseph (!995) and we will weigh the data from the previous trial when specifYing the
\par
prior distribution. This is achieved by using the observed mean from the previous
\par
trial(s) as the mean of the prior, and by multiplying the observed variance from the
\par
previous trials with a factor greater than one to define the variance of the prior. This
\par
factor is chosen greater than one to reflect additional uncertainty due to unknown
\par
factors.\textbf{Methods}
\par
