After calculating the posterior distributions for two treatment groups, the posterior
\par
distribution of incremental costs and incremental effects can be found by using the fact
\par
that the difference of two independent normally distributed variables is itself normally
\par
distributed, with the mean equal to the difference between the two means, and the
\par
(co)variance equal to the sum of the two (co)variances. And, with this multivariate
\par
posterior distribution, the ICER can be calculated with its 95\% posterior interval (see
\par
Fieller, 1954).
\par
Another way of describing the uncertainty surrounding the estimate of the ICER is
\par
to use the acceptability curve presented in chapter 2. Wakker a Klaassen (1995) pointed
\par
out that, in the frequentist framework, the acceptability curve can not be seen as a
\par
confidence interval in a formal statistical sense. Within the Bayesian framework
\par
however, the interpretation of the acceptability curve becomes straightforward: this
\par
curve gives for every limit (R) that society might place on the ICER the probability that
\par
the ICER is indeed below this limit As Briggs a Penn (1998) argue, the information
\par
contained in the acceptability curve will often be more in line with the information
\par
required by decision makers. It should be noted that an ICER is considered acceptable
\par
either if the incremental effects are larger than zero and the ICER is smaller than R or
\par
if the incremental costs and effects are both smaller than zero and the ICER is larger
\par
than R. This means that, assuming R=50,000, an ICER of 1,000/0.1=10,000 is
\par
acceptable, whereas an ICER of -1,000/ -0.1=10,000 is not acceptable.\textbf{An example}
\par
Here we will apply the ideas presented above to the analysis of the Benestent I and
\par
Benestent II studies. In both trials, the balance between costs and effectiveness of stent
\par
implantation in comparison with balloon angioplasty was assessed. The first study,
\par
carried out between 1990 and 1993 (see Serruys et al., 1994), was a landmark study
\par
\textbf{which showed that stems were superior in terms of the prevention of revascularisations}
\par
but that treatment was associated with increased risk of bleedings and increased
\par
hospital stay. In the second study, carried out between 1995 and 1997, a heparin-coated
\par
stent was used in combination with a better anticoagulation regimen, and as tested in
\par
a pilot study, this decreased the risk of bleeding and lead to no excess hospital stay (see
\par
Serruys et a!., 1996; Serruys et al., 1998). Effectiveness was defined as {$\cdot$}event-free
\par
\textbf{survival' after 1 year, where events were: death, myocardial infarction, cerebrovascular}
\par
accident and revascularisations. Costs included the costs of the initial procedure,
\par
follow-up [including the costs of events), medication and diagnostic procedures.
\par
For the analysis of the stent group in the Benestent I trial, it seems very reasonable
\par
\textbf{to assume a non-informative prior, since little was known about the costs and}
\par
effectiveness of stent procedures at that time. For the balloon angioplasty group one
\par
might have a less vague idea about costs and effectiveness prior to the analysis of the
\par
Benestent I, but here we will assume a non-informative prior for this group too.
\par
Before the results of the Benestent II were available, it seemed reasonable to expect
\par
the results of the balloon angioplasty group to be similar to the results of the Benestent
\par
I. For the stent group however, an improvement in effectiveness might be anticipated.
\par
With this in mind, a non-informative prior for the stent group was chosen for the
\par
analysis of the Benestent II. For the balloon angioplasty group, three prior distributions
\par
were specified: a non-informative prior (thus disregarding all information from the
\par
Benestent I), a prior equal to the posterior of the Benestent I analysis, and a prior using
\par
only 50\% of the information from the Benestent I trial (this latter prior is only used in
\par
the multivariate analysis).
\par
All calculations and simulations were done using a spreadsheet program (Quattro
\par
Pro).
