\par
(Note also that all values have positive incremental effects.) The question whether the
\par
three different posteriors lead to different decisions depends on the limit that society
\par
puts on the ICER. If the limit is about NLG 10,000 per additional event-free survivor,
\par
the probabilities that the ICERs are acceptable are almost identical (20\%). However, if
\par
the limit is about NLG 30,000, the probabilities differ substantially (65\%, 80\%, 90\%),
\par
presumably leading to quite different decisions.\textbf{Discussion}
\par
This chapter has explored a Bayesian approach to economic analyses of clinical trials.
\par
First, a univariate method was discussed where costs and effectiveness were analysed
\par
separately. Second, a multivariate approach for the analysis of the ICER was presented,
\par
taking account of the covariance between costs and effects. Both types of analyses
\par
were illustrated with data from two clinical trials. For the illustration of the
\par
multivariate approach, three different prior distributions for the balloon angioplasty
\par
group were defined, each leading to a different posterior distribution.
\par
Within our approach, the goal was to keep the computations as simple as possible,
\par
\textbf{as the main purpose was to get an impression of how the use of prior information}
\par
influences the outcome. Unfortunately, almost any full model leads to complicated
\par
posterior distributions that require advanced techniques to evaluate them. Even in the
\par
simple example of the Benestent study we had to rely on an approximation method
\par
that is expected to work well only if the sample size is large enough (some simple
\par
simulations indicate that n should be at least 50). In that case, one may assume that at
\par
least the average costs and effects follow a normal distribution and that the posterior
\par
(co)variance will be very close to the observed (co)variance. Naturally, the next step
\par
towards a multivariate approach must be to work with the full model for individual
\par
observations and to compare the results of MCMC approaches with the results of the
\par
\textbf{current approximation method.}
\par
In general, if non-informative priors are used, the results of the Bayesian and
\par
frequentist analysis will be similar, but as was mentioned in the introduction, an
\par
important advantage of using Bayes is the straightforward interpretation of the
\par
confidence interval and the acceptability curve. The 95\% confidence interval (or
\par
posterior interval) now tells us that the probability that the ICER is in this interval is
\par
0.95, and the acceptability curve actually tells us for various limits on the ICER what
\par
the probability is that the ICER is acceptable.
\par
\textbf{As such, using non-informative priors has its advantages. However, in order to take}
\par
full advantage of everything Bayesian analysis has to offer, one may want to use
\par
\textbf{informative priors. Here, an example was used where data from an earlier trial were}
\par
used as the prior for a later trial, an approach that leads to similar results as obtained
\par
by weighted pooling of the data in a frequentist meta-analysis. The example led to
\par
some striking results. At the start of the Benestent II trial, it was expected that the costs
\par
and effects in the balloon angioplasty arm would be similar to the results in the
\par
Benestent I trial. However, while costs were very similar, the effectiveness differed by
\par
more than 10\%, suggesting that the two balloon angioplasty groups may not have been
\par
as comparable as thought beforehand.
\par
This is why it is recommended, when using a Bayesian approach, to calculate a
\par
posterior distribution for a number of prior distributions. These priors should represent
\par
the various ideas that experts and decision makers may have on the subject. If the
\par
posterior distribution is more or less the same for each of these priors, the choice of
\par
prior is apparently not very important, If on the other hand the posterior distributions
\par
clearly differ, it might be an indication that more research is required.
\par
\textbf{Cn.qPTER 4 }64One aspect of Bayesian analysis that has not been discussed in this chapter is model
\par
checking. As in frequentist analysis, it is important to check the adequacy of the fit of
\par
the model to the data. Gelman et al. (1995) devote a chapter of their book on methods
\par
for model checking. In frequentist analysis, assumptions about the underlying
\par
distribution of costs and effects are seldom made explicit, and consequently model
\par
checking is hardly ever seen as part of a study. Within the Bayesian framework, explicit
\par
assumptions are made about the distribution of costs and effects and about the prior
\par
distribution, and thus an explicit, critical evaluation of the model may always be
\par
needed. An advantage of a Bayesian approach is that testing hypotheses about different
\par
models (model refers here both to the likelihood function and the prior distribution) is
\par
fairly straightforward, with no requirement about hypotheses being nested and no limit
\par
on the number of hypotheses considered simultaneously.
\par
In the analysis of the Benestent II, we found unexpected results in the balloon
\par
angioplasty group, i.e. an improved event-free survival compared to the Benestent !.
\par
This was probably the result of the availability of bailout stenting, permitting a more
\par
aggressive approach to balloon angioplasty. With this in mind, it might have been
\par
better to have used a completely different model for the data. The method used in this
\par
chapter, using the posterior of the Benestent I as a prior for the Benestent II, is
\par
comparable to a fixed-effect meta-analysis. Given the outcome of the Benestent II, a
\par
so-called hierarchical model might have been preferable. Such a model is similar to a
\par
random-effects model in frequentist meta-analysis. However, exploring such a model
\par
would require computations that are far more complex.
\par
\textbf{Finally, our exploration of Bayesian analysis in economic evaluations does not}
\par
answer the question whether all health economists should become Bayesians. We
\par
expect that disadvantages of a technical nature, for example having to calculate the
\par
posterior distribution, may be overcome. However, we also expect the problems about
\par
formulating the prior distributions will never be resolved. What is known and what is
\par
\textbf{not, and how to reflect this, may remain an art and may never become a science. On}
\par
the one hand, one would like to define one's prior knowledge beforehand. However,
\par
one may then get the peculiar results as presented in our example. On the other hand,
\par
one might be tempted to redefine the prior after assessment of the posterior, so that the
\par
posterior will lead to a certain decision. This seems extremely opportunistic, and this
\par
may also not be what we are searching for.
\par
In conclusion, we feel that the potentials of the Bayesian approach need to be
\par
investigated further. In Bayesian terms, we may conclude that adding our experience
\par
to our prior expectations has left us with a posterior distribution with a large variance.Acknowledgements
