Summary
\par
\emph{The objective of this study was to estimate the cost-effectiveness of treatment with a fixed}
\par
\emph{dose combination of diclofenac and misoprostol compared with diclofenac monotherapy in the}
\par
\emph{prevention of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory (NSA/0]-induced ulcers in rheumatoid arthritis}
\par
\emph{(RA) patients.}
\par
\emph{A model was used to incorporate estimates of costs, incidence of ulcers and their}
\par
\emph{complications, death rates and the efficacy of misoprostol. The costs per ulcer-free period}
\par
\emph{gained and costs per additional survivor were calculated. Cost-effectiveness was calculated for}
\par
\emph{the treatment af all RA patients, and of risk groups only.}
\par
\emph{The analysis showed that if 100 RA patients receive 3 months of treatment with dic/ofenac plus}
\par
\emph{misoprostol, instead of diclofenac alone, this will lead to overall additional costs of NLG 773,}
\par
\emph{while 0.82 symptomatic ulcers and 0.019 deaths will be prevented. If this treatment is given}
\par
\emph{only to patients at high risk for NSA/0-induced ulcer, cost-savings will occur instead of}
\par
\textbf{\emph{additional costs. Univariate sensitivity analysis showed that the outcomes are sensitive to}}
\par
\emph{changes in: the percentage af ulcers treated in the ambulatory setting, the price difference}
\par
\emph{between diclofenac and the fixed dose diclofenac/misoprostol combination, the percentage of}
\par
\emph{ulcers with complications, and the efficacy of misoprostol.}
\par
\emph{In conclusion it can be stated that treatment with dic/ofenac/misoprostol is cost-saving in RA}
\par
\emph{patients at high risk for NSA/0-induced ulcers. For RA patients in general, the cost-}
\par
\emph{effectiveness of this intervention compares favourably with that of other preventive policies.}\textbf{Introduction}
\par
Rheumatoid arthritis [RAJ is a chronic, multisystem disease that occurs in about 1 to
\par
2\% of the Dutch population (see Valkenburg, 1980). Women are affected about 2.5
\par
times as frequently as men, and the total direct medical costs related to rheumatic
\par
diseases amount to 160 million Dutch Guilders (NLG) per year (see e.g. Valkenburg,
\par
1980; Koopmanschap et al., 1991). Although pain, swelling and erosions of multiple
\par
joints are the most common characteristics of established RA, extra-articular features
\par
may also occur, for example in the lungs, heart, kidney and skin.
\par
Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAJD) play an important role in the
\par
treatment of RA, because of their anti-inflammatory effect and ability to relieve pain.
\par
Unfortunately, NSAJD treatment is associated with serious adverse effects such as
\par
gastritis, peptic ulceration (complicated by bleeding or perforation) and platelet
\par
dysfunction, which may even be fatal. Much research has been done to find drugs that
\par
may prevent these gastrointestinal (GI) adverse effects. In 1988, Graham et a!. found
\par
that NSAJD-treated osteoarthritis patients who were receiving misoprostol developed
\par
significantly fewer gastric ulcers compared with controls.
\par
Several cost-effectiveness analyses of prophylactic treatment with misoprostol were
\par
based on this study (e.g. Hillman a Bloom, 1989; Edelson et al., 1990; Knill-Jones et
\par
al., 1990; Drummond eta!., 1992; Jonsson a Haglund, 1992; Gabriel et al., 1993). Some
\par
of these analyses showed that cost-savings were associated with misoprostol
\par
prophylaxis, whereas others did not. A critical appraisal of these studies was published
\par
by Stucki et al. (1994), addressing the causes for those differences. However, since then,
\par
the results of trials investigating the use of misoprostol in RA patients, trials including
\par
patients with both gastric and duodenal ulcer as endpoints, and trials in which
\par
misoprostol was administered in a fixed dose combination with the NSAJD didofenac
\par
have been published (see Bolten eta!., 1992; Verdickt eta!., 1992; Melo Gomes et al.,
\par
1993; Graham et al., 1993; Barradell et al., 1993). Also, the effects of misoprostol in
\par
serious GI complications, such as bleeding ulcers, has been investigated by Silverstein
\par
et al. (1995). With this new information, the balance between costs and effects of
\par
misoprostol prophylaxis may be re-evaluated (see Pouvourville, 1995).
\par
The objective of this study was to compare the costs and effects of a fixed dose
\par
combination (fdc) of didofenac and misoprostol with those of didofenac alone in the
\par
treatment of RA patients.
\par
\textbf{Methods}
\par
The current analysis evaluated the cost-effectiveness of an fdc of misoprostol and
\par
didofenac in comparison with didofenac monotherapy in patients with RA, for the
\par
Dutch situation. An attempt was made to calculate medical costs from a societal
\par
viewpoint, i.e. where possible, real costs were used. Nonmedical and indirect costs were
\par
not taken into account. The analysis is mainly based on two recently published studies
\par
\textbf{C'-!APTER 5 }68of the clinical effectiveness of misoprostol in patients with RA.
\par
The first study compared an fdc of diclofenac/misoprostol with diclofenac alone in
\par
the prevention of endoscopically diagnosed ulcers, in treatment groups of about 170
\par
patients (see Verdickt et al., 1992). The trial lasted for 12 weeks. At the end of this
\par
period, all patients had an endoscopy to investigate the gastric and duodenal mucosae
\par
for lesions and ulcers. In the fdc group, 6 out of 137 (4.40/o) patients had developed an
\par
ulcer, compared with 17 out of 153 (11.1\%) patients in the didofenac group. no;o of
\par
patients in the fdc group withdrew because of adverse events, compared with 8.5\% of
\par
those in the didofenac group.
\par
In the second study, patients receiving continuous treatment for RA symptoms with
\par
NSA!Ds were either assigned to misoprostol or to placebo to study the effectiveness of
\par
misoprostol in the prevention of serious GI complications (perforated or bleeding ulcer,
\par
endoscopically proven, see Silverstein eta!., 1995). Both treatment groups consisted of
\par
about 4400 patients and the duration of the trial was 6 months. In the NSAID plus
\par
misoprostol group, 16 out of 4404 (0.360/o) patients had a perforated or bleeding ulcer
\par
(with a proven lesion), while in the NSAID plus placebo group this proportion was 33
\par
out of 4438 (0.74\%) patients. Premature withdrawal because of adverse effects occurred
\par
in 27.5\% of the patients in the misoprostol group and 20\% of those in the placebo
\par
group.
\par
\textbf{The model}
\par
Because: (D no data on costs were gathered alongside the trials; (IT) both trials
\par
addressed different aspects ofNSAID-induced GI complications; and (Ill) both trials did
\par
not completely reflect common medical practice in The Netherlands, we constructed a
\par
mathematical model to convene data from various sources. Using the model, the costs
\par
and effects of several scenarios were compared.
\par
We defined two baseline scenarios. In the first scenario, 100 RA patients receive
\par
didofenac [50 mg, twice daily (bid) or 3 times daily (tid)] for 3 months. In the second
\par
scenario, they receive fdc didofenac/misoprostol (50 mg + 200 f.lg, bid or tid). In
\par
several alternative scenarios, only RA patients with an elevated risk for ulcers are
\par
treated with fdc didofenac/misoprostol. We used the results of a study by Fries et al.
\par
(1991) to identify the most important risk factors, namely prednisone use, history of GI
\par
\textbf{events, age{$>$} 75 years, or a combination of these.}
