a!., 1987; Farah et aL, 1988). In tbese studies, a random selection of RA patients
\par
underwent gastroscopy and were asked whetber tbey had experienced GI symptoms.
\par
However, it is not to be expected tbat all patients who reported GI symptoms visited a
\par
physician to seek treatment for these symptoms. Since we were only interested in ulcers
\par
tbat require ambulatory treatment, tbe higher percentages were not used in tbe baseline
\par
scenario, but were included in a separate sensitivity analysis.
\par
The proportion of complicated ulcers that are preceded by ambulatory treatment was
\par
derived from two studies in which patients admitted to hospital for complications were
\par
asked about previous symptoms and about medication for ulcer treatment (see
\par
Armstrong a Blower, 1987; Jorde eta!., 1988). Witb tbese findings, it was estimated
\par
that approximately 10\% of tbe patients who were admitted to hospital for
\par
\textbf{complications were started on antiulcer medication for an endoscopically proven ulcer}
\par
\textbf{before their admission.}
\par
Using Dutch nationwide hospital registration data (see SIG Zorginformatie, 1993),
\par
we determined tbe number of admissions for complicated ulcers in The Netberlands in
\par
1991. This was related to tbe number of surgical procedures for complicated ulcers in
\par
1989, after adjusting tbis number to 1991 values according to tbe linear time trend
\par
between 1985 and 1989 (see De Nederlandse Lever Darm Stichting, 1992). Thus, we
\par
calculated tbat 22.5\% of patients witb complicated ulcers require surgery.
\par
Since treatment of ulcers varies witb type, an assumption about tbe relative
\par
proportions of gastric and duodenal ulcers had to be made. In tbe baseline scenarios,
\par
it was assumed tbat didofenac treatment causes 20\% more gastric tban duodenal ulcers
\par
(i.e. 6 gastric ulcers for every 5 duodenal ulcers; see e.g. Mellem et aL, 1985; Farah et
\par
al., 1988; McCarthy, 1989).
\par
Several studies have reported the percentage of patients taking fdc
\par
didofenac/misoprostol tbat discontinued tbe study medication because of adverse
\par
effects. The overall percentage of patients witbdrawing was estimated to be 4.2\% (see
\par
Geis, 1992).
\par
To estimate tbe proportion of patients who die as a result of GI complications we
\par
used tbe results of a large prospective study by Fries (1991) of 2,700 RA patients
\par
(average follow-up 3.5 years) tbat reported an excess GI deatb rate of 0.15\% per year.
