\par
Combining information from the literature (see Van Berge Henegouwen 8: Bijlsma,
\par
1991; McGuigan, 1994) and information about treatment policy in two Dutch general
\par
hospitals, we developed treatment profiles for an 'average' patient. For ambulatmy
\par
treatment, it was assumed that patients are referred for gastroscopy by either their
\par
general practitioner (GP) or rheumatologist. During the gastroscopy, biopsies are done
\par
(gastric ulcer is checked for both helicobacter pylori and malignancy, duodenal ulcer
\par
for helicobacter pylori only). If a gastric ulcer is found, the patient has to return for a
\par
control gastroscopy after 6 or 8 weeks, to check whether the ulcer has healed. Of the
\par
patients with an ulcer, 43\% receive treatment with ranitidine (for 8 weeks), 38.6\% with
\par
