Sensitivity analysis
\par
The sensitivity of the results to variation in the input variables was studied both
\par
univariately and multivariately. The univariate analysis shows which variables
\par
influence the outcome significantly. This analysis was carried out by subsequently
\par
increasing and decreasing all variables with 20\%. If changing a variable with 20\%
\par
altered the outcomes by 100/o or less, the outcome was considered not sensitive to that
\par
variable. Changes in the outcomes of more than 10\% were reported. The multivariate
\par
analysis determines the most probable outcome, and also the 'worst' and the 'best'
\par
possible outcomes. For this purpose, a probability distribution was defined for each
\par
variable. Where possible, distributions were based on trial data. For cost estimates, a
\par
range of either \textpm 10\% or \textpm 200/o (depending on the uncertainty regarding the estimate)
\par
was used. For the percentage of ulcers that were treated ambulatory, a log normal
\par
distribution was defined with a mean of 10\% and an upper boundary of 44\%.
