78\textbf{Discussion}
\par
The current cost-effectiveness analysis shows that preventive treatment with fdc
\par
didofenac/misoprostol in all RA patients will generate symptomatic ulcer-free periods
\par
\textbf{and life years in return for extra costs. However. if this preventive treatment is given}
\par
only to risk groups, such as patients treated with prednisone and patients with a history
\par
of GI events, it will also save costs.
\par
Of course, this conclusion depends on the assumptions incorporated in this model.
\par
\textbf{Therefore, an extensive sensitivity- analysis was performed. The univariate analysis}
\par
demonstrated that, in particular, the results are influenced by the costs of didofenac
\par
and fdc didofenac/misoprostol, the probability of endoscopically proven ulcers and
\par
complicated ulcers, and the percentage of ulcers that require ambulatory treatment. The
\par
multivariate analysis showed that the median costs per additional survivor amount to
\par
NLG 42,788, but that these costs may vary between -NLG 172,000 and NLG 569,000
\par
(table 5.6).
\par
Our estimates on the number of complicated ulcers and the efficacy of misoprostol
\par
in preventing these ulcers were obtained from a study by Silverstein et al. (1995). It
\par
should be noted that in this study the average daily dosage of misoprostol was 650 J.Lg,
\par
whereas in our model an average dosage of 500 J.Lg was used. The latter equals the
\par
average recommended daily dosage for fdc didofenac/misoprostol of 50 mg/200 J.Lg bid
\par
or tid in The Netherlands (see Dutch Health Insurance Funds Council, 1995). Since the
\par
effectiveness of misoprostol is known to be dosage-dependant, the percentage of
\par
complications in the fdc group may well be an underestimate (see Graham eta!., 1988).
\par
On the other hand, in the trial by Silverstein et al., various NSA!Ds were used, some of
\par
them known to cause more, others to cause fewer, ulcers than didofenac. Clearly, every
\par
adjustment of the estimate would be arbitrary, so the results as reported by Silverstein
\par
et a!. were used without modification.
\par
To ensure comparability of our results with those of other cost-effectiveness studies,
\par
we also included costs per life-year gained as a measure of effectiveness. As already
\par
pointed out by Stucki et al. (1994), it would have been even more appropriate to use
\par
quality-adjusted life-years. Quality of life may be affected by misoprostol in two
\par
different ways. On the one hand, patients who have an ulcer may experience a
\par
temporary decrease in their quality of life, particularly patients with a bleeding ulcer.
\par
Misoprostol will prevent part of this decrease. On the other hand, some patients taking
\par
misoprostol will develop diarrhoea, which may also affect their quality of life. Since
\par
this diarrhoea is often self-limiting, it is most likely that the decrease in quality of life
\par
is transient in nature. However, without additional research it is hardly possible to
\par
quantify both effects, and therefore we decided not to estimate quality-adjusted life-
\par
years.Several studies on prophylactic treatment with misoprostol have been published.
\par
However, most of these studies evaluate the cost-effectiveness of misoprostol
\par
prophylaxis in osteoarthritis patients with abdominal pain (see Hillman 8: Bloom, 1989;
\par
Knill-Janes et al., 1990; Jonsson 8: Haglund, 1992; Gabriel eta!., 1993). In a study by
\par
Edelson et al. (1990) prophylaxis for RA patients was considered. Comparing our
\par
results with those of Edelson eta!. is not straightforward, since they measured costs per
\par
episode of GI bleeding prevented as the main outcome. Furthermore, in our study,
\par
misoprostol was administered as an fdc with the NSAJD, as opposed to separately,
\par
which leads to a better compliance. What can be said is that the cost estimates for the
\par
treatment of ulcers and for misoprostol are much higher in the study by Edelson et a!.
\par
than in our study, which may be explained by the differences in the structure of the
\par
health care systems (see OECD, 1987).
\par
If the costs per life-year gained (N!.G 4, 179) are compared with those of other
\par
preventive policies, treatment with fdc diclofenac/misoprostol seems favourable. For
\par
instance, the costs of screening for breast cancer were estimated at N!.G 7,650 per life-
\par
year gained (see De Koning et al., 1991), screening for cervix carcinoma at N!.G 24,000
\par
per life-year gained (see Van Ballegooijen et al., 1992), and cholesterol-lowering
\par
therapy for men with cholesterol level above 8 mmol/1 at about N!.G 30,000 per life-
\par
year gained (see Martens et a!., 1989).
\par
At present, the Dutch Health Insurance Funds Council approves of the use of fdc for
\par
long term NSAJD users at high risk for GI complications. They currently define high
\par
risk patients as the elderly and patients with a histoty of proven GI ulcers. From our
\par
analysis, it appears that fdc diclofenac/misoprostol is not only cost-saving for these
\par
patients, but also for patients using prednisone. The choice of whether or not to
\par
approve of fdc for other RA patients taking NSAJDs long term remains arbitraty. Payers
\par
will probably take into account not only the cost-effectiveness ratio - which seems
\par
favourable compared with other (approved) preventive therapies - but also the
\par
nationwide costs involved. Whereas the additional costs per patient per year are only
\par
minor (N!.G 31), the relatively high prevalence ofRA (I to 2\%) could lead to an annual
\par
investment of several million N!.G.
\par
Even though the results clearly indicate that treatment with fdc
\par
diclofenac/misoprostol in RA patients at high risk for GI adverse reactions leads to both
\par
health gains and economic benefits, physicians may be reluctant to adopt such a
\par
treatment policy. This reluctance may be attributable to a dislike of prescribing
\par
combination therapy, the feeling that fdc treatment does not leave any space for
\par
experimentation with combinations of dosages, and the feeling that it is not rational
\par
to adopt a prophylactic treatment against the adverse effects of NSAJDs when the
\par
treatment itself may cause unpleasant adverse effects. This may be outweighed by the
\par
much lower price of misoprostol in the fixed dose combination (NLG 9 per month in
\par
an fdc, compared with N!.G 64 per month when prescribed separately) and the
\par
convenience of an fdc for patients who are obliged to take many different drugs each
\par
day.
\par
\textbf{CHAPTERS }80\emph{The cost-effectivelll!ess of didofetnac I misoprostaff}
\par
Based on currently available data on tbe effectiveness of misoprostol, epidemiology
\par
of ulcers and costs, it may be concluded tbat treatment witb fdc didofenac/misoprostol
\par
is cost-saving for RA patients at high risk for NSAID-induced Gl adverse effects. For
\par
RA patients in general, tbe cost-effectiveness of tbe treatment policy compares
\par
favourably witb tbat of otber preventive policies.
