\emph{intervention: survival and quality of life improve substantially after transplantation. The costs}
\par
\emph{per life-year gained are relatively high, compared with other interventions and other types af}
\par
\emph{transplantation. Restricting the patient inflow and/or raising donor supply improves cost-}
\par
\emph{effectiveness to some degree. Limiting the extent af inpatient screening or lower future costs}
\par
\emph{of immunosuppressives may slightly improve the cost-effectiveness of the program.}\textbf{Introduction}
\par
Lung transplantation is a fast-growing and expensive medical intervention. World-
\par
wide, about 6,000 lung transplants have been performed in more than 100 centres (see
\par
St. Louis International Lung Transplant Registry, January 1997). However, reliable
\par
information on cost-effectiveness of lung transplantation programs is lacking. To our
\par
knowledge, only one retrospective pilot study on this topic was published thus far, by
\par
Ramsey et al. (1995a). Its small sample size and limited cost-analysis prohibited firm
\par
\textbf{conclusions. In this chapter, we will estimate the cost-effectiveness of various scenarios}
\par
of future lung transplantation, based on detailed data from a large technology
\par
\textbf{assessment of the Dutch lung transplantation program, as performed during 1990 to}
\par
1995 (see TenVergert et al., in press). This study was initiated by the Dutch National
\par
Health Insurance Board, to support public reimbursement decisions.
\par
The scenario analysis describes future transplantation programs, operational for 15
\par
years. This simulation period is necessary to reach a stable number of patients on the
\par
waiting list, which has not yet been established during the observation period. Socie-
\par
tal costs, survival and qualiry of life are followed up to 40 years, comparing the situa-
\par
tion with and without a lung transplantation program.
\par
Several scenarios will be presented: a baseline scenario, a prolongation of the
\par
current program; a policy scenario, restricting the inflow of patients on the waiting list;
\par
and a donor scenario, assuming a larger supply of donor lungs.
\par
\textbf{Material and methods}
\par
From November 1990 until April 1995, data were gathered on all patients who entered
\par
the Dutch lung transplantation program. Patients are eligible for the program if they
\par
have irreversible, progressively disabling end-stage pulmonary or cardiopulmonary
\par
disease with a predicted life expectancy of less than 12 to 18 months (see Boer tt
\par
Mannes, 1993). The first phase of the program is the application phase in which
\par
potential candidates are identified on the basis of written information of the referring
\par
physician. The other phases of the program are outpatient screening, inpatient
\par
screening, pretransplantation, waiting list, transplantation (perioperative and intensive
\par
care), inpatient follow-up, and outpatient follow-up. A total of 425 patients was
\par
referred to the program. Of these patients, 303 entered the outpatient screening phase
\par
and 179 were accepted for the inpatient screening. One hundred-and-twenty patients
\par
were placed on the waiting list. Finally, 57 patients received a transplantation. Two
\par
patients died during the transplantation phase and nine patients died during the
\par
follow-up.
\par
During the screening phase, patients may be rejected, screening may be deferred, or
\par
patients do not contact the lung transplantation team for more than 12 months. With
\par
exception of the application phase, for all phases and for all patients, length of stay
\par
was registered, with the reason for leaving the phase. Furthermore, all costs (direct
\par
\textbf{CHAPTER 6 }84medical, direct nonmedical and indirect nonmedical) and data on quality of life were
\par
collected. In addition, several other patient characteristics were registered, of which we
\par
used diagnosis, age, body length and blood type. Diagnosis and age were used as
\par
explanatory variables for length of stay in various phases and for survival. Length and
\par
blood type were used for matching donor and recipient
\par
For the same period, we also collected data on donor lungs: acquisition date of the
\par
lung and length and blood type of the donor.
\par
Quality of life
\par
The health-related quality of life of the patients was measured through a self-
\par
\textbf{administered questionnaire. It contained several domain-specific instruments}
\par
(Karnofsky Performance Index, Index of Well-Being, Self-rating Depression Scale,
\par
State-Trait Anxiety Inventory, activities of daily living) and two generic instruments:
\par
the Nottingham Health Profile and the EuroQol. Patients were asked to fill out the
\par
questionnaire at the entry of the outpatient screening phase, and from then on every
\par
3 months. Following transplantation, quality of life was measured after 1, 4, and 7
\par
months and from then on every 6 months.
\par
In this analysis, effectiveness is measured both as life-years gained and quality-
\par
adjusted life-years (QALY) gained. For the latter, it is necessary to express quality of
\par
life as a number between 0 and I (utility), where 0 represents the worst possible health
\par
state, and I the best. The EuroQol group has developed an algorithm that allows the
\par
calculation of the utility that represents the health state reported by the patient (see
\par
Bjork, 1992). Note that this utility reflects the value the general population assigns to
\par
health states.
\par
Table 6.1 presents the average quality of life (as measured by the EuroQol score) of
\par
patients who did not die while on the waiting list. It shows that the health-related
\par
