Costs
\par
Data on all direct medical, direct nonmedical and indirect nonmedical costs, i.e., value
\par
of production losses (paid or unpaid work), related to the lung disease, were gathered
\par
for all patients, from the moment they entered the outpatient screening phase until they
\par
left the program. Where possible, full resource costs were estimated (see Enckevort et
\par
a!., 1997; base year 1992\textbraceright. Table 6.3 presents for each phase the average costs per
\par
patient per cost category. The highest costs occur during inpatient screening, on the
\par
waiting list, in the transplantation phase and during follow-up. In general, average
\par
costs per patient are higher than median costs and standard deviations are substantial,
\par
reflecting skewed distributions of costs (see table 6.3\textbraceright. This skew is due to a minority
\par
of patients causing very high costs (e.g. due to complications\textbraceright. This pattern is very
\par
normal in numerous studies of medical consumption (e.g. Duan et a!., 1983\textbraceright.
\par
We used the sum-limit method as described by Rout et a!. (1993\textbraceright{} to calculate
\par
cumulative costs by length of stay per phase, per patient, and per reason for leaving
\par
the phase. These cumulative costs were then used to extrapolate the cost data beyond
\par
the observation period (or beyond the date for which fewer than five observations were
\par
available\textbraceright. Almost all cumulative costs could be estimated by a linear function or by a
\par
combination of two linear functions (R' {$>$} 95\%\textbraceright. For instance, for patients who died
\par
during the inpatient screening phase, direct nonmedical costs were NLG 104 per week
\par
whereas indirect nonmedical costs were NLG 17 4 per week in the first 15 weeks, and
\par
from then on NLG 110 per week.
