\textbf{Method}
\par
A micro-simulation model was used to calculate the cost-effectiveness of the Dutch
\par
lung transplantation program in the next IS years. A period of IS years was chosen to
\par
make sure that a steady state was reached, i.e., a situation in which the number of
\par
patients per phase (before transplantation) is stable. After these IS years, no
\par
transplantations are performed, but survival, costs and quality oflife are calculated for
\par
a follow-up period of 2S years after the program has stopped; thus, the total evaluation
\par
period is 40 years.
\par
The model simulates individual patient histories, containing the exact date a patient
\par
enters and leaves a phase. By linking length of stay in each phase with cost and quality
\par
of life estimates, the total costs and effects of the program are estimated. To simulate
\par
length of stay in, for instance, the outpatient screening phase, four distributions were
\par
estimated, based on the Groningen data: one regarding duration until death, one until
\par
referral to the next phase, one until rejection and one until the patient had not
\par
contacted the lung transplantation team for more than 12 months. These distributions
\par
were estimated by means of survival analysis, thus allowing for censoring of the data.
\par
If the duration distribution until death is estimated, censoring will occur if the patient
\par
is rejected, referred or 'has no contact', or if no event has taken place before the end
\par
of the study. With the same technique, duration distributions for the other three events
\par
are calculated.Using these distributions, for every patient and for each of the four events, a date
\par
for the event is simulated, The event related to the first of these dates is assumed to
\par
have taken place, If the event is referral to the next phase, this procedure is repeated
\par
until the patient leaves the program or dies,
\par
First, only patient history until waiting list is simulated, For every patient on the
\par
\textbf{waiting list, a date of death is simulated using the cumulative survival curve as}
\par
presented in figure 6, L This reflects the situation without transplantation program, So,
\par
by using this simulation method, a control group was constructed,
\par
Subsequently, a fixed number of donor lungs is simulated to become available,
\par
distributed randomly over a year, When a donor lung becomes available, the model
\par
checks the waiting list, and of those patients having the appropriate blood type and
\par
body length, the longest waiting patient receives the donor lung, From that moment,
\par
the date of death as predicted earlier is cancelled and a new date of death according to
\par
survival after transplantation is simulated,
\par
After having simulated patient histories, costs and effects were linked to each phase,
\par
Then, costs and effects were summed per year, and for years I to 15, the fixed program
\par
costs were added, To take into account different time profiles for costs and effects, both
\par
costs and effects were discounted by 50Jo per year, taking year I as the base year,
\par
In the baseline scenario, which is basically the situation of 1995, it is assumed that
\par
every year the program is effective, 100 patients enter the outpatient screening phase
\par
and 17 donor lungs are approved for transplantation,
\par
The cost-effectiveness of two other scenarios will be assessed as welL First, it is
\par
anticipated that the baseline scenario will show a rapid increase of the number of
\par
patients on the waiting list, due to the small number of available donor lungs,
\par
Therefore a scenario (the policy scenario) will be assessed in which the number of
\par
patients entering the program is restricted,
\par
Second, it has been estimated that with extensive effort, the supply of donor lungs
\par
in The Netherlands may be increased to 27 per year (see TenVergert et aL, in press), In
\par
the donor scenario the impact of such an increase on the cost-effectiveness of the
\par
program is calculated,
\par
\textbf{CHAPTER 6 }90\textbf{Results}
\par
\textbf{Base-line scenario}
\par
\emph{Patient flow: }Each program year, 100 patients will enter the outpatient screening phase.
\par
After four years, the number of patients per year who enter a specific phase becomes
\par
stable: 65 patients enter the inpatient screening; 50 patients are placed on the waiting
\par
list; and 17 undergo transplantation.
\par
The first years of the program, more patients enter the waiting list than leave (either
\par
because \emph{of }transplantation or death). After 10 program years, the number of patients
\par
on the waiting list at the end of the year stabilises (n= 105). In this situation, 50 patients
\par
enter the waiting list, 17 undergo transplantation, and 33 die. During the 15 program
\par
years, the number of patients in the follow-up phase at the end of each year increases.
\par
This reflects the fact that each year, more patients undergo transplantation than die.
\par
The number of deaths per year during the follow-up phase is 12 in year 10, and 15 in
\par
year 15.
\par
Because survival, both on the waiting list and after transplantation, differs for each
\par
diagnosis group, we also studied the distribution across diagnosis groups before and
\par
after transplantation. In the outpatient phase, 42\% of patients have emphysema/COPD
\par
versus 58\% of the patients with transplants. The percentage of patients with pulmonmy
\par
hypertension drops from 17\% in the outpatient screening phase to 11 \emph{Ofo }after
\par
transplantation; for lung fibrosis patients the percentage decreases from 19 to 10\%. The
\par
share of patients having cystic fibrosis \emph{or }other diagnoses remains the same before and
\par
after transplantation.
\par
\emph{Costs: }The total discounted costs with the program amount to NLG 246 million and
\par
without the program to NLG 130 million. Table 6.4 presents the total costs in four
\par
categories for the evaluation period of 40 years.
