\textbf{CHAPTER 6}
\par
92\textbf{Policy scenario}
\par
In the baseline scenario, two thirds of patients on the waiting list die, which is a highly
\par
undesirable situation, both for the patients and for the physicians involved in the
\par
program. Therefore, we calculated cost-effectiveness if the program inflow is restricted
\par
in such a way that the number of patients admitted to the outpatient screening is such
\par
that no more than 50\% of the patients eventually die on the waiting list (assuming that
\par
the probability of entering the waiting list remains unchanged). This would mean that
\par
no more than 68 patients per year should enter the outpatient screening phase. Of these
\par
68 patients, 35 would be placed on the waiting list, and 17 patients would then
\par
undergo transplantation per year. If the number of patients entering the program would
\par
decrease to {$<$}65, not enough patients would be on the waiting list to find a match for
\par
all 17 donor lungs. After 10 years, a steady state would be reached, where at the end
\par
of the year, 55 patients would be waiting for transplantation.
\par
In this scenario, the additional costs with the program, after discounting, amount to
\par
NLG 95 million, NLG 22 million less then in the baseline scenario. The number of life-
\par
years gained and QALYs gained are 550 and 656, respectively (after discounting). Thus,
\par
the costs per life-year gained are more favourable, NLG 172,000, and the costs per
\par
QALY gained are NLG 144,000. If only direct medical costs are taken into account,
\par
these ratios are NLG 168,000 and NLG 141,000, respectively.
\par
\textbf{Donor scenario}
\par
In this scenario, the number of patients entering the screening phases and waiting list
\par
are the same as in the baseline scenario. From year 4 on, 27 patients undergo
\par
transplantation per year, whereas 23 patients die on the waiting list In the steady state,
\par
which is reached after 10 years, the number of patients waiting for a transplant is 75,
\par
compared to 100 in the baseline scenario.
\par
With 100 patients entering the outpatient screening each year, all 27 available donor
\par
lungs match with at least one patient on the waiting list If the number of patients
\par
entering the program falls below 95, this may not always be the case.
\par
The total costs in this scenario are 26\% higher than in the baseline scenario, NLG
\par
147 million. The number of life-years gained and QALYs gained are about 55\% higher,
\par
923 and 1,089, respectively. The cost-effectiveness ratios are NLG 159,000 per life-year
\par
gained and NLG 135,000 per QALY gained (after discounting costs and effects).
\par
Very recently, the legislation in The Netherlands concerning organ donation has
\par
changed (see Eerste Kamer der Staten-Generaal, 1995-1996). The previous system
\par
assumed no permission for donation, unless explicit permission was given by the donor
\par
(e.g. by means of a 'donor codicil') or his/her relatives. In the new system, any Dutch
\par
citizen will be invited to fill in a response card giving (or not) permission to donate
\par
specific organs. A national registry will keep an up-to-date database of these responses,
\par
which can be consulted if necessary. It is expected that if this system is fully
\par
\textbf{operational, the number of donor organs will increase.Sensitivity analysis}
\par
The lifelong use of immunosuppressive medication during follow-up after
\par
transplantation is a major element in the costs of lung transplantation. For one of the
\par
most often used immunosuppressive drugs, cydosporine, the future costs may fall as a
\par
result of completing the patent period. It is difficult to predict the extent of a possible
\par
price decrease. However, if we would assume a 50\% cost reduction in follow-up
\par
medication, total incremental costs for the baseline scenario would fall by NLG 11.8
\par
million (5\% discounting). The costs per life-year gained (and per QALY gained) would
\par
\textbf{be lQO/o lower. However, new, more expensive, immunosuppressive medication is}
\par
already being used. Widespread application of these drugs will lead to cost increases,
\par
but this might be offset by a better survival and/or less drug toxicity, which may
\par
improve quality of life.
\par
During the inpatient screening phase, patients are hospitalised for several weeks in
\par
the University Hospital Groningen to undergo an extensive number of tests. As lung
\par
transplantation is still relatively new, it may be expected that evaluation of the
\par
\textbf{screening process will result in a more limited, but equally effective, screening in the}
\par
future. If the costs of inpatient screening in the transplant centre could be halved,
\par
incremental costs and cost per life-year gained (and QALY gained) would decrease by
\par
\textbf{6\% in the baseline scenario.}
\par
The long-term survival after lung transplantation is still vezy uncertain. If future
\par
long-term survival would deviate significantly from the survival as assumed above, it
\par
could dearly result in a substantial change in effectiveness. The influence on cost-
\par
effectiveness could be considerable, but depends also on the specific costs during the
\par
additional life-years gained.
\par
\textbf{CHAPTER 6 }94\textbf{Discussion}
\par
Lung transplantation is an expensive, but effective intervention; survival and quality
\par
of life improve substantially after transplantation. This analysis suggests that regard-
\par
less which scenario would effectuate in tbe near future, lung transplantation remains
\par
expensive in terms of costs per life-year (or QALY) gained.
\par
\textbf{Crucial elements determining cost-effectiveness are the number of patients screened}
\par
and placed on tbe waiting list as compared to tbe number of available donor organs as
\par
well as tbe substantial costs of follow-up after transplantation. \emph{As }more patients are
\par
screened and more patients are waiting (longer) for transplantation, !ben more costs
\par
are incurred without any gains in health effects. Restricting tbe inflow in tbe screening
\par
phase (e.g. being even more restrictive concerning contraindications) can improve tbe
\par
balance between tbe costs of screening and tbe health effects of transplantation.
\par
We did not try to establish a true "optimal scenario" in terms of cost-effectiveness,
\par
but combining tbe policy and donor scenario (restricted inflow and more donors)
\par
would result in NLG 151,000 per life-year gained (NLG 124,000 per QALY), which is
\par
slightly more favourable !ban tbe results of tbe donor scenario. This scenario has tbe
\par
disadvantage that not all donor organs will be used for transplantation.
\par
The quality of life and utility scores for patients with transplants improved
\par
substantially. This is in accordance with tbe findings of Ramsey et al. (1995b) for lung
\par
transplantation patients. About tbe same improvements in utility scores were found in
\par
tbe Dutch heart transplantation study (see Rout et al., 1993).
\par
\textbf{Sensitivity analysis showed that a more limited inpatient screening process could}
\par
result in some cost reduction. However, tbe feasibility of such a rationalisation should
\par
first be investigated.
\par
Comparison with Dutch programs for heart and liver transplantation shows that
\par
cost-effectiveness for lung transplantation is relatively unfavourable. Costs per life-
\par
year gained (5\% discounting) for heart and liver transplantation were NLG 66,000 and
\par
NLG 54,000 (see Michel et aL, 1992; Rout et al., 1993; costs adjusted to 1992). This
\par
difference can not be explained fully by different methods of analysis or inclusion of
\par
different cost categories. For heart and liver transplantation, tbe average number of
\par
life-years gained per patient with transplant is higher: 10.5 and 7.6 years, respective-
\par
ly. Furthermore, tbe costs during a year on tbe waiting list or a year of follow-up after
\par
transplantation are substantially higher for lung transplantation as compared to heart
\par
and liver transplantation.
\par
ln tbe meantime, tbe Dutch National Health Insurance Board advised tbe minister of
\par
Health Affairs for tbe moment not to include lung transplantation in tbe benefit
\par
package. The transplantation program will proceed (subsidised by a development grant)
\par
but further research should indicate if costs can be reduced, especially during tbe
\par
screening phase (by reducing tbe number of patients screened and/ or lowering tbe costs
