\par
\textbf{\emph{Optimal allocation }of \emph{resources}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{over health care programs:}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{dealing with decreasing marginal utility}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{and uncertainty}}
\par
\textbf{Summary}
\par
\emph{This chapter addresses the problem how to value health care programs with different ratios of}
\par
\emph{costs to effects, specifically when taking into account that these costs and effects are}
\par
\emph{uncertain. First, the traditional framework of maximising health effects with a given health}
\par
\emph{care budget is extended to a flexible budget using a value function over money and health}
\par
\emph{effects. Second, uncertainty surrounding costs and effects is included in the model using}
\par
\emph{expected utility. It is shown that- depending on risk attitude }- \emph{taking account of uncertainty}
\par
\emph{may result in a different ordering of health care programs. Other approaches to uncertainty}
\par
\emph{that do not specify a utility function are discussed and it is argued that these too include}
\par
\emph{implicit notions about risk attitude. Hence, to take account of uncertainties in the ordering of}
\par
\emph{health care programs, attention needs to be paid to the marginal values of money and health}
\par
\emph{effects and to risk attitude.}\textbf{introduction}
\par
Cost-effectiveness analyses often conclude by stating that the obtained cost-
\par
effectiveness ratio is well within the acceptable limit and it is thus advisable to
\par
implement the therapy under investigation on a wide scale. An example can be found
\par
in the Dutch guidelines on cholesterol-lowering therapy. Supported by a cost-
\par
effectiveness analysis, the guidelines advise to treat all patients with a 22\% or higher
\par
10-year risk for a cardiovascular event. This type of advice is problematic. Although it
\par
has been estimated that for such a strategy the costs per life-year gained are under EUR
\par
20,000, no indication is given where the budget for treatment may be found. Given the
\par
number of untreated individuals in The Netherlands, the additional costs are estimated
\par
at EUR 100 million, a considerable amount. Thus, the question arises whether budget
\par
should be allocated to the new therapy. A related problem concerns the uncertainties
\par
surrounding this cost-effectiveness ratio. A point estimate of EUR 20,000 may be
\par
deemed acceptable in a league table, but this may not hold true for the uncertainty
\par
margins. Fear of being confronted with a higher than expected cost-effectiveness ratio
\par
when implementing this strategy may induce the decision maker to value the cost-
\par
effectiveness lower than the point estimate. More generally, the question can be raised
\par
how budget should be allocated when programs differ not only in terms of their cost-
\par
effectiveness but also in terms of the uncertainty surrounding both costs and effects.
\par
These questions are addressed here using a formal mathematical framework.
\par
First we will present the mathematical rules of cost-effectiveness as introduced by
\par
Weinstein tt Zeckhauser (1973) (see also Torrance et al., 1972). As we will see, these
\par
rules (that do not account for uncertainty) imply that the required budget for a new
\par
cost-effective therapy has to be taken from existing less cost-effective treatments in the
\par
health care sector. However, the additional budget to implement new medical
\par
treatments could also be sought in other governmental departments (e.g. education), or
\par
\textbf{might be obtained from an increase in e.g. taxes or insurance premiums. A pragmatic}
\par
alternative to the standard model is the use of a fixed trade-off between costs and
\par
health effects. However, as argued by Gafni tt Birch (1993), that approach would lead
\par
to an ever-increasing health care budget.
\par
Next. a rule similar to that derived by Weinstein and Zeckhauser (still assuming
\par
certainty) is explored, that can be derived to support a more flexible trade-off between
\par
budgets. Formulating the model clarifies the need to establish the right moment to stop
\par
increasing the health care budget at the cost of either money available for other
\par
\textbf{departments or increases in taxes and premiums. In other words, consideration should}
\par
be given to the formulation of a value function characterised by the decreasing
\par
marginal value of both money and health effects.
\par
Then an approach including uncertainty is presented which combines the results
\par
from the previous section with the theory of expected utility. This approach requires
\par
the explicit formulation of a utility function, expressing risk attitude towards money
\par
and health effects. A separate section discusses how to handle uncertainty without
\par
explicitly formulating such a utility function. In the final section, we discuss our results
\par
\textbf{(HRPTER 7 }98and compare them with other recent work on uncertain1y and budget allocation (e.g.
\par
Stinnett a Paltiel, 1997; O'Brien a Sculpher, 2000; Meltzer, 2001).
\par
