\textbf{CHAPTER 7 }112\textbf{Discussion}
\par
In this chapter the question was raised how a decision can be made between different
\par
health care programs with different ratios of costs to effects, specifically when taking
\par
account of uncertainty surrounding the estimates of costs and effects. It was suggested
\par
that attitudes towards risk should be taken into account as well as the idea of
\par
decreasing marginal value of money and health effects. In order to formalise this
\par
problem, we first considered the reasoning behind the use of cost-effectiveness ratios
\par
as introduced by Weinstein and Zeckbauser (1973). We then extended the standard
\par
approach of maximising health effects for a given budget to maximising the value
\par
derived from health effects and the budget available for purposes competing with
\par
health care spendings.
\par
When a value function is used, adding a new program means that some programs
\par
may have to be abandoned (but fewer than with a fixed budget approach) and at the
\par
same time that the budget will increase (but not unlimited as with a fixed critical ratio).
\par
Furthermore, the critical ratio will decrease. Moreover, it was shown that accepting a
\par
program without removing other programs may only be justified when assuming a
\par
very specific value function: one without decreasing marginal value for either money
\par
or health effects. It is unlikely that any decision maker has such value function,
\par
emphasising that budgets determine critical ratios instead of the other way around.
\par
Using a value function including health effects as well as money to decide between
\par
programs proves particularly fruitful when the ordering of programs of which the
\par
outcomes are surrounded with uncertainty is addressed.
\par
We have shown that, if uncertainty is taken into account, the optimum budget
\par
allocation may differ from the allocation that would have been derived for the situation
\par
with no uncertainty surrounding costs and effects. However, for that purpose we
\par
assumed that a utility function for both money and health effects was defined. In
\par
practice, it may not be easy for a decision maker to specifY preferences over uncertain
\par
outcomes so explicitly. Thus, we also addressed some other approaches to handling
\par
uncertainty without such an explicit utility function.
\par
The intuitive generalisation of the fixed budget approach is to maximise the
\par
expected health effects while staying within budget with some specified probability.
\par
However, this has some less favourable implications, because it assumes that the
\par
decision maker is risk neutral towards health, but risk averse towards costs.
\par
Furthermore, the approach ignores interdependencies between a program's costs and
\par
effects. Finally, the approach can be rewritten as a special case of the expected utility
\par
approach.
\par
The generalisation of the fixed trade-off formalisation is to derive confidence
\par
intervals around cost-effectiveness ratios and implement programs only if the upper
\par
limit of the interval is below the critical ratio. These intervals are very informative;
\par
however, using them to decide on the implementation of programs may imply rather
\par
strong risk aversion towards both costs and effects. One might say that any alternative
\par
approach, without assuming an explicit utility function, also has its own implicit utility
\par
function. Moreover, these functions are arbitrary as they suggest that all decisionmakers have the same preferences over uncertain situations, which is unlikely to be the
\par
case. This stresses the need for further research into the risk attitude of decision makers.
\par
It is important to realise that in this chapter we have discussed the characterisation
\par
of the optimum for each approach, which is not the same as a decision rule or
\par
algorithm to find the optimal allocation. When only independent health care programs
\par
are considered, for the fixed critical ratio approach the decision rule follows directly
\par
from the optimality conditions, and for the fixed budget approach it is also relatively
\par
easy to derive the decision rule from the characterisation of the optimum. However,
\par
\textbf{once the collection of programs also contains some mutually exclusive programs,}
\par
optimality conditions concern the ratio of costs to effects of a single program whereas
\par
\textbf{the decision rule is concerned with the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio.}
\par
The value function approach under certainty and the expected utility approach
\par
under uncertainty look very similar. Therefore, it is tempting to compare these two
\par
approaches, for instance assuming risk neutrality and then comparing results. However,
\par
because both approaches are built on different sets of axioms, the results are not
\par
directly comparable. As was pointed out by Dyer tt Sarin (1982), in the expected utility
\par
approach, the strength of preference and attitude towards risk taking are confounded.
\par
Thus, a linear utility function indicating risk neutrality automatically also indicates
\par
constant marginal preference. In contrast, the formalisation under certainty had
\par
\textbf{decreasing marginal value.}
\par
Some other approaches to the problem of budget allocation under uncertainty have
\par
been published. For example, O'Brien tt Sculpher (2000) propose formulating the
\par
problem of budget allocation under uncertainty as a portfolio selection problem. In
\par
\textbf{portfolio analysis the ultimate goal is to maximise expected return on investment and}
\par
minimise uncertainty. These two goals will often be conflicting, hence a trade-off needs
\par
to be made between expected return on investment and uncertainty. This might be
\par
done, for instance, by setting an aspiration level for expected return and minimising
\par
uncertainty for that level. A more general approach described by O'Brien and Sculpher
\par
is to use a utility function defined on expected return and variance of return. Note that,
\par
although a different approach is used here than that presented in this chapter, both
\par
methods require that a utility function be defined describing preferences and risk
\par
attitude over two attributes. One problem of the approach presented by O'Brien and
\par
Sculpher is that it uses the variance of the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio, which
\par
will often not be defined.
\par
A paper by Meltzer (2001) uses expected utility to model the choice how much of a
\par
specific treatment should be fmanced. In his model, all money not used for the specific
\par
treatment is used for non-medical consumption, and there is a fiXed budget constraint.
\par
In contrast to our analysis, Meltzer goes on to assume perfect insurance at the
\par
population level, for both effects and costs. Then, he finds optimality conditions in
\par
terms of the ratio of expected costs to expected effects, similar to those we find for a
\par
\textbf{risk neutral decision maker.}
\par
\textbf{This raises the question whether it is reasonable to assume that decision makers are}
\par
risk averse and whether they will be risk averse for health costs, health effects, or both.
\par
The effects of health programs accrue to a small group of people, for whom it may
\par
\textbf{CHP.?TER 7 }114affect a large part of their utility whether or not a program is available. Thus, referring
\par
to \emph{Arrow }a Lind (1970), it may be argued that decision makers who use the societal
\par
perspective are risk averse for health effects. For other decision makers, e.g. HMO
\par
managers or insurers, the argument proposed by \emph{Arrow }and Lind at the end of their
\par
article may apply so that they are also risk averse for costs.
\par
Thus, it becomes dear that in order to achieve optimal budget allocation much
\par
information is required: information on costs, effects and size for each health care
\par
program and information on goals, preferences and risk attitude of decision makers.
\par
While it may not be feasible to collect all the required information on costs, effects and
\par
size, the fact that information is required about goals, preferences and risk attitude of
\par
decision makers should be seen as an advantage of the described approaches. At this
\par
moment, decision makers already weigh information on total costs, total effects and
\par
the amount of uncertainty when making decisions, and the approaches in this chapter
\par
compel decision makers to make this explicit. It is not the intention that the model
\par
prescribes what the decision makers should decide. Rather, the model has two
\par
applications. First, it compels decision makers to be explicit about their preferences and
\par
informs them about the consequences of certain choices. Second, decisions can be
\par
analysed afterwards, and fed back to decision makers to confront them with the risk
\par
attitude that is reflected by their choices.
\par
Other attributes, such as equity and ethics, may of course play a role in the decision-
\par
making process as well. An advantage of the expected utility approach is that the
\par
function may easily be extended to include such attributes. As such, this approach
\par
presents a very broad general framework for budget allocation, that better describes
\par
reality than the currently assumed model of maximising health effects for a fixed
\par
\textbf{budget.}
