\par
\textbf{Statistical methods for dealing with uncertainty}
\par
\textbf{In chapter 2, a general approach was discussed to assess the uncertainty surrounding}
\par
the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio (ICER) in situations where data have been
\par
collected at the patient level in a randomised clinical trial. This approach included the
\par
calculation of a 95\% probability ellipse and the concept of a so-called C/E-acceptability
\par
curve was introduced. This latter curve defines for each predefined limit on the ICER
\par
the probability that the ICER found in the study is acceptable. Using this curve, a 95\%
\par
one-sided interval can be derived by finding at which limit the probability that the
\par
ICER is acceptable is 95\%.
\par
This acceptability curve was presented as an alternative for the confidence interval
\par
based on Taylor series that was proposed by O'Brien et al. (1994). Shortly after
\par
publication of the acceptability curve method, Wakker tt Klaassen (1995) critiqued this
\par
method. They stated "It should, however, be understood that acceptability curves are
\par
not confidence intervals in a formal statistical sense ... ". Further, they presented a
\par
formal statistical approach for calculating a confidence interval for the ICER. The
\par
method they proposed works as follows: Find a one-sided 97 .50/o confidence interval
\par
for costs and effects separately, with an upper limit for costs and a lower limit for
\par
effects. The ratio of these limits gives the upper limit of a 95\% one-sided confidence
\par
interval for the ICER. Unfortunately, this interval is \emph{at least }a 95\% confidence interval,
\par
which means that the interval may be much wider than strictly necessaJY, especially
\par
when few data are available. This is, for instance, the case for the data from chapter 2;
\par
the interval as proposed by Wakker tt Klaassen is ( -=, 224,514] compared with ( -=,
\par
42,000] in chapter 2. Thus, whilst the interval of Wak.ker tt Klaassen is easier to
\par
calculate than the acceptability curve, there may be situations where its usefulness for
\par
a decision maker is questionable. About the same time, Sacristan et al. (1995) published
\par
a paper using Fieller's theorem to calculate a confidence interval for the ICER. In 1954,
\par
Fieller presented a formula for the confidence interval of two normally distributed
\par
variables. This formula has interesting properties, as there may be situations where the
\par
interval is ( -=, =), which means that the data are consistent with all possible
\par
hypotheses about the ICER. Furthermore, an interval might have the form of
\par
( -=, -1000] u [1000, =), which will typically happen if the additional costs differ
\par
significantly from zero, while the effects in both groups are approximately equivalent.
\par
In 1996, ChaudhaJY tt Steams also proposed the use of Fieller's theorem but, as an
\par
alternative, they also proposed a simulation method that does not make assumptions
\par
about the distribution of costs and effects, i.e. the bootstrap method (see Efron tt
\par
Tibshirani, 1993). In their paper, they illustrated both methods with data on 300
\par
observations per group. For their data, Fieller's method and the bootstrap method
\par
produced similar results. The same was found in a study by Polsky et al. (1997) where
\par
Fieller's method was compared with the bootstrap method, the Taylor series method
\par
\textbf{CnA?iER 8 }118and the so-called box method (O'Brien et al., 1994). In this comparison, Fidler's method
\par
and the bootstrap method performed equally well, and much better than the other two
\par
methods.
\par
Despite that since the introduction of the acceptability curve other (more formal)
\par
methods for quantifYing uncertainty have been published, the acceptability curve is
\par
widely used (e.g. Gray et al., 2000; Moayyedi et al .{$\bullet$} 2000; Griffin et al .{$\bullet$} 2001;
\par
Chancellor et al., 2001). An advantage of the acceptability curve over confidence
\par
intervals is that it does not focus on 950/o confidence, but basically gives an infinite
\par
number of x\% one-sided confidence intervals (for each value x. the corresponding limit
\par
on the ICER can be found in the curve). As Briggs 8: Fenn (1998) wrote; "We believe
\par
that confidence surfaces on the cost-effectiveness plane, summarised in the form of
\par
cost-effectiveness acceptability curves, give greater guidance for decision-makers than
\par
confidence intervals around cost-effectiveness ratios{$\cdot$}: Several authors have pointed out
\par
that the acceptability curve does not have the interpretation of a confidence interval
\par
within the frequentist framework (Wakker Et Klaassen. 1995; Hei1jan et al., 1999).
\par
However. as also mentioned in chapter 4 (and by Briggs, 1999; Hei1jan et al., 1999).
\par
within the Bayesian framework, the acceptability curve does present posterior
\par
confidence intervals under the assumption of a non-informative prior distribution.
\par
Furthermore. Lothgren Et Zethraeus (2000) showed that the acceptability curve also has
\par
a net benefit definition, in that it represents the probability that the net benefits
\par
estimator \emph{(NB(\textbraceright.) }= \emph{Af.lu- f.l,c }, with \emph{A. }the limit on the ICER) is positive. With this
\par
interpretation, the curve may be used for testing the null hypothesis that the net benefit
\par
is non-positive. Consequently, it may be stated that the acceptability curve has both an
\par
interpretation within the frequentist and the Bayesian framework.
\par
In chapter 2 the acceptability curve was drawn based on simulated values from a
\par
bivariate normal distribution for costs and effects but, alternatively, it may also be
\par
drawn based on the simulated values from a bootstrap procedure. Furthermore, it can
\par
be used when outcomes are simulated in a probabilistic sensitivity analysis. as was
\par
done in chapter 5. It should be noted that in the latter case the curve does not have an
\par
interpretation in a frequentist framework, when parameters and their distribution are
\par
\textbf{for instance postulated based on expert opinion. However. as this may be seen as}
\par
defining a prior distribution for the parameter, within the Bayesian framework curves
\par
based on a probabilistic sensitivity analysis do have an interpretation. The curve was
\par
also used in a paper by Hunink et al. (1998) in which they use it to present the results
\par
of a Markov model that was used to simulate individual patient data. In their paper,
\par
Hunnink et al. also emphasise another advantage of the acceptability curve compared
\par
with a confidence interval for an ICER. The problem with presenting ICERs is that
\par
information is lost. A negative ICER can indicate that there are increased effects plus
\par
cost savings or reduced effects plus additional costs. While in the first situation the new
\par
treatment would be accepted, in the latter it would be rejected. The same is true for a
\par
positive ratio. Assuming that R is the maximum acceptable ratio. in case of increased
\par
effects the ICER should be smaller than R to be acceptable, while in case of a loss of
\par
effects the ICER should be larger than R. When using an acceptability curve as in
\par
chapter 2, i.e. subdivided into curves for each quadrant of the cost-effectiveness plane,no important information is lost. Clearly, the acceptability curve is a valuable addition
\par
to the methods available to describe uncertainty around cost-effectiveness ratios.
\par
Chapter 3 presented a simulation method that enables the calculation of sample sizes
\par
\textbf{for economic evaluations. This method uses a backward procedure, in the sense that}
\par
\textbf{for various sample sizes, the power is estimated and thus an estimate of the required}
\par
sample size for a certain power is found. After the article on which chapter 3 is based
\par
was published, several algebraic methods for sample size calculation have been
\par
published (Briggs \& Gray, 1998b; Laska et aL, 1999; Willan \& O'Brien, 1999). An
\par
advantage of these methods over the simulation method is that they are easier to apply.
\par
However, the simulation method also has some advantages. First, it is easy to change
\par
the hypothesis being tested, i.e. it is, for instance, possible to use a different threshold
\par
ICER in the area where the additional effectiveness is negative. This would reflect a
\par
willingness-to-accept a Joss of effects that differs from the willingness-to-pay for gain
\par
of effects (see O'Brien \& Gafui, 1996). Second, it is relatively easy to extend the
\par
simulation procedure such that instead of simulating costs and effects per patient from
\par
one overall bivariate distribution, values are simulated from components that are easier
\par
to specifY. For instance, instead of simulating whether or not a patient is an event-free
\par
survivor, it is simulated whether the patient had balloon angioplasty, bypass surgeJY, a
\par
myocardial infarction, etc., each with well-known costs. This method also
\par
automatically generates the correlation between costs and effects.
\par
When dealing with an economic evaluation that is 'piggy backed' to a phase Ill
\par
clinical trial, basing the sample size on economic data as well as clinical data may be
\par
deemed unethical as it will often increase the number of patients that has to be
\par
subjected to an experimental treatment. When the goal of a study is specifically to
\par
demonstrate cost-effectiveness after superior efficacy has been established, it may
\par
again be considered unethical to subject patients to a control treatment that has been
\par
proven to be Jess effective. Consequently, the number of situations where it is possible
\par
to base the sample size of a study on cost-effectiveness considerations will be limited,
\par
also because of the large number of parameters that need to be specified. As indicated
\par
in chapter 3, besides information on expected effects and their standard deviations, the
\par
same information for costs and the correlation between costs and effects is required.
\par
The conclusion of chapter 3 was, therefore, that given that such information is often
\par
incomplete there is a danger that these sample size calculations may be manipulated
\par
such that projected patient enrolment is suggested to be both sufficient and
\par
operationally feasible. The question remains, of course, whose interest would be served
\par
by such manipulations.
\par
In chapter 4, a Bayesian approach to cost-effectiveness studies was presented,
\par
consisting of a univariate approach for the analysis of the differences in costs and
\par
effects and a multivariate approach for the analysis of the ICER. These approaches were
\par
applied to datasets of two clinical trials which compared both the costs and effects of
\par
stent implantation versus balloon angioplasty. In the multivariate analysis, several
\par
prior distributions were defined for the analysis of the second trial and the influence
\par
\textbf{CHR?"TER a }120of these priors on the posterior distribution was studied. The point estimates of the ICER
\par
varied from NLG 16,269 per additional event-free survivor to NLG 21,984. If the limit
\par
that society puts on the ICER is about NLG 30,000, the probabilities that the ICERs are
\par
acceptable differ markedly (65\%, 80\%, 90\%), possibly leading to different decisions.
\par
The multivariate model presented in chapter 4 relies on a normal approximation of
\par
the mean of a sample of costs and effects, which may not always be realistic. A recent
\par
paper by O'Hagan \& Stevens (2001a) describes the full model for the same type of data
\par
as in chapter 4, i.e. a dichotomous distribution for effects and a log normal distribution
\par
for the costs. The posterior distribution was evaluated using Monte Carlo Markov chain
\par
\textbf{simulation, since a simple algebraic representation of the posterior distribution is no}
\par
longer available when the full model is used. In their analysis O'Hagan \& Stevens used
\par
interesting informative prior distributions. For the probability of 'success' in both
\par
treatment groups a positive correlation was assumed, indicating that higher
\par
effectiveness in one group is also likely to imply higher effectiveness in the other
\par
group. Furthermore, for the variances of the costs a prior distribution was postulated
\par
that gives weak prior information about individual variances but such that the ratios
\par
of variances are likely to be reasonably close to unity.
\par
The interest of health economists in Bayesian methods may partly be explained by
\par
the many papers that have recently been published advocating the use of Bayesian
\par
methods in biostatistics. The paper by Lilford \& Braunholtz (1996) had as subtitle "A
\par
paradigm shift is overdue': At this moment, it appears that this paradigm shift may
\par
actually be taking place. One indicator is a recently published article in The Economist
\par
(2000), with the title "In praise of Bayes". This article describes not only the differences
\par
between the Bayesian and frequentist paradigm, but also cites a statistician (at the
\par
pharmaceutical company Pfizer), who describes how Bayes may be used in dose-
\par
allocation trials. The idea is that, instead of analysing the results at the end of the trial,
\par
patients' responses will be evaluated during the trial and where necessazy the doses will
\par
be adjusted. With such an approach, more patients will receive (near) optimal doses,
\par
and consequently fewer patients may be required for such studies.
\par
As discussed in chapter 4, it is the issue of using prior information that can be seen
\par
as the largest obstacle in a broader use of Bayesian methods. It should be realised,
\par
however, that this problem is not unique to Bayesian statistics: a frequentist meta-
\par
analysis also requires some judgement on which data to include. It seems that the time
\par
has come to shift from discussing the advantages of using Bayes to actually applying
\par
\textbf{the methods in cost-effectiveness studies in order to gain experience, allowing us to}
\par
update our prior beliefs about the possibilities of Bayes.
\par
It is important to note that in all three chapters on statistical methods for dealing with
\par
uncertainty it was assumed that the average costs and effects would approximately
\par
follow a normal distribution, for large enough sample sizes, based on the Central Limit
\par
Theorem. A recent article by O'Hagan \& Stevens (2001b) makes clear that such an
\par
assumption should not be made too easily. It is well known that the sample average is
\par
not a robust estimator for the population mean, which means that if the underlying
\par
distribution is vezy skewed, we are likely to overestimate the population mean whenusing this normal approximation. O'Hagan a Stevens also show that using bootstrap
\par
simulation instead is not a solution because the bootstrap method is also based on
\par
approximation. When the sample size is relatively small and skewness is large, a
\par
modelling approach as presented in chapter 4 (univariate analysis) should be used.
\par
However, as also mentioned in the discussion of chapter 4, it is important to check the
\par
adequacy of the fit of the model to the data. Especially with a Bayesian approach,
\par
testing hypotheses about different models (model refers here both to the likelihood
\par
function and the prior distribution) is fairly straightforward, with no requirement about
\par
hypotheses being nested and no limit on the number of hypotheses considered
\par
simultaneously.
\par
The example presented by O'Hagan a Stevens (2001b) dearly shows that for a very
\par
skewed distribution, even a sample size of 75 is too small to rely on the normal
\par
approximation. However, in the case studies presented in chapters 2 and 4 this problem
\par
does not occur. In chapter 4, the estimated average costs using the log normal model
\par
are similar to the sample mean. Similarly, the costs in chapter 2 are far less skewed than
\par
in the example given by O'Hagan a Stevens; thus, even with the small sample sizes
\par
assuming normality does not have major consequences (assuming log normality leads
\par
to very similar results as those presented in chapter 2). Nevertheless, there are many
\par
situations with large skewness, where formulating a parametric model (i.e. a probability
\par
distribution) for the data should be preferred over normal approximation or
\par
bootstrapping.
