\textbf{Dealing with uncertainty in modelling studies}
\par
Chapters 5 and 6 presented examples of dealing with uncertainty in modelling studies.
\par
In chapter 5 the cost-effectiveness of a fixed dose combination of
\par
didofenac/misoprostol compared with didofenac monotherapy in patients with
\par
rheumatoid arthritis was assessed. Didofenac is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory
\par
drug (NSAID) and misoprostol is a gastroprotective agent that reduces the probability
\par
of NSAID-induced gastro-intestinal side-effects. A model was used to convene data
\par
from literature and databases, and expert opinion. The baseline result of the study was
\par
NLG 4,179 per life-year gained for the use of the combination drug, which may be
\par
considered cost-effective. Parts of the analysis were a univariate and a probabilistic
\par
sensitivity analysis. In the univariate analysis, all variables were varied (one at a time)
\par
by plus and minus 20\% and results were considered not sensitive to changes if the
\par
outcome changed by less than 10\%. This criterion is of course arbitrary, and it adds to
\par
\textbf{the problem of interpreting the results of such univariate analyses. The results}
\par
presented in chapter 5 identify those parameters for which the results are most
\par
\textbf{sensitive. However, as it is not clear how relevant the upper and lower limits of\textpm{} 20\%}
\par
are, it is also not dear how relevant the ordering is of the results. Another approach
\par
would have been to use limits (where possible) derived from the confidence intervals
\par
that were also used in the probabilistic analysis. As a criterion for sensitivity a
\par
threshold ICER might be used; if changing a parameter to its upper or lower limit
\par
causes the outcome to exceed the threshold, implying a different decision, the outcome
\par
is called sensitive to changes in the parameter. However, such a criterion is not ideal,
\par
\textbf{CHA?TER s }122\textbf{\emph{Disca::ssior.J}}
\par
as it dichotomises the idea of sensitivity. Assuming that realistic distributions have
\par
been defined (where possible based on actual trial data), the results of a probabilistic
\par
analysis are easier to interpret: they indicate how likely all possible outcomes are. This
\par
\textbf{is an important advantage above other approaches such as univariate analysis, where}
\par
outcomes are calculated for various input values without any indication how likely
\par
\textbf{these outcomes are.}
\par
Performing a probabilistic sensitivity analysis has become easier in recent years, as
\par
dedicated software is now available for such analysis, e.g. the decision analysis
\par
program DATA \emph{(www.treeage.com) }and the risk analysis program @RISK (which is used
\par
together with a spreadsheet program; \emph{www.palisade.com). }An interesting feature of
\par
@RISK is that it also allows for an assessment of the influence of each individual input
\par
parameter on the outcome, adjusting for all other input parameters. Given the
\par
information that it provides and the relative ease of its use, it is recommended that
\par
modelling studies include a probabilistic analysis in their sensitivity analysis.
\par
As discussed in chapter 3, not all uncertainties in health care are of a statistical
\par
nature. This became apparent shortly after the publication of the article on which
\par
chapter 5 is based. In 1996, the Ministry of Health, Welfare and Sport introduced new
\par
pricing regulations for pharmaceuticals, allowing no price that would exceed the
\par
average price of four neighbouring countries. As a result the price of diclofenac
\par
decreased by 50'/o, whilst the price of the fixed dose combination of
\par
diclofenac/misoprostol remained unchanged. Such an extreme scenario had not been
\par
included in the sensitivity analysis. Because of this, a new cost-effectiveness study for
\par
diclofenac/misoprostol was done (see Al, 2000; Brouwers et al., 2000). In this new
\par
study, other comparators were also included since clinical practice has also changed in
\par
the last few years. Nowadays, many patients using NSAJDs long-term receive proton
\par
pump inhibitors as gastroprotective agent (Herings et al., 2000). As expected, the new
\par
study showed that treatment with diclofenac/misoprostol was no longer cost-effective
\par
compared with NSAJD for the whole rheumatoid arthritis population, but only for
\par
patients at medium to high risk for developing gastro-intestinal side-effects.
\par
In chapter 6 a modelling study was presented that assessed the costs and effects oflung
\par
transplantation. Actual data were available for the situation concerning
\par
\textbf{transplantation, and an extensive simulation model was used to construct a control}
\par
group without transplantation and to extrapolate the available data to the future. The
\par
analysis showed that the costs per quality-adjusted life-year (QALY] gained would be
\par
NLG 167,000 (after discounting by 5\% per year). The model was subsequently used to
\par
assess additional scenarios: a donor scenario in which a larger number of donor lungs
\par
available was assumed (NLG 135,000 per QALY gained) and a policy scenario with a
\par
restricted patient inflow (NLG 144,000 per QALY gained). Based on the observations
\par
during the study period, the number of donor lungs available was estimated at 17 per
\par
year; thus, this estimate is surrounded by a certain amount of statistical uncertainty.
\par
However, the availability of donor lungs may also be influenced by decision makers
\par
(e.g. through legislation) and physicians (e.g. through changes in donor treatment and
\par
in selection criteria used for donor lungs). A separate study (Geertsma a ten Vergert,1995) showed that with concerted action a maximum of 27 donor lungs per year might
\par
be achieved. The same applies for the patient inflow, the definition of stricter inclusion
\par
criteria might restrict the number of patients eligible for screening per year, thus
\par
decreasing the inflow beyond the statistical limits.
\par
The model used in the present study is a micro-simulation model, which means that
\par
data are simulated at the patient level: for each patient a histOiy is created, with time
\par
spent in each phase, until death. It might have been possible to use a Markov model
\par
instead, where patients move from one state (in this study: phase) to another on fixed
\par
moments. However, because of the large variation of time spent in each phase, it would
\par
have been necessaJY to use a cycle of 1 day, or 1 week at most. Given that the total
\par
evaluation period was 40 years and that the transition probabilities are time-
\par
dependent, a micro-simulation model was in fact easier to build and work with.
\par
Moreover, it would have proven vel)' difficult to build the interaction between donor
\par
lungs and waiting list into a Markov model, while it is straightforward in the micro-
\par
simulation model.
