\textbf{Dealing with uncertainty in decision making}
\par
The purpose of developing methods to describe or reduce uncertainty, such as those
\par
\textbf{presented in this thesis, is to facilitate decision making under uncertainty. However,}
\par
these methods present only one part of the equation, as it is also necessaJY to describe
\par
preferences for possible outcomes before a decision can be made. In chapter 7 attention
\par
was given to risk attitude and preferences of decision makers. It has been argued that
\par
taking uncertainty into account in budget allocation decisions leads to different
\par
decisions than under certainty. A numerical example made it clear that under
\par
uncertainty a trade-off is made between the ratio of costs to effects and the degree of
\par
\textbf{uncertainty. When a decision maker is risk averse, programs with small ratios of costs}
\par
\textbf{to effects but large variances may be abandoned in favour of programs with higher}
\par
ratios of costs to effects but smaller variances.
\par
The model presented in chapter 7 is a static model, in the sense that the budget of
\par
one year is allocated without regards for the years thereafter. This is an unrealistic
\par
situation because in practice, budget allocation in one year may influence the budget
\par
allocation in succeeding years. Thus, a logical next step in the development of budget
\par
allocation models is to adjust the current model to a stochastic dynamic model.
\par
When decision makers have difficulty specif'ying the utility function, a risk premium
\par
on the riskless discount rate might be used to adjust the value of the mean outcome.
\par
This is what is proposed by Arrow 8: Lind (1970), and also suggested as an alternative
\par
\textbf{CHAPTER a }124Discussio\textasciitilde{}
\par
by Ben-Zion a Gafni (1983). Such risk premium should reflect how much risk is
\par
\textbf{involved, i.e. it should be large if the variance is large and small if the variance is small}
\par
(assuming risk aversion). Though this method may seem easier to use in practice,
\par
eliciting a value for a risk premium in such a way that it accurately reflects risk attitude
\par
may in practice be rather complicated. Furthermore, adjusting the riskless discount rate
\par
for risk assumes that our uncertainty is completely time-related, i.e. that we are sure
\par
about the outcome today, and become more and more uncertain as time progresses. The
\par
expected utility approach does not relate uncertainty to time, and therefore seems more
\par
pertinent to the type of uncertainty that has been discussed in this thesis and is
\par
generally discussed in health economics.
\par
It should be noted that there are several (competing) theories on whose risk attitude
\par
(utility function) should be used. Welfarists will argue that for each individual his or
\par
her own risk attitude should be used, whereas extra-welfarists suggest that the risk
\par
attitude of society as a whole should be used. Importantly, they all agree that someone's
\par
risk attitude should be included in the decision-making process.
\par
Another reason (besides risk attitude) why information on uncertainty is relevant to the
\par
decision maker are the so-called 'sunk costs: If decision makers disregard uncertainty
\par
and base their decision only on the point estimate of the ICER, a wrong decision may
\par
be made. If the decision was to introduce a new program, and it is later found that the
\par
actual ICER is much higher, the decision maker might want to switch back to the old
\par
\textbf{situation. However, switching is not without cost Examples of sunk costs are the non-}
\par
recoverable investment costs, such as the alteration of a building or equipment specific
\par
to the new technology. While some of these costs (such as the costs of special
\par
\textbf{equipment) are usually taken into account in an economic evaluation, some are not.}
\par
Not taking such cost categories into account is often a consequence of the choice to
\par
present the results independently from the initial situation. Examples of costs not taken
\par
into account are implementation costs, such as the hiring and training of personnel,
\par
and costs related to the loss of goodwill among the health care professionals as a result
\par
of frequent changes in treatment strategies. Another example are the "political costs".
\par
In practice, decision makers make decisions on which medical technology to add to the
\par
benefits package covered by social health insurance but it proves to be much more
\par
difficult to decide, based on cost-effectiveness arguments, to withdraw a currently
\par
available technology from the social insurance coverage. Presumably, the "political
\par
costs" of a policy reversal are veJY high. Sunk costs are often neglected as they are
\par
often intangible and rare (since we only switch if we have a highly reliably estimate of
\par
the ICER). If, on the other hand, no attention is given to the uncertainty surrounding
\par
the ICER, the probability of a policy reversal might be high, meaning that sunk costs
\par
\textbf{can become very relevant.}
\par
Claxton (1999b) discussed this idea of sunk costs in the context of deciding whether
\par
or not to collect additional data in order to reduce uncertainty. If all sunk costs that
\par
may be involved in a decision can be quantified, it is possible to calculate whether or
\par
not a new program should be introduced based on the current information and
\par
expected results of a new study. However, even if the decision maker decides not tostart a new trial it is still likely that new information will become available through
\par
other channels (literature, study initiated by a hospital, etc.). Oearly, the later such
\par
information is expected to become available, the smaller the influence of the sunk costs
\par
on the current decision. A decision maker may not be able to control which
\par
information becomes available at what time, but can (and should) consider what the
\par
likelihood is of making the wrong decision by changing to the new technology.
\par
Confidence intervals and acceptability curves can provide that information. By
\par
combining his/her knowledge or judgement (in case the costs can not be quantified)
\par
about the magnitude of the sunk costs related to making the wrong decision with the
\par
likelihood of that event occurring, the decision maker will then decide if the ICER
\par
found in the current study should result in a switch from the old to the new technology.
\par
An alternative method for dealing with uncertainty in economic evaluation is based on
\par
the real option approach (Dixit 8: Pindyck, 1994; Smit, 1996; Merton, 1998). This
\par
approach originates from the field of finance, where it has been successfully applied to
\par
explain why businesses do not behave as they are expected to on the basis of the net
\par
present value of an investment. Palmer 8: Smith (2000) discussed the idea of using
\par
\textbf{option values in economic evaluations in health care.}
\par
Similar to the valuation of financial options, the real option approach assigns values
\par
to the options the decision maker holds. Real options hold value as they allow the
\par
\textbf{decision maker to adapt and revise decisions in response to new information.}
\par
The real option approach assumes three features for a decision: first, the ability to
\par
delay the decision to introduce the new technology; second, that there is uncertainty
\par
about the future state of the world (which will be reduced over time); and finally that
\par
decisions involve sunk costs (i.e. the decision is at least partly irreversible). If any of
\par
the three features does not apply, the option has no value, and can therefore be
\par
discarded. Several types of options may be distinguished, two of which are the option
\par
to expand and the option to defer.
\par
The option to expand may be relevant when a decision to implement a new
\par
technology acquires an expansion option, i.e. a possibility to expand at relatively low
\par
costs. A new technology may, for example, yield a small negative net benefit that
\par
because of great variation could well be coincidental. However, implementing this
\par
technology on a small scale acquires the option to expand when new evidence proves
\par
that the new technology is cost-effective. If the value of this option is higher than the
\par
expected loss of fully implementing the program, then it is wise to implement the
\par
program on a small scale.
\par
The option to defer takes into account the opportunity costs of making a
\par
commitment now, and thereby giving up the option of waiting for new information.
\par
The option has value because it enables the decision maker to respond to new
\par
information at low cost. This new information will be valuable if returning on a
\par
decision involves sunk costs and the likelihood of this occurring is large. In case the
\par
new technology has a positive net benefit, but the value of the option to defer is larger,
\par
it may not be wise to implement the new technology at the current time. The value of
\par
the option is, for instance, related to the magnitude of uncertainty (the larger, the
\par
\textbf{CHRPTER }8 126Dis\textasciitilde r::ssion
\par
higher the value of the option), the expected informational content (the larger, the
\par
higher the value of the option), the speed with which the new information will become
\par
available (the quicker, the higher the value of the option) and the discount rate (the
\par
lower, the higher the value of the option).
\par
The option approach adjusts the ICER for both the degree of uncertainty and the
\par
reversibility of a decision. It is not immediately clear to what extent the option value
\par
approach and expected utility approach describe the same properties in decision
\par
making under uncertainty. In the set of axioms of expected utility, no notion of
\par
irreversibility occurs. Therefore, it would be interesting to compare both approaches to
\par
see if it is necessary and possible to combine both approaches.
\par
\textbf{Areas for further research}
\par
In this thesis, several sources of uncertainty have been discussed. One that has not yet
\par
been addressed is missing/censored data. When data are missing, uncertainty about the
\par
parameters of interest increases. When the data are missing completely at random, it is
\par
not difficult to adjust the estimates for that. However, if missingness depends on
\par
observed or unobserved data, special methods are required to adjust both the point
\par
estimate and the estimate of the variance. This problem of missing data is not new, and
\par
many methods for dealing with it are known (see Little 8: Rubin, 1987; Schafer, 1997).
\par
In health economics, the problem of dealing with missing data has not yet received
\par
much attention. When longitudinal data on quality-of-life are missing from a certain
\par
time point, it may be plausible to assume that patients have become too ill to fill out
\par
the questionnaire, and therefore it may not be assumed that the missingness is
\par
ignorable. The same is true for costs; it is possible that patients dropped out of a study
\par
due to serious side-effects causing high costs, which remain unobserved. However, it is
\par
unlikely that all methods that work well for quality-of-life data will work equally well
\par
for cost data, given that costs are usually very skewed, whereas quality-of-life data are
\par
often approximately normal. Both for costs and effects it is important to investigate
\par
how different methods for dealing with missing data perform for different types of
\par
missingness. This should provide some guidance for analysts in their choice between
\par
available methods.
\par
Chapter 7 deals with the influence of uncertainty in decision making. From a
\par
theoretical point of view, reasons may be given why decision makers should take
\par
uncertainty about costs and effects into account when making their decisions.
\par
However, we have little knowledge of whether decision makers actually do take
\par
uncertainty into account and if so, in what way. These questions might be answered
\par
through an empirical study among decision makers.\textbf{Epilogue}
\par
In the introduction it was stated that it is unrealistic to assume that all uncertainty can
\par
be eliminated. However, this should not prevent us from making optimal use of all
\par
methods available to first reduce uncertainty and then describe the remaining
\par
uncertainty. Unfortunately, it is clear that these goals have not yet been reached in
\par
\textbf{most economic evaluations of health care technologies; however, this is not surprising}
\par
because most methodological developments in this area are quite recent. Hopefully, the
\par
coming years will see a wide dissemination of methods for dealing with uncertainty
\par
and a growing understanding of preferences over uncertain decisions.
