ii\textbf{Abstract}
\par
A virtual team can be an effective tool for providing both flexibility and stability for
\par
organizations adapting themselves to today's turbulent environment. It can facilitate close
\par
customer involvement; interorganizational co-operation; and optimal labour related cost
\par
structure. However a virtual team can have the following weaknesses: disrupted team
\par
cohesion; difficult communication; conflicts of multi team members' utilization;
\par
requirement for multi skilled labor; longer time for accomplishing tasks.
\par
Applying virtual teams in business to business environment is continuously spreading.
\par
That is why it is useful to investigate their way of work in terms of communication,
\par
culture, group, leadership, knowledge work, productivity, and trust, which possess
\par
distinguished importance.
\par
One kind of a virtual team Philips Semiconductors use is the KAM (key account
\par
management) team, which is aimed to support sales to selected key customers. Members
\par
of a KAM team in different positions like key account manager, account manager, and
\par
customer business support have got direct contacts with the same global customers but at
\par
different levels and in different locations. Flat corporate hierarchy of PSC enables,
\par
supports, and even requests many internal and external direct contacts of KAM team
\par
members. They communicate mainly via e-mail and phone with applying special genres,
\par
which determines accurate, close context. The high contact number and the low
\par
communication frequency limit social awareness necessary for common understanding.
\par
Beside national culture several levels of Philips corporate culture like PSC's; particularly
\par
Philips National Organization's; and local division's culture have got great impact on
\par
KAM team members. The fact of KAM team members' belonging to groups determines
\par
their individual preferences; knowledge of and among them; their ways of daily work;
\par
power structure; stereotypes. Weak cohesion among KAM team members and strong
\par
cohesion among local colleagues result in "groupless teams" and "teamless groups".
\par
Leadership challenges KAM team members by limited visibility; limited availability;
\par
limited possibility for motivating other team members; limited possibility for maintaining
\par
personal contacts; complex cultural backgrounds; strong but ambiguous influencing
\par
groups in the background; competing preferences. Philips Semiconductor exploits
\par
knowledge workers' competencies, who work in virtual teams, by giving them multi-
\par
positions according to their competencies. Their productivity being facilitated by
\par
promoting leadership; direct contacts to the relevant persons; and computerized
\par
interorganizational database. Time consuming processes; inconsistency in adequate
\par
iiitraining's at product division level; and senseless spending are the most significant
\par
difficulties of knowledge management at PSC. KAM team members working without
\par
visual supervision are controlled by requests; scheduled actions; and objectives. They
\par
have got significant freedom in selecting activities. KAM team members can not establish
\par
traditional personal relationships and so they are stuck at initial trust level. Philips' strong
\par
corporate culture supports high initial trust level, which provides favorable environment
\par
for virtual teams.
\par
