vii\textbf{Introduction}
\par
Virtual teams have become focus topic of human resource management and
\par
organizational behavior since economical, social, and technological (EST) environment
\par
changed from a stable and simple one to a turbulent and complex one by the following
\par
movements: development of information technology (information availability,
\par
connectivity); globalization which creates "hipercompetition" [Kotler 2001]; and frequent
\par
change in organizational structures (acquisitions, merges, network organizations).
\par
The real challenge of today's organizations is providing both flexibility and stability. The
\par
first one is important while they are adjusting themselves to current movements of EST
\par
environment listed above in order to optimize their resource utilization. On the other hand
\par
solid knowledge and experience are required to provide outstanding quality, which is
\par
crucial from the customers' and ultimately profit's point of view. This paradox seems to
\par
be solved only through enhanced teamwork supported by new technology. One of the
\par
useful tools for this purpose is a virtual team. However a virtual organization and a virtual
\par
team certainly have got limitations. To avoid pitfalls careful investigations of ongoing
\par
virtual teams are needed. However most of the researches on this topic have been directed
\par
to ad hoc, project teams or technical oriented (development) teams. On the other hand, the
\par
application of virtual teams is more and more needed in trade, especially in business to
\par
business environment. So it is useful to collect, systematize, and analyze data about ways
\par
of ongoing teams working in this field. That is why I have carried out one and a half year
\par
investigation of six virtual account teams functioning in semiconductor sales, and
\par
summarized the results in this dissertation. I intended to answer the following main
\par
questions:
\par
\emph{What common and special genres do members of the virtual teams apply while}
\par
\emph{communicating? How group discussions and decision making are carried out in the}
\par
\emph{virtual teams?}
\par
\emph{What affect does corporate and national culture have on the virtual teams?}
\par
\emph{How do intra-and interorganizational group-structure influences virtual teams?}
\par
\emph{What special managerial difficulties do the virtual team leaders face and what techniques}
\par
\emph{do they apply to cope with them?}
\par
\emph{What is the relationship between virtual team and knowledge work?}
\par
\emph{How can a virtual team's productivity be assured and improved considering its main}
\par
\emph{factors?}
\par
viii\emph{How does trust affect the performance of the virtual teams?}
\par
Since line managers' active participation is inevitable in successful human resource
\par
management, this work targets direct practical applicability for them. That is why, it is
\par
different from pure scientific approach and should have an integrating view. So
\par
interdisciplinary analysis based on socio-psychology (human ethology) was required on
\par
the borderlines of human resource management, organizational behavior, total quality
\par
management, management across cultures, operations management, and strategic
\par
management.
\par
The paper has got two main parts. The first one is a theoretical description of the virtual
\par
team and its relations to selected key issues. That is based on the analysis and compilation
\par
of relevant literature. In the first section the virtual team is defined, after that importance
\par
and problems of virtual teams are presented. Then definition, importance, and problems
\par
of teams, team building and maintenance are discussed. Virtual team relationship to
\par
communication, culture, group, leadership, knowledge work, productivity, and trust are
\par
evaluated at the end of the first part.
\par
The second part presents case study research and interprets its findings based on key
\par
issues. Methodology of the research is outlined including ways of data collection, data
\par
analysis, and description of limitations. Then the external and internal environments of
\par
the teams under investigation are presented. The key account management (KAM) team is
\par
explained by definition, history, and structure. Further the roles, tasks, and working
\par
conditions of KAM team members are described. The next subchapter including
\par
specialties of each KAM team delineates practical background. Finally key factor
\par
appearances and their effects on KAM teams' work are described.
\par
Conclusion including exact answers to the basic questions listed above close the main
\par
body of the work.
\par
The appendix contains a description on major processes of Philips Semiconductors
\par
International Marketing and Sales; the Philips Leadership Competencies; Key areas of an
\par
account manager's responsibility; List of applied abbreviations. A bibliography is
\par
enclosed at the very end.
\par
ix1. \textbf{Theoretical description of virtual teams and analysis of related key issues}
\par
\textbf{1.1. Virtual team}
\par
1.1.1. Definition of the virtual team
\par
"Virtual teams are groups of geographically and/or organizationally dispersed coworkers
\par
that are assembled using a combination of telecommunications and information
\par
technologies to accomplish an organizational task. Virtual teams rarely, if ever, meet in a
\par
face-to-face setting. They may be set up as temporary structures, existing only to
\par
accomplish a specific task, or may be more permanent structures, used to address ongoing
\par
issues, such as strategic planning. Further, membership is often fluid, evolving according
\par
to changing task requirements" [Townsend et al 1998].
\par
A virtual team is quite a new phenomeon that is why several names are used
\par
simultaneously for it like dispersed team, remote team, tele-team, digital team, or e-team.
\par
Personally I would prefer e-team, because it points out the way of keeping contact most
\par
of the time and indicates that it is very new. However technical literature uses the
\par
expression virtual team, that is why I apply this name throughout this paper.
\par
Although virtual teams have not been existing for so long, sequential teams could be seen
\par
as antecedents of those. "Sequential teams: where team members may be out of sight or
\par
earshot from each other for much of the time"[Adair 1987] for example traditional home
\par
workers or subcontractors working remotely. The main difference is that the work, which
\par
was done formerly by physical team and which is connected and directed to core
\par
functions of the organization are carried out remotely in case of virtual team now.
\par
Anyway we are now in a transitional period when new technology has been adopted more
\par
and more. It makes the structures, which defines how work and especially office work
\par
should be done, liquid [Status 1999]. In this new environment virtual teams have been
\par
applied at a growing number of places. A typical example is in the relationship between a
\par
representation office and a corporate center. Virtual teams have been established with
\par
participation of members both from corporate center and representation to improve
\par
efficiency with enhanced communication and understanding between them. The typical
\par
new postindustrial organizations, which are small or consists of small subunits of larger
\par
organization with decentralized managerial structure [Fulk 1995] prefer to apply virtual
\par
teams.
\par
11.1.2. Importance and advantages of virtual team
\par
Virtual teams have became focus topic of strategic human resource management, since
\par
economical, social, and technological environment changed from a stable and simple one
\par
to a turbulent and complex one and organizations should be frequently reorganized
\par
according to that. Today organizations sweep away of old secure certainties and the
\par
corresponding difficulty of adapting not just to a few new situations but to a large number
\par
of changing situations and demands [Status 1999]. Three significant examples for
\par
nowadays' organizational movements are 1.) the increasing importance and weight of the
\par
service sector which requires more intensive customer involvement [Kotler 2001] 2.) the
\par
shift from clerical/routine work to managerial work, and 3.) increasing level of
\par
outsourcing supported by EDI (electronic data interchange) [Fulk 1995].
\par
A virtual team is a very convenient tool to cope with the challenge of providing both
\par
flexibility and stability for an organization. Flexibility is important while organizations
\par
are responding to environmental changes and adjusting themselves to optimize their
\par
resource utilization and maximize their achievements. So it is necessary the radical
\par
questioning of whole processes at the organization level [Status 1999]. Such
\par
rearrangements could mean enormous movements in current industries dominated by
\par
multinational companies. Divisions or whole companies with multi site production and/or
\par
sales employing several thousands of people are being restructured, are spinning off,
\par
being acquired, or are merging. Organizational form is thus no longer fixed. New,
\par
electronically-based organizations with networked structure and different centers have
\par
emerged [Fulk 1995]. Thus virtual team can be strategically important also for small or
\par
medium size companies while endeavoring to globalize their business. Global appearance
\par
and representation have became a feasible option nowadays for most small and medium
\par
size companies by the help of the internet, intranet, e-business and e-commerce.
\par
Nevertheless changing organizational structure [1995 Fulk] always entails comprehensive
\par
reorganizations causing uncertainty especially in workers' job security, which increases
\par
labor turnover. Another aspect affecting workers, is that frequent organizational changes
\par
have been continuously providing new carrier opportunities, which lure employees to
\par
change position more often. "It is hard to prevent the brains walking out of the door if
\par
they want to"[Handy 1995]. Further "More and more professionals think of themselves
\par
as on a temporary assignment with organization"[Handy 1995].
\par
2On the other hand preserving and maintaining stability is inevitable to assure outstanding
\par
product and process quality, which is crucial from the point of view of the organizations,
\par
the customers, the marketing, and ultimately the profit. It is only achievable with solid
\par
knowledge and competence.
\par
Human resource is the most important asset, because only people with appropriate skills,
\par
education, training, and experience are able to make organizations function. Nevertheless
\par
the organizations tend to avoid being absolutely at their work force's mercy, because it
\par
could weaken their competitive positions. That is why they tend to build structures
\par
nowadays. which are independent of particular persons [Fulk 1995]. Quality assurance
\par
system and corporate standards, further other written policies and procedures have been
\par
applied for this reason. However there is no perfect bureaucracy and/or technology, real
\par
life is too complex to set rules, which apply to all situations. Furthermore the changes
\par
today are so frequent, that many times written documents, which otherwise could
\par
adequately guide workers how to behave, are behind the organization's current status.
\par
All in all, the above described paradoxical situation requesting both flexibility and
\par
stability can be solved only through enhanced teamwork supported by new technology. A
\par
study, undertaken by Gallup over many years and countries, pointed directly to the prime
\par
importance of small groups and teams, rather than overall corporate culture, in
\par
galvanizing success. Good relationships between team members and with the team
\par
leader/manager, even when these are not physically established and maintained, can it
\par
seems outweigh all other factors in increasing productivity and creativity [Status 1999].
\par
Virtual team resists much fewer to structural (not process) changes than traditional one. It
\par
is supposed to dynamically adapt itself to the new situation. "This capacity to quickly
\par
bring together diverse expertise into an effective unit is the characteristic that marks a
\par
quantum leap beyond bureaucracy and that will create competitive advantage for the next
\par
economic phase" [Heckscher 1995]. Organizations applying virtual teams are less
\par
vulnerable to change, because the likelihood that the whole dispersed team fails is much
\par
lower than that of an individual. Although team members have got complementary tasks,
\par
they know each other's work well enough to properly express their requests and
\par
expectations to each other to guide each other, how to behave and improve performance.
\par
It is especially important during the introduction period of a new comer. Teams preserve
\par
knowledge required for the task they are directed to. Thus virtual teams are able to
\par
perform their job in a more stable way than separate individuals could do that [Fulk
\par
31995]. "The problem is how to get these more individualistic employees to work together.
\par
A major answer seems to be to a focus on the organization's purpose " [Heckscher 1995].
\par
Since virtual team is a team, its importance is described in chapter 1.2.2. can apply also
\par
for virtual team.
\par
Virtual team provides the following additional advantages:
\par
access to previously locally unavailable expertise
\par
enhanced cross-functional interaction
\par
inter- and intraorganizational co-operation in network structure
\par
close customer involvement
\par
optimal labor related cost structure
\par
time flexibility.
\par
Rapid technological change can barely be followed by the education and training of
\par
experts, that causes shortage in labor market. However virtual team provides a possible
\par
solution to this problem. For example German car producer BMW have given chance to
\par
their highly qualified employees in the research and development areas to work from
\par
remote locations and from home [Status 1999].
\par
Networked organizations widely relying on their suppliers and customers [Fulk 1995] can
\par
enhance their work efficiency with applying cross organizational remote teams.
\par
Furthermore multinational companies with complex and sometimes ramifying
\par
organizational structure can also follow this strategy to be more effective by the means
\par
better co-operation team work can provide.
\par
Companies strive to fully exploit opportunities of new technology. Time is coming
\par
unfixed. Organizations want more flexibility [Handy 1995]. Virtual team supports to
\par
build and enhance skills required for remote work. Thus members of virtual teams can
\par
work independently from place, which provide better availability and ultimately time
\par
flexibility as well [Solomon 2001]. For example account managers are able to participate
\par
in a conference call while driving a car.
\par
1.1.3. Problems with virtual teams
\par
Virtual team is quite a new phenomenon so the difficulties connected to it can be
\par
categorized as child diseases, and the problems arise from the lack of the necessary
\par
knowledge on training and working processes, what and how to train and how to work?
\par
On the other hand, there are difficulties which are inherent with the topic like
\par
communication problems, cultural differences. "Recreating teams in virtual mode requires
\par
4resolution of the challenges and opportunities inherent in virtual team technology, as well
\par
as the development of a new team sociology" [Townsend et al 1998]. Thus the questions
\par
arise how to cope with the following special difficulties?:
\par
disrupted team cohesion (very limited personal relationships)
\par
different, more professional interpersonal interaction (communication) is required
\par
conflict between leaders on utilizing common virtual human resource
\par
multi skills (including knowledge on computer) are required
\par
frequently changing members
\par
less time is allowed for introduction and induction
\par
longer time is necessary to accomplish tasks.
\par
The team cohesion is very important for a continuous co-operation, but unfortunately it
\par
has been weakened by the distance. "Often, shared physical experience is essential for a
\par
shared view of the process"[Davenport et al]. The face to face groups will have stronger
\par
relational links - cohesion [Warkentin et al 1997]. "Virtual team members must learn new
\par
ways to express themselves and to understand others in an environment with a diminished
\par
sense of presence. Virtual team members will be required to have superior team
\par
participation skills" [Townsend et al 1998]. "There is evidence that perception or
\par
understanding of each other and of the group's decision is poorer in CMC (computer
\par
mediated communication) groups when compared to FTF (face to face) groups. ...
\par
Researchers have also reported on how the group member, who finally records the group's
\par
choice or decision, makes more errors in the CMC condition than in the FTF condition"
\par
[Bordia 1997].
\par
"Developing effective virtual teams goes well beyond the technical problem of linking
\par
them together. As workers increasingly interact in a virtual mode, it is imperative that
\par
they rebuild the interpersonal interaction necessary for organizational effectiveness"
\par
[Townsend et al 1998]. "The complexities and subtleties of dealing with widely different
\par
personalities, cultures, and languages make communication far more difficult among
\par
virtual team members" [Solomon 2001].
\par
The multi team structure is a challenge to team leaders since they have to compete for
\par
members as for scared resources. From another point of view, virtual team members may
\par
have multiple (and even competing) alliances outside their specific virtual teams
\par
[Townsend et al 1998]. It can entail internal conflicts. Someone in the organization must
\par
have the authority to take command in a crisis. Furthermore a sound general principle for
\par
all kinds of organizations that any member of the organization should have only one
\par
'master' [Drucker 1998].
\par
5Since tasks are complex, being effective member of virtual teams requires several skills.
\par
"Virtual team members will have to become proficient with a variety of computer-based
\par
technologies" [Townsend et al 1998]. The new challenges require diverse management
\par
skills [Solomon 2001].
\par
"Because team membership will be somewhat fluid, effective teams will require members
\par
who can quickly assimilate into the team"[Townsend et al 1998].
\par
Although it takes more time to establish a virtual team than a physical one, it is usually
\par
requested to carry out faster than that of the physical one. "The potential for startup
\par
problems and deliberate resistance is substantially greater than for changes in structure or
\par
technology alone" [Townsend et al 1998].
\par
Where interpretive context does not exist or is not shared, FTF is the appropriate
\par
communication mode. EM (electronic messaging is effective and efficient within shared
\par
context [Zack 1993]. Thus when time is limited, CMC groups perform better than FTF
\par
groups on tasks involving less, and worse on tasks requiring more, social-emotional
\par
interaction, though also social cues can be transmitted via CMC [Walther 1994].
\par
"Unlike many traditional teams, virtual teams will be expected to be able to repeatedly
\par
change membership without losing productivity; little time will be available for team
\par
members to learn how to work together"[Townsend et al 1998].
\par
Further problems connected with virtual teams like cultural differences or media selection
\par
are outlined in the analysis in chapter 1.3. on key issues.
\par
Chapter 1.2. is about teams in general. It describes further issues relevant to virtual teams
\par
as well.
\par
6\textbf{1.2. Team work}
\par
To set out a more detailed analysis of the virtual team teamwork should be examined first.
\par
1.2.1. Definition of team
\par
The most important feature, which differentiate teams from other groups are co-operation
\par
among members, who provide complementary contribution towards achieving common
\par
objectives accepted by all of them. Team's job should be divided into tasks so that
\par
members could support each other in an optimal way from the whole team's point of
\par
view. "For team membership involves a contract to put the interest of the whole team
\par
before one's own, at least for the duration of the task"[Adair 1987].
\par
1.2.2. Importance of team
\par
Teamwork has got biological roots [Cs\'anyi 2000], so it played significant role in the
\par
history beginning with cavemen hunting in groups. However its importance has not been
\par
taken consciously for long, because bigger cultural units like nations disguised it. That is
\par
why inside the organizations it should be reinvented and emphasized from time to time.
\par
"During the past several years, one of the most dominant intraorganizational initiatives
\par
has been the development of team-based work systems. Many organizations have
\par
recognized that team based structures have the potential to create a more productive,
\par
creative, and individually fulfilling working environment. A majority of U.S. corporations
\par
use some form of team structures in their organizations, and many report that teams
\par
enhance their ability to meet organizational goals. In general, teams have provided firms
\par
with significant gains in productivity, and as such, have become a fixture among
\par
contemporary organizations. But what happens to the team advantage when fundamental
\par
organizational structures begin to change?" [Townsend et al 1998]
\par
I should highlight and emphasize the following characteristics of teamwork in general:
\par
it extends individual's abilities
\par
it enhances productivity
\par
it supports making products (including services) which requires multi skills
\par
it emphasizes different thinking necessary for creative problem solving
\par
it emphasizes loyalty
\par
7The most important advantage of working in team or in other words, working with others
\par
on a common project so that performance efficiency and effectiveness can be enhanced, is
\par
that a huge amount of more complex work can be done. Members of a good team do
\par
allow us to transcend our own individual limitations of knowledge, ability and
\par
performance [Adair 1987].
\par
If two or more people are working together it is not the same as they would do it
\par
independently. They can inspire, control, direct, and also hinder each other, so the
\par
interrelationship among them can significantly modify the output of the work. If there is a
\par
team it will approach these tasks in a different way from a more collection of individuals
\par
[Adair 1987].
\par
On one hand, in our complex life people have to specialize to be good at a particular
\par
issue. On the other hand, making something requires knowledge from several areas, for
\par
example, sales requires the knowledge of marketing, finance and logistics. That is why
\par
instead of individuals only bigger units of people (teams) are able to perform certain
\par
tasks. "Greater teamwork is required when customer satisfaction hinges on the efforts of
\par
more than one worker; when individuals do different things to create the final product;
\par
and when a lot of give-and-take is needed among those involved"[Drexler and Forrester
\par
1998].
\par
Teams can also support retainment, because man is a social animal and human aspects are
\par
very momentous while considering working conditions, for example, where or with
\par
whom to work. "Teams generate a climate of loyalty, writes Tom Douglas, which stems
\par
from the acceptance of dependence on other to achieve a desired outcome... Members
\par
accept the skills and knowledge of other members as a common resource and the sense of
\par
sharing and shared experience, which distinguish a member from non-members is
\par
high"[Adair 1987].
\par
1.2.3. Problems with team
\par
Although teamwork can provide a lot of benefits and opportunities, unfortunately special
\par
problems can come with it as follows:
\par
organizational issues - difficult to clearly set and locate team borders - resource
\par
allocation
\par
management issues - impossible to set independent responsibilities of each team
\par
members, on the other hand, if many people are responsible for something, then
\par
nobody is really responsible for it - defining accountability
\par
8personal issues - good informal relationship vs. power, people on the same level in the
\par
hierarchy.
\par
Inside a complex organization which have tensor structure with networked divisional
\par
relationships people have got different priorities, that is why they could interpret
\par
differently where teams are located. It is also subject to different considerations how
\par
many people can or should belong to one team (optimal number of members), or
\par
maximum how many team a man can belong to (membership question).
\par
The biggest advantage of teamwork is that tasks, which would be impossible or very
\par
ineffective for an individual, can be achieved by it. However, sharp separation of
\par
contributions of each members can be difficult. Furthermore, today s dynamically
\par
changing environment requires flexibility in defining tasks dedicated to particular
\par
persons. It hinders comprehensive responsibility charting, which can cause role conflict,
\par
role incompatibility, role overload, role underload, and role ambiguity. "When people are
\par
not clear about the limits of their accountability they tend to play safe and cramp
\par
themselves within that they guess the limits to be, whereas when limits are known the
\par
confidence thus generated sees them exploited to the full" [Adair 1987]. White areas in
\par
responsibility can lead to uncompleted tasks.
\par
Good personal relationship is inevitable for effective work. However, as also Bazerman
\par
states, people's views on word are very subjective and they favor themselves against
\par
others, that causes high possibility of conflicts. Furthermore, a conflict between two
\par
people being on the same level of the hierarchy is difficult to solve without involving
\par
somebody from the top, which requires extra energy while efficiency being spoiled.
\par
1.2.4. Building a team
\par
Nowadays a team is not something which is born from itself. It should be built and its
\par
existence should be openly expressed so that everybody who is connected to it and
\par
especially its members can be aware of it. The previous sentence may sound very natural
\par
but as it is described in chapter 1.2.3. borders of teams could be ambiguous for people
\par
with different perspectives. So I would suggest that the first step of building a team
\par
should be to examine the structure of the organization while considering internal and
\par
external relationships. Frontiers outlined in such way mean strict constraints for selecting
\par
team members in most of the cases. However, if there is further freedom of choice, then
\par
the candidates' human personalities should be taken into consideration. "The central factor
\par
in managing teams well is managing the interdependencies among team members. The9ease with which individuals can work interdependently depends on several factors,
\par
including: 1. trust, 2. extroversion, 3. dependency needs, and 4. conflict avoidance"
\par
[Drexler and Forrester 1998].
\par
Trust is considered as one of the key issues of virtual teams, thus chapter 1.3.includes
\par
definition and analysis of its relationship to other key issues.
\par
Extroversion is a personal characteristics which reflects people's willingness to deal with
\par
other persons so it is inevitable for co-operative work.
\par
Dependency needs come from the social nature of man while looking for fellows and
\par
partners.
\par
Conflict avoidance is necessary for stable, continuous co-operation, especially in case of
\par
teamwork with complementary contribution of members. Disagreements are unavoidable
\par
while working together. However, it does matter how they are handled. Radically
\par
questioning each other's work can demolish mutual respect and disrupt necessary personal
\par
relationships. So appropriate tolerance is required.
\par
The needs described above point out the importance of establishing informal relationship,
\par
which is inevitable for good co-operation. Thus next step of building a team is the first
\par
kick of meeting. "If you want to build a team it is essential that you do get it together...
\par
The members of a new group assembling for the first time require much energy to getting
\par
acquainted with one another.... A crucial factor in group or team formation is therefore
\par
the amount of time that has spent together" [Adair 1987].
\par
Although external factors like strong or weak corporate culture can influence the process
\par
how a team takes shape, all of them should get through the following phases:
\par
forming
\par
storming
\par
norming
\par
conforming
\par
performing.
\par
As described in the first paragraph of this chapter initial considerations are necessary
\par
before building a team. Then after starting common work initial problems or child
\par
diseases can arise resulting conflicts. Rules or internal standards are necessary for framing
\par
how work should go. However it is not enough to set these rules, members should accept
\par
and agree with them and the perfect work can come only after that.
\par
There are different ways to accelerate and make a team function. Adair describes two
\par
types of team building exercise substituting team task and real task. The first one can be
\par
10an exact business case study, the second one can be going away (for a weekend) to plan
\par
company strategy.
\par
Presentation of the team to the client is a major step towards building a team.
\par
The following characteristics set by Adair are useful tool for checking if a particular team
\par
does function properly:
\par
care for each other
\par
open and truthful
\par
high level of trust
\par
decision by consensus
\par
conflict is faced up
\par
listen to idea
\par
feelings expressed freely
\par
process issues are dealt with.
\par
A team has to be grown or built through the experience of working together, failures
\par
teach success [Adair 1987].
\par
1.2.5. Maintaining a team
\par
Teams like garden plants require care to be able to bear maximal fruits. Many times an
\par
existing team should be adjusted according to new circumstances like introducing a new-
\par
comer, or changing the organizational structure. Thus the leader's functions described in
\par
chapter 1.3. about leadership should also be mentioned here. These functions are about to
\par
visionize, get priorities kept and represent the whole team interests.
\par
Adair puts an emphasize on the distinction between task and maintenance behavior. Task
\par
behavior is related to the team's core functions and directed to complete the tasks they
\par
have been dedicated to. It is about proper way of work (what to do and how to do). While
\par
maintenance behavior is about what is necessary for and what can enhance the proper
\par
way of work (what support is needed to do). The characteristics of an effective team
\par
should be continuously kept in view while maintaining a team in order to retain its
\par
efficiency.
\par
Although I listed the difficulty with collective accountability among problems in chapter
\par
1.2.3., this is relevant only from the point of view of the process or the task. Common
\par
feedback on team performance can be useful to gain effectiveness of the whole team
\par
versus individual efficiency. "If team members are very interdependent in their work, it is
\par
counterproductive to base compensation systems only on individual performance. The
\par
11nature of such work demands compatible systems: team-based pay and compensation;
\par
performance appraisal and accountability at the team level; and recognition for team
\par
results"[Drexler and Forrester 1998].
\par
Sport analogy can help to understand the right approach to managing interdependency.
\par
For example, in a good football team each member can support each other while fighting
\par
to achieve the "goals", though they are not inferior or superior to each other and the tasks
\par
cannot be fully separated.
\par
As all teams are groups in a wider sense, section about group in chapter 1.3. can apply to
\par
teams in general.
\par
12\textbf{1.3. Virtual team's relationship to key issues}
\par
After studying relevant literature I have found the following key issues to be especially
\par
related to virtual teams while first of all the human aspects of this topic is considered:
\par
communication
\par
culture
\par
group
\par
leadership
\par
knowledge work
\par
productivity
\par
trust.
\par
Before providing one by one analysis between virtual team and the factors listed above,
\par
the meanings of communication, culture, group, leadership, knowledge work, and trust
\par
should be drawn up, because their meanings are different and more complex than those
\par
presented in a dictionary. I should give a reason why productivity, motivation and control
\par
are included as well. The critical issues interrelate to each other as well, which is reflected
\par
by the order of factors in the list. So the most significant connections are between
\par
communication - culture; culture -group; communication - leadership; leadership -
\par
knowledge work; leadership - productivity; productivity - culture; knowledge work -
\par
trust; trust -group. This set of critical issues and their relationship to the virtual team and
\par
to each other build a model (or a construct) [Figure 1.3.1.], which serves as framework for
\par
the analysis. So not only their meaning but also both relationship and hierarchy among
\par
them are important.
\par
Figure 1.3.1. Constructs of key issues related to virtual team
\par
13Communication
\par
From a technical point of view communication is encoding and passing information by a
\par
sender via a communication channel (media), which might be distorted by noise, to a
\par
receiver, who decodes it. However human communication is too complex to be
\par
understood based on this abstract model. Although new technology enables almost
\par
exclusively language-based communication (oral via phone, written via e-mail and fax), it
\par
does not make the investigation simpler. In case of human communication even if we
\par
eliminate channel distortion, the sender is restricted to a full knowledge in advance of
\par
how the receiver will interpret his/her message because of the subject matter. Language
\par
is not (as commonly thought) a system for transferring thoughts or meaning from one
\par
brain to another, but a system for organizing information and for releasing thoughts and
\par
responses in other organism. Furthermore the written language is symbolization of
\par
symbolization [Hall 1976]. Thus because of individual differences in perception
\par
interpretation of a message is very subjective. This statement can be true not only for
\par
language but also for other tools used for communication. For example, the format of
\par
presenting (framing) a mathematical problem can influence how easy it is to solve. [M\'er\H o
\par
1997] [Bazermann 2000].
\par
Media can also alter the interpretation [Daft and Lengel 1987]. Sending and receiving
\par
information through a communication medium is a decision that affects the meaning of
\par
the message. "Effective communication depends on the selection of a medium that has
\par
the capacity to engage both sender and receiver in mutual understanding of the message at
\par
hand" [Daft and Lengel 1986]. While the receiver and the sender can have different media
\par
preferences [Westmyer et al].
\par
"One study indicated that the richness of a medium may depend less on the characteristics
\par
of the channel, and more on the perceptions of the user" [Carlson and Davis 1998]. Thus
\par
both the importance of the media used in communication and the receiver's cognitive base
\par
in decoding message are important. It explains why scientists are debating on different
\par
media selection theories namely why people and especially managers select a particular
\par
media. "The media richness/social presence theories to include social interaction theories,
\par
made research very complex" [Carlson and Davis 1998]. Approaches that concentrate on
\par
the characteristics of new media may be less useful for certain purposes than those that
\par
focus on communicative practices [Orlikowski and Yates 1994].
\par
14To cut the Gordian knot and advance understanding of this complex problem it is worth
\par
introducing bigger units of communication, which includes media and is characteristic for
\par
a particular situation. Communicative genres: socially recognized types of communicative
\par
actions such as memos, meetings, expense forms, training seminars, that are habitually
\par
enacted by members of a community to realize particular social purpose. However this
\par
complex model also needs improvement, since "communicative action often involves the
\par
use of multiple genres that work together to produce a more complex communicative
\par
practice ... genres, in use, are rarely homogeneous and clearly separate, with particular
\par
communicative actions often being characterized by several generic strategies. Further
\par
more genre system is very dynamic. "People produce, reproduce, and change genres
\par
through a process of structuring" [Orlikowski and Yates 1994].
\par
All in all the following factors have got special importance, especially in language based
\par
communication:
\par
contexting
\par
media selection
\par
genre repertoire.
\par
"From the practical viewpoint of communications strategy, one must decide how much
\par
time to invest in contexting another person"[Hall 1976]. In practice it means how detailed
\par
and long introduction or description is applied to get the receiver understand the message.
\par
Media selection is about what media is used in particular cases and why it is used there.
\par
Genre repertoire is what mediums are employed in communication sequences directed to
\par
a particular purpose and how they are used.
\par
Communication - group
\par
What media are preferred in a group or between groups is a matter of agreement to a
\par
certain extent. "Social influence on technology-related attitudes and behavior were
\par
consistently stronger when individuals were highly attracted to their workgroups" [Fulk
\par
1993].
