Culture
\par
Culture has got biological roots. Working, performing, purposive cultural organizations
\par
by human individuals like village, city, religion, sect, party, war commando, school,
\par
theater, factory, state is easy to be build, because it has got biologically determined
\par
system organizer capability [Cs\'anyi 2000].
\par
15Culture has been defined on various ways. According to Kroeber and Kluckhohn culture
\par
consists of patterns, explicit and implicit, of and for behavior transmitted by symbols,
\par
constituting the distinctive achievement of human groups, including their embodiment in
\par
artifacts; the essential core of culture consists of traditional (i.e. historically derived and
\par
selected) ideas and especially their attached values; culture systems may, on the one hand,
\par
be considered as products of action; and on the other hand, as conditioning elements of
\par
future action.
\par
I put emphasize on the following characteristics of culture:
\par
defines latent but basic values
\par
appears in human behavior, actions, and physical artefacts
\par
common to a set of people
\par
learned.
\par
For managerial or business implication it is important, that an organization does have a
\par
particular culture, which can and should be investigated. "All companies have a culture,
\par
whether weak or strong, which has a powerful influence on the company, thus having a
\par
major influence on its success. Many researchers now accept that, to a degree,
\par
organizational culture can be evaluated in ways similar to those used to analyze national
\par
cultures" [Foster 1998]. Figure 1.3.2. shows a model structure of corporate culture while
\par
delineating its key elements and their relationships.
\par
Figure 1.3.2. the cultural web [Johnson and Scholes]
\par
Culture - communication
\par
The evolution of new type human communication systems was accompanied by a very
\par
important neurobiological change: the doubling of the external world's representations in
\par
the mind. Representations in the brain of the holder of even the most primitive human
\par
16culture can be separated into two main categories. His or her personal representations
\par
belong to the one, which have been gathered during his or her life based on his or her own
\par
experiences and thinking about his or her environment, fellows, himself or herself. Most
\par
of the animals use this kind of representations of the brain during their everyday life.
\par
However the man of culture have got memories which were born during group
\par
communication. These are memories about when another one expressed something during
\par
a communication act or in the contrary when he or she wanted to make something
\par
understood to other ones [Cs\'anyi 2000].
\par
"To convey cultural values, which drive much communication behavior, informal
\par
channels are used even more than formal ones and the channels are interrelated in
\par
complex ways"[Gilsdorf 1998]. Social interaction theories like symbolic interactionism
\par
emphasize social context as a source of influence in media selection [Carlson and Davis
\par
1998]. Meaning of a communication is always dependent upon the context, indirections,
\par
hierarchies of rhythms [Hall 1976]."Because of their shared organizational context, even
\par
different individuals who hold different opinions on the same matter and who are
\par
motivated by conflicting interests can end up with negotiated meanings for the same
\par
action and even choosing the same way in which to act. ...The same publicly observable
\par
behavior can have completely different meanings in different social contexts"
\par
[Ngwenyama and Lee 1997]. There is the world of the organization standing behind any
\par
e-mail communication [Lee 1994].
\par
"A genre established within a particular community serves as an institutionalized template
\par
for social action--an organizing structure--that shapes the ongoing communicative actions
\par
of community members through their use of it. ... Activities such as tax auditing, surgery,
\par
customer support, and personnel review all involve communicative action and
\par
communicative genres.... A genre of organizational communication, such as the business
\par
letter, shareholders' meeting, or report, is a distinctive type of communicative action,
\par
characterized by a socially recognized communicative purpose and common aspects of
\par
form" [Orlikowsky and Yates].
\par
All in all as culture is represented in the individuals' brains, it has got a direct effect on
\par
human communication. Culture defines certain context that frames and so determines the
\par
meaning of the message received.
\par
17Group
\par
"A psychological group is any number of people who (1) interact with one other, (2) are
\par
psychologically aware of another, and (3) perceives themselves to be a group.... The size
\par
of a group is thus limited by the possibilities of mutual interaction and mutual awareness.
\par
Mere aggregate of people does not fit this definition because they do not interact and do
\par
not perceive themselves to be a group even if they aware of each other as, for instance, a
\par
crowd on street corner watching some event. A total department, a union, or a whole
\par
organization would not be a group in spite of thinking of themselves as 'we', because they
\par
generally do not all interact and are not all aware of each other. However work teams,
\par
committees, subparts of departments, cliques, and various informal associations among
\par
organizational members would fit this definition"[Schein 1980].
\par
"Groups are not only there to carry out tasks - they provide you a series of unique
\par
opportunities to grow as a person"[Adair 1987].
\par
Advantages of groupwork:
\par
greater cooperation
\par
easier communication
\par
increased resistance to frustration
\par
reduced labor turnover
\par
lower absenteeism
\par
low tolerance of 'slackers'
\par
Disadvantages of groupwork:
\par
life more difficult for new entrants
\par
restricts entry for new ideas
\par
resist change in work practice
\par
combative by other groups
\par
[Cs\'anyi 2000] differentiate primeval groups based on big family, horde, or tribe from
\par
modern groups enabled by enhanced technology. Primer groups characterised by
\par
emotional cohesion and self-sacrifice, while modern groups are built on rational interest.
\par
Group - culture
\par
The nature of the man's system organizer capabilities are that it organizes groups from
\par
individuals and the individual is determined by the group while the group is determined
\par
18by the individuals which form it. Culture express this special double determination
\par
[Cs\'anyi 2000]. Also Hofstede's culture definition: "the collective programming of the
\par
mind distinguishes the members of one group or category of people from another" points
\par
out culture as a crucial factor for distinguishing a member of a group from others.
\par
So if we take an extended definition of group, then culture and group are inseparable.
\par
Every (even small) group has got a (micro)culture. The stronger the culture the sharper
\par
the group border and the bigger the group cohesion is.
\par
On the other hand, a group has got standards, which are more explicit and do affect the
\par
members' behaviors more directly than unconscious cultural values. Nevertheless group
\par
standards should be in harmony or based on cultural values otherwise contradiction
\par
between them cause confusion and demolish group cohesion.
\par
Leadership
\par
"Managerial jobs have changed from mainly supervising and giving orders to
\par
subordinates to, among other things, providing services to subordinates, forming cross-
\par
functional teams, shaping strategy, helping clients in the field, and leading cross-
\par
departmental and cross-company teams. This means that managers 'have to learn to
\par
manage in situations where they don't have command authority, where they are neither
\par
controlled nor controlling' and that they need to learn to manage upward and sideways in
\par
addition to managing downward. These changes have made the job of managers more
\par
complex" [Hooijberg 1996]. Since lead is to serve, the role of the leader is to help the
\par
group to achieve its common task [Adair 1987].
\par
A leader provides sense of direction; points and emphasizes goals; highlights and
\par
maintains structure; further he/she gets priority to keep. He/she has to know both his/her
\par
own and the other team members' strengths and weaknesses. He/she has to be able to
\par
inspire others while using his/her complex set of behavioral repertoires in response to
\par
complex organizational circumstances [Hooijberg 1996]. "A leader in any field should
\par
still have sufficient knowledge to be able to assess the professional worth of the members
\par
of his team. ...effective leaders must be able to deal with paradox and contradiction by
\par
performing multiple leadership roles simultaneously" [Leidner et al 1999].
\par
General functions of a leader are planning, initiating, controlling, supporting, informing,
\par
and evaluating.
\par
19Leadership - communication
\par
"Communication media are tools executives use to interpret work situations and influence
\par
or direct co-workers... It is important for senior executives to realize that in a very basic
\par
sense, a medium is an extension of self" [Lengel and Daft 1988].
\par
Knowledge work
\par
"Both knowledge and knowledge work are difficult to define precisely Quinn et al. equate
\par
knowledge with professional intellect. To them, professional intellect in organizations
\par
centers around know-what, know-how, know-why, and self-motivated creativity. Others
\par
define knowledge more narrowly as agreed-on explicit or formal facts, rules, policies, and
\par
procedures, whereas they see skills as competencies that can generate explicit knowledge.
\par
Skills are learned by doing; knowledge is learned by studying or investigating.... .
\par
Knowledge work processes include such activities as research and product development,
\par
advertising, education, and professional services like law, accounting, and consulting. We
\par
also include management processes such as strategy and planning" [Davenport et al].
\par
Knowledge work- leadership
\par
Basically leaders are such knowledge workers, whose responsibility is also to deal with
\par
people.
\par
For me both knowledge worker and leader mean a professional, who guide through
\par
his/her colleagues across the 'territory', which is unknown for most of the others and they
\par
provide significant contribution to achieving goals.
\par
Productivity
\par
This term is to emphasize the managerial approach of this paper. Nevertheless the
\par
majority of researches connected to virtual teams or similar topics are outside the business
\par
environment [Bordia 1997]. Furthermore, they are mostly very theoretical scientific
\par
papers, which usually do not answer and even formulate practical questions directly
\par
applicable for managers. The main target of this dissertation is to provide support for
\par
virtual team work with improved productivity. The following terms are connected to it:
\par
work methods
\par
response time
\par
motivation
\par
control
\par
time utilization
\par
20task allocation - responsibility charting
\par
replacing colleagues (both temporary and long term).
\par
Productivity - communication
\par
Since the major part of virtual team members' work is communication, their
\par
communication (working) method including response time has got a strong effect on
\par
productivity. "Because electronic communication technologies can provide technology-
\par
based means of coordination and control, human-based coordination can be reduced in
\par
some parts of the organization hierarchy" [Fulk 1995].
\par
Productivity - leadership
\par
Leadership is directly related to motivation, control, and time utilization. The term itself
\par
was formed to present a new way for approaching todays' (office) workers to enhance
\par
their productivity with new type and more apt human resource techniques, which
\par
correspond to modern motivation theories.
\par
Nevertheless control has not lost its importance, though leaders should exercise it less
\par
directly, to avoid biases from subjective nature of man [Bazerman 2000] in human
\par
resource management. So it is a crucial question how depersonalized control can be
\par
institutionalized.
\par
Productivity - culture
\par
Continuing the thought from the previous paragraph, potential of depersonalized control
\par
is very much up to culture. High collectivity and high power distance culture [Hofstede
\par
1991] can provide less opportunity for objective, person-independent judgement, because
\par
individual or group priorities bias it.
\par
Trust
\par
"Definition of trust: 'the willingness of a party to be vulnerable to the actions of another
\par
party based on the expectation that the other will perform a particular action important to
\par
the trustor, irrespective of the ability to monitor or control that other party'. In short, trust
\par
is based on the expectation that others will behave as expected" [Jarvenpaa et al 1998].
\par
Definition of trust in the Oxford English Dictionary is `confidence in or reliance on some
\par
quality or attribute of a person or thing, or the truth of a statement'.
\par
21Nevertheless after studying relevant literature it is clear that there are various theories and
\par
structures to set different types of trust. Unfortunately they seem to be incompatible with
\par
each other [Kramer 1999]. So I could not find a 'common multiplier' or establish one,
\par
which fits to or combines several theories. The reason for this might be that experts of
\par
different scientific areas have investigated trust, so their contexts are very different.
\par
I have found three main issues concerning trust from the point of view of this work:
\par
trust does positively relate to co-operation [Jones and Georg 1998] [Bateson]
\par
[Good 2000] [Gambetta 2000]
\par
initial trust is different than that of later on [Good 2000][Harrison et al 1998]
\par
frequency of interaction between trustor and trustee has got positive effect on trust
\par
[Good 2000][McAllister 1995].
\par
Trust - communication
\par
High-trust teams engaged in frequent communication [Jarvenpaa et al 1998].
\par
The greater the amount of communication there is between the players in a wide variety
\par
of games, the greater the likelihood of there being a mutually beneficial outcome [Good
\par
2000].
\par
Trust - group
\par
A group indicates a set of people for trust. "In practice it is hard to know more than 50
\par
people. Those 50, can each turn, know another 50 and so on. .... Large organizations are
\par
not therefore incompatible with the principle of trust, but they have to be made up of
\par
relatively constant, small groupings"[Handy Jun 1995].
\par
On the other hand, today's fragmented structure of individualized micro-cultures [Cs\'anyi
\par
2000] can support group cohesion less. Thus institutional trust that emphasizes building
\par
groups has gained special importance. So trust is necessary to facilitate group cohesion
\par
especially at an initial phase.
\par
Trust - knowledge work
\par
Since it is difficult to separate the knowledge work process from its outcomes [Davenport
\par
et al 1996] during the process there is limited possibility for checking and interrupting.
\par
Furthermore, it is ineffective to employ somebody only to supervise others. Thus the
\par
manager must trust knowledge workers at least by the time they complete their actual
\par
tasks.
\par
221.3.1. Virtual team and communication
\par
Communication technology now enables millions of people to work on virtual task teams,
\par
in which participants are often new to each other [Harrison et al 1998]. So members of
\par
virtual teams should not only keep but also establish contact to each other via e-mail,
\par
telephone (and videophone). However they might not get used to this dramatic
\par
elimination of personal meetings. Today means of communication is relational rather than
\par
routine [Fulk 1995]. That is why conscious and effective communication for enabling not
\par
only clear understanding but also personal awareness or social presence is even more
\par
important than in case of traditional teams. On the other hand, effective communication
\par
skills, clarity of goals, and a performance orientation will continue to be critical attributes
\par
for virtual team members [Townsend et al 1998].
\par
New communication technologies have not only introduced new media but also a
\par
communication processes containing new genres and hierarchy (priorities for distributing
\par
and asking for information) as well. Employees communicating directly with whosoever
\par
is most relevant for their current task [Symon 2000]. "Forget the hierarchy, use the
\par
network"[Handy 1995]. It is supported by shared computer databases, electronic mail and
\par
organizational Intranets, the Internet, computer conferencing, GroupWare.
\par
Although experiments show that short-term projects can be managed fully virtually,
\par
without any personal contact of the members [Kayworth and Leidner 1999], personal
\par
meeting is inevitable in the majority of cases, especially where virtual contacts have
\par
replaced traditional ones. "Video-conferences, voice mail and other technological devices
\par
help, but there is no real substitute for looking someone in the eyes while you talk or they
\par
talk"[Handy 1995]. "Periodic face-to-face meetings were also necessary to counteract the
\par
decay that occurred over time in confidence when electronic means of communication
\par
were used" [Jarvenpaa and Ives 1994].
\par
One of the most important fields for team development is communication. "Over time, the
\par
team will develop a variety of methods to ensure that their communication is both
\par
efficient and accurate" [Townsend et al 1998]. "In case of ambiguity in the assignments,
\par
the members in the high-trust teams did not hesitate to contact the coordinator with
\par
questions rather than making their own assumptions" [Jarvenpaa et al 1998]. Another
\par
aspect is that the "quality" (effectiveness) of the communication from working point of
\par
view is higher in case of remote contact because it is more collected and orderly.
\par
Although CMC (computer mediated communication) groups need more time and
\par
23exchange fewer comments, they perform nearly as well as FTF (face to face) groups. It is
\par
because CMC groups have more task-oriented remarks. Only the social-emotional
\par
exchange seems to be different [Walther 1994].
\par
Another aspect is that electronic communication at work is getting another function
\par
besides passing information. It is to increase social presence, in other words, to pay
\par
attention to the sender. It puts pressure on the receiver to focus on the sender's problem.
\par
Thus the form and content of the communication have changed. While formerly in remote
\par
communication conciseness got first priority beside clearness to avoid unnecessary
\par
conversation, now the frequency is critical. Earlier people located remotely
\par
communicated only if they had something to discuss. It was partly because of the
\par
structure of contacts and partly because of the cost of communication. For example, direct
\par
colleagues sat together and saw each other, thus most of the communication done
\par
physically directly. So there was no need for deliberate action to draw attention to each
\par
other. On the other hand, special abbreviations were used in telex messages to cut cost.
\par
Now even direct colleagues can work remotely and are in "continuous" communication
\par
via telephone and e-mail. That is why it is necessary not only to discuss issues but also to
\par
pay attention to each other. Electronic communication has gained special function of
\par
maintaining relationship, which is necessary not only for peer to peer but also for team
\par
co-operation.
\par
1.3.2. Virtual team and culture
\par
The main specialty of the relationship between culture and a virtual team is that inside a
\par
virtual team there are members with different national and organizational background. "In
\par
many organizations, virtual team membership will cross national boundaries, and a
\par
variety of cultural backgrounds will be represented on the team. This will complicate
\par
communications and work interactions, and will require additional team member
\par
development in the areas of communication and cultural diversity" [Townsend et al
\par
1998].
\par
Multicolored culture could be good for idea generation and creativity because it broadens
\par
perspective and supports multi point of view. On the other hand, it can make everyday
\par
work difficult, for example, with communication problems. It was discussed previously in
\par
this chapter how culture effects communication with highlighting the importance of
\par
context. A lot of preliminary knowledge is needed to interpret information and understand
\par
24the message included in it. However, by extending the cultural variety the volume of this
\par
previously necessary learning could be incredible high making work ineffective.
\par
Furthermore, it is not only about information and knowledge but also thinking and
\par
behavior. Different value system and traditions determine how individuals think and
\par
behave [M\'er\H o 1997][Bazermann 2000] and that can be very bounded. A particular culture
\par
defines certain priorities that are hard to be altered. If people who work together think
\par
significantly differently on what things are important, it can cause conflicts. Fortunately,
\par
there is a certain balance, because limited contact disguises the differences and people
\par
tend to concentrate rather on similarities. As an ancient Chinese proverb says 'we think
\par
someone is in his right mind until we get acquainted with him'. Hyperpersonalization
\par
theory for groups limited to computer-supported communication [Walther 1992] argues
\par
that members assume similarity and tend to reveal factors and cues about themselves that
\par
only reinforce this similarity. It is because individuating information (cues that help
\par
others understand if they are similar or different, for example, physically) is scarce in a
\par
virtual context. In turn, this overattribution of similarity may nurture perceptions of
\par
concern, care, and a belief in the virtue of a team relationship. In contrast as people are
\par
more sensitive to negative issues and virtual contact provides less opportunity to
\par
eliminate clash of views, a few negative experiences could significantly destroy the
\par
relationship between remote colleagues. "When users have time to exchange information,
\par
to build impressions, and to compare values. There is nothing radical in this; it is true of
\par
FTF interaction, too. It may take longer in CMC, however. On the other hand, when users
\par
even so much as expect to have a long-term association, CMC is no less personal than
\par
FTF" [Walther 1994]. This is the point where trust plays a great role. Its relationship to
\par
the virtual team is discussed in chapter 1.3.7.
\par
We are in a changing socio-cultural environment, and cultural value systems have been
\par
specialized and individualized. Individuals used to get ready-made culture of their group
\par
earlier, but culture in new societies has became optional, criticizable, refusable. They can
\par
realize a little personal cultural representation, a new and unique culture, an autonomous
\par
man [Cs\'anyi 2000]. Electronic communication technologies are enablers of changed
\par
forms by offering capabilities to overcome constraints on time and distance [Fulk 1995].
\par
There are new types of boundaries in space and time, for example, between work and
\par
private, family and community life, where these activities are not necessarily completely
\par
separated as they used to be in the past, but where choice and complementary are
\par
paramount [Status 1999]. In accordance to these the importance of social status have been
\par
25decreasing, while power distance became smaller. There is greater equality of
\par
participation in CMC groups. "In FTF groups, status differences in group members often
\par
determine participation, with higher status members participating more. In CMC groups,
\par
on the other hand, participation tends to be more balanced or equitable" [Bordia 1997].
\par
All of the movements described above do a favor in building virtual teams. However, it
\par
does not go smoothly, so dynamic characteristics and change of different cultures should
\par
be taken into account. Different geographical and cultural areas response to or follow the
\par
change differently. So it is necessary to take into consideration not only the current
\par
cultural differences [Hofstede 1991] but also their ability to change.
\par
1.3.3. Virtual team and group
\par
Passion of the man to belong to a group is so strong, that even virtual groups can be easily
\par
built [Cs\'anyi 2000]. As culture and group are inseparable the train of thought about
\par
nowadays microculture of individuals in chapter 1.3.2.developes here while projecting
\par
future social structures. The one-man group is free, though its freedom commands a high
\par
emotional price, it can construct a self-made culture, a total new group, together with
\par
those who are similar to it, which can provide an emotional cohesion again. For example,
\par
the Internet could be an effective tool for such activity. A creative, constructive man can
\par
search the company of those with unbelievable efficiency, who are similar to him or her,
\par
and if he or she finds it, the social forces for building bigger groups can start to function
\par
and entirely special new groups can take shape. From the point of view of human
\par
ethology it can be stated as a summary, that the next phase of the evolution will be
\par
characterized by the competition between the meeting of one-man groups, private
\par
cultures, the forming of supergroups with emotional cohesion, and a global, market
\par
centered 'phalanx'. A man's social cohesion will support building groups in any
\par
circumstances, and as we have seen, a man is able to build group of groups, a connection
\par
of multi level groups. The world shall be any kind, it will be based on connection of multi
\par
level groups competing and co-operating [Cs\'anyi 2000].
\par
The movements described above not only supports building virtual teams but also point
\par
them as main element of future society. Voluntary virtual organizations like linux
\par
community [Markus 2000] form exactly such multi groups already today. In addition,
\par
multinational companies are becoming more and more similar to such new multi groups,
\par
though they were not built clearly bottom up.
\par
26The theory of [Walther 1992] stating that people in virtual environment emphasizing
\par
rather personal similarities than differences also applies here, because it outlines how
\par
group cohesion is supported and the frontier of the group is determined. Thus people who
\par
feel to be similar to each other are able to form a group easier.
\par
[Jarvenpaa et al 1998] found, that team members need to set team border while clearly
\par
differentiating members from non-members.
\par
Anyway, personal contacts remain crucial condition of group cohesion. Paradoxically the
\par
more virtual the organization is the more people need to meet in person. The meetings,
\par
however, are different. They are more about process than task, more concerned that
\par
people get to know each other than that they deliver [Handy Jun 1995].
\par
However, the relationship between frequency of personal contacts and group cohesion is a
\par
critical question of further investigation. If common actions take part in all fields of
\par
everyday life, the cohesion will be stronger, if common action is only to emphasize some
\par
kind of obligation, the cohesion will be weaker. Organization building capability of the
\par
man results in the strongest cohesion, if the group living conditions are similar to those of
\par
the group societies, and if the number of people are between 50-100, the group provides
\par
highly isolated and total simultaneous being together [Cs\'anyi 2000].
\par
1.3.4. Virtual team and leadership
\par
The most significant affect of remote environment on leadership is that it could weaken
\par
the power of team leader's personal influence. This can be the reason why many decision-
\par
makers and managers still do not trust remote work, and those that do have problems to
\par
understand how they should support asynchronous distributed work [Status 1999]. Thus
\par
special care should be dedicated to techniques used for optimize management at a
\par
distance.
\par
Virtual teamwork eliminates power distance and enhances democracy [Bordia
\par
1997][Handy 1995]. An extreme example of that is how the leadership was managed in
\par
case of a remote projects carried out by university students, described by [Jarvenpaa et al
\par
1997]. In this case the high-trust teams rotated leadership among the members according
\par
to proficiency, but the actual leader was neither chosen nor consistent throughout. It can
\par
be questioned whether this 'anarcho-syndicalistic' community is the optimal solution,
\par
when the members are very different in power and the environment is highly competitive
\par
and there are strict financial targets. Furthermore, it is not clear how they decided on who
\par
is an expert and who should only follow him/her. However, it is a fact, that organizations
\par
27have been developing to this direction. Application of post bureaucratic or networked
\par
organizational structures have been widely spreading [Symon 2000][Markus 1994][Fulk
\par
1995]. Rather seniors and juniors and not superiors and subordinates are employed
\par
[Drucker 1999]. "In the newer, more high-tech organization in the USA the word
\par
'manager' has begun to disappear. People are not 'managers', they are 'team leaders',
\par
'project heads', 'coordinators', or more generally, 'executives'"[Handy 1992]. Thus
\par
leadership effectiveness was most closely associated in the virtual environment with the
\par
mentoring capabilities of the leader [Leidner et al 1999]. The necessary authority is not
\par
based on being able to do better a job but on the ability to help others to do a better job
\par
[Handy 1992].
\par
I should emphasize here probably the most important leadership function, that is
\par
professionalism, working as a native guide of a particular area showing a proper way as a
\par
licensed pilot while taking environmental circumstances and available resources,
\par
targeting common goals into account. This is valid both for virtual and non-virtual
\par
leaders. Thus individual persons have gained distinguished importance over bureaucracy
\par
in getting things done [Leidner and Jarvenpaa]. "It is people, not places, that now define
\par
an organization, and this is becoming a shifting, semi-permanent, group of people
\par
determined by the changing needs of the task in hand and the market"[Status 1999].
\par
Nevertheless a virtual connection makes leadership more difficult. Problems will enlarge,
\par
which are hard to solve anyway like how to visualize the results in order to provide a
\par
feedback that supports the motivation. In addition, remote workers do express more
\par
critique and dissatisfaction [Bordia 1997].
\par
1.3.5. Virtual team and knowledge work
\par
Knowledge has become the base of the value creation in the modern economy. "Business
\par
organizations are coming to view knowledge as their most valuable and strategic
\par
resource" [Zack 1999].
\par
The value of information comes from being shared across knowledge workers, across
\par
knowledge nodes, and across networks [Jarvenpaa and Ives 1994]. Thus the primary
\par
focus of the efforts has been on developing new applications of information technology to
\par
support the digital capture, storage, retrieval, and distribution of an organization's
\par
explicitly documented knowledge. On the other hand, a smaller number of organizations
\par
believe that the most valuable knowledge is tacit knowledge existing within peoples'
\par
28heads, augmented or shared via interpersonal interaction and social relationships [Zack
\par
1999].
\par
Thus virtual teams and knowledge work are very interrelated because a certain kind of
\par
knowledge could exist only 'among people' and not 'in people'. Furthermore, virtual team
\par
members are most of the time knowledge workers. Virtual team members are located in
\par
knowledge nodes and connected by networks and they are characterized by all of
\par
Drucker's six major factors, which determines the knowledge-worker's productivity as
\par
follows:
\par
they are task oriented
\par
they have got own responsibility and autonomy
\par
they have been innovating continuously
\par
they have been learning continuously
\par
quality work is expected from them, while quantity has got secondary importance
\par
they should be treated as asset (or resource) and not as cost.
\par
The features listed above provide necessary flexibility already pointed as key advantage
\par
of a virtual team, so a virtual team can serve as a significant building element of
\par
knowledge industry today.
\par
At this point an important question could arise, how knowledge can be given further
\par
among people and managed more effectively. New technology can answer the first one.
\par
"Advanced technologies may also be used to improve or streamline the socialization of
\par
new team members. For example, Intranets allow teams to archive a wide range of
\par
information. New team members potentially could access a complete electronic history of
\par
the team's work, including not only text-based and graphical information, but also video
\par
and audio recordings of important team meetings" [Townsend et al 1998].
\par
However the second task, knowledge management needs proper knowledge strategy
\par
while mapping it between exploration and exploitation and between internal and external
\par
so all in all selecting aggressive or conservative one [Zack 1999].
\par
1.3.6. Virtual team and productivity
\par
When a manager comes across with the term working at a distance (telework, e-work) for
\par
the first time he or she thinks about what makes remote workers work with maximal
\par
performance (efficiency) optimal performance (effectiveness), or appropriate
\par
performance (fulfilling core functions). However, there are several levels of productivity
\par
like personal, team, department, business unit, corporate. So what seems less productive
\par
29on personal level can be optimal for higher complexity structure for example on team
\par
level. Virtual team's and teamwork's advantages described in chapter 1.1.2. and 1.2.2.
\par
explain why it is worthwhile applying remote teams, though the difficulties which go with
\par
it and are listed in chapter 1.1.3. and 1.2.3. can limit personal productivity. Two of the
\par
main problems are the following:
\par
ambiguity in responsibilities
\par
longer response time.
\par
Handy introduced the term 'doughnut principle', which is in line with virtual work. It
\par
delineates today's trend when people have got freedom in selecting activities during their
\par
work after fulfilling some core tasks. For example, in my everyday work I have to provide
\par
adequate responses to customer's, top management's, or other colleagues' requests but I
\par
am unbound in selecting among further activities like searching for market information,
\par
enhancing my knowledge on products or system, socializing with colleagues, customers,
\par
and partners. Furthermore, by the application of portable computers the border between
\par
private life and work have faded, I can select activities which are not closely related to
\par
work, like improving my foreign language knowledge or preparing an MBA dissertation.
\par
However, this freedom can lead to false assumptions, because people generally think that
\par
others think and also act similarly as themselves, so they can expect similar kind of
\par
performance.
\par
Increased response time decreases productivity. Although parallel task processing can
\par
optimize time consumption, longer response time means that more tasks should be
\par
processed in the same time. It makes work more complex entailing higher possibility of
\par
failures and lower separability of tasks.
\par
Another aspect is that by studying motivational theories [Vroom 1982] [Herzberg 1987] it
\par
becomes clear that virtual team is very consistent with strategies for motivation included
\par
in modern management disciplines. As people become more educated and more
\par
expensive, it does not make sense to treat them as an automat [Handy 1995]. "We often
\par
find it easier to manage someone's time than their results, but contractual fringe does not
\par
allow us that luxury" [Handy 1992]. Managers do not have time to supervise and control
\par
continuously even if their subordinates sit nearby. Chapter 1.3.5. on knowledge work is
\par
also relevant here.
\par
The following tools for motivation and control by Herzberg do fit very well into virtual
\par
working environment:
\par
job enrichment
\par
empowerment
\par
30management by objectives
\par
enterpreneurship.
\par
Virtual work facilitates employing people who are very professional at particular areas
\par
and thus focusing on special activities. On the other hand, tasks can be freely rearranged
\par
in virtual environment. For example, new information technology enables sales
\par
estimations used to be done by people dedicated exclusively to this task, but nowadays it
\par
is to be processed as a part time activity by sales force.
\par
"Employees are considered empowered when they a) get information about organizational
\par
performance, b) are awarded for contributing to organizational performance, c) have the
\par
knowledge and skills to understand and contribute to organizational performance, and d)
\par
have the power to make decisions that influence organizational direction and
\par
performance" [Jarvenpaa and Ives 1994].
\par
Employing virtual teams facilitates application of MBOs (management by objectives),
\par
because it can provide the only effective way for employee appraisal.
\par
Virtual team members disposing over working times and other resources can manage
\par
them only while thinking about cost and benefit, risk and reward. Ultimately it is the base
\par
of enterpreneurship. "The new free agents have total flexibility in, and often total control
\par
over, how, where and when they work" [Status 1999].
\par
However, motives of individual 'entrepreneurs' should be in harmony or in other words
\par
point to the same direction in order to achieve common objectives. "If team members are
\par
very interdependent in their work, it is counterproductive to base compensation systems
\par
only on individual performance. The nature of such work demands compatible systems:
\par
team-based pay and compensation; performance appraisal and accountability at the team
\par
level; and recognition for team results" [Drexler and Forrester 1998]. The goals of the
\par
smaller units have to get with the goals of the whole [Handy Jun 1995].
\par
1.3.7. Virtual team and trust
\par
I have found the following three main reasons, why trust in colleagues' professionalism
\par
has got dedicated role in working relationships:
\par
visual supervision of employees is not recommended
\par
colleagues' freedom in selecting working activities outside core tasks
\par
difficulty with separation the knowledge work process from its outcomes
\par
Personal contact is restricted in virtual connections, and strict personal control like visual
\par
supervising is not recommended because it is abusive and does not motivate for quality
\par
31work [Drucker 1999] [Mintzberg 1998]. Today relationships are governed by
\par
interdependency rather than dependence [Fulk 1995]. "Trust is critical in new
\par
organizational arrangements where the traditional social controls based on authority give
\par
way to self-direction and self-control. Trust is reported to reduce transaction costs,
\par
increase confidence and security in the relationship, and promote open, substantive, and
\par
influential information exchange" [Jarvenpaa et al 1998]. "It is no longer the manager and
\par
the managed, but the designer of the doughnut and the occupant; different relationship,
\par
one built more on trust and mutual respect than control"[Handy 1995]
\par
Another aspect is that it is difficult to separate the knowledge work process from its
\par
outcomes [Davenport et al 1996] [Fulk 1995]. Management of virtual teams can appraise
\par
only based on output and results while during the process they do have only very limited
\par
possibility to interrupt or control. "In the complex, uncertain situations involving
\par
reciprocal interdependence, typical in managerial and professional work, traditional
\par
mechanisms of coordination (rules, plans, routines, and such) are usually inadequate as
\par
contingencies cannot always be properly planned for. Under these conditions,
\par
coordination is a continuous process in which all the actors involved adjust their actions
\par
to one another and self-initiated mechanisms of coordination are critical" [McAllister
\par
1995].
\par
However increased demand for trust can entail problems. "With choice always comes
\par
responsibility. The individual gets more freedom but can choose to abuse that freedom by
\par
poor quality work, by cheating or by laziness. The organization gets more flexibility but
\par
can abuse that flexibility by exploiting the outsider, by tightening its conditions and
\par
reducing reward" [Handy 1992]. Furthermore, the openness in organizational
\par
communication is not a natural approach [Eisenberg 1984].
\par
Despite these real risks outlined above, current movement striving to optimize conditions
\par
point to direction of more freedom and flexibility. Thus positive approach can result in
\par
positive reaction. Employees are good because they know that they will be trusted with
\par
big responsibilities, including the chance to make mistakes [Handy 1992]. "Trust in a
\par
virtual-team context might therefore be more strongly related to ability and integrity, and
\par
less to benevolence" [Jarvenpaa et al 1998]. It indicates trust in organizational
\par
environment to be connected rather to positions than to persons emphasizing the
\par
connection between trust and group described above. My personal experiences strongly
\par
support that in such circumstances initial trust and also other attitude like preference or
\par
sympathy towards a colleague, customer, supplier can be inherited by predecessors. So
\par
32people working in virtual environment can face increased stereotyping with all of its
\par
advantages and disadvantages.
\par
332. \textbf{Presenting case study research, interpreting its findings based on key issues}
\par
\textbf{2.1. Research Methodology}
\par
This dissertation is based on a case study research I carried out in business to business
\par
environment at the Semiconductor Product Division of Philips Electronics as native
\par
ethnographer. "Ethnographic methods seem to fit well with knowledge work"
\par
[Davenport]. I possess five-year experience in foreign trade while working in
\par
multinational interorganizational remote teams in the semiconductor industry. I began to
\par
work when computer mediated communication became a generally accepted business
\par
practice. As an account manager I have been actively participating in the activities of each
\par
team under investigation, which are indicated by B, D, F, S, T, R letters except for F.
\par
These conditions enable me to properly handle "subject matter" and contextual kind,
\par
which are inherent characteristics of the topic and which caused the greatest weakness of
\par
former researches on this topic. Most experimental studies used student subjects.
\par
Consequently, the generalizability of the results to other subject populations in other
\par
settings (for example, managers in the work place) is problematic [Bordia 1997].
\par
Besides that the relatively short duration of former researches could cause deficiencies in
\par
validity. That is why my research analyzed results of one-year observation, which could
\par
be long enough to consider seasonal things and infrequent but crucial phenomena
\par
throughout a longitudinal examination. My approach is als supported by finding of
\par
Davenport et al: "Most projects we studied involved fewer outsiders (such as consultants)
\par
than we had observed in classical reengineering projects. We also saw outsiders with
\par
softer analysis methods, such as ethnographic studies. By this, we mean intervention
\par
techniques involving analysts' participation in the knowledge work, usually over a long
\par
period of time and involving detailed description of the broad work environment and
\par
cultural and behavioral issues. In short, rather than a consultant interviewing workers over
\par
a brief period, in ethnography, anthropologists observe the workers under analysis for
\par
several months. These methods involve systematic observation of work approaches,
\par
sometimes with detailed analysis of videotapes to understand work activity"[Davenport et
\par
al].
\par
To highlight theoretical base of this work I may emphasize the impressions [Mintzberg
\par
1973] and [Eisenhardt 1989] put on me.
\par
34I avoided using survey and policy capturing methods, because negative experiences from
\par
pilot interviews showed that managers usually tell that they think optimal and not the
\par
reality.
\par
On the other hand ethnography certainly has got limitations like unrepresentative samples
\par
and single perspective. To enhance external validity I used other researches as references.
\par
2.1.1. Data collection
\par
Preliminary data like process descriptions, job descriptions, orgcharts, contact diagrams
\par
were available from company archives.
\par
Structured observations and unstructured interviews were the main sources of this
\par
research. Nevertheless because of my insider position this has to be covered. My
\par
experience and several references prove that 1) "formal interviews would reveal only
\par
official expectations", 2) "it is difficult to gain access to tell it like it is", 3) "managers are
\par
poor estimators of their own activities" [Mintzberg 1973], which are against overt
\par
research. Recording observations ran with picking up 'anecdotal' data, preparing list of
\par
impressions occurred, and questions raised. To review critical incidents and the sequence
\par
of episodes, chronology record was prepared based on event patterns while the
\par
unrepresentative actions can be eliminated. Inter- and intra-team communication could be
\par
best examined via genre repertoire and sequences of conversations.
\par
I studied the work of 5 colleagues at customer business support working in teams
\par
B,D,R,S,T; 3 key account managers of teams B,D,S; 6 account managers working in core
\par
staff of teams B, D, F, R, S, T. I interviewed 3 colleagues at customer business support
\par
working in teams B,D,S; 1 account manager from team D, 4 account manager working in
\par
core staffs of team D,F,R,T; 2 key account managers of teams D,S.
\par
All interviews were informal and I put my aimed questions into the general conversation.
\par
I selected the following interview topics :
\par
demand for and importance of personal meetings with team members
\par
frequency of communication with colleagues
\par
the fact of belonging to different groups inside Philips.
\par
Analyzing my e-mail record provided unique advantage, because it includes not only the
\par
messages received and sent but complete conversations similarly to a voice record. So
\par
whole stories could be reviewed with all the attached documents like financial figures,
\par
35technical documentation received and sent. Furthermore, it was a good support besides
\par
the diary to identify dates of particular events.
\par
2.1.2. Data analysis
\par
Interpretation of the collected data enables both quantitative and qualitative analysis.
\par
The first categories and dimensions were set as base of comparison.
\par
Then similarities and differences of cases were searched.
\par
The next steps were sharpening the constructs and verifying the emergent relationships
\par
between constructs to fit with evidence.
\par
Finally a comparison of the emergent concepts to literature was carried out.
\par
2.1.3. Limitations
\par
Although I strove to have a neutral perspective while seeing things as open-eyed as
\par
possible and eliminate biases deliberately, they could have impact on my work as follows:
\par
as a member of the virtual teams under investigation I was not able to observe my
\par
colleagues regularly while they were working. Most of the time I could only contact
\par
them via e-mail and phone. So the research itself was also 'virtual'
\par
ethnography is subjective in any case, especially if it has been carried out solely, so
\par
my personality provides a special filter or scope via which the reader of this paper can
\par
have a view on the topic
\par
I have been working in Hungary, that is why I have a special Central and Eastern
\par
European point of view while possessing more knowledge on this special culture with
\par
developing business environment, but having less experience on corporate center
\par
I am an account manager, so first of all I know the problems connected to this job and
\par
I am less familiar with those of a key account manager or a customer business
\par
executive.
\par
