product divisions are consolidated. Thus they provide services for them while dealing
\par
with recruitment and selection, preparing training plan, following employee development
\par
and evaluation.
\par
I would put an emphasis on the following advantages of using virtual teams from Philips'
\par
internal point of view:
\par
intraorganizational co-operation in network structure
\par
optimal labor related cost structure
\par
providing flexibility and stability together.
\par
46People are often located remotely from each other inside an organization which have
\par
such complex tensor structure as described above, though their jobs can be very
\par
interdependent. So a virtual team is an apt tool for connecting colleagues working on the
\par
same goals but doing different functions and being located remotely.
\par
To find an appropriate person at an acceptable salary level is also a challenge in our very
\par
changeable world, which requires continuous learning. A virtual team can eliminate the
\par
geographical constraints of employee selection. Thus the company can conceive the
\par
optimal employee structure more freely. If the cost of the employee is higher at the
\par
corporate center, they can place certain jobs remotely where salary level is lower and vice
\par
versa. Philips Electronics like other multinational companies have targeted Central and
\par
Eastern Europe to hire software developers for the reason described above. A further
\par
advantage is that induction can be expedited since the apts trainees can deal with the new
\par
entrants, and they are able to establish a contact with their future colleagues quickly who
\par
work remotely. Nevertheless there are some difficulties to go with virtual connection, as it
\par
is described in chapter 1.1.
\par
Another aspect is how PSC can react to the environmental changes. The following list
\par
contains actions taken by PSC to follow them in the past two years:
\par
semiconductor vendor company VLSI has been taken over by PSC
\par
new Global Market Segment structure has been introduced, while Regional Sales
\par
Organizations disappeared
\par
down turn from extra profit to negative in one year from 2000 to 2001
\par
travel restriction because of bad profit projection in 2001
\par
new executive board of PSC has been appointed
\par
PSC built up a new Business Unit structure
\par
new product grouping has been introduced
\par
new CEO was appointed on the top of PSC.
\par
Fundamental changes are difficult to be followed by the whole organization. Thus it
\par
requires building elements which can be shaped according to the newest situation. A
\par
virtual team with several internal and external connections can function as such a building
\par
block.
\par
One of my biggest impressions concerning PSC as a multinational organization is how
\par
particular persons are able to influence it. It emphasizes the importance of personal
\par
contacts. However an organization should not depend on the mercy of a couple of people.
\par
Team work and in case of multi location a virtual team can decrease this effect, because
\par
contact system should build up so that it should not belong to persons but teams.
\par
47\textbf{2.3. KAM teams}
\par
2.3.1. What is a KAM team?
\par
The importance of major global customers, in other words, key accounts is emphasized in
\par
chapter 2.1.1. In addition, the outline of major processes in Appendix A also points
\par
strategic or key accounts as one major customer segment of PSC. So key account
\par
management (KAM) is intended to realize global strategic co-operation together with
\par
local support. It means multi contacts to a customer with parallel peer to peer
\par
interorganizational relationships at several levels in the hierarchy and in different
\par
functions as Figure 2.3.2. shows. For example, a key account manager keeps contact with
\par
the customer's top management. Customer Business Support (CBS), or in other words
\par
customer service communicates with the customer's logistics. An account manager deals
\par
with the purchasing people at a local production site. Finance and accounting
\par
communicates to the customer's finance and accounting. Marketing supports the
\par
customer's development engineers. Strategic account manager or coordinator negotiates
\par
the customer's strategic purchasers located at corporate center. Quality Service Support
\par
(only in case of Automotive Market Segment) receives and handles complaints from the
\par
customer's quality engineers. Further description of activities can be find in chapter 2.3.3.
\par
All in all, many people with different functions are dedicated to continuously satisfy a key
\par
account or as it is defined nowadays a strategic partner in business to business
\par
environment in the most professional way.
\par
Although teamwork is emphasized by slogans and internal public relations materials at
\par
Philips, the term "KAM team" is not stated in any written process or job description.
\par
KAM teams are presented in printed form only in orgcharts prepared by the key account
\par
managers and the segment managers. They delineate relationships among the key account
\par
manager, the strategic account managers or coordinators, the account managers, and the
\par
customer business support, who care for the same global customer as Figure 2.3.1. shows.
\par
However titles like "strategic account manager" and "coordinator" do not appear in any
\par
official corporate documents either but on business cards and e-mail signatures. KAM
\par
team is an example of eliminating bureaucracy and striving from flexibility. This situation
\par
could evolve, because fundamental changes in organizational structure take place so
\par
frequently that corporate documents became out of date shortly after issuing a new
\par
edition. For example, a quality certificate can be valid for 3 years, though the orgchart in
\par
48the quality manual should be revised yearly or biennially. Even the corporate home page,
\par
which can be modified simpler, can not be updated frequently enough to reflect the
\par
current situation.
\par
2.3.2. History of KAM teams
\par
A short view on how the current situation evolved can support gaining better
\par
understanding. As chapter 2.2.1 describes limited number of multinational companies
\par
dominate the electronic market nowadays and they form the major part of the customer
\par
base of PSC. However, this was not always so.
\par
Components division of Philips produced electronic elements in the seventies almost
\par
exclusively for internal customers like Philips Consumer Electronics, Philips Medical
\par
Electronics and so on.
\par
When the production capacity exceeded their demand they opened to external markets.
\par
That time they had limited product portfolio based on standard devices and they strove to
\par
have as many customers as possible. Thus they kept direct contacts even to relatively
\par
small ones. With expanding applications of electronics (video and audio, computing,
\par
telecom) the demand on parts also increased rapidly. However, stepping up production
\par
capacity could not follow it quickly, because establishing a new fab takes approximately
\par
one or two years. Thus allocation periods followed consecutively in three or four years in
\par
the eighties and early nineties. Meanwhile the Semiconductor Division became
\par
independent from the Components Division in 1993. The Components Division continued
\par
to produce only passive elements like resistors and capacitors and the Semiconductor
\par
Division produced semiconductor devices.
\par
Advanced technology enabled them to increase the level of integration, which facilitated
\par
creating more and more special devices, which enables more and more complex functions
\par
to be realized. However, the development of these devices are only profitable in case of a
\par
huge volume. On the other hand, the technical specifications have became so special that
\par
one type can be sold only to a very limited number of customers. Thus economies of
\par
scale started to play a dominant role in selecting or targeting customers in the late
\par
nineties.
\par
Philips Semiconductor's sales organization has been adjusted according to the changes
\par
described above. Sales to external customers used to be organized in harmony with
\par
regions and accounts, which meant many separated customers. Account managers were
\par
49responsible for sales to particular customers. Their activities included pricing and
\par
contracting as well. They reported to PSC's regional managers. This structure can be
\par
traced in the outline of major processes in Appendix A, where besides strategic key
\par
accounts other direct accounts are also mentioned as main costumer base. However
\par
concentration and globalization of electronic production requested another system.
\par
Different production plants of the same multinational customer all over the world can not
\par
be handled separately, because it could result in contradiction in pricing and other
\par
business conditions. For example, different prices could be given for the same type and
\par
for the same customer depending on whether it is based in Europe or in the USA. Further
\par
to estimate market "weight" and the total demand of a particular customer, main
\par
conditions given to separate purchasing places had to be considered centrally. From the
\par
customer side a request was formulated to keep contact at higher level, because they knew
\par
that the bigger volume they negotiate the better price they can achieve.
\par
That is why global key account managers were appointed to harmonize activities directed
\par
to different sites of the same big customer. On the other hand, the importance and power
\par
of regional sales managers decreased gradually. Globalization became so momentous that
\par
PSC's International Marketing and Sales (IMS) structure were totally reorganized in 1999,
\par
while global market segments according to different application areas were introduced at
\par
higher level in the organization structure than regional sales organizations. PSC's IMS
\par
used to be split into regional sales organization first and only after that segments by
\par
application, but nowadays IMS is splitt into global market segments first and only after
\par
that into regional sales. Furthermore strategic key accounts do not belong to any regional
\par
sales, so strategic key account managers report directly to global market segment
\par
managers. This story summarizes how key account management became a dominant part
\par
of PSC's IMS while providing explanation why KAM teams are so important.
\par
2.3.3. Structure of KAM team
\par
Although there is no written corporate document, which would directly describe the
\par
construction of a KAM team, all of them have got more or less the same structure [Figure
\par
2.3.1.]. The key account manager is on the top. His core staff, which consists of a couple
\par
of account managers, supports him and/or sales engineers taking care for customer's
\par
corporate center. In addition, there are other account managers and CBS persons dealing
\par
with one or more production site(s) of the particular customer. In case of teams D, T, and
\par
R there is a separate position for a European coordinator, who also belongs to the core
\par
50staff but sits on another continent and keeps contact with oversea staff, especially with the
\par
key account manager.
\par
One team can consist of 12-40 people. However an account manager can be member of 9
\par
teams, a CBS executive can be member of 4 teams depending on the importance of the
\par
sites they represent. This multi team membership situation entails competing priorities.
\par
Team members are located far from each other, though they have to work in very close
\par
cooperation because the tasks of different team members supplement each other. So they
\par
have to face and cope with special difficulties and communicating electronically is
\par
certainly a must. For example, in case of a production site of customer D located in
\par
Austria, the key account manager sits in Kokomo, Indiana, USA, the European co-
\par
ordinator in Paris, France, the key account manager in Budapest, Hungary (myself), the
\par
customer business executive in Hamburg, Germany. This customers' European corporate
\par
center is in Wuppertal, Germany, and its world wide headquarter is in Kokomo, Indiana,
\par
USA.
\par
Figure 2.3.1. Structure of a KAM team
\par
(*European coordinator is optional)
\par
2.3.4. The roles, tasks, and working conditions of KAM team members
\par
Roles of KAM team members are defined according to different levels and segments of
\par
supporting a customer. Since the organization structure tends to be flat, there are a lot of
\par
51contacts at the same level [Figure 2.3.2.], so this important figure can reflect real
\par
relationships clearer than an orgchart.
\par
Figure 2.3.2. Internal and external contacts of a KAM team
\par
(* Existence of Quality Support Center depends on GMS)
\par
A key account manager is appointed to keep strategic contact with a particular customer's
\par
head office and to coordinate the demand creation and the fulfillment activities like
\par
design in, production start, forecasting, which are connected to the particular customer.
\par
He leads price negotiations, signs price agreements and global contracts, maintains
\par
relationship with the customer's top management, prepares and follows up the global sales
\par
budget. He/she also has got his own cost budget. He (I came across only with male key
\par
account manager at PSC) reports to the global market segment manager, who prepares his
\par
performance appraisal. On the other hand, he keeps contact with the manager of
\par
international marketing and sales and even with the chief executive officer of PSC. So a
\par
key account manager possess quite high position in the corporate hierarchy.
\par
Since he keeps contact to the whole team all over the word plus makes customer visits, he
\par
is a very frequent traveler. Thus he has got a mobile office with a laptop and a cellular
\par
phone, though a working room is also allocated to him in one of the PSC's office
\par
buildings near his home.
\par
52He has got limited availability via phone, since he speaks on it a lot, or sits on an airplane,
\par
or visits oversea locations where cellular standards are different from that of Europe. So
\par
the main channel to contact him is e-mail.
\par
An account manager's responsibilities are connected to a particular account in other
\par
words a local production site of a global customer. It includes the following activities:
\par
keeping personal contact with local purchasing, procurement and other decision makers,
\par
providing technical support, forecasting sales of the particular site, discovering
\par
opportunities (design in), reporting on market situation and competitor activities, follow
\par
up local budget fulfillment and comparing sales vs. volume in the yearly contract,
\par
advising on prices, accepting order cancellations above a certain amount. He or she
\par
reports to the key account manager(s) responsible for the global customer the particular
\par
account belongs to. However, the particular key account manager does his or her
\par
performance appraisal only if he or she is a member of his core staff. If not, the local
\par
manager of a particular global market segment appraises his or her performance. It is
\par
crucial for productivity, because team members focus on different tasks according to their
\par
personal objectives set and evaluated by their managers. If the local manager accepts or
\par
establishes objectives which are not fully in line with KAM team's objective, productivity
\par
will not be optimal.
\par
An account manager pay a lot of customer visits, which means he is out of office building
\par
many times, that is why he or she is also well equipped. There are some account
\par
managers who work from a home office. So they have got the necessary facilities like fax
\par
and ISDN telephone lines at home paid by the company.
\par
He or she can be available more or less continuously, except for meeting time.
\par
Customer Business Support and Logistics (CBS) process by the customers' request for
\par
deliveries, postponements or bringing forward and track deliveries while using IMS's
\par
logistics software. They keep contact with logistics and purchasing people from the
\par
customer local production site, planners at business lines (a BL is smaller fragment of a
\par
BU) at PSC's production, and account managers. They visit customers very rarely,
\par
normally only at the beginning of work as an introduction. Their performances are
\par
appraised by the regional customer business support manager.
\par
They work in PSC's office buildings. To assure opportunity for replacement in case of
\par
holiday, training, or illness several colleagues work in the same office and deal with
\par
similar customers. Similar means either different local purchasing sites of the same global
\par
53customers or customers of the same global market segment, and so having demand on
\par
types from the same business lines.
\par
They speak to customer frequently and usually for long. That is why they can hardly be
\par
reached via phone. They prefer e-mail for communication.
\par
Sales engineers work in some KAM teams to provide technical support to customer and
\par
account managers. In addition, he or she does project follow up in the initial
\par
(development) phase. Although his task is similar to that of an application engineer at a
\par
business line, he or she is rather customer (project) and not product oriented.
\par
Although people with the following position at PSC are not members of KAM teams
\par
strictly speaking, they work with strong co-operation. Another aspect is that as I
\par
mentioned before, there is no corporate quality manual or process description, which
\par
would set team borders. So it can be handled in a flexible way, while also considering
\par
positions in the halo of KAM teams.
\par
A planner at a BL is responsible for continuous and smooth supply of a particular product
\par
range or ranges to the customers. He or she manages wafer capacity allocated to the
\par
product range while sharing it between different device types and versions.
\par
PSC applies double site marketing. Thus BUs and IMS' market segments have got their
\par
own marketing staff. However they should closely cooperate and harmonize the activities.
\par
A product-marketing engineer at a BL manages application engineers' design in activities.
\par
An application engineer provides technical support for customer's development engineers
\par
to select certain device and prepare a design and prototype with it. They contribute to all
\par
necessary marketing action like creating reference design, presentation and promotion.
\par
The financial department deals with customer verification, invoicing, and money
\par
collection from local purchasing sites.
\par
2.3.5. Specialties of KAM teams under investigation
\par
(B) team deals with a German OEM (original equipment manufacturer) customer.
\par
So it is obvious that both the key account manager and the core staff are from Germany.
\par
Long term relationship has been existing between customer B and Philips
\par
Semiconductors/Components, which might be because both companies' headquarters are
\par
located in Europe.
\par
54Thus KAM team members, especially the core staff, have got personal contacts to B for
\par
long (more than ten years).
\par
The key account manager reports directly to the automotive global market segment
\par
manager. He has got another position as General Manager Automotive Germany - he uses
\par
his second title to gain better respect and influence, it is a good example for the
\par
transitional period while key account menagers have been acquiring bigger power.
\par
(D) team is directed to an American OEM customer, former part of the biggest car
\par
manufacturer in the world.
\par
Both the key account manager, who reports directly to the automotive global market
\par
segment manager, and the core staff are North American extended with a European co-
\par
ordinator from France. He acts on behalf of the key account manager and they
\par
communicate several times a day.
\par
The European coordinator keeps contact with account managers in Europe. His position is
\par
quite new for the last 2 years, it also indicates a structural change towards customer
\par
oriented market segmentation instead of a geographically based one.
\par
He prefers teleconferences to enable just in time solutions.
\par
(F) team's customer is a Singapore headquartered EMS (electronic manufacturing
\par
services) customer owned by US investors, with US top management.
\par
Since this customer is quite a new organization the team is also young, less than 4 years
\par
old.
\par
As the majority of PSC's sales goes to European sites that is why the key account
\par
manager is from Austria.
\par
There is unusually frequent and close contact between the key account manager and CBS
\par
because they are from Austria. He reports to the European EMS segment manager.
\par
Communication between the key account manager and account managers is extremely
\par
frequent at least once a week.
\par
Account managers also have got regular direct contact to GMS top management core
\par
staff, once a months.
\par
(S) team works with the automotive division of the largest electronic company based in
\par
Germany.
\par
It was built this year by merging two major automotive suppliers. Thus two former KAM
\par
teams was united.
\par
55The key account manager reports directly to the automotive global market segment
\par
manager. He communicates personally to account managers only in special cases. On the
\par
other hand, he regularly forwards internal information from top management.
\par
He uses a special action plan: "Close the gap", to achieve special targets.
\par
The key account manager is not directly involved in preparing the sales budget proposal.
\par
A typical example for very virtuality of this team that there are an account manager and a
\par
customer business support executive, who have never meet personally, though they have
\par
got weekly contact for more than half a year.
\par
(T) team's costumer used to be part of Daimler Chrysler. However the key account
\par
manager is more in contact with European top management of GMS and reports rather to
\par
European market segment manager than to Daimler Chrysler's key account manager.
\par
The only account manager in Europe does not have direct contact with the key account
\par
manager in the US.
\par
(R) team takes care of a US-based manufacturer.
\par
There is no real key account manager, the European coordinator acts so.
\par
She reports to the European automotive segment manager.
\par
She fulfills both the key account manager's and the account manager's duties.
