56\textbf{2.4. KAM team's relationship to key issues}
\par
2.4.1. Communication and KAM teams
\par
Communication has got distinguished importance for KAM teams, because it takes the
\par
majority of the work of each member. Its ratio compared to the sum of all activities is 50-
\par
70 per cent.
\par
Table 2.4.1. shows an estimation of the number of official, regular contact persons of
\par
each type of KAM team members. The second column includes how many contacts do
\par
different KAM team members have within one team. The third column contains how
\par
many contacts do different KAM team members have in one or more KAM team(s) they
\par
are members of. The fourth column indicates the number of other internal contacts inside
\par
Philips Electronics. The fifth column shows the total number of regular, official contacts.
\par
However, I can provide only a rough estimation because it is difficult to define what a
\par
regular contact is. Although communication is sometimes infrequent between peers, they
\par
can be stated as contact partners because of the organizational structure.
\par
Table 2.4.1. Number of contacts of each type of KAM tem members
\par
Figure 2.3.2. is also relevant here, because it shows the internal and external
\par
communication structure of a virtual team. Bold lines indicate frequent regular
\par
connections; slim lines mean infrequent, ad hoc ones.
\par
Mediums
\par
The following mediums are used for keeping these contacts:
\par
personal meeting
\par
telephone
\par
e-mail (including online chat)
\par
telephone (or video) conference
\par
fax (or printed documents)
\par
video speech or animation.
\par
57The section below describes what medium, for what purpose, and how frequently do
\par
people working in KAM teams select [Table 2.4.2].
\par
Table 2.4.2. Frequency of communication between KAM team members
\par
(* it depends on if they sit on the same location)
\par
To get a whole KAM team together is almost impossible because geographically it is very
\par
scattered, and the appropriate schedule is hard to be agreed because of multi team
\par
membership. Several members are members of other KAM team(s) as well. However, a
\par
meeting with the participation of the whole team is not only unnecessary but also it could
\par
be ineffective, since the number of topics, which are interesting for each member is very
\par
scarse. So too much time could be wasted while listening to irrelevant issues.
\par
Nevertheless partial team meetings can be organized in special occasions like a product
\par
training or a sales conference or an exhibition, when several team members participate
\par
anyway. However it can happen once or maximum twice a year.
\par
Peer to peer meetings with the participation of the virtual team members are more
\par
frequent. Especially the key account manager and the core staff meet regularly (at least
\par
monthly) to discuss actual issues, with the exception of the European coordinator, who
\par
does not travel oversea monthly. Nevertheless during budgeting period in June and budget
\par
alignment in September they work together physically for days.
\par
The key account manager or European coordinator usually meets the account managers
\par
when they visit a local production site. It is one to four times a year depending on the
\par
importance of the local production site.
\par
An account manager meets the core staff during product training, and sales conferences.
\par
Account managers meet customer business support bi- or three-monthly depending on the
\par
arising issues. Someteimes they do not see each other for more than half a year, if travel
\par
restrictions forbid internal meetings.
\par
None of the interwievers found the number of personal contacts inadequate, when I asked
\par
them for their opinions. So they seem to accept the working conditions and do not go out
\par
58for improvement in this field. Only one account manager from team D added that his
\par
manager thinks their personal meetings are too few. The key account manager of team S
\par
said that the number of contact depends very much on the arising issues. If there is no
\par
problem with a certain account, he does not initiate communication to its account
\par
manager.
\par
Telephone can provide a possibility for a more informal connection after personal contact.
\par
It is used by KAM teams in the following situations:
\par
ad hoc contact
\par
quick feed back is essential and inevitable
\par
to direct attention to an issue previously described in an e-mail
\par
making accurate a written message or document
\par
avoiding clear statement under uncertainty
\par
eliminating confusion
\par
informal conversation
\par
technical trouble with computer.
\par
It depends on the person if he or she prefers using the telephone or the e-mail for non-
\par
regular contacts. Nevertheless when an issue arises a quick telephone gives the biggest
\par
chance to complete it remotely as soon as possible.
\par
Sometimes getting a prompt answer is necessary. For example when an urgent issue is
\par
being negotiated during the customer visit of an account manager and it can be answered
\par
only by the customer business support located thousands of kms away.
\par
Telephone is also used if additional explanation to numerical data is necessary to outline
\par
the logic behind them.
\par
Another aspect is uncertainty. Preliminary information is communicated rather via phone
\par
to avoid documenting it. Otherwise the person who has given out the information can get
\par
into trouble.
\par
On the other hand, to eliminate confusion the phone used most frequently, because it
\par
gives an opportunity to clear the meaning of the message even word by word.
\par
The telephone provides a more intimate atmosphere than written conversation, because
\par
the presence of the other party is more direct. That is why confidential or unofficial
\par
information is discussed rather via phone. However, the unofficial part of the phone talk
\par
among KAM team members is usually restricted to small things like weather, weekend
\par
activities, holiday plans or holiday adventures. It is aimed to offset strict and serious
\par
59working issues by more pleasant topics. Nevertheless e-mails contain even fewer private
\par
topics, which emphasizes personal relationship less.
\par
If e-mail connection is troubled because of the breakdown of a computer or
\par
communication line, a telephone conversation can partly replace it. Although I know
\par
about a case when a top manager of EMS global market segment, who generally receives
\par
more than a hundred e-mails per day, could work without his computer for a week, it is an
\par
extremity and usually impossible even just for couple of days.
\par
As English is the official language inside Philips, usually all teammembers use it while
\par
communicating. However, there are some difficulties with special accents. Especially
\par
Scottish are hard to be understood by non-native speakers, it appears at team F. French
\par
and Italians have also got a very strange accent, but that does not seem to cause any
\par
problems.
\par
E-mail is the most complex medium used by KAM teams. They can live more without
\par
phone than without E-mail, since it is applied for several different reasons as follows:
\par
traditional mail
\par
acquiring written statement for later use just in case
\par
multi participant discussion
\par
online peer to peer conversation
\par
reporting
\par
distributing business data and multi media materials.
\par
The members of KAM teams send and receive contracts, quality statements, product
\par
information, marketing leaflets, request for quote, quotes, announcements in E-mails. It
\par
has the same function as formerly normal letter used to have.
\par
The case should be highlighted separately when somebody wants someone else to take a
\par
stand on something in written, though it could be done by mail as well. However since e-
\par
mail is handled more quickly and easily than mail, it is used more frequently for this
\par
reason by KAM teams as well.
\par
Discussions with the participation of several people inside and outside a KAM team are
\par
carried on most often via e-mail. It enables the partners to exchange information and adds
\par
opinions among people who are not available at the same time because of time shift or
\par
other occupation.
\par
If communication partners are working on computers connected to the net, E-mail can
\par
function like a phone enabling a two-way communication while quick questions and
\par
answers can be changed. This form is mainly used between the account manager and the
\par
60customer business support, the account manager and the core staff. Although on line chat
\par
programs have not been applied by KAM teams yet, Philips supports its usage since it can
\par
be downloaded from the corporate Intranet.
\par
E-mail is the greatest tool for reporting, because it can be edited and forwarded easily,
\par
provides good information availability and traceability. It can easily be binded to records,
\par
which makes the collected data retrievable.
\par
E-mail functions as a carrier of other media as well. Mpeg audio and video records,
\par
PowerPoint presentations, and financial or sales figures in Excel files are forwarded
\par
through it.
\par
Difficulties arising from using abbreviations, which are not clear for everybody should be
\par
mentioned here, because it impedes the understanding written messages, especially e-
\par
mails. This problem happened most frequently at team F, where the segment manager is a
\par
native speaker and he did not care how easily the others can interpret his text.
\par
Top management use video speech for point to multipoint communication of their visions.
\par
PowerPoint presentations contain product, organizational and customer information.
\par
E-mail enables both peer to peer and point to multipoint communication. Further there are
\par
different intentions to receive (directly, as cc, as bcc, as history) and to send (directly, as
\par
cc, as bcc, forwarding) it. That can indicate if the sender expects active participation from
\par
the receiver or just wants to inform him or her. Thus analysis based on simply the
\par
frequency of changing e-mails between KAM team members is ineffective. On the other
\par
hand considering all categories, intention, goal of e-mails makes the analysis very
\par
complicated and might not provide satisfactory result.
\par
Telephone (or video) conference with the participation of KAM team members is
\par
organized on the following occasions:
\par
regular discussion on schedule fixed well in advance
\par
personal meeting, which should be extended with remote participants, who are not
\par
able to appear physically
\par
urgent issue arose and people with different function have to be put together
\par
quickly to discuss it and find solution.
\par
The key account manager of F team organizes monthly virtual meetings between core
\par
staff and himself. In such cases each member involved should participate at the given
\par
time independent of place.
\par
61Two KAM teams merged this year and formed the S team. From this occasion a kick of
\par
meeting was organized. Although it was the first time for everybody to become
\par
acquainted with one another colleagues from overseas were not allowed to participate
\par
because of cost reason. Nevertheless, the key account manager did involve them virtually
\par
and during the meeting each of them held virtual presentations.
\par
During a difficult supply situation (capacity shortage) it is common to arrange telephone
\par
conferences among the key account manager, the customer business support, the account
\par
manager(s), the customer's strategic purchasing, and the purchaser(s) of customer's local
\par
production site(s). It is to discuss delivery possibilities, adjust demand to capacity, and
\par
allocate available quantity to particular sites.
\par
The fax is used almost exclusively to forward printed information coming from outside.
\par
For example, supplier evaluation form or product authorization form from customers are
\par
distributed in such way inside KAM team. However if the information is important for
\par
further work, like a yearly production plan, it is rather scanned, stored, and forwarded
\par
electronically.
\par
Video speech and animation can be presented at meetings, put on corporate Intranet, or
\par
sent by e-mail. PSC top management favors using mpeg video speech to communicate
\par
information on general situation, vision and strategy.
\par
PowerPoint presentations also include more and more animation to increase attention.
\par
Besides these there is possibility for more sophisticated shows nowadays. Once a special
\par
video animation, a cartoon was presented in a PSC product training where several
\par
members of F team participated. Cartoon heroes formulated the persons from market
\par
segment's top management. It draws high interest verifying the power of visualizing in
\par
team building. Sometimes leaders play in movies or cartoons that emphasize social
\par
presence by "self made heroes".
\par
Genres
\par
Communication can be studied not only based on the media but its purpose, as chapter
\par
1.3. describes. Since this more holistic approach when reason for communication is
\par
investigated is nearer to practical life than analyzing media selection, it is important for
\par
better understanding. Communication genres used by KAM teams are connected to
\par
particular events. The following list contains the list of genres KAM teams use in their
\par
external and internal communication:
\par
62monthly Hi/Lo report
\par
visit report
\par
business plan
\par
announcement
\par
budget file
\par
price contract
\par
request for quote (RFQ)
\par
order book
\par
customer's forecast
\par
discussing special issues (supply or delivery problems)
\par
product presentation
\par
customer meeting
\par
KAM meeting
\par
budget alignment meeting
\par
sales (or leadership) conference.
\par
Account managers are requested to submit monthly Hi-Lo (high lights with positive, low
\par
lights with negative issues) reports to the key account manager, then these individual
\par
reports are compiled by his assistant and it is distributed among the KAM team. This tool
\par
assures management to get comprehensive up to date picture by continuous change of
\par
information.
\par
The account manager prepares a visit report after customer visits. It describes the current
\par
demand and supply situation, and the news at customer side. Furthermore, actions to be
\par
taken can also be included, indicating the responsible person as well. Thus the visit report
\par
is a good example for the appearance of decreasing power distance with enhanced
\par
democracy described in chapter 1.3.4., since an account manager can arrange actions to a
\par
key account manager, though the second one is higher in the corporate hierarchy than the
\par
first one.
\par
Business plan includes types, projects, volumes, prices, and quantities for five years
\par
ahead. It is the account managers' responsibility to prepare it, and then the key account
\par
manager aggregates and forwards it to top management for acceptance.
\par
An official announcement informs about changes in the organizations. Most of the time it
\par
is to announce that somebody has been appointed to a new position, joined or left the
\par
company, or it may contain further information on current status of the company (profit
\par
projection). However it is never announced, when somebody went to a competitor.
\par
63(Sales) Budget file includes forecast for the rest of current year and the whole next year
\par
with significant type or article group, prices, volumes, and quantities. It is created by co-
\par
operation of the key account manager and the account managers each June and serves as
\par
the basis of corporate sales (income) budget.
\par
The price contract is negotiated between the key account manager and the customer's
\par
management. Then it is distributed among all KAM team members. It includes prices
\par
valid for a year, volume, customer part numbers, and PSC part numbers.
\par
RFQ is sent by the customer's purchaser to the account manager to enquire about
\par
device(s) not included in current price contract. However, it is answered by the core staff
\par
or the key account manager based on the advice of a relevant product-marketing engineer
\par
from a business line.
\par
An order book lists devices on order per particular customer. It is to check the order
\par
amount by comparing it to the data at the customer site. The customer business support
\par
has got access to the database from where it can be downloaded. So the account manager
\par
asks them to forward the current one usually right before the customer visit. Then account
\par
manager makes the customer check if it corresponds to the customer's logistic records.
\par
Customer's forecast is sent usually by fax to the customer business support. It includes the
\par
customer's demand for one year. However, the account manager also needs this
\par
information. So he or she asks either the customer's purchasing or the customer business
\par
support to forward it. This is an example when fax makes the proper information change
\par
difficult.
\par
When special issues arise like band stop alert from customer, KAM team members strive
\par
to clear it as soon as possible while entering to intensive discussion via phone and/or e-
\par
mail.
\par
The product presentation usually based on a PowerPoint format is held in product
\par
training, published on Intranet, or sent via e-mail.
\par
The participants of a customer meeting are the account manager and the customer's
\par
purchaser, and sometimes the key account manager and a customer business support
\par
executive. Its purpose is to exchange information and to harmonize databases, to discuss
\par
current issues and to set action plan for an optimal solution. The time between two
\par
customer visits is one to three months depending on the importance of the customer or the
\par
issue which arises in the meantime.
\par
The participants of a KAM meeting are the key account manager and the account
\par
managers and sometimes somebody from the customer business support. It is usually
\par
organized yearly, to summarize the issues that happened during the year, to present the
\par
64business plan and the budget figures, to outline the strategy connected to particular
\par
customer.
\par
Participants of a budget alignment meeting are key account managers and top managers
\par
of PSC. It is organized every autumn to complete the following year sales budget.
\par
The sales or leadership conference is organized for corporate top 30 per cent of PSC IMS
\par
(about 200-300 managers at higher level).
\par
All the above-described meetings have a common feature, they are complex genres. For
\par
example, an order book is checked, a product presentation is held on a customer meeting.
\par
Budget files are applied on a budget alignment meeting. Product presentation is held on
\par
sales conference.
\par
Contexting
\par
Many genres listed in the previous section determine accurate, closed context, so the
\par
meaning of the message should be clear for each insider having necessary background
\par
information. Most of the time the information is very simple, it is broken down into basic
\par
parts like yes or no, a date, or a number ultimately. However there are some cases when
\par
further explanation is needed, making oral conversation inevitable. A particular genre
\par
determines a sequence of conversations, which can last till each participant understands
\par
the message.
\par
Since team members work under time pressure they have to optimize their activities
\par
including communication. Thus easy access (treat) theory [Carlson and Davis 1998] is
\par
valid not only for media but for persons as well. Managers do not select a media or a
\par
person consciously but they concentrate on getting a particular task completed as soon as
\par
possible.
\par
Another important aspect is proper psychological conditions, which is necessary to
\par
understand the message, because communication also means common understanding and
\par
view on the negotiated topic. Thus informal communication has got an important role.
\par
Unofficial topics like the whether, weekend, holiday, travel, marriage, children provide
\par
rapport between peers. As key account and account managers are very frequent travelers
\par
it is an excellent topic for rapport to give a short description about the situation they are
\par
currently in. For example, when the key account manager of team S and me were on a trip
\par
in the Czech Republic, he was very often on the phone and before official topics he was
\par
reporting to other team members like 'we are just approaching the border of Slovakia' or 'I
\par
told Zsolt that we have got lead in this crossing, but he said that the truck is stronger'.
\par
65Such 'small talk' expedite involvement of the remote party which is necessary to make
\par
him or her focus on the actual topic.
\par
Another aspect is the critical frequency of contacts, which is needed to get used to
\par
communicating with somebody, to remember his appearance and to trust him or her,
\par
while working with confidential information.
\par
Unfortunately the number of contact persons and the volume of information limit
\par
personal communication within KAM teams, that decreases social awareness. For
\par
example, people in the same KAM team do not know each other. Furthermore it happens
\par
that somebody ignores his/her team member even after receiving an e-mail from that
\par
person.
\par
Another aspect is that most of the time people are rather passive receivers than active
\par
contributors of communication that decreases their interest in the topic. The ratio between
\par
active and passive part is 25 vs. 75 per cent. It restricts involvement, which is bad for
\par
quality, because one of the three fundaments of total quality management is employee
\par
involvement [Oakland 1991].
\par
2.4.2. Culture and KAM teams
\par
Members of a KAM team are subjects of several cultures and subcultures like:
\par
national culture
\par
local domestic subculture
\par
Philips Electronics' corporate culture
\par
Philips Semiconductors' culture
\par
subculture of a Global Market Segment of Philips Semiconductors
\par
a Philips national organization's culture
\par
culture of Semiconductor Division of a Philips national organization
\par
subculture of particular group of colleagues
\par
national and corporate cultures of customers.
\par
National cultures [Table 2.4.3.] and local domestic subcultures make a significant impact
\par
on KAM team members. I came across several cultural issues. One of them, for example,
\par
that ladies at customer business support in Hamburg insist more on keeping the regular
\par
working hours than my colleagues outside Germany. Nevertheless majority of cultural
\par
differences are caused by external things like the different summer and bank holiday
\par
66system and not by personal attitude. For example, French people do not work in August at
\par
all. The number of bank holidays is the highest in Austria.
\par
On the other hand national culture has an effect on individuals living in the Western
\par
world less and less. After Hofstede's work in the 80's, the globalization has extended and
\par
shaped such form there, which directs towards a hypersegmented mass of individual
\par
micro-cultures [Cs\'anyi 2000]. It is especially valid for employees of multinational
\par
companies like Philips, who live in an open society and are continuously connected and
\par
exposed to various cultures.
\par
Table 2.4.3. KAM team members' nationalities
\par
67Philips Electronics' (219 400 employee) culture touch KAM team members while
\par
communicating with people from other product divisions on training or at other events;
\par
holding Philips products in their hands; seeing advertisements; surfing on corporate
\par
Intranet; reading corporate or public newspapers. Symbols, structures, standards
\par
consciously emphasize this strong corporate culture. The company is so big and well
\par
known all over the world, that it has got a particular meaning in many domestic cultures.
\par
Philips Semiconductors a product division and Philips Hungary a national organization of
\par
Philips Electronics have inherited this strong culture.
\par
Philips Semiconductors' (30 000 people) culture has impacts on KAM team members
\par
while communicating with colleagues and partners, especially managers; seeing
\par
presentations; visiting different locations of Philips Semiconductors. As Philips is known
\par
by consumer products, Philips Semiconductors producing industrial components is quite
\par
unique also inside the organization. A further specialty, which effects PSC's culture, is
\par
that internal multinational relationships are most characteristic for this PD.
\par
Each Global Market Segment has got a distinct subculture as well as their own heroes,
\par
rituals, stories, organization structure, power structure, control system. However, they are
\par
parts of Philips Semiconductors culture and thus they are very interrelated with it.
\par
A Philips national organization has the most direct physical affect on KAM team
\par
members if they are regular office workers. Working environment, local colleagues sitting
\par
near them, everyday routines like common lunch is latent but dominant factors, which
\par
strongly determine individual behavior. Home workers of course are exposed less to these
\par
factors.
\par
Although at some places the Semiconductor Division of a Philips national organization
\par
consists of only a couple of people (6 persons in Hungary), they make separate
\par
