712.4.3. Groups related to KAM teams
\par
If we consider relationship of KAM teams to groups, the main issue is group cohesion, in
\par
other words, to what extent does a person belong to a particular group, because it
\par
determines the following critical issues:
\par
individual preferences and behavior
\par
knowledge
\par
way of daily work
\par
power structure
\par
stereotypes.
\par
I found that people's value system and their behavior are directly influenced by his or her
\par
companions. It is reflected by external and internal things like clothes, discussion topics,
\par
or what they think to be important i.e. attending an MBA course. All of these are
\par
consistent with Asch's, Vroom's, Cartwright's, and Zander's findings.
\par
Group cohesion has an impact on the knowledge of people. Members of KAM teams
\par
learn the most from their colleagues. In addition knowledge exists not only in but among
\par
people as well. It means problemsolving in groups or teams and the ability for other
\par
common intellectual groupworks.
\par
Interaction (communication) between colleauges and other activities like common lunch,
\par
or visit to the canteen determine the daily routine. So the closer contact you have the
\par
bigger the mutual impact is. Working methods like handling different software and
\par
hardware devices are taken over from each other thus internal semi-standards are
\par
established.
\par
Charismatic personality is a source of personal power, which can be exercised better on
\par
members of a group with strong cohesion.
\par
A further aspect is stereotyping. Other people's opinion on a person very much depends
\par
on what groups he or she belongs to. I experienced, that a predecessor's reputation has got
\par
great impact on that of a new member. He or she was accepted easily if his or her
\par
predecessor has already made a good impression.
\par
Another observation of mine is that when team members (or only colleagues) meet at first
\par
they are inquiring about the responsibility of each other. For example, when I introduced
\par
myself to the key account managers of teams' B,D,S, each of them asked what other
\par
customers I take care of beside his, and who my most important customer is. They needed
\par
it to decide on my availability and the possibility of my involvement.
\par
72Groups, which include KAM team members, can be set according to the following
\par
common features in decreasing order of strength of cohesion:
\par
working at the same local product division
\par
sitting in the same location like office in Heathrow, Hamburg, Budapest, Kokomo, or
\par
SaoPaolo
\par
having any personal relationships
\par
dealing with the same purchasing site (employees from customer side are included as
\par
well)
\par
being member of the same KAM team
\par
nationality
\par
having same position like key account managers, account managers, CBS\&Logistics
\par
executive
\par
age
\par
education.
\par
Working at the same product division means both frequent physical and working contact,
\par
so it provides the strongest cohesion, though colleagues' tasks are not directly related to
\par
each other. For example they deal with different customers.
\par
Sitting at the same location means regular physical contact.
\par
The more frequent the personal relationships are the more opportunity there is for strong
\par
group cohesion. Even very infrequent personal contact is significantly better than 'total
\par
virtuality'. I find that relationships are deliberately better if peers have been personally
\par
introduced to each other.
\par
Without personal relationship the rest of the factors are unable to build a strong cohesion
\par
among people, not even if they are members of the same KAM team having common,
\par
and/or complementing tasks and objectives.
\par
Thus a special situation of "groupless teams and teamless groups" can arise, when
\par
members of a team rather belong to different groups.
\par
2.4.4. Leadership and KAM teams
\par
The biggest challenge of a key account manager and an account manager might be
\par
leadership. They have to cope with the following resisting forces of multi remote teams:
\par
limited visibility
\par
limited availability
\par
limited possibility for motivating team members (difficult to visualize results)
\par
73limited possibility for maintaining personal contacts
\par
complex cultural background of members - different value systems
\par
strong but ambiguous influencing groups in the back ground
\par
competing preferences.
\par
Since a significant part of human communication goes without words, out of site situation
\par
makes it difficult. For example, it takes only a short look to realize whether somebody
\par
near us is available to talk, but it is more complicated remotely. It seems to be a very
\par
simple thing but the uncertainty if somebody is available or not is frustrating especially
\par
under time pressure. Furthermore personal impressions are also restricted by distance.
\par
Another aspect is learning undeliberately while working together. Leaders can hand over
\par
their knowledge during common activities, because they provide a behavioral pattern for
\par
decision making and problem solving. Virtual contacts limit these oportunities, because
\par
the number of joint actions are less, furthermore it gets partners to focus on main points
\par
eliminating details which can become important later. My observations at teams B, D, S,
\par
T showed that the key account managers and the account managers are very smart in
\par
organizing quick (partial) team meetings while they meet for other reasons like in the
\par
course of a product training or a customer visit. Thus the 'multi purpose' meetings have
\par
got distinguished importance in KAM teams' work.
\par
As it is described in chapter 2.3.4. key account managers usually do not have direct
\par
influence on their team members' incentive schemes, appraisals, and even personal
\par
objectives, which are normally used to push employees to achieve common goals.
\par
Motivational factors [Herzberg 1987] are not under their control either and results are
\par
difficult to be visualized, while it would be necessary for motivation.
\par
An informal relationship is necessary to accept somebody as a human being. Knowing his
\par
or her personality is essential for optimal communication in long term relationships.
\par
Nevertheless infrequent personal contacts make it difficult to maintain.
\par
Virtual team structure especially in multinational environment entails cultural (value)
\par
differences and ambiguous influencing groups in the background as it is described in
\par
chapters 2.4.2 and 2.4.3. in more details. It causes different priority structures. For
\par
example, the European coordinator of team T is an account manager of team D in one.
\par
Nevertheless he is rather a European coordinator because it is a higher responsibility
\par
position. That can cause confusion for D team members and raises the question of the
\par
'doughnut principle' in other words deciding on favor or responsibility explained in
\par
chapter 1.3.6.
\par
74A remote colleague usually keeps contact to several parties located far from him or her,
\par
which gives reason for the collisions of requests in case of causing delay. Thus key
\par
account managers should emphasize their requests. For example, they send request (and
\par
urging) to their team members for hi/low reports when the submission is due (or over) at
\par
the end of each month.
\par
Leadership means bigger challenge for account managers than for key account managers,
\par
because nobody reports to them. So they should be respected while inspiring, motivating
\par
their colleagues on the same or higher level in the hierarchy including also key account
\par
managers. It is the case of being a manager without being a manager, which has also been
\par
described by Adair as 'self leadership', and Handy as 'partnership'.
\par
On the other hand Philips' strong corporate culture described in chapter 2.4.2., further
\par
more standards and corporate initiatives [Appendix B] support leadership, while
\par
establishing proper environment where tasks and responsibilities are clear for people. For
\par
example an advanced genre system which contains dedicated formats like hi/low report
\par
supports the leaders' work.
\par
Philips tends to develop human resources, which are similar to professional session
\par
musicians, who are able to provide excellent work after a few rehearsals, when they come
\par
together.
\par
2.4.5. Knowledge work and KAM teams
\par
Key account managers, account managers, and application engineers are definitely
\par
knowledge workers, because a dominant part of their work is marketing and planning, in
\par
other words intellectual activities which require experience and co-operation. For
\par
example, they do negotiating, marketing analysis, pricing, budgeting, and design in.
\par
Employees at customer business support also do some knowledge work. A part of their
\par
work can be replaced by EDI (electronic data interchange - a system that establishes
\par
contact between supplier's and customer's computerized logistics system). On the other
\par
hand, they are working with information while continuously receiving, prioritizing,
\par
sending it, so they should make decisions when and what interruption is needed to
\par
facilitate proper communication, which requires experience. Furthermore, many of my
\par
colleagues at customer business support are members of different cross-functional venture
\par
75teams directed to improve supply processes. So they give advice and participate in group
\par
discussions.
\par
All in all, KAM team members are task oriented, they have got their own responsibilities
\par
and autonomy, and are innovating and learning continuously.
\par
Knowledge management at Philips Electronics including the Semiconductor Division as
\par
well goes with application of methods advised by management scientist quoted in chapter
\par
1.3.5. Philips 'community' has been striving to maximally exploit and improve its
\par
members' knowledge as follows:
\par
employees can have different positions simultaneously according to their
\par
competencies
\par
leadership is promoted among managers [Appendix B] - manager development
\par
direct contacts are supported as Figure 2.3.2. shows
\par
standard, organization wide training structure at corporate and national organization
\par
levels
\par
computerized interorganizational database.
\par
People can have more positions at the same time. For example, the account managers
\par
carry out product marketing and quality oriented functions; the key account managers can
\par
be country managers as well; many people at the customer business support are also
\par
members of different cross functional venture teams. My direct chief is both sales
\par
manager of Japanese automotive customers in Europe and sales manager in Hungary.
\par
Another colleague of mine in Austria is both account manager and quality manager. I
\par
know account managers in Italy and England, who are also marketing (product) managers
\par
of power discrete devices. One of my colleagues at the customer business support in
\par
Hamburg contributes as an advisor in the development of the new logistics software,
\par
which is being prepared curently.
\par
Leaders are the best vehicles for handing over and distributing knowledge, because they
\par
provide a model for proper behavior, and prioritizing. Who have not got enough
\par
experience, can rely on or should consider their leaders' knowledge many times. As it is
\par
described in chapter 2.4.4. Philips has got a written strategy for facilitating, expediting
\par
leadership in general, but remote leadership has got particular difficulties.
\par
PSC's flat corporate hierarchy and complex contact structure not only enables but also
\par
requires direct relationships to the most competent persons, giving opportunity for
\par
acquiring knowledge from the apts source. Unfortunately, the topics discussed are
\par
76restricted because of process optimization and time constraints. So there is a limited
\par
possibility for detailed explanations on side issues, which might be useful another time.
\par
Philips has got a standard training system while people attending one-week courses on
\par
marketing, financial and accounting, quality, and other topics. The top of this education
\par
scheme is the so called Philips University where the subjects are similar to that of an
\par
MBA course, but it is connected to Philips' life, so it is more practice oriented. Not only
\par
the studied material but also personal contacts with experiences from various areas are
\par
very useful at a training. For example, I met a person from the business line, who
\par
explained to me the method of wafer allocation.
\par
To expedite distribution of knowledge inside Philips even among people, who are not in
\par
contact, there is a so-called Philips Yellow Pages site on the corporate Intranet. It
\par
includes answers for frequently asked questions. Each employee can ask for information
\par
they are interested in, which is answered by internal experts. It is moderated by the so
\par
called gate away persons, who verifies responses.
\par
As Philips' employees are members of KAM teams, they are also involved in issues
\par
described previously. Nevertheless I considered three general difficulties of knowledge
\par
management at Philips Semiconductors:
\par
time consuming processes
\par
inconsistency in adequate training needs at product division level
\par
senseless spending.
\par
The 'inertia' of this big organization is so great that carrying out changes takes a while.
\par
Reaching an up to date status requires great efforts. For example, developing a state of the
\par
art software for production and logistics has lasted for more than 5 years and it is not
\par
finished yet. I have got my personal experience as well, namely that more than half a year
\par
was necessary to install logistics software on my computer, though I need it for my every
\par
day work. Since establishing direct contacts is heavily supported, the number of them can
\par
be so big that it takes a while to get to know each other.
\par
Although there is a standard, organization-wide training system inside Philips, there is no
\par
former prescription or recommendation for adequate initial training needs of a particular
\par
position. It is because training is organized rather at total or national organizational levels
\par
and less at product division level.
\par
Philips applies the lawnmower method to carry out general cost cut during tough times
\par
from the point of view of profit and it cause strange situations. For example, since travel
\par
restriction has came into force this year, many people are not allowed to participate in any
\par
77internal meetings including trainings, even if it is directly needed for their daily work, for
\par
example a training on the forecasting software. Contrast to this if the profit projection is
\par
good they spend money for unnecessary things, like get the whole sales staff (600 people)
\par
to travel to the Far East.
\par
2.4.6. Productivity of KAM team members
\par
Work methods and response time
\par
Chapter 2.4.1. about communication containing genres and chapter 2.4.2. about culture
\par
including rituals and routines provides a comprehensive description related to this point.
\par
Another important issue is how consciously the different team members apply and select
\par
their methods. To emphasize it they are let and made to participate in various trainings.
\par
On the other hand, the training system on product division level is not standard. There are
\par
not particular trainings in which every colleague of the same position should take part.
\par
So it is quite ad hoc on what training can team members participate in. For example only
\par
members of team F participated remote team training. Although its context was useful
\par
and applicable, it did not catch much interest. The interviewed participants did not really
\par
realize how important it was, because they thought if it so momentous were then all
\par
people with the same position should have participated in it.
\par
Task allocation, responsibility charting, and replacing colleagues
\par
Chapter 2.3.4. about roles and tasks of KAM team members contains relevant
\par
information. However, there is another more dynamic way off allocating tasks. Since job
\par
descriptions are only draft definitions in order to enable flexibility, the actual activities
\par
should be set exactly when they are necessary. Thus they are usually determined during or
\par
after meetings in the form of an action list including the description of the task, the dead
\par
line, and the responsible person.
\par
Another issue is replacing a colleague either temporarily or for a long term. Both
\par
problems are quite difficult especially in case of key account and account managers. As
\par
chapter 2.4.5. describes they are knowledge workers and so there are more ways to
\par
achieve results. In addition their works directed to a special problem is hard to be split
\par
into subtasks. Practically their replacement is viable only if another colleague is fully
\par
involved parallel in the particular issue.
\par
78Control and time utilization
\par
Although KAM team members work far from each other without continuous visual
\par
supervision, the following factors exercise control on their work:
\par
requests for information
\par
scheduled activities
\par
activity list
\par
sales objectives - achieving budget
\par
other objectives like successful design in
\par
personal supervision - common visits
\par
special action plans (i.e. closing the gap, order line reduction program)
\par
cost control
\par
(clocking in clock).
\par
All KAM team members have been continuously receiving and sending request for
\par
information from and to the management, customers, other KAM team members, other
\par
colleagues that determines significant part of their working activities. However
\par
responding to a request depends very much on the personal relationship between the
\par
sender and the receiver. If the person, who should respond does not know the requester,
\par
the answer is less likely.
\par
Rituals and routines listed in chapter 2.4.2. and customer visits set a schedule for daily,
\par
monthly, and yearly activities.
\par
An activity list can be completed while concluding the result of a meeting to set what
\par
have to do next. Here people lower in the hierarchy can point to activities for managers
\par
possessing higher rank. However managers tend not to follow their subordinates direct
\par
prescriptions.
\par
Sales budget extended with other objectives provides priority while selecting among and
\par
focusing on actions.
\par
Management's visits also direct attention to particular activities. For example, when the
\par
European Automotive Segment manager visits us twice a year, we prepare a business
\par
review with sales and demand analysis.
\par
IMS or GMS management, and key account managers initiate special action plans to
\par
achieve common goals. For example, an order line reduction program has been targeted to
\par
eliminate order lines below one thousand Euro amounts. The results have been monitored
\par
and communicated to the staff from time to time.
\par
Cost control functions both at product division and national organization level. For
\par
example, travelling outside the domestic region has to be authorized by GMS manager in
\par
79the current cost cut situation. His agreement is also necessary for increasing headcount.
\par
Expense reports and travel authorizations are required at national organizations to check
\par
proper spending.
\par
Applying a clocking in clock is not a general method of Philips Semiconductor, but it has
\par
been still functioning at some places, for example, in the Austrian office. Nevertheless it
\par
is mainly for administrative purposes, though I find it very anachronistic and inconsistent
\par
with proper management methods. However, people including account managers,
\par
application engineers, and customer business executives (also some members of F team in
\par
Austria) have to clock in and clock out and have their card endorsed by the office
\par
manager. Anyway, they know how to elude it.
\par
Motivation
\par
The key account manager and the European coordinator are the main drivers of the KAM
\par
team's productivity. Their personal objectives are very strongly related and cover the
\par
team's objectives as a whole.
\par
The core staff's objective is directed to particular activities of one customer, like technical
\par
advice on selected products, giving quotes, follow up design in.
\par
The account manager's and the customer service's objectives are connected to production
\par
sites of customers. However, they usually deal with several production sites of different
\par
customers simultaneously. Thus they have got priority which limits the effort they put
\par
into achieving objectives connected to one particular customer.
\par
The key account manager and the account manager have quite large freedom in deciding
\par
the order of activities while they work without strict supervision. Furthermore, they are
\par
empowered because their jobs fulfill almost all criteria set by [Jarvenpaa and Ives 1994]
\par
as follows:
\par
get information about organizational performance - they receive actual sales figures
\par
weekly and profit projection quarterly
\par
are awarded for contributing to organizational performance - their bonus is based on
\par
fulfilling sales budget
\par
have the knowledge and skills to understand and contribute to organizational
\par
performance - Philips training system supports it
\par
have the power to make decisions that influence organizational direction and
\par
performance - key account managers usually initiate and account managers carry out
\par
actions to increase profit.
\par
80Of course there are constraints listed in the first section of this chapter, which they have to
\par
fulfill, and which determines the tempo of the work according to the actual business
\par
situation. However, they are quite unbound in selecting activities like searching for
\par
market information, enhancing their knowledge on products or systems, socializing with
\par
colleagues, customers, and partners. Furthermore by the application of portable computers
\par
the border between private life and work have faded so, they can select activities which
\par
are not exactly related to work like attending a business school.
\par
Although job descriptions include KAM tem member's core functions. For example, that
\par
of an account manager in Appendix C, these are quite complex activities, which can be
\par
carried out on various ways also requiring more or less optional actions like socializing.
\par
Work at customer business support is less flexible, however it is enriched by other jobs
\par
like advising in a cross-functional venture team.
\par
2.4.7. Trust around KAM teams
\par
Trust was the most difficult to be investigated and evaluated during my research, because
\par
of the following things:
\par
it belongs to people's private and very confidential area
\par
difficult to get total frankness
\par
there is no standard scale to measure it
\par
there is limited number of apt indicators for it.
\par
I find trust to be closely related to personal relationship and especially to preference, that
\par
supports the statement in chapter 1.3. that initial trust is different from that later on.
\par
However, according to my experience, virtual team members can not establish traditional
\par
personal relationships because they focus almost exclusively on work-related issues.
\par
Since they are not involved on each others' private life necessary enough to establish
\par
deeper personal contact, they stick to an initial level of trust that impedes group cohesion.
\par
That is consistent with my finding on groups described in chapter 2.4.3. Paradoxically the
\par
more frequent the communication is the higher the trust is between peers and the higher
\par
the trust is the less communication is needed between peers.
\par
Nevertheless Philips tends to iron out problems by creating a professional environment
\par
with high initial trust level and containing as many direct contacts as possible, as it is the
\par
81precondition of efficient co-operation. However, dominant organizational culture
\par
indicating high initial trust entails depersonalization that results in stereotyping becoming
\par
dominant. "Institution-based trust means that one believes the necessary impersonal
\par
structures are in place to enable one to act in anticipation of a successful future endeavor"
\par
[Harrison et al 1998]. I have felt the vote of confidence to be given not personally to me
\par
but to my position.
\par
I also tried to explore person-related trust by using the following scale, which especially
\par
applies to organizational environment:
\par
A. not relying on or asking for something work related from him/her even if it belongs to
\par
his/her core responsibility
\par
B. not relying on or asking for something work related from him/her, though he/she is
\par
supposed to be able to advise or help
\par
C. relying on or asking for something work related from him/her because it belongs to
\par
his/her core responsibility
\par
D. relying on or asking for something work related from him/her, because he/she is
\par
supposed to be able to advise or help
\par
E. relying on or asking for something private from him/her, because he/she is supposed
\par
to be able to advise or help.
\par
Most of the virtual relationships I checked fell into category C. and less into D.
\par
I find only slight indication of distrust as follows:
\par
involving somebody at higher level by using cc:
\par
sending parallel requests on the same issue.
\par
However all of them were between a non-member and a member of a KAM team and not
\par
inside it.
\par
Although there is a high possibility for availability bias steaming from negative
\par
feedbacks, I came across only good intentions and did not register any 'floor politics' and
\par
'conspiracy' among KAM team members.
\par
82\textbf{Conclusion}
\par
The virtual team is an effective tool for providing both flexibility and consistency. The
\par
first one is necessary for organizations while adapting themselves to today's turbulent
\par
environment. The latter one is required to satisfy customers with providing outstanding
\par
quality.
\par
To analyze virtual teams a construct should be built on communication, culture, group,
\par
leadership, knowledge work, productivity, and trust as these factors have got
\par
distinguished importance.
\par
\emph{What common and special genres do members of the virtual teams apply while}
\par
\emph{communicating? How are group discussions and decision making carried out in the}
\par
\emph{virtual teams?}
\par
Every KAM teams use genres like monthly Hi/Lo report, visit report, announcement,
\par
budget file, price contract, request for quote (RFQ), order book, customer's forecast,
\par
discussion on emergent issues, product presentation, customer meeting, KAM meeting,
\par
budget alignment meeting, sales conference. For business plan (long term plan) however
\par
there is no standard format. This set of common genres provides compatibility among
\par
members of different KAM teams, which is required for flexibility. Further it determines
\par
adequate, close context, which facilitates better understanding and maintains discipline. It
\par
is necessary to control the activities of team members who are out of sight. It can be
\par
generally stated that the genre system is fundamental for virtual teams because it provides
\par
the necessary platform for communication and control.
\par
KAM team members have got high number of contacts and the low communication
\par
frequency among them restricts social awareness needed for common understanding. It
\par
led to the fact that informal part of the conversations gained dedicated importance. It
\par
makes the receivers focus on the discussed topic with increasing interest towards the
\par
sender. Another aspect is that most of the time being only a reader of messages limits
\par
involvement.
\par
\emph{What affect does corporate and national culture have on virtual teams?}
\par
National cultures in the Western world make differences rather in external issues like the
\par
holiday system and fringe benefits. Corporate (business) culture outweighs national
\par
culture while having more significant impact on KAM team members' individual values
\par
and preferences.
\par
83\emph{How intra-and interorganizational group-structure influence virtual teams?}
\par
Weak cohesion among virtual team members and strong cohesion among local colleagues
\par
result in 'groupless teams' and 'teamless groups'. People who work physically close
\par
together are members of different KAM teams most of the time, so they do not have
\par
common objectives and complementing tasks. Thus good informal relationships, which
\par
are necessary for strong cohesion, have been established among people who do not have
\par
common business.
\par
To facilitate the virtual team's cohesion it is required that team members should be aware
\par
of their membership and that their closest colleagues are usually not the ones who sit
\par
nearby but other team members who may be working thousands of kms away.
\par
Furthermore they have to spend as much time as possible together. From the
\par
management's point of view it is necessary to evaluate the members' external preferences
\par
coming from the fact of belonging to other groups. It is to understand their willingness
\par
and capabilities.
\par
\emph{What special managerial difficulties do the e Team leaders face and what techniques do}
\par
\emph{they apply to cope with them?}
\par
Leadership challenges virtual team members by limited visibility; limited availability;
\par
limited possibility for motivating other team members; limited possibility for maintaining
\par
personal contacts; complex cultural backgrounds; strong but ambiguous influencing
\par
groups in the background; competing preferences. So virtual team leaders have got a lot
\par
of constraints, that is why they need external support. The biggest problem could be how
\par
to get others to make actions you point out or achieve team tasks without direct influence
\par
on his or her personal objectives and incentives. Human resource management and top
\par
management should provide aids to solve this. Top management should supervise if the
\par
corporate, team and individual objectives are optimally in harmony. Further they should
\par
provide opportunities for physical meetings (allocating cost for this purpose). On the
\par
other hand human resource management should select and build 'professionals', who are
\par
able to work together, within a very short time in an excellent way. In case of virtual
\par
teams not only the leader's but also the "follower's" quality plays a very important role in
\par
assuring 'professional partnership'.
\par
\emph{What is the relationship between virtual team and knowledge work?}
\par
84The majority of virtual team members are knowledge workers. Thus virtual teamwork can
\par
be improved with enhanced productivity of knowledge workers. Their competencies can
\par
be exploited by multi-positions according to their capabilities; and facilitated by direct
\par
contacts among relevant persons. Their work can be expedited by computerized
\par
interorganizational database. Very time consuming (bureaucratic) processes should be
\par
eliminated if possible. They need to have adequate basic training generally allocated to
\par
particular positions to enhance confidence and eliminate uncertainty.
\par
\emph{How virtual team's productivity can be assured and improved?}
\par
A dedicated training can enhance productivity while participants are consciously thinking
\par
through the working methods and criteria for media selection. Top management should
\par
follow up action lists regularly to assure their accomplishments. Responsibility charts
\par
including necessary overlaps should be applied to allocate tasks as clear as possible and to
\par
enable replacements. Depersonalized control can enhance objectivity while possibility of
\par
personal conflicts being decreased. It could be realized through requests from direct
\par
partners; scheduled actions; and objectives, while people have got freedom in prioritizing
\par
and selecting among different activities.
\par
\emph{How do trust affect the performance of the virtual teams?}
\par
Trust is inevitable for teamwork because it supports adequate co-operation. Virtual team
\par
members can not establish traditional personal relationships and so they are stuck at initial
\par
trust level. So institution based trust should be emphasized to keep initial trust at high
\par
level. One of the best tools for it is strong corporate culture.
\par
