\par
ramp-up is handled by Logistics/tactical marketing , customer service and
\par
logistics/materials (customer).
\par
5.5 Business Fulfilment
\par
5.5.1 Forecasting \& Planning Cycle
\par
The forecast activity takes the information from the customer on their anticipated usage
\par
and trends and collates this information for the Product group.
\par
The systems used are:
\par
TROPIC = The Regional Order Planning and Information Control system, which is used
\par
in all Sales
\par
Offices in the RSO.
\par
5.5.2 Conditioning transactions
\par
This is the sub-process ensuring all conditions are considered for the sales transaction. It
\par
is to
\par
ensure all criteria are in place for the rest of the fulfilment process to run smoothly. It
\par
includes:
\par
{$\cdot$} Part number and customer numbers
\par
{$\cdot$} Price quotations/contract details are valid and correctly loaded (MOQs, Orderline value,
\par
EDI,
\par
leadtime, stock targets).
\par
{$\cdot$} Budget/ Annual contract
\par
5.5.3 Processing Orders
\par
Orders are entered into the TROPIC system by EDI or manually by the Local Sales
\par
Office. The
\par
task of the Product Planners is to see if the orders fit the sales plan and confirm the orders
\par
to the
\par
customer. Product Planners inform customer service on reconfirmations/supply issues.
\par
5.5.4 Delivering product - Physical Distribution
\par
Physical Distribution process is about delivering products from RSO warehouse to the
\par
customer in
\par
88line with the customers requirements. The products are delivered to the RSO European
\par
warehouse
\par
designated as
\par
RSO Europe.
\par
from the various Product Division factories. The distribution area covers the territory
\par
5.5.5 Money Collection
\par
Invoices are generated automatically when products leave the European Warehouse. For
\par
other
\par
deliveries manual invoices are raised by the customer service group.
\par
Following the invoices being issued the correct money is received within the payment
\par
terms.
\par
Corrective action is taken with customers who repeatedly pay late. Query management is
\par
done to
\par
ensure errors are monitored, their causes established and corrective and preventive action
\par
is
\par
implemented.
\par
5.6 Personnel Planning, Remuneration and development
\par
This process is about rewarding and recognising employees for their contribution to the
\par
business.
\par
Also ensuring employees have the necessary skills for the workplace as it develops and
\par
that
\par
employees are responsible for their own career development This process is includes
\par
managers developing new skills (such as coaching and empowerment) to enable a culture
\par
change to be brought about so the organisation can move towards a learning organisation
\par
and world class performance.
\par
Leadership Competencies not only serve as a guideline and target for the personal
\par
development of current and future leaders; they also provide the organization with a clear,
\par
Philips-wide, reference framework for selection and development.
\par
89Appendix B
\par
The Philips Leadership Competences
\par
Six Leadership Competencies define the behavior that is required to achieve business
\par
success.They provide a balanced approach, stating the importance of task aspects while
\par
recognizing that results are obtained through people. These competencies are:
\par
Shows determination to achieve
\par
excellent results
\par
Effective managers develop a vision of where their business
\par
should be going. They translate this into demanding and chal-lenging
\par
goals, and take responsibility for moving the company
\par
towards this vision.
\par
Focuses on the market
\par
External input and excellent business knowledge are prerequisites
\par
to developing a vision. Other vital factors are a profound market
\par
knowledge, customer insight and a broad perspective on the
\par
business. These imply that a manager must think strategically.
\par
Competitive advantage can be achieved by applying a sound
\par
understanding of the market and the external environment to all
\par
business activities.
\par
Finds better ways
\par
True value creation means that a manager has to put his or her
\par
vision into practice in the organization; putting the systems and
\par
processes in place and managing them. Philips managers
\par
should pursue profitability and champion change - using each
\par
and every opportunity to improve their business processes.
\par
Leaders optimize key processes by finding ways to get things
\par
done faster, more efficiently and more effectively in co-operation
\par
with others.
\par
In the three competencies above the emphasis is on the task
\par
aspects of the leadership role. The following competencies
\par
describe how results are achieved through people.
\par
Demands top performance
\par
Managers should turn their vision into achievable targets while
\par
acting as a role model for top performance. They need to show
\par
direction and lead by example while addressing difficult people
\par
issues at the moment when they should be addressed. Managers
\par
should demand that high performance goals and standards are
\par
achieved and maintained, while clarifying expectations and acti-vely
\par
managing performance to ensure success.
\par
Inspires commitment
\par
True leaders are able to persuade their team to share the same
\par
vision. They demonstrate excellent communication skills; they
\par
win commitment at a rational and emotional level, apply an
\par
understanding of people to motivate and inspire colleagues, and
\par
so get the best out of them.
\par
90Develops self and others
\par
Good managers raise the capability of the organization by
\par
continuously developing both themselves and others, and by
\par
creating a learning organization in which knowledge is actively
\par
managed. Learning faster than other organizations gives a real
\par
competitive advantage. True leaders are masters in giving and
\par
receiving feedback, and helping others to improve through
\par
coaching and mentoring.
\par
The six leadership competencies show a balance between the
\par
rational and emotional, between yin and yang, between task and
\par
people, providing a solid basis for sustainable business success.
\par
