\par
Contaminated land and groundwater is a problem of growing concern in our society. An in-
\par
creased environmental awareness, addressed in Sweden's new environmental legislation
\par
(0LOM\textbar EDONHQ, 1999-01-01), has resulted in land and water contamination now being a major
\par
factor in land use planning and management, real estate assessment, and property selling. In-
\par
vestigation and remediation of contaminated areas are often associated with high costs. The
\par
Swedish EPA currently estimates that there are 22 000 contaminated sites in Sweden, of
\par
which approximately 4000 are in need for remediation.
\par
The high costs and large number of contaminated sites are strong incentives for cost-efficient
\par
investigation and remediation strategies. 0LOM\textbar EDONHQ states that the environmental value of
\par
the remediation process must be higher than the investment costs. Critical issues to be ad-
\par
dressed in order to meet the intentions of 0LOM\textbar EDONHQ and to provide cost-efficient handling
\par
of contaminated sites to landowners, operators, and the society are:
\par
{$\bullet$} What level of certainty is required to make sound decisions at a specific site, i.e. where,
\par
how and to what extent should sampling be performed?
\par
{$\bullet$} What remediation strategy is the most favorable with respect to investment costs and effi-
\par
ciency to meet specific clean-up goals?
\par
Risk-based decision analysis is a theoretical approach to handle benefits, investment costs,
\par
and risk costs in a structured way to identify cost-efficient alternatives. Today, decisions re-
\par
garding investigation strategies and remediation alternatives are in Sweden most often taken
\par
without completely or openly evaluating the cost-efficiency of alternatives.
\par
The present report is one of two literature reviews prepared within the project 5LVNEDVHGGH
\par
FLVLRQDQDO\textbackslash VLVIRULQYHVWLJDWLRQVDQGUHPHGLDODFWLRQVRIFRQWDPLQDWHGODQG5LVNEDVHUDG
\par
EHVOXWVDQDO\textbackslash VI\textbar UXQGHUV\textbar NQLQJDURFKnWJlUGHUYLGPDUNRFKJUXQGYDWWHQI\textbar URUHQDGHRP
\par
UnGHQThe project is sponsored by the Swedish Geotechnical Institute (SGI) and carried out
\par
in co-operation between SGI and the Department of Geology at Chalmers University. The
\par
main purpose of the project is to evaluate and describe risk-based methods for cost-effective
\par
investigation and remediation strategies with respect to Swedish conditions. The two reports
\par
are:
\par
{$\bullet$} 6DPSOLQJVWUDWHJLHVDQGGDWDZRUWKDQDO\textbackslash VLVIRUFRQWDPLQDWHGODQG, by P\"ar-Erik Back
\par
(Department of Geology, Publ. B 486 and SGI, report Varia 500).
\par
{$\bullet$} 'HFLVLRQDQDO\textbackslash VLVXQGHUXQFHUWDLQW\textbackslash, by Jenny Norrman (Department of Geology, Publ. B
\par
485 and SGI, report Varia 501).
\par
The main purpose of the reports is to provide comprehensive descriptions of the state of the
\par
art of decision analysis and sampling strategies for contaminated land. The reports form an
\par
important basis for future work, not only in the specific project, but also in the wide topic of
\par
risk-based decision analysis.
