\par
A literature review of sampling strategies for contaminated soil and groundwater is presented.
\par
The different types of uncertainties associated with a site-investigation are reviewed. The un-
\par
certainties are classified as pre-sampling, sampling, and post-sampling uncertainties. Theories
\par
to quantify different types of uncertainties are reviewed, especially the different sampling
\par
uncertainties. The particulate sampling theory was found to be of interest because it identifies
\par
all the relevant sampling uncertainties and presents them in a structured way. The question on
\par
how many samples to collect under different conditions is addressed. A conclusion is that the
\par
sampling objective must be clearly stated prior to the development of any sampling plan. Two
\par
of the most common sampling objectives are to detect hot spots and to estimate mean con-
\par
centrations.
\par
Methods to determine the economic worth of sampling are reviewed. Traditionally, sampling
\par
has been performed with the objective to either (1) minimize uncertainty for a fixed sampling
\par
budget, or to (2) reach a prespecified accuracy at lowest possible cost. However, in recent
\par
years data worth analysis has been used increasingly. The valuable feature of data worth
\par
analysis is the ability to assess the worth of a proposed site-investigation program prior to
\par
performing any sampling or measurements. Data worth analysis is performed in a risk-based
\par
decision framework by combining decision theory with e.g. geological, geochemical, hydro-
\par
geological, and economic information. A conclusion is that the use of data worth analysis for
\par
contaminated land problems has a potential but the complexity of existing methods has lim-
\par
ited its use. To be used on a more regular basis, data worth methods need to be further devel-
\par
oped.
\par
A review of more than 30 related software packages is presented in an appendix. It was found
\par
that public domain or low cost commercial software packages exist for a number applications
\par
related to site-investigations, e.g. sampling design, data evaluation, geostatistical techniques,
\par
stochastic simulation, data worth analysis, and decision analysis.
