\par
Contaminated soil and groundwater is a problem that has received increased attention in
\par
the last decade. The risk of exposure to contaminants for humans and the environment
\par
makes it necessary to investigate the degree of contamination at a site. Usually the in-
\par
vestigation of a contaminated site results in a risk assessment, where the risk for humans
\par
and the environment is assessed. Sometimes, a preliminary assessment of the site is per-
\par
formed before any samples or measurements have been taken. This preliminary assess-
\par
ment is based on \'a-priori knowledge about the site, such as the type of contaminants that
\par
has been used at the site, the geologic conditions, spill events etc.
\par
When a site with contaminated soil or groundwater is investigated, a number of deci-
\par
sions have to be made. They are of different nature but typically include which sam-
\par
pling strategy to use (how many samples to take, where to take them, which media to
\par
sample, control and duplicate samples etc.) and if remediation of the site is necessary.
\par
Usually, many of these, and similar questions, have no easy answer, mainly due to un-
\par
certainties in the site-investigation.
\par
All stages of a site-investigation involve uncertainties. These include uncertainties in:
\par
- \'a-priori knowledge about the site,
\par
- conceptual models of the site,
\par
- sampling, handling, field measurements and laboratory analyses,
\par
- data handling and prediction of contaminant transport, and
\par
- risk assessment
\par
This can be summarised as uncertainties in (1) contaminant characterisation and (2)
\par
geological characterisation. The site-investigation results in an estimate of the extent of
\par
the contamination at the site. The level of contamination is uncertain but will influence
\par
the remediation costs. If there is a large uncertainty in the estimate of contamination, the
\par
remediation costs will also be uncertain. Often, the remediation cost will be much
\par
higher than anticipated. Therefore, it would be preferable if the uncertainties in a site-
\par
investigation could be estimated.
\par
Because of stiff competition on the market the consultant with the cheapest site-
\par
investigation (smalles number of samples) often gets the job (Bosman, 1993). A conse-
\par
quence is that it will be difficult to discriminate between ``nothing found'' because there
\par
was nothing there or ``nothing found'' because of a poor site-investigation (Bosman,
\par
1993). The latter may well be regarded as a success by the involved parties but may in
\par
fact lead to long term human health or environmental effects. It is obvious that the un-
\par
certainties are large in many site-investigations of contaminated soil and groundwater.
\par
Often, the overall uncertainty is underestimated, especially in cases where only a few
\par
field samples has been collected, and data analysis cannot recover more information
\par
than the samples contain (Flatman and Yfantis, 1996). Collecting only a few samples
\par
can therefore result in poor site characterisation, which in turn can result in poor and
\par
expensive remediation decisions (James and Gorelick, 1994).
\par
Today, laboratory methodology has reached a point where analytical error contributes
\par
only a very small portion of the total variance seen in data (Mason, 1992; Shefsky,
\par
1997). Typically, errors in the taking of field samples are much greater than preparation,6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 7 (96)
\par
handling, analytical, and data analysis errors (van Ee et al., 1990). Unfortunately, many
\par
decisions are made in ignorance or contempt of the uncertainty of the sample data
\par
(Taylor, 1996). However, neglecting uncertainties does not mean that they do not exist
\par
(Lacasse and Nadim, 1996). Instead, uncertainties need to be considered and if possible
\par
be reduced.
\par
There are several ways to reduce the overall uncertainty, but the most effective one is
\par
often to increase the number of samples taken, provided that the samples are represen-
\par
tative of the sample location. This will usually reduce the uncertainty but increase the
\par
costs of sampling and analyses. Also, in many cases the uncertainty can be economi-
\par
cally reduced by going from a random to a spatial variable sampling design (Flatman
\par
and Yfantis, 1996).
\par
7KHSUREOHP'HFLVLRQXQGHUXQFHUWDLQW\textbackslash{}
\par
The main problems that will be addressed in this report is:
\par
1. 2. how to quantify the uncertainties that exist in a site-investigation, and
\par
how to find an ``optimal'' level of uncertainty (i.e. optimal to make the proper deci-
\par
sion), with respect to sampling.
\par
These problems are a part of the main problem at a contaminated site, namely how to
\par
make GHFLVLRQVXQGHUXQFHUWDLQW\textbackslash. Therefore, these questions should be handled in a
\par
framework based on GHFLVLRQDQDO\textbackslash VLV. The principle of decision analysis is how to
\par
make well-founded, defensible decisions under uncertainty. Typically, a reduction in
\par
uncertainty has a cost, which can be weighed against the benefit of making a more well-
\par
founded decision. It is practical to use monetary terms to quantify the costs and benefits,
\par
as described in chapter 6, but there are several other methods.
\par
The optimal level of uncertainty in a site-investigation is reached when the cost for ad-
\par
ditional information (from additional sampling etc.) is equal to the expected benefits
\par
associated with the new information. Additional information is only cost-efficient up to
\par
that point. If the cost for additional information exceeds the benefit, sampling is no
\par
longer cost-efficient. 'DWDZRUWKDQDO\textbackslash VLV is thus the key to a cost-efficient site-
\par
investigation. In a decision analysis framework the data worth analysis sets the stopping
\par
rule when no additional collection of information should be made. More information
\par
will have a cost but the decision will not be sufficiently more well-founded to justify
\par
that cost. Decision analysis can also be used to compare alternative data-collection pro-
\par
grammes, such as different sampling strategies.
\par
All relevant uncertainties in the pre-sampling, sampling and post-sampling phases of the
\par
investigation have to be handled to make the data worth analysis complete. Optimally, it
\par
should be possible to quantify all uncertainties involved. Taking all uncertainties into
\par
consideration will make it possible to demonstrate the overall uncertainty of a site-
\par
investigation, especially the uncertainty associated with the sampling process.6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 8 (96)
\par
3XUSRVH
\par
The purpose of the literature review is to present the state-of-the-art regarding uncer-
\par
tainties and data worth analysis in site-investigations. The focus is on sampling and data
\par
worth analysis. Both contaminated soil and groundwater are addressed. The literature
\par
review covers several related topics, primarily the following:
\par
- Uncertainty in general
\par
- Uncertainty in geologic and conceptual models (e.g. geologic interpretation and de-
\par
scription)
\par
- Sampling uncertainty and sampling strategies
\par
- Data worth analysis
\par
A review of relevant software packages is also presented in an appendix.
\par
/LPLWDWLRQV
\par
In the literature review, the focus of uncertainty in site-investigations is on geochemical
\par
properties, but geologic and hydraulic properties are also considered. The two latter are
\par
associated with the important concepts of SDUDPHWHUXQFHUWDLQW\textbackslash{} and PRGHOXQFHUWDLQW\textbackslash.
\par
Methods to estimate the uncertainty in field and laboratory analyses are only addressed
\par
in short. These methods have been described thoroughly in the chemical analysis lit-
\par
erature. Also, uncertainty in human health and ecological risk assessments is important
\par
to be aware of but is not addressed in the report. This question is more related to reme-
\par
diation decision problems.
\par
In the literature there is an abundance of qualitative routines for sampling but they are
\par
of minor concern in this report. The reason is that they rarely support any quantitative
\par
estimation of uncertainty.6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 9 (96)
