\par
A quantity is something that can be quantified in some way. Parameter is a similar term
\par
(note that the term parameter has a more specific definition in statistics). Quantities and
\par
parameters can be classified into a number of groups. Morgan and Henrion (1990) dis-
\par
tinguish at least 7 different types of quantity:
\par
\ding{51} Empirical quantities
\par
\ding{51} Defined constants
\par
\ding{51} Decision variables
\par
\ding{51} Value parameters
\par
\ding{51} Index variables
\par
\ding{51} Model domain parameters
\par
\ding{51} Outcome criteria
\par
These quantities can be explained in the following way, primarily as described by
\par
Morgan and Henrion (1990):
\par
(PSLULFDOTXDQWLWLHV represent measurable properties of the real world, such as hydrau-
\par
lic conductivity. The uncertainty of an empirical quantity can be expressed in the form
\par
of a probability density function (PDF). Often, the empirical quantities constitute the
\par
majority of quantities in models and they are often uncertain. The commonly used term
\par
``parameter uncertainty'' refers to uncertainty in empirical quantities.
\par
'HILQHGFRQVWDQWV are by definition certain, such as the mathematical constant {$\pi$} or the
\par
number of days in December. Many physical constants, such as the gravitational con-
\par
stant, are actually empirical quantities but with only a small degree of uncertainty.
\par
A GHFLVLRQYDULDEOH (or control variable) is a quantity for which it is up to the risk ana-
\par
lyst or the decision-maker to select a value. Examples of decision variables at a site-
\par
investigation are the number of sampling points and the sampling depth. A decision
\par
variable has no inherent uncertainty but the difficulty is to find its ``best'' value.
\par
9DOXHSDUDPHWHUVrepresent values or preferences of the decision-maker. Examples of
\par
value parameters are the discount rate in cost-benefit analysis, parameters of risk toler-
\par
ance or risk aversion, and ``value of life''. It is debatable if value parameters can be
\par
treated as probabilistic. Usually it is a serious mistake to treat value parameters in the
\par
same way as empirical quantities. However, the difference between a value parameter
\par
and an empirical quantity is not always clear. It is also a matter of intent and perspec-
\par
tive.
\par
,QGH[YDULDEOHVare used to identify a location in the spatial or temporal domain of a
\par
model. Examples include x and y co-ordinates in a 2-dimensional model. Index-
\par
variables are certain by definition.
\par
0RGHOGRPDLQSDUDPHWHUV specify the domain of the modelled system, generally by
\par
specifying the range and increments for index variables. Domain parameters define the
\par
level of detail of a model, both spatially and temporally. Examples of domain parame-
\par
ters are grid spacing in a model, time increment in transient simulations etc. Usually,6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 10 (96)
\par
there is uncertainty about the appropriate values for domain parameters but it is inap-
\par
propriate and impractical to represent the uncertainty with a probability distribution.
\par
The choice of value is up to the modeller.
\par
2XWFRPHFULWHULD are variables used to measure the desirability of possible outcomes of
\par
a model. Examples include the calculated measure of risk in a risk model. Outcome cri-
\par
teria will be deterministic or probabilistic depending on whether any of the input quan-
\par
tities on which they depend are probabilistic.
\par
Gorelick et al. (1993) make a distinction between parameters and variables in ground-
\par
water problems. They use the term parameter for time-independent properties such as
\par
hydraulic conductivity, while the term variable is used for time-dependent indicators
\par
such as hydraulic head and contaminant concentration.
