\par
It is important to notice the difference between terms like error and uncertainty, al-
\par
though they often are used as synonyms. Terms like uncertainty, reliability, confidence
\par
and risk are probability-related and refer to \'a priori conditions, i.e. before an event.
\par
Probability is related to a statistical confidence before an event (Myers, 1997). Thus, an
\par
estimate is only a rational ``guess'' of the outcome. Error, on the other hand, can only be
\par
measured \'a posteriori, i.e. after an event has occurred. It is not possible to know what
\par
the errors will be before the event has occurred. Error relates to a known outcome or
\par
value and is therefore a more concrete item than the probability-related terms (Myers,
\par
1997). However, uncertainty may be present even after an event has occurred if the er-
\par
