It is not an easy task to define what uncertainty really is. The variety of types and
\par
sources of uncertainty, along with the lack of agreed terminology, can generate consid-
\par
erable confusion (Morgan and Henrion, 1990). Rowe (1994) defines uncertainty as ab-
\par
sence of information, information that may or may not be obtainable. Taylor (1993)
\par
defines uncertainty as a measure of the incompleteness of one\textasciiacute s knowledge or informa-6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 11 (96)
\par
tion about a quantity whose true value could be established with a perfect measuring
\par
device. Though not easily defined, it is important to distinguish between the different
\par
types and sources of uncertainty. Morgan and Henrion (1990) argue that probability is
\par
an appropriate way to express some of these kinds of uncertainty, but not all of them. In
\par
their view, the uncertainties should be handled differently depending on what type of
\par
quantity they refer to (see section 2.1).
\par
Rowe (1994) distinguishes four classes of uncertainty:
\par
1. 2. 3. 4. Temporal, i.e. uncertainty in future and past states
\par
Structural, i.e. uncertainty due to complexity
\par
Metrical, i.e. uncertainty in measurements
\par
Translational, i.e. uncertainty in explaining uncertain results.
\par
All four classes occur at the same time but for a specific problem one or more domi-
\par
nates.
\par
For human health risk assessments Hoffman and Hammonds (1994) refer to the Inter-
\par
national Atomic Energy Agency, who distinguishes between Type A and Type B un-
\par
certainty. Uncertainty about a deterministic quantity with respect to the assessment end
\par
point is called Type B uncertainty (example: specific individual). When the assessment
\par
end point is a distribution of actual exposures or risks, the uncertainty is of Type A (ex-
\par
ample: a group of individuals). To separate these uncertainties in a model, it is neces-
\par
sary to perform stochastic simulation in two dimensions.
\par
Lacasse and Nadim (1996) divide uncertainties associated with geotechnical problems
\par
into two categories:
\par
1. 2. aleatory (inherent or natural) uncertainties, i.e. uncertainty that cannot be reduced
\par
epistemic (due to lack of knowledge) uncertainties, i.e. uncertainty that can be re-
\par
duced
\par
This grouping excludes human error, which would fall into a third category.
