In classical statistics a frequentistic view is applied. This implies that a probability dis-
\par
tribution can only be created by the collection of data. If no data exists there will be no
\par
way of quantifying the uncertainty (the uncertainty is infinite), and it will make no sense
\par
defining a probability distribution. As more and more data are collected, the confidence
\par
in calculated distribution parameters increases. Two of the most important probability
\par
distributions are the 1RUPDO and the /RJQRUPDO distribution.
\par
Many of the tools used in classical statistics assume normally distributed data, which
\par
makes calculations relatively simple compared to if this assumption is not employed.
\par
This can be a problem, since contaminant concentrations in soil are inherently lognor-
\par
mally distributed (Ball and Hahn, 1998). The methods of classical statistics will not be6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 15 (96)
\par
discussed further since they can be found in any textbook on the subject. An excellent
\par
presentation of classical statistical methods for environmental sampling problems can be
\par
found in Gilbert (1987). U.S. EPA (2000b) describes a large number of statistical meas-
\par
ures, plots and statistical tests for environmental sampling.
\par
Thompson and Ramsey (1995) note that uncertainty can be expressed as a standard de-
\par
viation (the standard uncertainty) or as a half-range (the expanded uncertainty). The
\par
latter is obtained by multiplying the standard uncertainty by a coverage factor, normally
\par
between 2 and 3.
\par
If few data exist it may be problematic to use classical statistics to estimate the uncer-
\par
tainty.
\par
Lacasse and Nadim (1996) mention that VKRUWFXWHVWLPDWHV can be used in these cases.
\par
This is a method to estimate the standard deviation for limited data sets of symmetrical
\par
data. Ball and Hahn (1998) also address the issue of small data sets, but for environ-
\par
mental problems, and a small literature review on the subject is presented. They con-
\par
clude that the issue of small data sets is not addressed directly in current textbooks on
\par
statistics for environmental problems.
\par
Myers (1997) concludes that care should be used when applying classical statistical
\par
models to spatial data since classical statistics assumes uncorrelated data while spatial
\par
data often is correlated.
