\par
In Bayesian statistics Bayes\textasciiacute{} theorem is employed to determine SRVWHULRU probability
\par
distributions of a variable by combining SULRUL information with observed data (Vose,
\par
1996) (a more formally correct term for probability distributions is probability density
\par
functions, PDFs). In other words, the prior information is updated with additional data,
\par
e.g. from sampling, to reduce the uncertainty. Sometimes an analysis is performed when
\par
prior information is available but before additional information has been collected. This
\par
stage is called SUHSRVWHULRU analysis (Freeze et al., 1992). To further reduce the uncer-
\par
tainty another sampling round can be performed. The posterior estimate now becomes6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 16 (96)
\par
the prior estimate, which can be used together with the new data to produce another
\par
posterior estimate with less uncertainty. This step can be repeated several times.
\par
McLaughlin et al. (1993) calls this procedure ``sequential updating''.
\par
As formulated above, Bayes\textasciiacute{} theorem is relatively simple but when applied to continu-
\par
ously distribution functions the mathematics can become laborious. Therefore, risk
\par
analysts appear to split into two camps: those who use Bayes\textasciiacute{} theorem extensively and
\par
those who do not (Vose, 1996).
