\par
There is an important aspect of Bayesian statistics that often is used even if calculation
\par
with Bayes\textasciiacute{} theorem is not performed. In contrary to classical statistics it is possible to
\par
define probability density functions (PDFs) although no ``hard data'' exists. Subjective
\par
PDFs can be used to characterise an individual's belief about the value of a parameter
\par
(Hammitt, 1995). Such SULRUGLVWULEXWLRQV should reflect the prior knowledge before
\par
measurements have been made, something that is not possible in classical statistics.
\par
Therefore, the difference between classical and Bayesian statistics is more or less philo-
\par
sophical since the mathematics are the same. However, some authors, e.g. Rowe (1994),
\par
argue that probability distributions should only be used to address future temporal un-
\par
certainty since probability does not exist in the past, and that they are improperly used
\par
otherwise.
\par
Prior distributions are defined at early stages in a project when measurements are
\par
scarce. If sufficient KDUGGDWD is available it is relatively easy to define a probability
\par
distribution by classical statistical methods. +DUGGDWD are direct measurements or ob-
\par
servations (Koltermann and Gorelick, 1996). If measurements or similar hard data is not
\par
available, so called VRIWGDWD can be used to define a prior distribution. The soft data are
\par
indirect information such as historical information, expert opinions, professional experi-
\par
ence and all other information that may be difficult to quantify. Freeze et al. (1992) and
\par
Johnson (1996) also refer to indirect measurements as soft data, such as geophysical
\par
measurements.
\par
A distribution based on soft data is to some extent subjective or judgmental but should
\par
contain the uncertainty associated with the information. Judgmental approaches to gen-
\par
erate probability distributions can be formal or informal in nature (Taylor, 1993). For-
\par
mal methods are to be preferred. Several formal methods to assign subjective probabili-
\par
ties and construct subjective probability distributions exist. A review of these methods
\par
is given by Olsson (2000). Vose (1996) also discusses how distributions are defined
\par
from expert opinion and the sources of error in subjective estimation. Many procedures
\par
for combining information from multiple sources to construct prior distributions have
\par
been proposed but none are clearly superior (Hammitt and Shlyakhter, 1999). It is bene-
\par
ficial if objective and subjective information can be considered together when distribu-
\par
tions are developed (Taylor, 1993).
\par
Examples of probability distributions can be found in many textbooks on probability
\par
theory. Typical examples of distributions constructed from soft data are the QRQ
\par
SDUDPHWULFGLVWULEXWLRQV, which include the Cumulative, Discrete, Histogram, General,
\par
Triangular, and Uniform (Rectangular) distributions (Vose, 1996). Common SDUDPHWULF
\par
GLVWULEXWLRQV include the Exponential, Normal (Gaussian) and Lognormal distributions.6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 17 (96)
\par
Vose (1996) argues that parametric distributions based on subjective information should
\par
be used with caution.
\par
The shape of the prior distribution is very important since it reflects the current infor-
\par
mation. However, there is a well-documented tendency of both experts and lay people
\par
to underestimate uncertainty in their knowledge of quantitative information (Hammitt,
\par
1995; Hammitt and Shlyakhter, 1999). This will often result in a prior distribution that
\par
is too narrow (Taylor, 1993). It has been concluded that empirical distributions of
\par
measurement and forecast errors have much longer tails than can be described by a
\par
Normal distribution (Hammitt and Shlyakhter, 1999). Hammitt (1995) suggests that the
\par
tendency for overconfidence most be taken into account when the prior distribution is
\par
estimated. Some researchers recommend that the uncertainty should be increased delib-
\par
erately to combat this bias (Taylor, 1993).
\par
In Bayesian statistics all uncertain quantities are often assigned probability distributions.
\par
This makes analytical calculations troublesome, and often even impossible. Therefore,
\par
different types of techniques for propagation and simulation of uncertainty has been
\par
developed. Morgan and Henrion (1990) provide an introduction and review of the prin-
\par
cipal techniques. A common name for many of these techniques is VWRFKDVWLFVLPXODWLRQ
\par
(Koltermann and Gorelick, 1996). Some of these include Monte Carlo simulation (U.S.
\par
EPA, 1997b; Vose, 1996), Monte Carlo simulation with Latin Hypercube sampling
\par
(Vose, 1996) and first-order second-moment (FOSM) (James and Oldenburg, 1997).
\par
The latter is less computationally intensive than Monte Carlo simulation. A simpler
\par
simulation technique that requires even less computational capacity is the Point Esti-
\par
mate Method (Al\'en, 1998; Harr, 1987).
\par
Hoffman and Hammonds (1994) and Taylor (1993) argue that it under certain circum-
\par
stances may be useful to separate uncertainty (due to incomplete knowledge) and vari-
\par
ability (due to natural variation) when Monte Carlo simulation is performed. One such
\par
example can be simulations in human health risk assessments.
