The DQO process (U.S. EPA, 1994) is a planning approach to develop sampling de-
\par
signs for data collection activities that supports decision-making. The system should be
\par
used on a project level but it is not mandatory to follow the DQO in the U.S. EPA qual-
\par
ity system. The process is iterative. The DQO enables evaluation of two opposing con-
\par
ditions, such as if a site should be regarded as contaminated or not. The outcome of the
\par
DQO process is a sampling design for collecting data, including the number of samples
\par
to collect, where and how to collect them, and acceptable limits on the probability of
\par
decision errors. The DQO process can be summarised as (Robbat, 1996):
\par
1. State the problem
\par
2. Identify the decision to be made
\par
3. Identify the inputs to the decision
\par
4. Define the boundaries of the study
\par
5. Develop decision rules
\par
6. Specify tolerable limits on decision errors
\par
7. Optimise the conceptual model
\par
In step 5, the choice of a statistical decision rule is encouraged. Two types of decision
\par
errors are considered in step 6; false acceptance and false rejection. In this step the Total
\par
Study Error is broken down into sampling design errors and measurement errors ac-
\par
cording to
\par
Figure 5.
