In VLPSOHUDQGRPVDPSOLQJ (or strict random sampling) the sample position is located
\par
randomly, i.e. all personal bias relating to landscape position is avoided and each loca-
\par
tion has an equal probability of selection (Figure 6a). Simple random sampling is ap-
\par
propriate for estimating means and totals for the entire population, but only if the popu-
\par
lation does not contain major trends (Gilbert, 1987). For small ({$<$} 0.5 ha) and uniformly
\par
affected sites, as few as five to ten samples may be sufficient, while larger sites may
\par
need up to 25 samples. If a sufficient confidence level cannot be achieved with 25 sam-
\par
ples, the site should be divided into less variable units (Cr\'epin and Johnson, 1993). It is
\par
important to note that random sampling is not equivalent to picking sampling locations
\par
haphazardly (Gilbert, 1987). A major problem with simple random sampling is that
\par
clusters tend to arise, which can lead to non-representative sampling (Myers, 1997).
\par
This can be avoided if systematic random sampling is used (see below).6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 34 (96)
\par
In VWUDWLILHGUDQGRPVDPSOLQJ the site is divided into a number of non-overlapping parts
\par
or subareas called strata, and within each strata sampling positions are located randomly
\par
(Figure 6b). The site can be divided into different strata based on expected differences
\par
in contamination levels (Huesemann, 1994) or on geological grounds. The method of
\par
stratified sampling can be used to (1) make statements about each strata separately, or
\par
(2) to increase the precision of population estimates by distributing the samples over the
\par
entire area. An advantage of the stratified random sampling is that the increased homo-
\par
geneity in each strata, increases sampling precision. Stratified sampling also makes it
\par
possible to apply a number of statistical techniques (Cr\'epin and Johnson, 1993).
\par
In PXOWLVWDJHVDPSOLQJ the population is divided into primary units and a set of primary
\par
units is selected by simple random sampling (Gilbert, 1987). Then, each sample is ran-
\par
domly subsampled (two-stage sampling). An example of two-stage sampling in con-
\par
taminated land investigations is the random collection of soil samples, followed by ran-
\par
dom selection of aliquots (test portions) from each soil sample.
\par
Another definition of multistage sampling is when an area is sampled several times with
\par
data evaluation performed between each stage. This strategy is similar to the adaptive
\par
sampling strategy. It results in better spatial definition of contaminants for a given sam-
\par
ple size (Ferguson, 1993). If two-stage sampling is performed, about half of the total
\par
number of samples should be assigned to each stage, as a rule of thumb (Ferguson,
\par
1993). A more formal solution can be derived by Bayesian statistics.
\par
\&OXVWHUVDPSOLQJ is where a cluster of individual units are chosen at random, and all
\par
units in the cluster are measured (Gilbert, 1987). This sampling design is of little inter-
\par
est at contaminated sites and will not be discussed further.
