Borgman et al. (1996b) distinguishes between two types of sampling theories:
\par
1. design-based sampling (classical finite sampling theory), and
\par
2. model-based sampling
\par
The main distinction between the two is that in model-based sampling a model is used
\par
to account for patterns of variability within the population. One example of the model-
\par
based approach is when a geostatistical model is used to design the sampling. Model-
\par
based sampling is usually more effective than design-based sampling because it makes
\par
more complete use of information about the population. In design-based (classical)
\par
sampling no assumption of the underlying population is made, which makes it funda-
\par
mentally objective (Borgman et al., 1996b). The theories to determine mean concentra-
\par
tions (section 4.9) and to detect hot spots (section 4.10) are examples of design-based
\par
sampling.
\par
A third sampling theory is available, except from the two mentioned: The particulate
\par
sampling theory of Pierre Gy. This theory will be described below.
