Although several articles and books about sampling have been written there is only one
\par
sampling theory that can be regarded as general and comprehensive. This sampling the-
\par
ory was developed by the French mining-engineer Pierre Gy in the 1960s, 1970s and
\par
1980s for the mining industry. The theory is applicable to the sampling of particulate
\par
materials and fluids (Borgman et al., 1996b). Other theories only cover small parts of
\par
the sampling problem but Gy\textasciiacute s theory is the only theory of sampling of particulate ma-
\par
terial that is accepted and undisputed world-wide (Pitard, 1993). However, it is not evi-
\par
dent that the theory is applicable to all aspects of environmental sampling, for example
\par
sampling of volatile organic compounds (VOC) (see section 4.7.7).
\par
The theory has connections with both classical statistics and geostatistics (Borgman et
\par
al., 1996b). Geostatistical aspects of Gy\textasciiacute s theory are addressed by Flatman and Yfantis
\par
(1996) and Myers (1997). A improvement upon classical statistics is that the various
\par
error sources are directly addressed. The separation of error sources is an important as-
\par
pect because some errors may represent bias, not simple random variation (Borgman et
\par
al., 1996b). The various components of variance of this sampling theory sound trivially
\par
obvious when pointed out, but they are easily overlooked (Flatman and Yfantis, 1996).
\par
Also, sampling theory shows that seemingly harmless assumptions may result in sub-
\par
stantial sampling errors (Myers, 1997).
\par
Gy has presented his work in a number of French and English publications but the high
\par
complexity has restricted the use of his sampling theory by engineers and scientists. A
\par
more accessible presentation of the theory is presented by Pitard (1993). Shorter pres-6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 36 (96)
\par
entations of the theory, with the focus on environmental sampling, are available in for
\par
example Myers (1997), Shefsky (1997), Borgman et al. (1996b), Flatman and Yfantis
\par
(1996), and Mason (1992).
