\par
The fundamental error is an important error because it still remains when a sampling
\par
operation is perfect. Since the fundamental error affects the measured concentration it is
\par
critical to manage this error, and it is the only error that never cancels out. It is also the
\par
only error that can be assessed independently of the sampling method (Borgman et al.,
\par
1996b).
\par
The fundamental error is a statistical consequence of the particulate nature of geologic
\par
samples (Shefsky, 1997), see also the Representative Elementary Volume in Figure 8.
\par
As defined by Gy, the fundamental error is the relative error in estimating the grade
\par
(grade of ore may be analogous to the percentage of some chemical present) (Borgman
\par
et al., 1996b). It is caused by the range of particle sizes in the medium and the fact that
\par
often only certain sized particles contain the pollutant of interest (Flatman and Yfantis,
\par
1996). Also, the contaminant itself can exist as particles such as lead shots or paint
\par
chips, which may result in large fundamental errors as illustrated by Shefsky (1997). On
\par
the other hand, if all the contaminant particles are very small, the fundamental error will
\par
also be small. Pitard (1993) provides equations and sampling nomographs for quantifi-
\par
cation of the fundamental error as a function of some physical properties. The most im-
\par
portant of these properties is the maximum particle size. Mason (1992) provides a prac-
\par
tical guide for determining the maximum and minimum variance of the fundamental
\par
error. A statistical basis of the fundamental error, with illustrative explanations, is pre-
\par
sented by Shefsky (1997). He also provides practical advice on how to avoid or reduce
\par
fundamental error and bias in soil sampling.
\par
It is important to note that a fundamental error is introduced at each stage of sampling.
\par
Therefore, repeated subsampling leads to accumulation of fundamental errors, which
\par
quickly can exceed 50-100 \% (Myers, 1997). Because only a small mass of soil usually
\par
is used in laboratory analysis, the stage of subsampling for the analytical sample is the
\par
