An important concept in the particulate sampling theory is the dimension of the lot to be
\par
sampled. Lots can be classified as zero-, one-, two- or three-dimensional lots (Pitard,
\par
1993). One example of a ]HURGLPHQVLRQDOORW is randomly distributed coloured balls in
\par
a jar, i.e. a lot that is considered a statistical population. A RQHGLPHQVLRQDOORW could be
\par
a stream of water or material on a conveyor belt. The one-dimensional lot always has a
\par
linear or chronological behaviour (Pitard, 1993). An example of a WZRGLPHQVLRQDOORW is
\par
a piece of flat contaminated land where the depth of the lot is small compared to the
\par
areal extent. 7KUHHGLPHQVLRQDOORWVare for example piles of material, truckloads, or soil
\par
units where the thickness is not negligible.
\par
It is possible to perform statistically correct sampling only from zero- and one-
\par
dimensional lots. Acceptable sampling is possible for two-dimensional lots under fa-
\par
vourable conditions but it is practically impossible to collect correct samples for three-
\par
dimensional lots, except in liquids (Myers, 1997). However, it is always possible to di-
\par
vide a three-dimensional lot into a series of two-dimensional strata.
\par
Derived equations in the sampling theory (Myers, 1997; Pitard, 1993) are primarily ap-
\par
plicable to zero- and one-dimensional lots. Two- and three-dimensional lots require
\par
geostatistical techniques.
