The process of compositing is a physical averaging of the material used to form the
\par
composite sample. Samples are taken from different locations and then mixed to in-6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 44 (96)
\par
crease sample support (Shefsky, 1997). The measured value for a composite sample
\par
should be similar to the arithmetic mean of the measured values from the individual
\par
samples forming the composite sample (Fabrizio et al., 1995). For bulk populations,
\par
such as soil and water, Gilbert (1987) provides statistical methods for composite sam-
\par
pling. Fabrizio et al. (1995) describe procedures for formation of composite samples
\par
from segmented populations, i.e. populations with identifiable units such as fish etc.
\par
G. Lovision et al. identified five stages of composite sampling (Fabrizio et al., 1995):
\par
1. identification of the target population
\par
2. statistical sampling
\par
3. compositing (the actual formation of composite samples)
\par
4. subsampling
\par
5. measurement
\par
Compositing is an effective way to reduce intersample variance caused by the heteroge-
\par
neous distribution of contaminants (Jenkins et al., 1996). Another advantage of compo-
\par
siting is the cost-savings since only the composite sample need to be analysed. Myers
\par
(1997) points out that composite samples may save money but that they effectively wipe
\par
out any understanding of the long-range heterogeneity. They may be useful for calcu-
\par
lating average concentrations but not for spatial mapping of contaminants. Gilbert
\par
(1987) and Garner et al. (1996) provide equations for cost-effective strategies of de-
\par
tecting hot spots using composite sampling. U.S. EPA (2000a) provides practical infor-
\par
mation about composite sampling, as well as equations.
\par
A problem when composite samples are used to estimate the mean concentration is that
\par
a certain amount of homogeneity must be assumed, otherwise the result cannot be
\par
trusted. Naturv\aa rdsverket (1997) present a rule of thumb of when homogeneity in the
\par
soil can be assumed (regarding contaminant concentrations). If the coefficient of varia-
\par
tion (CV = standard deviation/mean) is less than 1, composite samples can generally be
\par
used.
