Most studies of spatial variability are performed at scales with sampling points ranging
\par
from meters to hundreds of meters apart. In contrast, Schumacher and Minnich (2000)
\par
studied the extreme short-range variability in VOC-contaminated soil. They found that
\par
soil properties (TCO, sand content and clay content) and contaminant concentrations
\par
generally varied between 1 and 4 times at a distance of 15 cm (vertical direction). How-
\par
ever, extreme cases with concentration differences up to 43 times were noted. Their
\par
findings are closely related to the concept of sample support, as described in section
\par
4.7.5.
