In every site-investigation the question arises of how many samples to collect and how
\par
certain the result will be. The answer to these questions depends on the objectives of the
\par
investigation, the criteria that must be met (confidence levels etc.) and how the sam-
\par
pling strategy is designed. Here, the question of the required number of samples will be
\par
addressed for the objective to calculate mean concentration.
\par
For economic reasons it is often necessary to limit the number of collected samples.
\par
However, there is no empirical procedure to decide the minimum sampling effort that
\par
should be performed in advance, at least not in waste material (Pitard, 1993). To deter-
\par
mine the required number of samples a decision must be made whether or not the sam-
\par
ples should be regarded as spatially correlated or not, i.e. if the variables are UDQGRP or
\par
VSDWLDO variables. Correlation is a statistical measurement of the fact that samples taken
\par
close together are more similar in value than samples taken further apart (Flatman and
\par
Yfantis, 1996). Random variables are field samples that are further apart than the range
\par
of correlation, and spatial variables are field samples closer together than the range of
\par
correlation (Flatman and Yfantis, 1996).
\par
