Some authors have studied how correlated data affect the required number of samples.
\par
Gilbert (1987) provides equations and examples on how time and space correlated data
\par
affect the required number of samples. A conclusion is that if sample units are posi-
\par
tively correlated, and this correlation is not taken into account, the required number of
\par
samples will be greater than if no correlation exists (the opposite is true for negative
\par
correlation). The reason for this is that each sample unit contains less information when
\par
sample units are correlated (Gilbert, 1987). On the other hand, when Webster and Bur-
\par
gess (1983) took the correlation into account with geostatistical techniques they found
\par
that the required number of samples was much smaller than if correlation was ignored.
\par
This is because the correlation contains information that can be handled by geostatistics.
\par
Myers (1997) presents the concept of decline curves to determine the number of sam-
\par
ples at a site. A decline curve is a graph showing how the statistical error (mean estima-
\par
tion) declines when the number of sample increases. The statistical error is plotted on
\par
the y-axis and the number of samples on the x-axis. Each sampling program has its own
\par
decline curve. Kriging can be used to develop a decline curve for a particular site and
\par
sampling program.
\par
Chang et al. (1998) have demonstrated the use of semivariograms, kriging and cokriging
\par
techniques to determine the minimum number of samples for reliable concentration es-
\par
timates. In a case study, 28 samples where determined to be a minimum. They con-
\par
ducted duplicate analyses on 10 \% of the samples and used certified reference materials
\par
to verify the analytical procedure. The kriging/cokriging approach was compared to the
\par
robust ANOVA technique (see section 4.9.4) and the conclusion was that the latter re-
\par
quires more sample duplicates to achieve a reliable measure of the sampling precision if
\par
spatial anisotropy exists.
