(Ferguson, 1993). Frequently asked questions when searching for hot spots are (Myers,
\par
1997):
\par
What grid spacing is needed to locate a hot spot with a specified confidence?6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 53 (96)
\par
For a given grid spacing, what is the probability of hitting a hot spot of a specified
\par
size?
\par
What is the probability that a hot spot exists when no hot spots were found?
\par
Gilbert (1987) addresses these questions. He presents procedures to determine grid
\par
spacing, the size of a hot spot likely to be hit, the probability of not hitting a hot spot,
\par
how prior information can be taken into account, and the probability that a hot spot ex-
\par
ists when none has been found. A number of nomographs for different cases are pre-
\par
sented. An important design parameter for the sampling plan is the critical hot spot size,
\par
i.e. the largest hot spot that may go undetected.
\par
Drew (1979) evaluated different drilling patterns when searching for elliptical shaped
\par
targets. Rhombic, rectangular, triangular, and square patterns were evaluated. Situations
\par
when particular patterns are more efficient than others were identified. It was found that
\par
the optimum hole spacing is a function of (1) drilling cost, (2) the value of a target, (3)
\par
the target shape, and (4) the target occurrence probability. The optimum hole spacing
\par
corresponds to the maximum of a gross drilling return (GDR) function. Different cases
\par
are considered; single target, multiple targets, and the case when target occurrence is
\par
uncertain. Also, a multistage pattern-drilling scheme was proposed, where prior target
\par
occurrence probabilities are updated using Bayes\textasciiacute{} rule.
\par
Ferguson (1992) presents a sampling grid, the herringbone, that is demonstrated to be
\par
better than the square grid in detecting hot spots, especially elongated hot spots. He also
\par
demonstrates the effect of target shape, target size, target orientation, and the number of
\par
existing targets, on the required number of samples to locate targets. Equations and no-
\par
mographs are provided for several cases. In his presentation it is assumed that all parts
\par
of a site have equal probability of containing a hot spot, i.e. prior information is not
\par
taken into account. The drawback of this assumption is that the required number of
\par
samples may become very large.
\par
In addition to the equiprobable sampling design discussed above, Ferguson and Abbachi
\par
(1993) demonstrates how expert judgement about likely hot spot locations can be incor-
\par
porated in the sampling design. One approach is to partition the site into subareas and
\par
then assign scores to each subarea, reflecting the strength of belief as to where the target
\par
is likely to be. Fewer samples is required from subareas with lower scores (lower ex-
\par
pectation that a hot spot exist in the area) to achieve a given success probability. By a
\par
provided nomograph it is possible to determine the probability that a hot spot does not
\par
exist when no hot spot was located, given specified priori probabilities that a hot spot
\par
exists. In this way it is possible to determine if the sampling was successful or not.
\par
Ferguson and Abbachi (1993) recommend multistage sampling so that geostatistical
\par
