A number of uncertainties are involved when a sampling activity is planned. Most of
\par
these uncertainties are discussed elsewhere in this chapter. Here, two important uncer-
\par
tainties related to the planning of laboratory analyses will be described briefly. The first
\par
one is whether or not the relevant chemical substances are analysed at all. The risk as-
\par
sessment of a site is restricted to the substances that has been analysed, but there may
\par
well be other substances of great concern present. Few authors have addressed this un-
\par
certainty specifically. This question, and the one mentioned below, is closely related to
\par
the uncertainty in risk assessment. However, the risk assessment issue is not addressed
\par
in this report.
\par
A second uncertainty associated with laboratory analyses can be illustrated by the fol-
\par
lowing question: Is the selected analysis method the right type of analysis for the con-
\par
taminant? Even if a standardised analysis method is used, the result may not be applica-
\par
ble, e.g. for risk assessment purposes, because guideline values may have been devel-
\par
oped based on a different analysis method. An example of this uncertainty is whether or
\par
not the result from a certain leaching test is applicable on a particular contaminated soil
\par
