Traditionally, the uncertainty in analytical techniques has been the focus of research.
\par
However, as have been previously described, the analytical error constitutes only one
\par
part of the overall error.
\par
Ramsey and Argyraki (1997) distinguish two ways of estimating the uncertainty in
\par
chemical analysis: The ``bottom up'' approach and the ``top down'' approach. Both ap-
\par
proaches have their advantages and drawbacks. In the ``bottom up'' approach the random
\par
error from each procedural step of a laboratory method is quantified separately as a
\par
standard deviation (VL), and the corresponding variance (VL ) is calculated. The overall
\par
analytical uncertainty (VD ) is quantified by summing the variances for all procedural
\par
steps of the method (Huesemann, 1994):
