Additional errors can be introduced when data are analysed and evaluated. Van Ee et al.
\par
(1990) identify a problem when data are analysed: whether a transformation of data is
\par
needed to stabilise the variance or not. The dependence of measurement error variances
\par
on sample concentration is frequently encountered (van Ee et al., 1990).
\par
Myers (1997) describes two types of errors in data evaluation; HVWLPDWLRQHUURU and PLV
\par
FODVVLILFDWLRQHUURU. He illustrates the errors with a simple example: Consider a block
\par
area where the true concentration is 22 ppm and where a threshold for remediation of 25
\par
ppm has been determined. If the concentration is estimated to be 29 ppm the HVWLPDWLRQ
\par
HUURU equals 7 ppm. In this case there is also a PLVFODVVLILFDWLRQHUURU since the esti-
\par
mated concentration exceeds the threshold concentration while the true concentration is
\par
below the threshold. With the baseline condition as a null hypothesis, two types of mis-
\par
classification errors can occur (Myers, 1997; U.S. EPA, 1994):
\par
1. 2. Type I Error: False rejection decision error (false positive or overestimation)
\par
Type II Error: False acceptance decision error (false negative or underestimation)
\par
In the example above there is a Type I Error because the concentration is falsely esti-
\par
mated to exceed the threshold. If the true concentration is above the threshold while the
\par
estimated concentration is below there would be a Type II Error. Type I Errors lead to
\par
excessive remediation costs while Type II Errors can bring human health and ecological
\par
risks. Therefore, Type II Errors are sometimes called \&RQVXPHU\P V5LVN (Gilbert, 1987).
