During a data collection program there are two primary issues of concern (James and
\par
Gorelick, 1994):
\par
1. 2. where to take the next sample, and
\par
when to cease sampling (a stopping rule).
\par
Closely connected to the latter question is the concept of GDWDZRUWK, i.e. the worth of
\par
additional information. How the concept of data worth is defined depends on the phi-
\par
losophy of the decision-maker. In a decision analysis framework, the purpose is often to
\par
find a cost-efficient solution to a problem (e.g. to maximise an objective function). In a
\par
remediation project, data collection such as sampling, will continue until a VWRSSLQJUXOH
\par
terminates the sampling. Different decision frameworks will have different stopping
\par
rules, and therefore the worth of data can be defined differently. One common definition
\par
of data worth is that it equals the reduction in project cost (or risk) that the new data will
\par
result in. Another definition is that additional data only has value if it can alter the
\par
choice of decision to make (Hammitt, 1995).
\par
In section 6.4 some of the most common or most interesting frameworks will be pre-
\par
sented. Many are based on sequential sampling or adaptive sampling, i.e. the sampling
\par
is performed in stages. This may require several visits to the contaminated site for the
\par
sampling team.
\par
