A tool with growing applications in decision analysis is the influence diagram, also
\par
called relevance diagram. Heger and White (1997) argue that influence diagrams are
\par
more effective than decision trees for data worth analysis. There are two main advan-
\par
tages of influence diagrams compared to decision trees:
\par
1. 2. The size of the influence diagram is a linear function of the number of variables,
\par
whereas the size of the decision tree is an exponential function.
\par
Influence diagrams provide a better visual representation of the relationship among
\par
variables.
\par
Another advantage is the ease of understanding for the decision-maker even for prob-
\par
lems with many variables, in contrast to decision trees that easily become difficult to
\par
understand. Other authors have also compared decision trees and influence diagrams,
\par
for example Call and Miller (Heger and White, 1997). They also describe the Decision
\par
Programming Language (DPL) as a tool that captures the useful features of both deci-
\par
sion trees and influence diagrams.
\par
An influence diagram consists of chance nodes (ovals), decision nodes (rectangles),
\par
value nodes (diamonds), and sometimes deterministic nodes. Typical functions assigned
\par
to value nodes include cost-benefit-risk or simple cost functions (Heger and White,
\par
1997). The nodes of the influence diagram are inter-connected by arcs. Two types of
\par
arcs exist; information arcs and conditional arcs. Together they show the flow of infor-
\par
mation and uncertainty through the decision-making process. Figure 13 shows an exam-
\par
ple of an influence diagram.
