In principle, this function is identical to the objective function presented by Freeze et al.
\par
(1990). The expected failure cost is the probability of failure multiplied by the cost of
\par
failure. The Smart Sampling approach requires explicit decision rules, such as whether
\par
or not a piece of ground is contaminated or uncontaminated. In this example the failures
\par
are identical to the type I and type II errors described in section 5.4:6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 71 (96)
\par
Type I error: Removal of uncontaminated soil, if not required.
\par
Type II error: Failure to remove contaminated soil, if required.
\par
Both types of failures involve a cost that is taken into account by the objective function.
\par
The Smart Sampling process is designed to be iterative. This means that samples are
\par
taken few at a time, the information is analysed, the objective function updated, and a
\par
decision to take more samples is based on predictions of the worth of additional samples
\par
towards minimising the total cost of the remediation (Kaplan, 1998). Since spatial cor-
\par
relation of contaminant concentrations usually exists between sample locations at a site
\par
(Flatman and Yfantis, 1996), geostatistics is used as a tool. The distribution of contami-
\par
nant concentrations is studied in a histogram and the spatial correlations in a variogram.
\par
Before additional samples are taken a SUHSRVWHULRUDQDO\textbackslash VLV is performed. This is carried
\par
out by geostatistical simulation, typically kriging. The purpose of the simulation is to
\par
calculate how much the probability of failure will decrease when the additional infor-
\par
mation from sampling will be supplied. Since the probability of failure will change, so
\par
will the value of the objective function. This makes it possible to calculate the worth of
\par
additional sampling before it has been undertaken. When additional sampling has been
\par
performed a new analysis will be carried out. This iterative procedure continues until
\par
additional samples will no longer reduce the objective function.
\par
The Smart Sampling approach, as presented by Kaplan (1998), develops graphical rep-
\par
resentations for decision makers, site owners, and the general public so that they may
\par
better understand the costs and consequences of achieving varying levels of cleanup at a
\par
contaminated site. Smart Sampling is developed at Sandia National Laboratories in
\par
USA. The purpose is to automate the decision framework into a user-friendly software
\par
tool.
\par
A bibliography of projects where the Smart Sampling approach has been used is avail-
\par
able at the internet link http://www.nwer.sandia.gov/sample/bibliography.html.
