An interesting but very different approach to sampling is the adaptive sampling ap-
\par
proach as described by Cox (1999). It does not require any specific statistical model for
\par
the spatial distribution of contaminants, but instead constructs an increasingly accurate
\par
non-parametric approximation to it as sampling proceeds (Cox, 1999). In other words,
\par
the system ``learns'' from data as sampling goes on. 'HFLVLRQUXOHV must first be devel-
\par
oped and a vector of decision parameters describes them. The decision rules are created
\par
for the specific problem at hand, but are typically rules that promote cost-effective sam-
\par
pling and cleanup. When the decision parameters have been determined the adaptive6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 73 (96)
\par
sampling strategy can be optimised by VLPXODWLRQRSWLPLVDWLRQ for identifying approxi-
\par
mately optimal adaptive sampling decision rules. The simulation is performed using
\par
existing sample data. In the simulation, different combinations of values for the decision
\par
parameters are used and the combinations of parameter values that meet the perform-
\par
ance criteria best are considered optimal.
\par
Cox (1999) provides the following example of the adaptive sampling approach applied
\par
to contaminated land. Four decision parameters are used:
\par
N = initial number of random samples to be taken from the area of concern. These sam-
\par
ples are in addition to already collected samples.
\par
S = number of soil samples to take from each newly sampled property (the area of con-
\par
cern is divided into a number of properties; in the example, each property is either
\par
to be remediated in its entirety or not at all).
\par
K = maximum allowed length of a ``search list''. This is a list of properties with the
\par
highest average sample concentration seen so far.
\par
T = action threshold. A property is to be remediated if, and only if, the mean concentra-
\par
tion of its S sample values exceeds T.
\par
The decision parameter vector (N, S, K, T) is used in the simulation-optimisation to find
\par
the optimal combination of values. Then, the procedure of the adaptive sampling fol-
\par
lows (a property is called IDWKRPHG when S samples have been taken from it and from
\par
each of its immediate neighbours):
\par
1. 2. 3. 4. 5. 6. Randomly sample N previously unsampled locations in the area of concern.
\par
Place the K properties with the highest mean concentrations so far on the search list.
\par
Fathom each property on the search list by taking S samples at the property and its
\par
neighbours, and remove the property from the list when it has been fathomed.
\par
Whenever a property is found with a mean concentration greater than the values for
\par
properties not included on the initial search list (step 2), place that property on the
\par
search list.
\par
Continue steps 3 and 4 until the search list is empty, i.e. until both the initial proper-
\par
ties on the list and the ones added later has been removed. This terminates the adap-
\par
tive sampling phase.
\par
Remediate those properties with a mean concentration exceeding T.
\par
Cox (1999) calls this procedure ZRUVWILUVWDGDSWLYHVHDUFK, in that the search concen-
\par
trates on properties with high soil contamination levels. He also presents a strategy for
\par
the simulation-optimisation of the decision parameter vector.
\par
The technique of adaptive sampling provides a practical approach to decide where to
\par
sample next, when to stop, and what to do to optimise an overall performance objective
\par
(Cox, 1999). The performance objective can be of different types, such as to minimise
\par
the number of misclassification errors, or expected utility. It can also be applied with
\par
risk-cost-benefit criteria.
\par
One drawback in the example presented above is that the sampling may require many
\par
visits to the area of concern due to the multi-stage nature of adaptive sampling. How-
\par
ever, it is possible to use only a few stages (e.g. a two stage sampling procedure) if the
\par
set of decision parameters is modified.6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 74 (96)
\par
Note that the term adaptive sampling can be used for sampling approaches that are very
\par
different from the one presented here, e.g. those described in section 6.4.3.
