\par
The amount of data needed to understand the behaviour of a physical system may ex-
\par
ceed the amount of data needed to make a good decision (Maddock, 1973). The physical
\par
model that uses the data may be far more complex than can be handled by the decision
\par
process. This may lead to collection of excessive data and overly sophisticated models
\par
(Maddock, 1973). Some of the models that have been encountered in this literature re-
\par
view have been quite complex, while some have been fairly simple. In many cases a
\par
simple model will supply sufficient information at a low cost. In other situations more
\par
advanced models will be needed. A conclusion is that there is a need for a methodology
\par
how to select a model of optimal complexity level. The methodology should be risk-
\par
based, taking the model uncertainty and the cost of application into account.
