Several of the different types of sampling errors presented in this report can be difficult
\par
to quantify. Practical experience of the size of each error can rarely be found in the lit-
\par
erature. Information on how to quantify these errors is appreciated. As an example, the
\par
variability of contaminant concentrations in soil around a sampling point (short range),
\par
has only been addressed in a small number of papers but the conclusions have been that
\par
this type of uncertainty can be very large. However, these conclusions are only valid for
\par
these specific studies. The short-range variability will most likely depend on the site
\par
history, the geology, and the type of contaminant present. In situations where the vari-
\par
ability is large, a single sample may randomly indicate ``small contamination'' or ``large
\par
contamination''. In this case, there will be a potential for making wrong decisions if this
\par
type of sampling error is not taken into account. Another example of sampling error is
\par
the bias that can be introduced by the sampling equipment. Generally, a methodology to
\par
handle systematic sampling errors would be appreciated.
