The question on how many samples to collect is always encountered in practice. One
\par
answer is to use data worth analysis as discussed below. Today, other methods are being
\par
used based on limited economic budgets or a desired accuracy of estimated properties.
\par
The literature on this matter is often restricted to certain types of problems, often sam-
\par
pling to estimate mean concentrations. Normally distributed data without any spatial
\par
correlation is often assumed. There is definitely a need for a review article or a publica-
\par
tion on how to determine the necessary number of samples in a structured way under
\par
different assumptions, taking for example different sampling objectives, non-normally
\par
distributed data, spatial correlation, prior knowledge, and sampling with multiple objec-
\par
tives into account. No such well-structured presentation has been found in the literature.
\par
The perfect guide to sampling is yet to be published.
\par
A common misunderstanding regarding the systematic sampling pattern requires a
\par
comment. The misunderstanding can be found among consultants and in rare cases even
\par
in the literature. Contrary to what many believe, sampling according to a systematic grid
\par
does not imply that randomness therefore is missing and that statistical or geostatistical
\par
techniques cannot be used. As long as all sampling locations have the same probability
\par
of selection (as is the case for systematic sampling if the first sampling point is selected
\par
randomly) the sampling can be regarded as random. Therefore, samples collected ac-
\par
cording to a systematic sampling pattern can very well be analysed statistically or with
\par
geostatistical techniques (provided that the grid itself is laid out randomly).
