The literature review has shown that data worth approaches have been used infrequently
\par
for three decades. There are probably a number of reasons why the concept has not been
\par
applied to real world problems more often. One reason is that the concept is relatively
\par
unknown. Another is that some methods are very computational intensive. The main
\par
reason is however that much of the previous work on data worth analysis has been quite
\par
complicated and difficult to grasp for an average consultant or researcher working in the
\par
field of contaminated land. This is especially true for the combination of data worth
\par
analysis and optimisation techniques. The complexity has restricted the practical use of6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 77 (96)
\par
the data worth approach although the potential for its application is huge. Data worth
\par
analysis could for example be applied not only contamination problems, but also to
\par
classical geotechnical problems.
\par
An exception to the complex papers discussed above is the work by James et al. (1996b)
\par
who present a stripped-down approach to data worth analysis. For broad picture analysis
\par
this kind of approach could be sufficient, for example to determine whether a sampling
\par
program is cost-effective or not. The methodology could be developed further to handle
\par
common problems encountered in contamination problems in an effective but under-
\par
standable way. For in-depth analysis more sophisticated methods will probably be
\par
needed. Here, the Bayesian-Markov chain analysis is such a method that could be ap-
\par
plied to data worth analysis of contaminated land on a relatively detailed level. Of
\par
course, more traditional geostatistic tools can also be applied, such as kriging tech-
\par
niques.
\par
Preferable, a range of methods of different complexity should be available for data
\par
worth analysis. This will make it possible to select a simple approach when needed, for
\par
example in early stages of a project. If such an analysis turns out to be insufficient, more
\par
complex methodologies could be applied.
\par
In cases when data worth analysis has been applied to sampling problems, the sampling
\par
uncertainties have often been neglected. Some of the uncertainties may have been con-
\par
sidered, such as natural variability, while others have been ignored. If a practical data
\par
worth methodology is developed, it must be able to handle the different types of uncer-
\par
tainty that evolve in a site-investigation.
\par
In the risk-based decision analysis approach a cost arises when failure occurs, i.e. when
\par
the failure criterion is met. The importance of the choice of failure criterion needs to be
\par
evaluated. How will the result of the analysis change if a different failure criterion is
\par
used? Also, the criterion for when to start remediation must be chosen properly (this is
\par
identical or closely related to the failure criterion). To begin remediation if the mean
\par
concentration exceeds an action level may not be an appropriate approach in all situa-
\par
tions. The choice to start remediation must also be coupled to the different exposure
\par
routes for humans and how the contaminants affect the environment. A basis for the
\par
choice of failure and remediation criterion should be the source-path-target concept.
\par
Therefore, the data worth analysis will also have connections with the risk assessment
\par
methodology.6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 78 (96)
