\par
Using computer software can facilitate the practical application of several methods and
\par
techniques described in the literature review. Here, some of the most common software
\par
packages will be presented in short and the focus is on public domain software or rela-
\par
tively cheap packages, although some more expensive ones have been included. The
\par
presentation shows only a selection of available programs and it is by no means com-
\par
plete. The author has used only a few of the programs and no guarantee of the capabili-
\par
ties of the different software packages can be given. Further information about the soft-
\par
ware may be found at the listed web pages. A selection of other environmental decision
\par
support software can be found in Sullivan et al. (1997).
\par
\$ 6\$03/,1*'(6,*1\$1''\$7\$(9\$/8\$7,2162)7:\$5(
\par
\$ \$66(66
\par
\$66(66 is a public domain quality assessment program from U.S. EPA. It is based on
\par
the principles described by van Ee et al. (1990) and is a tool for statistically assessing
\par
measurement errors in the collection of soil samples. The \$66(66 software is available
\par
from U.S. EPA on a CD (http://www.epa.gov/crdlvweb/databases/datahome.htm).
\par
\$ 'DWD48(67
\par
Data Quality Evaluation Statistical Toolbox ('DWD48(67 is a public domain DOS
\par
software developed by U.S. EPA for data quality assessment purposes. The program is
\par
designed to provide a quick and easy way to review data and analyse assumptions even
\par
for those not familiar with standard statistical packages (U.S. EPA, 1997a). The soft-
\par
ware and manual (U.S. EPA, 1997a) can be downloaded from U.S. EPA
\par
(http://www.epa.gov/quality/tools-prj.html).
\par
\$ '()7
\par
The Decision Error Feasibility Trials ('()7) software is public domain software devel-
\par
oped by U.S. EPA for use in the DQO process. '()7 was originally written for DOS
\par
but a beta version for Windows is now available (U.S. EPA, 2000c). The program uses
\par
hypothesis testing to calculate the required number of samples for not exceeding speci-
\par
fied probabilities for decision errors (false positive and false negative) regarding a given
\par
action level (mean concentration). No account is made for spatial correlation between
\par
samples and normally distributed data values are assumed. Three different sampling
\par
designs can be handled: simple random sampling, stratified sampling, and composite
\par
sampling. The program also calculates sampling cost. The DEFT software and manual
\par
(U.S. EPA, 2000c) can be downloaded from U.S. EPA
\par
(http://www.epa.gov/quality/tools-prj.html).6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 90 (96)
\par
\$ '42352
\par
'42352 is a public domain Windows-based software in the form of a calculator in-
\par
terface. It has three different functions: (1) to calculate the required number of samples
\par
for a specified confidence level (Success-Calc), (2) to calculate the number samples
\par
necessary to estimate an average concentration within a specified error (Enviro-Calc),
\par
and (3) to calculate the number of samples necessary to detect a hot spot (HotSpot-Calc)
\par
when no prior information is considered (Keith et al., 1996). The purpose of the soft-
\par
ware is to be a tool in data quality objectives (DQOs) and for quality control (QC). It
\par
can be used in conjunction with the commercial program 3UDFWLFDO(QYLURQPHQWDO4\&
\par
6DPSOHV (Keith et al., 1996) described below. The two programs are part of the com-
\par
mercial software package for environmental sampling and analysis, sold under the name
\par
of 3UDFWLFDO4\& (Keith et al., 1996). '42352 is available from an internet link at the
\par
American Chemical Society (http://www.instantref.com/dqopro1.htm).
\par
There appears to be a problem with the decimal point in the program, complicating its
\par
use on some computers.
\par
\$ (/,3*5,'3\&
\par
(/3*5,'3\& is a public domain DOS-program originally developed by D. A. Singer
\par
for locating geological deposits (Gilbert, 1987). The purpose of the current version is to
\par
search for hot spots of contaminants. The program calculates the probability of detect-
\par
ing a hot spot, sampling cost and the required size of the sampling grid. The theoretical
\par
basis for ELPGRID is summarised in Gilbert (1987). (/3*5,'3\& can be downloaded
\par
from Oak Ridge National Laboratory (http://homer.hsr.ornl.gov/ets/elipgrid.html).
\par
\$ 3UDFWLFDO(QYLURQPHQWDO4\&6DPSOHV
\par
3UDFWLFDO(QYLURQPHQWDO4\&6DPSOHV (Keith et al., 1996) is a low cost expert system
\par
that provides answers, explanations, and definitions for selecting quality control (QC)
\par
samples. It covers 15 types of QC samples, such as field blanks, trip blanks, equipment
\par
blanks, local control site background samples, replicate analyses, replicate samples etc.
\par
The software is available from Instant Reference Resources, Inc.
\par
(http://www2.outer.net/iref/pracqc.htm).
\par
\$ 5REXVW\$129\$
\par
Ramsey (1998) describes a simple, non-comercial DOS-software for the statistical
\par
method Robust ANOVA. It can be used to separate different types of variability in the
\par
measurement process. The DOS executable file is called ROBCOOP4 and is available
\par
at the Journal of Analytical Atomic Spectrometry website
\par
(http://www.rsc.org/suppdata/ja/1998/97/).
\par
\$ 6LP6LWH
\par
6LP6LWH is a public domain software for Windows 95. The software simulates a site-
\par
investigation to evaluate the need for remediation. The purpose of the software is edu-
\par
cational and to develop site characterisation strategies. The program was developed by6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 91 (96)
\par
the University of Texas at Austin
\par
(http://www.ce.utexas.edu/prof/gilbert/public\_html/SimSite.html).
\par
\$ 6LWH\$66(66
\par
6LWH\$66(66 (Assessment of Sampling Strategies Expert Support System) (Nathanail et
\par
al., 1998; Tucker et al., 1996) is a computer decision support system developed under
\par
contract for the UK Department of the Environment. Its purpose is to optimise sampling
\par
strategies for locating contamination hot spots and is centred on expert knowledge. It
\par
allows soft information such as prior knowledge acquired during a desk study to be in-
\par
corporated by Bayesian statistics.
\par
Prior information from maps, archive data on previous site use, interviews with former
\par
workers or residents, visible evidence of contamination, geophysical measurements etc.
\par
are incorporated in the analysis by a scoring system. The cells in an information grid
\par
covering the site are assigned scores depending on the prior information. The scores are
\par
converted to DSULRUL probabilities. The number of samples required is expressed as a
\par
function of hot spot size and the area of the site. The sampling locations are generated
\par
by an optimisation technique, taking the prior information into account.
\par
Site-ASSESS also allows the number of samples to be expressed as a function of hot
\par
spot size. The program makes it possible to compare sampling strategies designed on
\par
the basis of prior information with strategies based on the assumption of equiprobabil-
\par
ity. Unfortunately, Site-ASSESS is not available on a commercial basis (year 1999).
\par
\$ 9LVXDO6DPSOH3ODQ
\par
9LVXDO6DPSOH3ODQ (VSP) is a new software for the planning of sampling activities.
\par
VSP has a graphic environment and calculates the number of samples to be collected,
\par
their location, and total sampling cost for a given sampling objective. The algorithms
\par
for detection of hot spots are the same as in ELIPGRID-PC. A free beta version of VSP
\par
is available from Oak Ridge National Laboratory
\par
(http://etd.pnl.gov:2080/DQO/software/vsp/vspbeta.html).
\par
\$ *(267\$7,67,\&\$/62)7:\$5(
\par
\$ ,QWURGXFWLRQ
\par
A multitude of different geostatistical software packages exists on the market. The ones
\par
presented here is only a subjective selection. A number of other geostatistical programs
\par
can be found at The Central Server for GIS \& Spatial Statistics on the Internet (www.ai-
\par
geostats.org).
\par
\$ '5,//*8,'(
\par
'5,//*8,'( is a commercial software for geostatistical analysis by kriging. The
\par
software differs from many other geostatistical packages in that the variogram analysis
\par
is performed in the form of expert system algorithms. The variogram is multi-
\par
dimensional, taking the trends in data in different directions into account. '5,//6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 92 (96)
\par
*8,'( is part of the Environmental Visualization System (EVS) and is sold by C Tech
\par
Development Corporation (http://www.ctech.com).
\par
\$ (\&266(
\par
(\&266( (Ecological Spatial Statistical Evaluation) is a Windows 95 product with 2D
\par
tools such as basic statistic analysis, variogram analysis, kriging and conditional simu-
\par
lation. It is available from Geostokos (Ecosse) Limited in Scotland
\par
(http://www.geoecosse.com).
\par
\$ *(2(\$6
\par
In 1987, U.S. EPA released the public domain software *(2(\$6 (Geostatistical Envi-
\par
ronmental Assessment Software) for geostatistical analysis of spatially correlated data
\par
(Englund and Sparks, 1991). It is probably the most widely used geostatistical software
\par
for environmental applications, despite its limitations. It is DOS-based (UNIX and Li-
\par
nux-versions also exist) and offers many useful geostatistical features such as basic sta-
\par
tistics, scatter plots/linear regression, variogram computation and different options for
\par
kriging. Unfortunately, *(2(\$6 has not been upgraded since 1991. There are some
\par
peculiar errors in the program which may complicate the use at times. *(2(\$6 is
\par
available from U.S. EPA (http://www.epa.gov/ada/geoeas.html).
\par
\$ *(23\$\&.
\par
Another public domain software from U.S. EPA is *(23\$\&. (Yates and Yates, 1990).
\par
It is a geostatistical software system written for users with little experience in geostatis-
\par
tical techniques but with the requirements to also satisfy more advanced users. It is a
\par
menu-driven DOS-program with tools for basic statistics, variogram analysis, linear
\par
estimation (different kriging options) and nonlinear estimation. GEOPACK can be
\par
downloaded from U.S. EPA (http://www.epa.gov/ada/geopack.html).
\par
\$ *HRVWDWLVWLFDO7RROER[
\par
The *HRVWDWLVWLFDO7RROER[ is a public domain DOS package from 1990 developed by
\par
Roland Froidevaux. It is one of the few geostatistical freewares with 1D, 2D and 3D
\par
tools and it contains several modules, e.g. univariate and bivariate statistics, variogram
\par
analysis and kriging. *HRVWDWLVWLFDO7RROER[ is available from the same resource as
\par
9DULR:LQ (manual included).
\par
\$ *6/,\%
\par
Another public domain geostatistical software package is *6/,\% (Geostatistical Soft-
\par
ware Library). It is a collection of geostatistical programs developed at Stanford Uni-
\par
versity (Deutsch and Journel, 1998). *6/,\% is a 3D package geared to professional us-
\par
ers and runs under DOS (Linux-versions are also available). *6/,\% offers advanced
\par
types of geostatistical analysis, such as cokriging, universal kriging, and conditional
\par
simulation. No manual is included in the public domain version of *6/,\%. The software
\par
is available at the *6/,\% web-site (http://www.gslib.com).6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 93 (96)
\par
The original *6/,\% inspired the writing of the well-known geostatistic book *6/,\%
\par
*HRVWDWLVWLFDO6RIWZDUH/LEUDU\textbackslash DQG8VHUV*XLGH by Clayton and Journel (1998). The
\par
book is delivered with a CD-ROM with *6/,\% source code.
\par
A commercial supplement to the *6/,\% package is available at a relatively low cost
\par
under the name :LQ*VOLE. It is a Windows 95/98/NT interface to *6/,\% and related
\par
programs. :LQ*VOLE includes all tools in the *6/,\% and some additional ones. :LQ*VOLE
\par
can be obtained from Statios (www.statios.com).
\par
\$ 6XUIHU
\par
6XUIHU is a 3D and 2D surface modelling package for interpolation and with some geo-
\par
statistical features. The 7.0 version of 6XUIHU offers e.g. variogram analysis and kriging
\par
options. 6XUIHU is commercial available from Golden Software
\par
(www.goldensoftware.com).
\par
\$ 81\&(57
\par
A more recent public domain package is 81\&(57, developed under contract to the U.S.
\par
Bureau of Reclamation (Myers, 1997). It was developed for evaluating uncertainty in
\par
subsurface geology, hydraulic properties, and the migration of hazardous contaminants
\par
in groundwater flow systems. 81\&(57 runs under UNIX operating system, but work
\par
are in progress rewriting 81\&(57 into Java, making the software platform independ-
\par
ent. The software contains a broad range of geostatistical tools, such as:
\par
modules to visualise 3D data
\par
2D contouring and gradient analysis module
\par
2D and 3D gridding module, using Inverse-Distance, Simple and Ordinary Kriging,
\par
and Trend-Surface Analysis
\par
semivariogram analysis in1D and 2D
\par
81\&(57 and user\textasciiacute s manual can be obtained from the Colorado Schools of Mines
\par
(http://uncert.mines.edu/).
\par
\$ 9DULR:LQ
\par
9DULR:LQ is a software for spatial data analysis in 2D. It includes a number of graphical
\par
tools for variogram modelling and analysis. 9DULR:LQ was originally sold together with
\par
the book "VARIOWIN: Software for Spatial Data Analysis in 2D" by Yvan Pannatier
\par
(Springer-Verlag). However, the first edition of the book is out of print and the author
\par
has now made the program available free of charge. 9DULR:LQ can be obtained from the
\par
University of Lausanne (http://www-sst.unil.ch/research/variowin/index.html).
\par
\$ 352\%\$\%,/,7{$<$}(67,0\$7,2162)7:\$5(
\par
A number of spreadsheets and software programs for assigning subjective probabilities
\par
or probability density functions (PDFs) are included as appendices in Olsson (2000).
\par
They include:6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 94 (96)
\par
a spreadsheet for assigning probabilities according to the eigenvector method (in
\par
Swedish)
\par
a spreadsheet for assigning probabilities according to a method by A. O\textasciiacute Hagen (in
\par
Swedish)
\par
a spreadsheet for assigning PDFs (in Swedish)
\par
the public domain software 63\$7 for assessing probability distributions. The pro-
\par
gram runs under DOS and was released in 1989 by P. Terlouw, Rijksuniversiteit
\par
Groningen, Netherlands.
\par
\$ 672\&+\$67,\&6,08/\$7,2162)7:\$5(
\par
\$ \&U\textbackslash VWDOEDOO
\par
\&U\textbackslash VWDO\%DOO is a commercial software from Denver, Colorado. It is a graphically ori-
\par
ented forecasting and risk analysis program (Decisioneering, 1996), designed for sub-
\par
jective selection of probability distributions. The program is an add-in to Microsoft Ex-
\par
cel or Lotus and has a wide range of built-in probability distributions. Monte Carlo
\par
simulation and Latin Hypercube sampling can be performed. The program is available
\par
from Decisioneering (http://www.cbpro.com).
\par
\$ \#5LVN
\par
As Crystal Ball, \#5LVN is a commercial software. It is an add-in program to Microsoft
\par
Excel or Lotus and has similar functions to Crystal Ball. The software is available from
\par
Palisade (http://www.palisade.com).
\par
\$ 5LVN6LP
\par
5LVN6LPis a low-cost software for Monte Carlo simulation. It is an add-in program to
\par
Microsoft Excel. The simulation results are presented in histogram and cumulative dis-
\par
tribution charts. RiskSim is available from Decision Support Services
\par
(http://www.treeplan.com).
\par
\$ '\$7\$:257+\$1''(\&,6,21\$1\$/{$<$}6,662)7:\$5(
\par
\$ *HQHUDO
\par
A list of more than 20 Bayesian network software packages (most of them public do-
\par
main) is available at http://http.cs.berkeley.edu/\textasciitilde murphyk/Bayes/bnsoft.html. Links to
\par
other decision analysis software is also provided at this web site. A discussion of some
\par
of these software packages for decision analysis in real-time environment is available in
\par
Younes (1998).
\par
\$ \%D\textbackslash 0DU
\par
The \%D\textbackslash 0DU Windows software uses Markov-chain analysis for estimating probabilities
\par
of indicators for mutually exclusive states in 2D and 3D (Ros\'en, 1995). Probability es-6*, 2001-01-10 Varia 500 95 (96)
\par
timates are updated using Bayesian statistics. Although no pre-programmed function for
\par
data worth analysis is available in the software, such analysis can still be performed
\par
with a simple sequence of functions in the program. The BayMar software is not com-
\par
mercially available but a demo version of the software is available in Ros\'en (1995).
\par
\$ '\$7\$
\par
'\$7\$'HFLVLRQ\$QDO\textbackslash VLV7UHH\$JH is a commercial software for decision analysis. Its
