consent, and it tries to account for it in contemporary legal and ethical theory. The authors
\par
argue that informed consent is relatively new in medical history, only developing
\par
significantly after the atrocities of World War II. They note that it developed as a device to
\par
protect patients from physicians and researchers who sought to manipulate patients for
\par
research or experimental reasons without providing any benefit to the patients. Since then
\par
it has developed to encompass those who participate in psychological experimentation as
\par
well.
\par
The book first illustrates the moral foundations of informed consent, and the authors
\par
ground informed consent on the moral principles of autonomy, beneficence and justice.
\par
The legal principles of liability, full disclosure and causation are seen as the legal
\par
foundations of informed consent. They note that the legal doctrine of informed consent not
\par
only requires that informed consent be obtained, but that information be disclosed. \textbf{It }is
\par
interesting that this positive duty is incumbent on physicians, for many argue that the law
\par
cannot compel physicians to take any positive actions. \textbf{In }light of this claim, if the law can
\par
compel physicians to undertake positive actions such as providing information, it would
\par
seem that it could also compel them to provide routine, customary care and routine patient
\par
maintenance.
\par
Beauchamp and Faden then provide a history of the doctrine of informed consent, and
\par
this section appears somewhat disappointing because it appears to be historically and
\par
ethnocentrically limited. There is virtually no mention of the development of this doctrine
\par
in antiquity, the middle ages and the early modern period. The authors did little
\par
investigation of the thought of medieval or early modern moralists, and one wonders if this
\par
was done because it was feared that it would compromise their claims that informed
\par
consent should promote pure contentless autonomy in decision-making.
\par
The sections describing the emergence of informed consent in America are helpful, but
\par
they seem to be rather narrow in scope. The doctrine of informed consent has developed in
\par
other countries where there are other perspectives on it and yet nJ hing was mentioned of
\par
those developments. The chapter discussing the evolution of the U.S. federal policies on
\par
informend consent doctrine suggests that this doctrine developed primarily to promote the
\par
autonomy of the patient, while in fact it evolved to prevent individuals from suffering
\par
psychological or physical harm as a result of research or experimentation.
\par
The authors describe the development of the requirement for informed consent in
\par
psychological research, and much information is given regarding contemporary
\par
controversies about the ability of individuals to give informed consent is provided. And the
\par
controversy between ethicists, psychiatrists and researchers over the ability of patients to
\par
give informed consent is concisely presented, well-explained and insightfully criticized.
\par
The most interesting section of this work is that dealing with the theory of informed
\par
consent. Beauchamp and Faden develop a rather complete and well-developed theory of
\par
human action, and they analyze closely the conditions required for informed consent. They
\par
suggest that informed consent in senseI as an autonomous authorization is not always and
\par
everywhere possible, but informed consent in sense2 as effective consent is possible in most
\par
circumstances. They argue that informed consent is possible when there is "substantial
\par
understanding" of the relevant material, and this is a reasonable claim.
\par
They hold that disclosure requirements should demand that health care professionals
\par
reveal to patients the benefits which accrue to a treatment, the expected risks, the optional
\par
May, 1987 85treatments available and the freedom of the patient to reject the treatment. They admit that
\par
it is sometimes difficult to properly communicate with the patient, but they claim that the
\par
best way of assuring that the pertinent information has been received is by confirming that
\par
it has been communicated to the patient.
\par
They discuss the problem of manipulation, coercion and persuasion of patients.
\par
Manipulation occurs when physicians only provide patients with certain forms of
\par
information, but withhold other forms of information so that the patient can only make
\par
one decision. This is generally considered to be objectionable, but in circumstances where
\par
there is sound reason to believe that the patient will make an immoral choice, the
\par
withholding of information which would justify such a choice would not seem to be
\par
immoral. This is pejoratively called "paternalism", but it is not clear how this differs from
\par
responsible professional use of information to protect a patient from immoral and harmful
\par
choices.
\par
The authors strongly object to coercion of patients. They seem to define coercion as
\par
forcing a patient by means of a threat to consent to a procedure that is in itself immoral. In
\par
contrast to coercion, manipUlation would seem to be the use, not of force or threat, but
\par
information, to bring the patient to a morally good decision. Persuasion, on the other hand,
\par
would seem to be the use of arguments or reasons to bring the patient to consent to a
\par
morally good action. It would seem that physicians should be morally free to try to
\par
persuade patients to consent to morally good decisions and to employ medical information
\par
to prevent a patient from making an unethical choice. These distinctions are not fully seen
\par
by the authors, and they voice, without adequate justification, objections to all of them.
\par
The authors suggest that informed consent exists to promote the requirements of justice,
\par
autonomy and beneficence. Yet when their theory is closely analyzed, it becomes clear that
\par
they see this doctrine exists primarily to promote the pure contentless autonomy of the
\par
patient. This is made evident for they do not discuss at any length the conditions under
\par
which an informed and competent consent can be overridden and treatment applied. The
\par
authors provide almost no discussion of the role that informed consent plays in making
\par
morally good health care choices possible. If concentration camp patients consented to
\par
their torture and malicious experimentation, that would not make those actions morally
\par
acceptable and good. Informed consent is in the service of morally good choices, and not
\par
merely in the service of the formal and contentless autonomy of the patient.
\par
We must be careful that we do not lose this perspective, for many who are promoting
\par
euthanasia by ommission of morally required care and treatment are arguing that the
\par
wishes of the patient, irrespective of their content, must be given full authority. Medical
\par
decisions made by patients with full and complete consent and info \textasciitilde{} mation can still be
\par
immoral, and there is a moral obligation incumbent on the physician to do what is possible
\par
to protect the patient from those decisions.
\par
It is unfortunate that the authors do not note how respect for the requirements of
\par
informed consent promotes medical practice. The patient-physician relationship should
\par
not be one that is dominated by either the physician or the patient, but should be a
\par
covenantal relationship between the two. In this relationship, the patient should strive to
\par
promote not just his or her own health, but also the development of medical practice and
\par
the fulfillment of the objectives of healing. This would mean that the patient must not stand
\par
in the way of the health care professional who is seeking to fulfill his or her ordinary,
\par
common and routine healing or nursing responsibilities. On the other hand, the physician is
\par
to respect the right of the patient to refuse any and all procedures that are purely
\par
experimental or not of any clinical benefit. Even further, the patient is to be free to refuse
\par
any medical treatment that is judged to be too physically painful, clinically useless or so
\par
burdensome financially that relevant ordinary financial obligations could not be met. The
\par
requirements of fully informed consent protect patients from unjust harm, but they also
\par
protect health care providers who attempt to be fully responsible healing professionals.
\par
What is needed at the present time are laws to protect physicians who attempt to provide
\par
emergency care or aspects of routine patient maintenance.
\par
86 Linacre QuarterlyTwenty years ago, the popular model of the physician was that of Dr. Kildare, who
\par
boldly rushed in to do everything possible to save the last ounce of life. Now the popular
\par
image is that of a patient boldly fending off medical treatments which are unwanted, for
\par
whatever idiosyncratic reason the patient might have. It is time to find a median between
\par
these two extremes and recognize that both the patient and physician must be involved in
\par
these decisions, which are the most important in any patient's life. What is needed now is a
\par
legal and moral theory of informed consent which protects both the rights of patients to
\par
accept and refuse treatment and the right of health care professionals to provide it in accord
\par
with their professional responsibilities. Unfortunately, while Beauchamp and Faden
\par
provide much useful information about informed consent, they have not been able to
\par
provide a foundation for the sort of theory of informed consent that can achieve this
\par
objective.
\par
- Rev. Robert Barry, O.P., Ph.D.
