\textbf{1. Summary}
\par
\textbf{1.1 Overview}
\par
'I'is report summarizes first year technical progress on SFRC N00014-84-K-0734, "Rcscarch in Distributed
\par
Tactical lecision Making: Decentralized Resource Management in Tactical Computer Executives," for the
\par
period \textbf{I }August 1984 (contract start date) through 31 July 1985. This research reported herein is jointly
\par
funded by Office of Naval Research and Space and Naval Warfare Systems Command.
\par
\textbf{Thc }objective of this effort is to conduct research in the area of distributed tactical decision making.
\par
Carnegie-Mellon University (CMU) proposed to conduct research on primarily two tasks in the first contract
\par
year. Task A considers best effort decision making for decentralized resource management. Task B focuses
\par
on the development of atomic transaction theories to support highly concurrent and reliable real-time com-
\par
putation. Innovative research under the guidance of the Principal Investigator was conducted on both of
\par
these tasks. Work will continue on both tasks in the second contract year. K \textbf{\emph{,}}
\par
\textbf{1.2 Progress on Task A: Best Effort Decision Making}
\par
Task \textbf{A }is divided into three subtasks. Subtask \textbf{Al }deals with the development of best effort decision
\par
making algorithms for dynamic system reconfiguration for the purpose of enhancing system survivability.
\par
Subtask A2 is a basic research task which investigates the theory of best effort team decision making. Subtask
\par
A3 investigates real-time multi-processor scheduling. In the following section, we summarize the progress
\par
made in Task A. A detailed discussion of these three subtaks is presented in Chapters 2 through 4.
\par
1.2.1 Subtask \textbf{Al: }System Reconfiguration Decision Algorithms
\par
\textbf{CMU }has developed a set of reconfiguration algorithms with the following properties:
\par
\textbf{" }Disturbance to functioning processes during the process of reconfiguration is minimized,
\par
\textbf{" }System vulnerability to future failures after re-configuration is minimized,
\par
\textbf{" }Reconfiguration is performed in real-time.
\par
\textbf{These }algorithms are directly applicable to a variety of distributed tactical systems.\textbf{1.2.2 Subtask A2: Best Effort Decision Theory}
\par
\textbf{CM }Ll has developed results showing that \textbf{the }lower bound on the performance of best effort team decision
\par
making in the context \textbf{of }load balancing is \textbf{62\% }of the idcalized peririnance with 'uill and instantaneous
\par
information. In this analysis, only local information is used for decision making. Since this result is \textbf{\emph{the }}\emph{lower}
\par
bound of best cflort decision making in the context of load balancing, it demonstrates the promise of
\par
decentralized best effort team decision making. A protocol for the efficient use of active information has
\par
been invcstigated. Further research on this topic will \textbf{be }continued in the next year.
\par
\textbf{1.2.3 Subtask A3: Multi-processor Real-time Scheduling}
\par
Subtask A3 represents our initial work on a value function based approach for multi-processor real-time
\par
scheduling. This research involves the study of deadline management in a multi-processor environment, in
\par
which the time allocation decisions must be made using a best effort approach. A considerable amount of
\par
research has been done when deadlines could be met, but relatively little information is available about
\par
scheduling decisions when available resource limitations require that one or more deadlines cannot be met.
\par
Our approach is designed to maximize the value of the available state information to make the deadline
\par
scheduling decisions, particularly in those cases where deadlines cannot be mel
\par
\textbf{1.3 }Progress on Task B: Atomic Transaction Theory
\par
Task B is divided into two subtasks. Subtask \textbf{B1 }represents the conclusion of a long term research of atomic..
\par
transaction theory for the development of transaction facilities in decentralized operating systems. Atomic
\par
transaction facilities are important for the reliable and efficient management of distributed data. Therefore,
\par
these facilities are also important for implementing any distributed decision making protocol. Subtask B2
\par
initiates the investigation of co-operating transactions, transactions that share information and co-operate as a
\par
team to achieve some common goal. In the following section, we summarize the progress made in Task
\par
B. The detailed report of Task B is presented in Chapters 5 and \textbf{6.}
\par
