\textbf{2.3 }Basic Concepts: Disturbance and Vulnerability
\par
When a failure of a processor or other hardware element occurs, there is an immediate interruption of
\par
various functioning processes either because the processor in which they were running failed or because the
\par
communication paths being used were disrupted. It is important to realize that processes \textbf{are }the fundamental
\par
unit of reconfiguration; however, modes are the fundamental unit of disturbance. \textbf{A }system mode consists of
\par
a collection of processes. \textbf{If }any of those processes fails (because a processor fails or a communication path is
\par
interrupted), the mode is disrupted. Thus, modes are the unit of system functionality.
\par
One must now reconfigure the system to overcome the failure. \textbf{This }can \textbf{be }done \textbf{by }rerouting messages,
\par
relocating processes, shedding load or all of these three. There are three distinct aspects to be considered:
\par
\textbf{1. }The present disturbance incurred \textbf{by }the failure.
\par
2. The additional disturbance caused \textbf{by }reconfiguration.
\par
\textbf{3. }The vulnerability of the subsequent reconfigured system.
\par
We next analyze each of these aspects. Let us begin \textbf{by }quantifying the disturbance.
