\textbf{2.1 Introduction}
\par
Conceptually, the three goals of reconfiguration are minimal disturbance, minimal vulnerability and max-
\par
imal feasibility, the ability to reconfigure the current task set without shedding load. \textbf{The }goal of \textbf{minimal}
\par
disturbance requires \textbf{the }reconfiguration algorithm not to disturb the critical modes in functioning processors,
\par
so that the system can carry out its mission without much additional disruption. The goal of maximal
\par
feasibility focuses on the restoration of as many disabled modes as possible. The goal of minimal vul-
\par
nerability is introduced to ensure that as a result of reconfiguration the system is made as fault tolerant \textbf{to}
\par
failures as possible, so that the impacts of future system failures are lessened. Conceptually, the notions of
\par
minimal disturbance and vulnerability help to eliminate the undesirable regions in the solution space, while
\par
the notion of maximal feasibility seeks to have as large a solution space as possible.
\par
We have successfully developed a system of algorithms that
\par
\textbf{1. }minimize the disturbance to the functioning tasks in the course of reconfiguration,
\par
2. reduce the vulnerability to future system failures,
\par
\textbf{3. }and operate in real-time.
