\textbf{3.1 Introduction}
\par
In a decentralized computer system, processes often function co-operatively as a team to enhance system
\par
* level pcrformancc. One basic goal of'the Archons project of which this research is part is to develop concepts
\par
* and mechanisms which are intended to function co-operatively \textbf{by }using co-operative decision making. These
\par
- mechanisms must function efficiently even though they will have both inaccurate and incomplete information
\par
upon which to act [Jensen 84]. In this chapter, we introduce a framework for dealing with co-operative
\par
decision making problems arising in decentralized systems in general and the Archon system specifically.
\par
Concepts drawn from queueing theory, team decision theory [Marschak \textbf{72] }and information theory will be
\par
used to analyze the value of system status information for resource management. We focus specifically on the
\par
trade-offs between the completeness and the timeliness of information. Rather than treating the consensus
\par
problem abstractly, we will single out a particular problem for study: the problem of decentralized load
\par
balancing. This problem will nicely illustrate the application of one particular paradigm of consensus deci-
\par
sion making \textbf{- }team decision theory. Furthermore, it illustrates the trade-offs among the cost of information
\par
(loading information at each processor), its completeness, and its timeliness. This chapter is organized as
\par
follows. Section \textbf{3.2 }provides an overview of decentralized decision making Section \textbf{3.3 }describes the load
\par
balancing problem and its formulation as a consensus decision making problem. Specific results charac-
\par
terizing the value of system status information are derived. Section \textbf{3.4 }presents some ideas for further study.
\par
