In the following, we attempt to answer some these questions in the context of load balancing.
\par
\textbf{3.3 The Load Balancing Problem}
\par
In this section. we consider the load balancing problem in the context of a local distributed computer
\par
system such as Archons [Jensen \textbf{83] }in which users create jobs at the nodes they log into. Since both the \textbf{job}
\par
arrival process and the required computational time for each node is unpredictable, the loads at each of the
\par
* nodes will be widely variable. Some will be lightly loaded, while others will be heavily loaded. The task of
\par
* the decision makers (local operating systems) is to equalize the loads in the system in order to improve the
\par
* system wide performance. Balanced loads will create short queues and short response times.
\par
Each decision maker can manage its own \textbf{job }queue if it chooses to process it locally. Load balancing
\par
necessitates the shifting of load from one processor to another, and this necessitates some form of consensus
\par
and negotiation involving at least a subset of the processors. At the other end of the spectrum, one processor
\par
* acting as a central scheduler will assign the \textbf{job }to one of the processors. The no negotiation approach can be
\par
* carried further \textbf{by }letting each of the processors schedule its own \emph{jobs. }This arrangement would then be
\par
* equivalent to the team decision approach whereby no consensus is needed, but the individual decision makers
\par
act to improve overall system performance. There is no a priori necessity for consensus. Rather, the degree
\par
of negotiation must be dictated \textbf{by }performance.
\par
Generally, there are two aspects of load balancing. The first aspect is the matching of the structure of the
\par
data flow in the application with the architecture of the distributed computer system. This includes clustering\textbf{25}
\par
closely coupled processes in the \textbf{siame }or nearby nodes, sdhcduling tasks with respect to precedence relations
\par
in such a way that more tasks can \textbf{bc }executed concurrcntly. and matching \textbf{the }special resource rcquircments
\par
of tasks with special hardware etc. From a modelling point \textbf{of }vi w. the optimization of these aspects is
\par
typically an \textbf{integer }programming problem. In many dedicated real-time control systems, this is done off-line
\par
\textbf{due }to the lack of efficicnt algorithms for solving integer programming problems. \textbf{A }second aspect of load
\par
balancing is the control of system dynamics. In a distributed system, there is substantial redundancy built into
\par
the system. Often a class of tasks can be executed with near equal efficiency \textbf{by }any of a group of similar or
\par
identical processors. 'Thc problem then is the equalization of the load \textbf{st) }as to improve the throughput and
\par
response times. In this chapter. we will concentrate on this latter aspect.
\par
Traditionally, \textbf{the }load balancing is carried out \textbf{by }a centralized scheduler. However. this approach is
\par
inadequate for many larger scale systems which are typically loosely coupled. The inadequacy is in part due
\par
\textbf{to }reliability considerations and in part due to the time delay involved in routing all relevant information to a
\par
centralized location, \textbf{in }this chapter, we will investigate a \textbf{highly }parallel and \textbf{highly }decentralized approach.
\par
In this approach, each processor is considered to \textbf{be }a member of a management team, the individual actions
\par
of which are geared to increasing the system level performance. The crucial requirement is the development
\par
of an information exchange structure and an associated decision procedure which makes such an approach
\par
effective.
\par
In summary, it appears that one reasonable approach to load balancing is the \textbf{highly }decentralized team
\par
approach. In this approach, each processor acts individually to increase system performance. \textbf{The }key
\par
'ingredient is to develop an information exchange structure which will lead to increased system performance
\par
\textbf{by }allowing each individual processor to schedule its own jobs. The most difficult aspect lies in obtaining the
\par
relevant system status information in a timely fashion. In addition, the actions of the individual processors
\par
must be sufficiently coordinated \textbf{to }generate a coherent global strategy, even though it is implemented in a
\par
\textbf{highly }decentralized fashion. We present some results on this team formulation in the rest of this section.
\par
