\textbf{3.3.3 }Approaches to Decentralized Load Balancing
\par
Once onc has introduced a measure of dic valuc of information, it remains to develop an algorithm which
\par
will nearly achieve the theoretical upper bound on performance. In gencral, processors will send messages to
\par
inform other processors of their status. T'he more frcquent the messages, the more information other proces-
\par
sors will have. Unfortunacely, these messages create overhead which serves to degradc system performance.
\par
Tlo deal with this tradeoff, one can draw an analogy with team decision theory. Information is valuable and
\par
worth the cost only if it causes the recipient to change his decision and results in a lower overall cost. \textbf{A}
\par
message should be sent only if it has a sufficiently large information content to justify the cost of sending it.
\par
Here we \emph{are }ignoring the reliability considerations which may dictate that a message \textbf{be }sent periodically. We
\par
use the word 'information' in the Shannon sense. The information gained from a message is equal to the
\par
uncertainty removed. Only messages describing relatively unusual or surprising system states should be
\par
transmitted. In the load balancing context, the high information content messages are those which describe
\par
overloading or underloading conditions. This suggests that processors should transmit status messages ac-
\par
cording to a control limit policy: either when it is underloaded or overloaded relative \textbf{to }its equilibrium
\par
distribution. The exact control limits are determined to optimize the tradeoff between the cost of information
\par
and the gain in performance from it.
\par
The control limit policy described in the krevious paragraph should provide a very efficient decentralized
\par
control algorithm. The policy is, however, one based on long run equilibrium calculations which ignore short
\par
run fluctuations. \textbf{A }processor will send a status message when its workload deviates from its long run
\par
expectations. It is possible that there could be a heavy influx of jobs over a short period (or a very small
\par
influx), and many processors will have above (below) average workloads. This causes many status messages
\par
to \textbf{be }sent which further degrades the system. This effect can be overcome \textbf{by }having control limits which are
\par
determined dynamically and change based on short term load fluctuations. Unfortunately, this would seem
\par
to necessitate sending even more messages to identify these short run fluctuations. There is, however, an
\par
alternative approach. This approach is based on having each processor construct a system status model. \textbf{If }we
\par
assume that the processors are linked \textbf{by }a bus connection, then each processor must do intensive bus
\par
monitoring. Each processor must keep track of all traffic on the bus, noting its source, its destination, and if\textbf{\emph{30}}
\par
pos\textasciitilde sible its cstimajcd processing time. This information allows each processor to build and update a system
\par
status model. Stich at model would predict the current workload at each processor. Based on this model. \textbf{\emph{tie}}
\par
procewsor could act its \textbf{at }central schcduler to determine the best processor to handle any particular ask. T'he
\par
quality of these decisions will \textbf{be }totally a function of the accuracy of the mnodel uscd. At first glance, it would
\par
seem that dic system status model might bc accurate for a short time period, but its quality would qu'ickly
\par
deteriorate as time passes. due in part to the uncertainty of thc exact processing requirements of any par-
\par
ticular task. It is necessary to introduce some sort of feedback control to \textbf{keep }thc model accurate. Ib7is can
\par
\textbf{be }also done in a \textbf{highly }decentralized efficient manner. Under the assumption that the processors are
\par
coupled \textbf{by }a bus, they will all see the same traffic. As a result, they will all build identical system status
\par
models. lhus the models used \textbf{by }each of the processors gives essentially identical predictions of the
\par
workload at any particular processor. In addition, each of the processors has extra information in that it
\par
knows the exact workload for itself. Each processor can compare its own workload with the workload as
\par
predicted \textbf{by }the common system status model. 'The two will of course deviate. It is only important to notify
