\par
In this chapter, we develop a theory of modular concurrency rules - a theory of provably consistent and
\par
correct concurrency control methods that allow one to write, modify and schedule one's transactions indepen-
\par
dently of the others. Our theory is a generalization of serializability theory and provides at least as much
\par
concurrency as any other modular scheduling method. Before the formal investigation, we give an informal
\par
overview of our theory. From a programmer's point of view, our work is a formal theory for decomposing a
\par
transaction system. Under serializability theory each transaction is treated an atomic unit of operations and
\par
the database is treated as a non-divisible unit of data objects. In our approach, both the database and
\par
transactions are decomposed as follows.
\par
\textbf{1. }The database is partitioned into consistency preserving \emph{atomic data sets. }As long as the consis-
\par
tency constraints of each atomic data set are satisfied individually, the consistency of the database
\par
is maintained. However, data objects in different atomic data sets need not be independent or
\par
unrelated, and data objects can be added to or deleted from any of the dynamic data structures
\par
(e.g. linked lists) in an atomic data \textbf{set.}
\par
\textbf{2. }Each transaction is independently partitioned into a partially ordered set of correctness preserving
\par
\emph{elementary transactions. }As long as the post-condition of each executed elementary transaction is
\par
individually satisfied, the post-conditions of the partitioned transaction (called \textbf{a }\emph{compound}
\par
\emph{transaction) }are also satisfied.
\par
An elementary transaction can have the structure of nested transactions, and it preserves the
\par
consistency of the accessed atomic data sets. However, elementary transactions in a compound
\par
transaction need not be independent computational units. That is, the results of computations can
\par
\textbf{be }exchanged \textbf{by }the elementary transactions of the same compound transaction.
\par
Having divided the database and transactions, we next employ some concurrency control protocol to ensure
\par
that the elementary transactions of each of the compound transactions are executed serializably on a atomic
\par
data \textbf{set by }set basis. The protocol defined in this chapter ensures that:46
