\textbf{5.1 Introduction}
\par
Scrializability theory has been widely accepted as the basis for concurrency control, because it ensures the
\par
consistency and correctness of concurrency control. Under serializable schedules, the results of executing a
\par
set of transactions will satisfy both the consistency constraints of the database and the post-conditions of
\par
transactions as long as each individual transaction is consistent and correct when executing alone. Further-
\par
more. with serializable schedules one can write, modify and schedule 6 any single transaction independent of
\par
any knowledge of the rest of the transactions in the system. Such a modular property of concurrency control is
\par
very attractive in the development of a general purpose transaction facility in which transactions are fre-
\par
quently modified. We believe that the properties of consistency, correctness and modularity account for both
\par
the popularity of serializability theory and the continuing interest in the study of various protocols that
\par
support the serializability of concurrency control [Weihl 84, Attar 84, Papadimitriou 84. Mohan \textbf{851.}
\par
On the other hand, there are many proposed non-serializable scheduling methods that can provide a higher
\par
degree of concurrency than that available using serializable schedules [Allchin \textbf{82, }Schwarz 82, Garcia-'Molina
\par
\textbf{83, }Lynch \textbf{83, }Schwarz 841. Since serializability theory is optimal when only transaction syntax information is
\par
used for scheduling transactions [Kung \textbf{79]. }many proposed non-serializable scheduling approaches have
\par
typically focused on various methods of using transaction semantic information to maximize system concur-
\par
rency. This approach can lead to significant increases in concurrency. There is, however, a potential accom-
\par
panying disadvantage. When a set of transactions are jointly scheduled through the use of semantic infor-
\par
mation, the modification of even a single step of a single transaction could result in the rescheduling of the
\par
rest of transactions. There is a second problem which arises with the use of non-serializable schedules.
\par
