\textbf{4.2.2 Non-monotonic Logic}
\par
Decision making is dependent on a determination of the state of the relevant world as assessed \textbf{by }the
\par
decision maker. \textbf{Tle }decision to be made can be viewed as a consequence (deduction) from this perceived
\par
state, which can \textbf{be }represented as a set of predicates. Normal formal logic, from which inference rules would
\par
\textbf{be }taken to produce such dcductions, can be described as \emph{mnonolonic }in the sense that the addition of any new
\par
axiom (i.e.. an additional observation of state) which is not in conflict with existing axioms, can never
\par
invalidate a previously drawn conclusion. In a situation characterized \textbf{by }a large quantity of incomplete or
\par
inaccurate information, such deductions may later be invalidated \textbf{by }the presence of new information Logic
\par
in which inferences may be drawn using such data is called non-monotonic logic [McDermott
\par
\textbf{80] }[McDermott \textbf{821, }and is generally characterized \textbf{by }the use of a logical operator meaning \emph{"is consislent}
\par
\emph{with" }on conclusions drawn.
\par
In another form of monotonic logic, inferences which can be described only as "consistent" with perceived
\par
state information can be handled \textbf{by }the use \emph{of fuzzy }sets. Normal set theory deals with sets whose boundaries
\par
are clear-cut; an element is either a member of a given set or it is not. Members of fuzzy sets [Kaufmann \textbf{75]}
\par
have an associated membership value which determines the degree of membership of that member in the set.
\par
Fuzzy logic refers to the manipulation of predicates with fuzzy implications (e.g., High value processes should
\par
be executed early). Discussions of such logic inference rules are given in [Zadeh \textbf{791.}
