\textbf{4.1 Introduction}
\par
An operating system managing a real-time application shares a large number of functions with a normal
\par
timesharing operating system, but differs most significantly in one principal area \textbf{-- }the management of time
\par
* deadlines. The typical time sharing operating system manages a number of independent applications.
\par
promoting some concept of fairness (defined \textbf{by }the system administrator) among them. In the real-time
\par
system, a single application3 uses the system resources to solve a single problem, and includes a set of
\par
deadlines which must be met in order to satisfy its specifications.
\par
The research which we undertake here is to study deadline management in a multi-processor environment.
\par
in which the time allocation decisions must be made using a best effort approach. In fact, for this problem a
\par
best effort approach must be used because of the built-in uncertainties even if complete and accurate global
\par
\textbf{- }system state information were available, because of the stochastic nature of the information available about
\par
schedulable processes and the limited decision making time available in a real-time system.
\par
\textbf{A }considerable amount of research has been done when deadlines could be met but relatively little
\par
\textbf{-. }information is available about scheduling decisions when available resource limitations require that one or
\par
\textbf{" }more deadlines cannot be \textbf{met. }Our approach will be designed to maximize the value of the available state
\par
information to make the deadline scheduling decisions, particularly in those cases where deadlines cannot be
\par
met.
\par
\textbf{{$\bullet$} . }3Asngle application means \textbf{a }set of processes working together and sharing resources toward \textbf{a }common goal. While \textbf{it }is possible for
\par
more \textbf{than }one such application to ran concurrently in \textbf{a }real-time system, it \textbf{is }usually true that the real-time commitment of the system
\par
will \textbf{be made }only to one: any others will execute \textbf{as }background applications.
