\textbf{The }concepts of modularity and atomic data sets formally defined in this chapter have intuitive meanings.
\par
A modular concurrency control method is one which allows a programmer to write, modify and schedule his
\par
transaction independently of other transactions. For example, serializability theory is a modular scheduling
\par
method, because a programmer can write, modify and then schedule (e.g. using the two phase lock protocol)
\par
his transaction independently of other transactions in the system. In other words, a modular concurrency
\par
control method ensures that any particular written and scheduled transaction need not be changed, when
\par
other transactions are written, modified or rescheduled.
\par
*n -* We assume throughout that the consistency constraints of the database are fixed while transactions are
\par
being added or modified. It is. of course, possible that the specification of database consistency constraints
\par
could be changed during the development of the transaction system and this could in turn force the trans-
\par
actions to be rewritten and rescheduled because transactions must maintain the database consistency con-
\par
straints. However, the task will \textbf{be }easier if a modular approach is used, because one can examine the impact
\par
of new consistency constraints on each of the transactions \emph{individually }and perform the necessary modification
\par
\emph{individually.}
\par
Intuitively, an atomic data set is a set of data objects whose consistency can be maintained \textbf{by }a transaction,
\par
-independently of how other atomic data sets are being updated \textbf{by }other transactions. We call this property of
\par
an atomic data set "consistency preserving". It is important to point out that consistency preserving implies
\par
* neither that data objects in different atomic data sets are unrelated nor that data structures must be static. For
\par
example, we might have a document queue associated with each of the computers in a local network and
\par
several print queues associated with printing devices in the network. Each queue has its own consistency
\par
constraints in the form of \textbf{"0 :5 }QueueSize \textbf{: }MaxSize". There is a clear "producer-consumer" relation
\par
-
\par
, between document queues and printer queues. Nonetheless, each queue forms an atomic data set because the
\par
\textbf{. ,}
\par
consistency of any queue \emph{can }be satisfied independently of the others.
\par
