\textbf{5.2 A Model of Modular Scheduling Rules}
\par
We begin our formal investigation \textbf{by }developing a model of modular scheduling rules. Intuitively, a
\par
scheduling rule specifies how the steps of a transaction can be interleaved with those of other transactions. In
\par
our model, a scheduling rule performs this specification \textbf{by }partitioning the steps of each transaction into
\par
equivalence classes called \emph{atomic step segments. }Schedules satisfy \textbf{the }specification of a given scheduling rule
\par
\textbf{by }interleaving the atomic step segments of each transaction serializably with the atomic step segments of
\par
* other transactions. For example, serializability theory is a particular scheduling rule which takes all the steps
\par
of a transaction as a single atomic step segment. Serializable schedules are the set of schedules that satisfy this
\par
rule, because the atomic step segments specified \textbf{by }serializability theory are interleaved serializably in
\par
\textbf{'- }serializable schedules.
