\textbf{5.3 The Setwise Serializable Schedling Rule}
\par
Having developed a formal model of modular scheduling rules, we now introduce an important modular
\par
scheduling rule called the setwise serializable scheduling rule. In essence, this rule states that if the database is
\par
partitioned into consistency preserving \emph{atomic data }\textbf{\emph{sets, }}then we can replace the global serializability of
\par
concurrency control \textbf{by }setwise serializability. Intuitively, an atomic data set is simply a set of data objects
\par
with a set of associated consistency constraints that can be satisfied independently of other atomic data sets.
\par
For example, in a distributed computer system a \textbf{job }queue at a computer waiting for execution is an atomic
\par
data set, the local directory to a computer's files is an atomic data set, and each user's mailbox is also an
\par
atomic data set. This is \textbf{because }the consistency of each of \textbf{these }entities is defined \textbf{by }a set of consistency
\par
constraints that can be satisfied independently of \textbf{others.75}
