\textbf{5.4 Compound Transactions}
\par
\textbf{A }compound transaction \textbf{consists }of a partially \textbf{ordered }set of elementary transactions. When an \textbf{elementary}
\par
transaction is executed scrializably and in an order consistent with \textbf{the }partial ordering of the elementary
\par
transactions in \textbf{the }compound transaction, it has the following two properties. First, given a consistent state of
\par
\textbf{the }database and the results passed from preceding clementary transactions, an elementary transaction
\par
produces another consistent state of the database and satisfies its own post-conditions. Second, the conjunc-
\par
\textbf{tion }of the post-conditions of the constituent \textbf{elementary }transactions is equivalent to the post-conditions of
\par
the compound transactions. When the database is partitioned into atomic data sets and transactions are
\par
partitioned in the form of compound transactions, the consistency and correctness of concurrency control will
\par
be ensured as long as the elementary transactions of the compound transactions are run setwise serializably.
\par
\textbf{This }result is formally expressed as the generalized setWise serializable scheduling rules. We also prove that
\par
our approach of partitioning the database and transactions provides as least as much concurrency as any other
\par
modular concurrency control approach. This optimality result is formally expressed as the completeness of
\par
generalized setwise serializable scheduling rules.
