\textbf{," 6. Subtask B1 -2: Atomic Transaction Theory:}
\par
\textbf{Modular Failure Recovery}
\par
In this chapter, we summarize our additional work on Subtask \textbf{BL the }failure recovery part of our atomic
\par
transaction theory. \textbf{"[he }failure recovery theory addresses the problem of guaranteeing the consistency and
\par
correctness of concurrency control in the face of system failures. \textbf{This }work is primarily \textbf{carried }out \textbf{by }Dr. Lui
\par
Sha and Prof. John P. I.ehoczky.
\par
\textbf{6.1 Introduction}
\par
In the previous chapter, Modular Concurrency Control, we have studied the subject of concurrency control
\par
without considering the impact of possible system failures. We now extend our work \textbf{by }taking the effect of
\par
* system failures into consideration. Computer systems can fail in many ways. We limit ourself to the so-called
\par
"clean and soft" failures. \textbf{A }failure is said to be clean and soft if the effects of this failure can be modelled \textbf{by }a
\par
network of computers some of which stop running and lose the contents of their main memories. However,
\par
the database residing in the stable storage remains intact.
\par
The objective of using failure recovery rules is \textbf{to }ensure that concurrency control can be carried out
\par
consistently and correctly despite failures in the system. \textbf{A }failure recovery rule'specifies the conditions under
\par
which the executed steps of a transaction commit or abort. Committing a sequence of executed steps means
\par
non-divisibly transferring the result of the computations from the main memory to the database residing in
\par
the stable storage. The commit operation represents irrevocable changes made to the database. For example,
\par
the recovery rule associated with serializable schedules is known \emph{as failure alomicity. }This rule requires that a
\par
transaction either commits or aborts all executed steps at the end of execution. In other words, serializable
\par
schedules creates a virtual "executing alone" environment in which the concurrently executed transactions
\par
" maintain database consistency and produce correct results. At the end of its execution, a transaction will
\par
either non-divisibly transfer the results of computation to the database or abort and re-start later. In this way,
\par
the consistency and correctness of concurrency control is ensured despite clean and soft system failures.
\par
Failure atomicity is widely accepted as the criterion for failure recovery, and many new protocols are being
\par
designed to efficiently implement \textbf{it }[Bernstein \textbf{83, }Mohan \textbf{83, }Attar 84, Svobodova 84].
\par
Unfortunately, the non-divisibility in the transferring of the computational results of a transaction to the
