Before the formal investigation, we first give an informal overview of our failure recovery approach.
\par
According to our modular concurrency control theory, the database is partitioned into atomic data sets and
\par
transactions are written in \textbf{the }form of compound transactions, each of which consists of a partially ordered set
\par
of elementary transactions. Given a compound transaction, the failure safe rule examines the transaction
\par
\textbf{ADS }segments in each of the elementary transactions. \textbf{A }transaction \textbf{ADS }segment consists of all the steps in
\par
an elementary transaction that access the same atomic data set. \textbf{If }the steps of a transaction \textbf{ADS }segment are
\par
not interleaved with those of others, then the \textbf{ADS }segment is named an \emph{atomic commit segment. }Otherwise,
\par
interleaved \textbf{ADS }segments are taken to be a single atomic commit segment. That is, the failure safe rule
\par
partitions a transaction into a partially ordered set of atomic commit segments. \textbf{A }transaction must non-
\par
divisibly commit each of its atomic commit' segments in an order consistent with the partial order of the
\par
atomic commit segments in the transaction.
