\textbf{6.2 A Model of Modular Failure Recovery Rules}
\par
We now develop our model of modular failure recovery rules. We first define the concept of a modular
\par
failure recovery rule. Second, we model the effect of clean and soft failures upon concurrency control. We
\par
then define the concept of consistency and correctness for a modular failure recovery rule in the face of clean
\par
and soft failures. Next, we introduce the concept of safety for a recovery rule. We conclude this section \textbf{by}
\par
addressing the issues of re-scheduling aborted transactions.
\par
Conceptually, a failure recovery rule is a specification of the way to commit the executed steps of a
\par
transaction. For example, failure atomicity is a failure recovery rule that specifies that at the end of a
\par
