\textbf{6.4 Conclusion}
\par
In this chapter, we have shown that when the transaction scheduling rule is the generalized setwise serializ-
\par
able scheduling rule, the optimal modular and safe recovery rule is the \emph{failure safe rule. }From an application
\par
point of view, there are two important points one should be aware \textbf{of. First, }one should \textbf{write }one's \textbf{compound}
\par
transaction carefully to avoid the interleaving of transaction \textbf{ADS }segments, whenever this is possible. Un-
\par
necessarily interleaving the \textbf{steps }of transaction \textbf{ADS }segments in an elementary transaction could lead to \textbf{a}
\par
serious loss of system concurrency. Second, one must realize that the spirit of the failure safe rule is to provide
\par
"check-points", so that a transaction can resume its execution after being interrupted \textbf{by }failures. As a matter
\par
of \textbf{fact, }once we have committed one single atomic commit segment of a transaction, we can only \textbf{abort}
