We give a few informal suggestions regarding the implementation of the failure safe rule. The standard
\par
distributed version of the two phase commit protocol {$^\circ$} [Bernstein \textbf{831 }can be adopted to commit the atomic
\par
commit segments. ''he major modification is that we also need \textbf{to }store the values of local variables of a
\par
commit segment in the stable storage. Saving these values in the stable storage is important for resuming the
\par
execution of a transaction that has been interrupted \textbf{by }a failure. When an atomic commit segment commits,
\par
the local variables contained in this segment should also be saved in the stable storage. To optimize the
\par
storage utilization, we can save only those local variables that are shared \textbf{by }different commit segments. \textbf{\emph{The}}
\par
identification of those shared variablcs can be made easy if one is willing to request the explicit declaration of
\par
them as \emph{atomic variables }in the program of a compound transaction.
