* real-time executives (operazing systems) is inadequate to support the efficient realization of reconfiguration
\par
- algorithms and transaction facilities for distributed real-time command and control systems. \textbf{The }develop-
\par
ment of a theory of distributed scheduling algorithms and its integration with reconfiguration algorithms and
\par
a transaction facility has emerged as a critical problem to the success of any real-time decentralized computer
\par
* system.
\par
Research over the next year on Tasks \textbf{A }and B will continue largely as described in CMU's proposal. Based
\par
on the results of research accomplished over the past year, emphasis will be directed at incorporating real-
\par
time constraints into transactions and developing scheduling algorithms so that the real-time constraints are
\par
* met. The expected results from this work should be directly applicable \textbf{to }real-time distributed tactical
\par
decision making. Task \textbf{A }will emphasize time-driven resource management \textbf{--- }managing system computation
\par
* and communication resources efficiently so that real-time constraints will be met. This effort will concentrate
\par
on \textbf{(1) }a value function based approach for multi-processor scheduling, (2) scheduling problems of \emph{a}
\par
* decentralized system, \textbf{(3) }investigation of a decentralized team decision model, and (4) architectural support
\par
* for the scheduling of distributed computations. Task B will examine extension of the model of compound
\par
transactions to allow for the specification of timing constraints and development of the new theory of co-
\par
* operating transactions.94
