@article{kramerExpertSystemsTechniques1988,
  title = {Expert Systems: Techniques, Tools, and Applications},
  shorttitle = {Expert Systems},
  author = {Kramer, Bryan M},
  year = 1988,
  pages = {0--4},
  langid = {english},
  note = {AI Magazine Volume 9 Number 1 (1988) (\copyright{} AAAI)
\par
Expert Systems: Techniques, Tools, and Applications
\par
Bryan M. Kramer
\par
Expert Systems. Techniques, Tools, and Applications is a collection of research
\par
papers covering a range of topics in expert systems and knowledge engineering.
\par
The book is edited by Philip Klahr and the late Donald A. Waterman, both of
\par
Rand Corporation. The papers are selected from RAND technical reports pub-
\par
lished from 1977 to 1985. The book is most valuable to people learning knowl-
\par
edge engineering. Four of the papers provide interesting glimpses at the prob-
\par
lems involved in transforming knowledge about a domain into computer repre-
\par
sentations. In addition, the book contains one or two interesting papers for
\par
researchers in each of the areas of knowledge acquisition, reasoning with uncer-
\par
tainty, and distributed problem solving.
\par
The book has four sections: intro-
\par
duction, tools, applications, and tech-
\par
niques. Each section begins with an
\par
overview that lists the contributions
\par
of each of the papers contained there-
\par
in.
\par
The introductory paper is authored
\par
by the editors of the book. It presents
\par
a list of the contributions to artificial
\par
intelligence of RAND scientists and
\par
consultants. For example, Newell,
\par
Shaw and Simon were associated with
\par
RAND when they did their work on
\par
GPS. It is interesting to note how
\par
many important researchers have
\par
been associated with RAND at one
\par
time or another.
\par
The second section contains two
\par
papers describing tools for building
\par
systems.
\par
The first is a description by Henry
\par
A. Sowizral and James R. Kipps of
\par
ROSIE, a rule-based expert system
\par
shell. The design of ROSIE is based on
\par
the assumption that a near-English
\par
allows non-programmers to read and
\par
understand ROSIE programs. The lan-
\par
guage is described in great detail, gen-
\par
erally using English definitions rather
\par
than formal specifications of syntax.
\par
Because of the amount of detail, many
\par
of the concepts are not very well
\par
motivated, and the knowledge repre-
\par
sentation issues that lead to the
\par
design decisions are not discussed.
\par
The second tool is ROSS, an object-
\par
oriented simulation system. The
\par
paper is written by David J. MC-
\par
Arthur, Philip Klahr and Sanjai
\par
Narain. The ROSS system consists of
\par
a fairly standard object-oriented lan-
\par
guage, a predefined object for manag-
\par
ing simulation time, some menu-
\par
based browsing/command facilities,
\par
some EMACS macros for editing
\par
ROSS ojbects, and an interface to a
\par
color-graphics system. Programmers
\par
are encouraged to make ROSS code
\par
English-like by using verbose message
\par
templates and by using the abbrevia-
\par
tion facility. The paper is valuable as a
\par
brief introduction to simulation in an
\par
object-oriented programming system.
\par
The third section of the book, appli-
\par
cations, contains four papers describ-
\par
ing the development of prototype
\par
expert systems in four different
\par
domains
\par
In the first paper, Donald A. Water-
\par
man and Brian M. Jenkins describe an
\par
expert system for discovering
\par
attributes of terrorist groups given
\par
descriptions of their activities. Much
\par
of the paper consists of a justification
\par
of the expert systems approach and a
\par
description of RITA, a rule language
\par
developed at RAND in the 1970's.
\par
This is followed by a brief description
\par
of terrorism, a description of user
\par
interaction with the prototype expert
\par
system, and examples of the rules and
\par
frames used by the expert system.
\par
The second paper in this section is a
\par
discussion of the use of an expert sys-
\par
tem to evaluate claims in product lia-
\par
bility disputes. The authors, Donald
\par
A. Waterman and Mark A. Peterson,
\par
devote much of the paper to a discus-
\par
sion of the settlement process and a
\par
list of a large number of unanswered
\par
questions. A very small prototype
\par
written in ROSIE is then presented.
\par
There is then a long discussion of
\par
what is yet to be done and of the
\par
potential benefits of a completely
\par
developed system.
\par
The subject of the third paper is
\par
TATR, a prototype expert system for
\par
tactical air targeting. The authors are
\par
Monti D. Callero, Donald A. Water-
\par
man, and James R. Kipps. This is
\par
another example of the use of ROSIE.
\par
Again, although only a very simple
\par
prototype is presented, many of the
\par
issues that arise in the knowledge-
\par
engineering process are discussed.
\par
The final paper of the applications
\par
section is by Philip Klahr, John W.
\par
Ellis, William D. Giarla, Sanjai
\par
Narain, Edison M. Cesar and Scott R.
\par
Turner. The authors present TWIRL, a
\par
ROSS-based simulation of tactical
\par
warfare. This paper differs from the
\par
previous three in that a fairly detailed
\par
knowledge base is presented.
\par
The fourth section of the book is
\par
entitled ``Techniques''. It contains four
\par
papers describing research in the com-
\par
ponents of knowledge-based systems.
\par
The areas addressed are exemplary
\par
programming, knowledge acquisition,
\par
uncertain inference, and distributed
\par
problem solving.
\par
Exemplary programming is a tech-
\par
nique for developing programs from
\par
traces of user interactions. The first
\par
paper, written by Donald A. Water-
\par
man, William S. Faught, Philip Klahr,
\par
Stanley J. Rosenchein and Robert B.
\par
Wesson, begins with descriptions of
\par
two existing exemplary programming
\par
systems, EP-1 and EP-2. These are
\par
programs that monitor a user per-
\par
forming a series of actions on the
\par
computer and create programs for per-
\par
forming similar sequences of actions.
\par
In the paper, the shortcomings of EP-1
\par
and EP-2 are described and a detailed
\par
proposal for the next generation pro-
\par
gram is presented. Appendices to the
\par
paper contain example sessions with
\par
EP-1 and EP-2.
\par
SPRING 1988 123In the second paper of this section,
\par
an architecture for knowledge pro-
\par
gramming is described by Frederick
\par
Hayes-Roth, Philip Klahr and David J.
\par
Mostow. Knowledge programming is
\par
described as a type of knowledge
\par
acquisition in which advice expressed
\par
in the expert's language is converted
\par
into actions in a knowledge-based sys-
\par
tem. The authors focus on a step they
\par
call operationalization; other steps in
\par
the process are parsing, interpretation,
\par
integration and application. The game
\par
of hearts is used as an example; the
\par
rules and objectives of the game are
\par
transformed into strategies for making
\par
moves in the game.
\par
The next paper is written by J. Ross
\par
Quinlan. He describes INFERNO, a
\par
program embodying a unique scheme
\par
for uncertain inference. INFERNO is
\par
based on the assumptions that 1)
\par
there should be no a priori assump-
\par
tions of the probabilities of proposi-
\par
tions 2) it should be possible to state
\par
probabilistic relationships between
\par
propositions 3) it should be possible to
\par
state information about any subset of
\par
the propositions and draw inferences
\par
4) the system should detect inconsis-
\par
tencies. This is a very interesting
\par
approach to reasoning with uncertain-
\par
ty.
\par
The last paper describes experi-
\par
ments using different strategies for
\par
distributed problem solving. The
\par
authors are Randall Steeb, David J.
\par
McArthur, Stephanie J. Cammarata,
\par
Sanjai Narain, and William D. Giarla.
\par
A strategy for distributed problem
\par
solving is described as having two
\par
parts: a policy for assigning the parts
\par
of a task to different agents, and a pol-
\par
icy for communication between
\par
agents. Two sets of experiments are
\par
presented. The first is a simulation of
\par
air traffic control. Here the distributed
\par
agents are the various planes and the
\par
control tower. The second set of
\par
experiments is in the domain of
\par
remotely piloted fleet control.
\par
In summary, this book is most
\par
interesting for the papers in the appli-
\par
cations section. Each of these papers
\par
describes a domain of expertise and
\par
the questions that arise in represent-
\par
ing these domains in a knowledge-
\par
based system. Each paper gives exam-
\par
ples of the final computer representa-
\par
tion of the knowledge involved. The
\par
124 AI MAGAZINE
\par
TWIRL paper is particularly interest-
\par
ing in this respect. This section
\par
makes the book valuable to anyone
\par
learning knowledge engineering, par-
\par
ticularly since so few detailed descrip-
\par
tions of complicated systems exist in
\par
the literature In addition, the large
\par
number of unanswered questions
\par
raised in each paper provide a sobering
\par
reminder of how difficult it really is
\par
to capture knowledge in a computer
\par
system. For builders of expert system
\par
tools, the book provides examples of
\par
two systems and discussion of some
\par
of the questions whose answers will
\par
shape future generations of knowl-
\par
edge-based systems.
\par
The Principles and
\par
Applications of Decision
\par
Analysis and Decision Analysis
\par
and Behavioral Research
\par
Jack Breese
\par
Increasingly, artificial intelligence
\par
researchers attempting to develop
\par
machine encodable theories of knowl-
\par
edge and action are realizing the
\par
importance of 1) explicit considera-
\par
tion of the uncertainty (incomplete
\par
knowledge) inherent in any real-world
\par
domain and 2) the need to handle
\par
multiple, possibly conflicting objec-
\par
tives in developing plans of action.
\par
Decision analysis is an engineering
\par
discipline which embodies both a the-
\par
ory of decision making under uncer-
\par
tainty and a set of engineering tech-
\par
niques for applying this theory in the
\par
real world of multiple objectives. This
\par
review examines two important
\par
works in decision analysis: The Prin-
\par
ciples and Applications of Decision
\par
Analysis edited by Ronald Howard
\par
and James Matheson (SDG Publica-
\par
tions, Strategic Decisions Group,
\par
Menlo Park, California, 1984, \$75.00)
\par
and Decision Analysis and Behavioral
\par
Research by Detlof von Winterfeldt
\par
and Ward Edwards (Cambridge Uni-
\par
versity Press, 1986, \$19.95). Though
\par
decision analysis typically emphasizes
\par
assisting human decision makers in
\par
high-stakes complex problems, it has
\par
much to offer artificial intelligence
\par
both in terms of a theoretical frame-
\par
work for resource allocation in a
\par
dynamic and uncertain world, as well
\par
as a set of well-grounded and tested
\par
knowledge engineering methods for
\par
applying the theory.
\par
At the outset it is important to dis-
\par
tinguish between decision theory and
\par
decision analysis. Decision theory is a
\par
formal system of choice under uncer-
\par
tainty which prescribes that a deci-
\par
sion-maker who wishes to abide by a
\par
set of axioms that describe a notion of
\par
rational and plausibly desirable behav-
\par
ior (von Neumann and Morgenstern,
\par
1947) should choose a course of action
\par
that maximizes expected utility.
\par
Expected utility is calculated using
\par
the standard laws of probability,
\par
where the probabilities are subjective,
\par
in that they summarize the decision
\par
maker's information and beliefs about
\par
a proposition. Both of the books
\par
reviewed here provide summaries of
\par
these basic theoretical underpinnings
\par
of decision analysis, as well as some
\par
extensions to the theory made by the
\par
authors. However, decision theory
\par
does not provide any guidance on how
\par
to construct a utility function, struc-
\par
ture and represent problems, elicit or
\par
construct probabilities, and explicitly
\par
solve (or search) for recommended
\par
courses of action given a particular
\par
model or representation. These activi-
\par
ties, which are analogous to the tasks
\par
of knowledge engineering, representa-
\par
tion, and inference in knowledge-
\par
based artificial intelligence systems,
\par
are the domain of decision analysis.
\par
Because the books reviewed here are
\par
about decision analysis (as opposed to
\par
decision theory), they are devoted pri-
\par
marily to discussion of using rational
\par
principles to help people make diffi-
\par
cult decisions.
\par
The Principles and Applications of
\par
Decision Analysis by Howard and
\par
Matheson is a two-volume compila-
\par
tion of tutorials and research papers
\par
spanning over 20 years of decision
\par
analysis research by Howard and his
\par
colleagues at SRI International and
\par
Stanford University. Howard and
\par
Matheson's primary focus is a view of
\par
decision analysis as a procedure for
\par
constructing, evaluating, and refining
\par
a decision model. This perspective is
\par
summarized in the ``decision analysis
\par
cycle'' which is an idealization of a
\par
control cycle for decision analysis
\par
(Figure 1).
\par
Basis development is analogous tothe knowledge representation task in
\par
AI systems. Decision analysis pro-
\par
vides a specific taxonomy for this rep-
\par
resentation: It must be able to capture
\par
the decision alternatives, information,
\par
and preferences of the decision maker.
\par
Deterministic structuring is primarily
\par
a means of focussing attention
\par
through sensitivity analysis on those
\par
factors in the decision situation wor-
\par
thy of probabilistic treatment. Proba-
\par
bilistic evaluation includes develop-
\par
ment of probabilities, assessment of
\par
risk attitude [i.e. the decision maker's
\par
preferences for alternatives that have
\par
uncertain outcomes), and develop-
\par
ment of recommended alternatives
\par
based on maximization of expected
\par
utility. The final phase, basis
\par
appraisal, involves assessing the deci-
\par
sion model and its results. A critical
\par
concept at this stage is the value of
\par
information, which provides a mea-
\par
sure of whether it is worthwhile to
\par
gather additional information. In par-
\par
ticular, information is valuable only
\par
to the extent that what one learns
\par
might change a decision. The cycle
\par
iterates as long as it is necessary to
\par
revise the model or there are opportu-
\par
nities to profitably collect informa-
\par
tion (i.e. reduce uncertainty). The
\par
exposition of the view of decision
\par
analysis illustrated in Figure 1. is
\par
enhanced by many examples in the
\par
introductory articles of the two-vol-
\par
ume set, as well as published works
\par
reporting the results and findings of
\par
real applications of decision analysis
\par
in industry and government.
\par
The volumes are organized into six
\par
major areas- Introduction and
\par
Overview, Applications, Health and
\par
Safety, Professional Practice, Techni-
\par
cal Contributions, and Contributions
\par
from Psychology. Artificial intelli-
\par
gence researchers perhaps can benefit
\par
most from the sections on profession-
\par
al practice which contain papers on
\par
probability assessment, risk prefer-
\par
ence, and influence diagrams. Proba-
\par
bility assessment is the knowledge
\par
engineering task of helping an expert
\par
or decision maker express his infor-
\par
mation about an event as a probabili-
\par
ty. Risk preference refers to a theory
\par
describing an individual's propensity
\par
for engaging in risky actions. Influ-
\par
ence diagrams are a representation of
\par
decision problems under uncertainty
\par
which allows one to represent deci-
\par
sion and state variables, information
\par
flows, and value and utility relations
\par
in a directed graph. This representa-
\par
tion has been adapted by artificial
\par
intelligence researchers for doing
\par
strictly probabilistic (no decisions or
\par
values) reasoning (Pearl, 1986).
\par
The section on Technical Contribu-
\par
tions also contains several articles
\par
likely to be of interest to the AI com-
\par
munity-for example ``Information
\par
Value Theory', ``Value of Information
\par
Lotteries'', and ``Proximal Decision
\par
Analysis'' by Howard and ``The Eco-
\par
nomic Value of Analysis and Compu-
\par
tation'' by Matheson. These articles,
\par
most of which appeared originally in
\par
systems and management science
\par
journals, are technical works which
\par
very clearly and concisely develop and
\par
demonstrate key ideas in decision
\par
analysis. ``Proximal Decision Analy-
\par
sis,'' for example, shows how large
\par
decision problems can be analyzed
\par
with approximate methods by using
\par
derivative information and sensitivity
\par
analysis.
\par
Since it is a compilation of papers,
\par
the Howard and Matheson volumes
\par
contain very concise, insightful treat-
\par
ments of the basic theoretical under-
\par
pinnings of decision analysis such as
\par
basic probability, utility theory, and
\par
the decision analysis cycle, as well as
\par
focused discussion of several more
\par
advanced topics including value of
\par
information, probability assessment,
\par
sensitivity analysis, and others. How-
\par
ever, the volumes can be somewhat
\par
frustrating to use because much of the
\par
basic material on decision analysis is
\par
repeated in several articles; while
\par
some of the important technical
\par
material is difficult to find. A major
\par
deficiency for an uninformed reader is
\par
the lack of an index to the topics in
\par
the various papers.
\par
In addition, there are some gaps in
\par
the exposition. For example, there is
\par
no complete discussion of Howard's
\par
distinctions between value [risk-free
\par
scoring of alternative states) and utili-
\par
ty (scoring of alternative states under
\par
uncertainty) distinctions and associat-
\par
ed theoretical treatments. Except in
\par
extreme life and death situations,
\par
Howard generally reduces each
\par
attribute of a multiattribute decision
\par
problem to a single numeraire -
\par
money-in order to facilitate value
\par
function assessment. Utility is
\par
assessed by applying a transformation
\par
encoding risk preference to the value
\par
function. This an important heuristic
\par
that Howard has found useful for the
\par
business, strategic, and health and
\par
safety problems which have been the
\par
subject of much of his professional
\par
practice and research, however the
\par
formal basis and practical efficacy of
\par
this perspective is not discussed in
\par
these volumes except by example.
\par
This method for encoding values and
\par
preferences is an additional facet
\par
which distinguishes the Howard-
\par
Matheson school of decision analysis
\par
from other perspectives. D e c i s i o n
\par
Analysis and Behavioral Research
\par
represents one of these other perspec-
\par
tives. Howard and Matheson are engi-
\par
neers in that they view decision anal-
\par
ysis fundamentally as a technology for
\par
assisting people making difficult deci-
\par
sions. They regard contributions from
\par
psychology peripherally as corroborat-
\par
ing that people make errors in reason-
\par
ing and decision making in uncertain
\par
situations and secondarily as informa-
\par
tion for improving assessment proce-
\par
dures. Decision Analysis and Behav-
\par
ioral Research is a collaborative effort
\par
of von Winterfeldt, a psychologist,
\par
and Edwards, a decision analyst, origi-
\par
nally trained as a psychologist. This
\par
collaboration results in a book which
\par
is grounded in its discussion of the
\par
prescriptive facets of decision analysis
\par
with respect to its behavioral psychol-
\par
ogy precursors and implications. The
\par
relevance of psychological research to
\par
both the theory and practice of deci-
\par
sion analysis is integral to this vol-
\par
ume.
\par
This book contrasts with the
\par
Howard and Matheson work in that it
\par
is a unified and complete treatment of
\par
a wide range of topics in decision
\par
analysis. The technical material is in
\par
most cases first presented informally
\par
by way of example, and then followed
\par
by a more rigorous, mathematical
\par
treatment. Overall, the emphasis is on
\par
verbal description and motivation
\par
wherever possible. In addition to sev-
\par
eral chapters on basic decision analy-
\par
sis and probability, there are several
\par
sections on more advanced topics,
\par
including a fairly complete discussion
\par
of probability measurement from a
\par
SPRING 1988 125personal or subjective point of view.
\par
This is coupled with a discussion of
\par
the insights that psychological litera-
\par
ture provides for careful and accurate
\par
assessment of probabilities. Chapters
\par
on inference include a discussion of
\par
Bayesian statistics (use of Baye's rule
\par
for subjective probability in statistical
\par
inference). The more general inference
\par
problem is also discussed, including
\par
an extended example of legal decision
\par
making and reasoning under uncer-
\par
tainty. This discussion should be of
\par
particular interest to AI researchers,
\par
since it is an attempt by Bayesian ana-
\par
lysts to tackle complex real-world
\par
inference problems with probability
\par
machinery.
\par
Von Winterfeldt and Edwards con-
\par
tend that decisions are often difficult
\par
due to lack of clarity about the deci-
\par
sion maker's values and preferences
\par
about the outcomes resulting from
\par
alternative courses of action. There-
\par
fore, they emphasize careful structur-
\par
ing and assessment of preferences as
\par
being central to the process of doing
\par
decision analysis. The book encapsu-
\par
lates and extends previous work on on
\par
value and utility measurement
\par
(Keeney and Raiffa, 1976) for both sin-
\par
gle attribute and multiattribute cases.
\par
One of the major research results of
\par
this volume is a rejection of the dis-
\par
tinction between notions of value and
\par
utility . This distinction has played a
\par
major role in most other work in deci-
\par
sion theory and decision analysis,
\par
including that of Howard and Mathe-
\par
son. Von Winterfeldt and Edwards
\par
develop a set of arguments, on both
\par
theoretical and practical grounds, for
\par
rejection of the distinction, while at
\par
the same time recapitulating previous
\par
