\textbf{2.2.4. }Parametric Production Frontiers
\par
Parametric production frontiers are representations of average performance for a population of
\par
firms or for one firm over a time series of periods. They involve full mathematical specification
\par
of the relationships between inputs and output. While strong econometric methods exist to es-
\par
timate parameters for production functions, economists recognize that the model specification or
\par
choice of functional form for example, Cobb-Douglas, translog or min-flex Laurent can influence
\par
parameter estimates. Varian [35] has argued that the state-of-the-art in parametric production
\par
function estimation does not yet provide for the validation of the functional form to be tested.
\par
Recent work utilizing these methods has been done by Jonscher [l6, 171 and Loveman
\par
[21] whose objective was to isolate the economic value of information resources in the American
\par
economy. Jonscher presented a microeconomic model which specifies a functional relation between
\par
output efficiency and information inputs. In a subsequent paper he utilized transiog production
\par
frontier estimation to explore the effect of falling IT costs on total economic output over the last
\par
twenty years.
\par
2.2.5. Non-parametric Production Frontiers
\par
Non-parametric frontiers capture "best observed" or "state-of-the-art" production technologies,
\par
and so are likely to lie south-cvest of the empirically derived mean-value performance that
\par
parametric frontiers represent.4 Non-parametric frontiers are also known as "data envelopment"
\par
methods, since the frontier will create an envelope which contains all other input-output combina-
\par
tions of lesser efficiency. The measurement of best observed practice provides management with a
\par
set of exemplars for less efficient units in a firm. Moreover, the approach does not require a
\par
pre-specified functional form for the IT process box or require management to make assumptions
\par
that might have little basis in theory or practice.5 This is appropriate in view of how little
\par
management really knows about the workings of IT in the firm's creation of business value out-
\par
puts.
\par
Other authors have used non-parametric frontier analysis to relate IT investment to the ef-
\par
ficient production of firm level business value outputs. Stabell and Forsund \textbf{[30] }attempted to ex-
\par
plain efficiency for a large data set of Norwegian firms by investigating a set of surrogate
