\textbf{3. Identifying 'Business Value Linkages' for IT Investments}
\par
\textbf{Using BVL Correlation}
\par
\textbf{3.1. Requirements for Developing a Business Value Linkage}
\par
A basic requirement to the development of a business value linkage for an IT is that manage-
\par
ment spend the time to carefully develop a conceptual understanding of what kinds of impacts
\par
the IT has in the firm, and at what organizational levels they are felt. The direct, tangible out-
\par
puts of an IT will almost never capture the full range of impacts. Instead, management needs to
\par
begin by identifying the direct outputs that arise from an IT, and from there continue to make a
\par
conceptual tracing of the links between the direct outputs and higher order impacts in the firm.
\par
ATM networks provide a nice illustrative example.' Even a casual observer might note that
\par
transactions processed at an \textbf{ATh4 }alone do not tell the whole story of its business value to a
\par
bank. Yet transactions processed are a readily quantifiable direct output which is tangible at the
\par
local ATM level. If we continue to trace the impacts of the ATM to the branch level, we could
\par
argue that transactions processed provide an indirect means to gauge the additional service value
\par
that customers enjoy, which does not require the bank t o invest in more branch labor. In some
\par
situations, labor costs may even be displaced directly through a reduction in teller staff. An
\par
ATM network manager's business value assessment problcm would be to test whether this latter
\par
impact can be substantiated.
\par
