3.3.3. Theoretical Derivn50n of a Firm's Production Technology
\par
T o complete our contacts with the primal and dual analysis of microeconomics we turn to a
\par
brief discussion of the theoretical derivation of a firm's production technology from its cost func-
\par
tion. X basic result in micro\textasciitilde conomics is that all the infor\textasciitilde nation necessary to reconstruct a firm's
\par
production function is present in a well-behaved cost function. More precisely, given a cost func-
\par
tion \textbf{c(w,y) }for a vector of input prices w and output y, we can solve for the conditional factor
\par
demand for an input, \textbf{x(w,y), }via Shephard's lemma the derivative property, provided the cost
\par
function is twice differentiabIe and input resource prices are strictly greater than zero.8 Sub-
\par
sequent manipulation of the conditional factor demand will yield \textbf{\emph{ffx), }}a function corresponding to
\par
the production technology of the firm.
\par
If it were possible to derive one in practice, a production function of this sort would be the ideal
\par
statement of the business value linkage for IT. But, we are typically prevented from doing so
\par
because it is hard to write a representative cost function linking the higher order impacts of IT
\par
and the investment itself. \textbf{An }additional difficulty is that there is no guarantee that the produc-
\par
tion technology of a firm ac\textasciitilde ually meets the strict require\textasciitilde nents needed to derive a unique produc-
\par
tion technology. A third ccncern is that such a production technology would need to capture all
\par
the relevant aspects simultaneously. This means that we would require a flawless method for
\par
separating out various kinds of costs related to production.
\par
Lest we leave the impression that the results of microeconomics are swamped by the com-
