production say, in a division or department, and higher order IT-related outputs can be iden-
\par
tified via BVL correlation, this theoretical approach is more useful. The remaining difficulty for a
\par
manager is to be assured that the necessary structure on the production technology can be reason-
\par
ably assumed.
\par
\textbf{3.4. Tests of BVL Correlation: Caveats When Regression is Used}
\par
Implementing our B'I\% regression procedure requires considerable time on the part of manage-
\par
ment to thoroughly understand how IT fits into the firm's production. Since we do not advocate
\par
defining or testing a large number of models, management will need to do a substantial amount
\par
of reflecting up front about the role of the IT they wish to assess and the outlines of the business
\par
value linkage. Management should attempt to characterize the best possible model based on its a
\par
\textbf{\emph{priori }}beliefs about the "possible" impacts of IT and other variables affecting the output. If these
\par
expectations are later found to be unrealistic, modifications can be made to the original model.
\par
These models should be developed with the support of and intermittent feedback from senior
\par
management, who will make decisions based on the business value measures for IT. Without the
\par
commitment of senior management, the developers of models which test for the business value
\par
linkage are likely to meet with little success or acceptance of their work.
\par
Data availability and collection pose another serious problem which can hinder the use of B'iZ
\par
correlation. For some kinds of ITS it may not be feasible to collect data that management believes
\par
are relevant. The effort necessary to develop the data base of information to carry out a BVL
\par
correlation may be unattractive given the magnitude of the IT investment. Where management
\par
is making an investment in an entirely new IT, data may just not be available. To gauge its
\par
usefulness in a variety of circumstances, management should familiarize itself with BVL correla-
\par
tion by evaluating previous investments to support ex \textbf{\emph{post }}cost-benefit analyses. This will also
\par
provide a baseline of performance against which to evaluate the impacts of future changes to
\par
strategic information systems.
