\textbf{4.2. The IT Assessment Problem in the Corporate Treasury}
\par
T o meet some of these requirements, banks and other third party software vendors have
\par
designed treasury management information systems with various types of application software
\par
designed t o support the treasury function of a company. Treasury workstations generally deliver
\par
a packaged set of capabilities, which in some cases can be customized to the specific treasury
\par
requirements of large volume clients. Saturnia, Houlihan and Coluccio \textbf{[29] }suggest some ex-
\par
amples of what a treasury workstation can do for a corporate treasurer:
\par
Communication: Facilitates the electronic transmission of data from the bank to the
\par
treasurer's workstation and pre-formatted funds transfer requests from the treasurer
\par
to the bank.
\par
Balance Reporting: Arranges and presents historical bank account information in
\par
report format, allowing the user to review bank balances, transaction details and float
\par
information.
\par
Intraday Transactions: Allows the user to obtain ban\textasciicircum{} balances and detailed debit and
\par
credit information on a same-day basis.
\par
\textbf{e }Target Balance: Provides the ability to track and :arget required bank balances as
\par
well as to perform sensitivity analysis for cash flow planning.
\par
Investment/Debt: Tracks the maturities of securities in a portfolio and permits the
\par
user t o initiate investment transactions and to receive confirmation of purchases and
\par
sales of securities.
\par
