\textbf{4.3. }BVL Correlation for the Economic Impacts of TMIS
\par
Next we turn to the development of a simplified business value linkage for a TMTS, using
\par
Krcmar and Lucas' [19] results as our point of departure. In particular, we need to construct a
\par
BIT, correlation test which would provide stronger evidence to suggest that TMISs have such
\par
favorable impacts in commercial banking. Our initial investigation of bank production processes
\par
involving a TMIS would begin by attempting to identify a set of direct and potential indirect,
\par
business value outputs. From a bank's perspective, some of the outputs which provide expense
\par
reductions include:
\par
automated funds transfers which require no bank intervention;
\par
automated balance inquiries which require no bank staff;
\par
fewer funds transfer errors, due to pre-formatted input screens;
\par
growth in balances due to banking relationship concentration.
