\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
Pervasive and costly automation of information handling and other activities in the office, the
\par
factory, the marketplace and the executive suite continues to put pressure on senior managers to
\par
assess the extent to which information technology IT contributes to their strategic goals. In a
\par
recent survey of 26 firms, for example, Nolan, Norton and Company \textbf{[25] }found that IT expen-
\par
ditures had increased fourfold. Such investment levels reveal the revolutionary developments in
\par
the information technologies that corporations are rushing to incorporate into their operations. At
\par
the same time they also suggest the extraordinary expectations the investors have about the
\par
potential returns that IT investments should be producing. Reliance on the popular literature
\par
suggests that IT is a strong tool by which firms can gain increased market share, a broader cus-
\par
tomer base, and overall competitive advantage. Consider the Merrill LynchjBanc One Ohio Cash
\par
Management Account, large regional commercial banks' use of automated teller network \textbf{ATM}
\par
participation, and the airlines' use of proprietary reservation systems.l Because the beneficial
\par
impacts may seem obvious, it is unlikely that managers would think that any of these ITS present
\par
very difficult performance assessment problems.
\par
It is likely that a number of other similar investments in IT have been less favorably rewarded.
\par
How are these cases to be e-\textasciitilde aluated confidently by management? What claims can be made for
\par
the extent to which an IT with less obvious, higher order impacts in a corporation can be at-
\par
tributed 'business value'? What kinds of methodologies are suited to handling the difficulties of
\par
enterprise level IT performance assessment? Though managers might wish it were otherwise, it is
\par
not easy to make strong assertions about the extent to which IT investments dctually have the
\par
desired impact in practice.
\par
We will argue that microeconomics affords a useful vantage point from which to develop
\par
stronger methods for IT p2rformance assessment. Traditional planning tools for IT including
\par
budgets, project screening, and return on investment ROI models take a relatively "micro"
\par
view of the investment, and application of these tools may not always be appropriate in view of
\par
the range of technoIogica1 developments in the last two decades. In this era of expanding oppor-
\par
tunity for innovative, 'big hit' IT investments, managers require performance assessment tools
\par
that capture and translate IT'S impact on the achievement of enterprise level business goals into
\par
meaningful and convincing managerial information. This necessarily entails a "macro" view of
