methodology which we term "business value linkage \textbf{BVL }correlation." We argue that the ques-
\par
tion of which higher level IT impacts to include in a performance evaluation can be reduced to a
\par
set of hypotheses which a manager should attempt to test.
\par
We believe that use of BVL correlation will enable a broader range of IT investments to be
\par
adequately evaluated so that management need not succumb to the temptation to make IT in-
\par
vestments on "faith in the future." The method we propose puts the spotlight on the investiga-
\par
tion of production processes involving IT. It does not presuppose selection of a particular
\par
methodology by which to evaluate IT process boxes once the inputs and outputs have been
\par
defined and measured. Instead it provides management with a means to ensure that it is evaluat-
\par
ing the "right" outputs, regardless of how subsequent evaluation is carried out.
\par
1.1. Organization of the Paper
\par
In the section which immediately follows we lay out formal definitions for some of the terms
\par
that we will use to describe the methodology. We further review other approaches which have
\par
appeared in the IT assessment literature to determine the extent to which higher order output
\par
validation has been addressed. In Section \textbf{3 }we present our BVL correlation approach, ways in
\par
which to focus the analytic process leading to the creation of testable BVL models and some
\par
caveats for the potential user. We argue that the business value linkages of IT can be tested by
\par
providing evidence for links to proposed higher order outputs. Then, in Section 4 this procedure is
\par
applied to a generalized treasury management information system \textbf{TMIS. }Our example sug-
\par
gests the potential of BVL correlation as a means to improve upon current approaches where
\par
management's intuition and faith play the dominant role in the identification of outputs sub-
\par
mitted to cost-benefit analyses for ITS. The paper concludes with a discussion of where we think
\par
the method will provide the best results.
\par
\textbf{2. }Basic Definitions and Review of Approaches for IT
\par
Performance Assessment
\par
In order to break ground for the exposition of our new approach to IT performance assessment,
\par
it is worthwhile a t the outset to clearly define a basic set of terms that will be used throughout
\par
this paper. This will also enable us to contrast our own views more sharply with those of other
\par
authors that have proposed various approaches to IT performance assessment.
