\par
\textbf{2.1. Defining Terms: 'Business Value' and the 'Business Value Linkage'}
\par
Our approach to assessing the impact of ITS in the financial services and other industries in-
\par
volves identifying its role in the value maximization of the firm. Where the availability of data
\par
and appropriate methodology allow we seek to quantify the focused and diffuse impacts of IT.
\par
Our referent discipline for the analysis of the 'business value' of IT is the theory of the firm.
\par
Managers possess the goals of maximizing the value of the firm, its profitability and revenue
\par
flows, subject to the constraints of limited budgets. Managers also attempt to make well-informed
\par
decisions about the purchase and mobilization of a variety of input resources which can be or-
\par
ganized to produce outputs that possess economic value in a market. IT possesses 'business value'
\par
when its application contributes to management's goal of profit maximization. The organizational
\par
level of the impact determines whether the impacts are strategic or operational, diffuse or
\par
focused. At both levels, managers should have tools and mechanisms a t their disposal to enable
\par
them to gauge the impact of their resource allocation decisions.
\par
Business value can be measured in a number of ways. For example, the "net economic
\par
contribution" of an input resource to an output product or service is measured by the unit profit
\par
sales price less cost of the output, multiplied by the marginal product of the input. This method
\par
presumes that the marginal revenue product can be readily identified, not very often the case for
\par
IT investments. In other situations where management feels it is difficult to discern the relevant
\par
underlying costs, net economic contribution may be replaced by "marginal value product."
\par
However, this still requires management to identify a market price for the output or otherwise
\par
attribute some value to the output within the firm.
\par
We further term the process which links IT inputs to firm level economic outputs as the
\par
"business value linkage" for IT. Management's ability to measure the business value of an IT
\par
requires that that the business value linkage from IT in-iestment to higher order, economic im-
\par
pacts be defined. Some of the impacts of IT will be first order, and relativeiy easier t o measure.
\par
However, some investments yield much higher order impacts which will be very difficult to
\par
measure. Consider the difficulty that business value assessment for firm-wide electronic mail
\par
would present. Unfortunately, this is precisely the sort of problem that every manager doing per-
\par
formance assessment for IT can expect to face.
\par
"IT business value measures", simply stated, are measures of the economic impact of an I T
\par
within the business value linkage defined by management. Business value measures should
\par
originate in the basic business goals of a firm which in turn acquire coherence as they relate to
