senior management's vision of the firm's corporate strategy. Since ITS are likely to have multiple
\par
impacts on a firm with varied levels of focus, it follows that a number of business value measures,
\par
rather than a single measure, should be developed to best inform management about the aspects
\par
of IT performance most relevant to the success of its strategy. Though unitary measures will con-
\par
tinue to be attractive and simple for managers to understand -- the net present value NPV of
\par
an IT investment is a good example -- it is unlikely that they will provide the robustness,
\par
validity, responsiveness, informativeness and transferability necessary to support powerful
\par
management insights into the effectiveness of IT investmenfs.
\par
\textbf{2.2. Critique of Approaches}
\par
Overall, we believe there will be substantial payoffs to management from methodological
\par
progress in the IT assessment area. We should state a t the outset that we do not expect that any
\par
one approach will capture and/or validate the range of diffuse impacts provided by IT. We also
\par
expect that some impacts will either never be captured or not be worthwhile for management to
\par
consider. But to achieve success in evaluating a broad range of ITS, work on this issue should
\par
proceed in a step-wise, scientific manner to initially gain experience with easier to quantify im-
\par
pacts, and then progress to IT impacts which are increasingly more difficult to quantify and
\par
validate.
\par
At this point, our discussion turns to recent approaches to the I T assessment Some
\par
approaches have already won support and acceptance among managements; others, the results of
\par
more recent research efforts, are less well-tested, but appear to hold out significant promise. In
\par
our critique, we will conside: the tools already available to managers in light of whether they can
\par
be adapted or naturally extended to support IT output validation, rather than as performance
\par
methods in isolation. Figure 2, shown below, presents an executive summary of the methods we
\par
will discuss further below.
