2.2.1. \textbf{Cost }- Benefit Analysis Methods
\par
There is already a well-developed tradition among managements to treat IT investment analysis
\par
in much the same way as investments in property, plant and equipment. Typically, one computes
\par
the NPV of two sets of offsetting cash flows, one representing revenues and the other representing
\par
costs. The problem in evaluating cash flows, of course, is that costs are normally quite tangible,
\par
while the benefits of an IT investment besides operational cost savings are more difficult to assess.
\par
Chismar [8] commented that one potential outcome of IT assessment via NPV or discounted cash
\par
flow DCF methods is that they will lead to under-investment in IT, as acceptable projects are
\par
turned down due to the relative non-quantifiability of higher order impacts.
\par
Recognizing this problem, Emery [13! recommended extending NPV/DCF-based cost benefit
\par
analyses for IT by considering intangible benefits in terms of the costs management is willing to
\par
bear to support them. He stopped short of suggesting that intangibles be assigned monetary
\par
values by management. Strassman [31] argued that a logical approach to the improvement of
\par
firm-level productivity via IT was to make the information systems executive more accountable
\par
for the management of non-computer costs, e.g., office machines and personnel, related to infor-
\par
mation handling activities. \textbf{As }an outgrowth of his earlier work, Strassman 1321 later recom-
\par
mended a market-based value added measure for the worth of a system. Also based on the well-
\par
accepted methods of accounting and finance, his measure involved a ratio of system-generated
\par
market value less input resource costs to the overhead cost of labor.
\par
NPV/DCF assessment is particularly well-suited to the inclusion of a validation procedure for
\par
the relatively intangible outputs that are considered apart from tangible costs and benefits.
\par
Provided the output validation test can be crafted by managers in a straightforward way and its
\par
results are readily interpreted, the addition of such a procedure would strengthen the results
\par
managers might gain from hTV assessments of IT performance. Strassman's approach suffers, in
\par
our view, from the assump2ion that the system-generated outputs are evident, measurable and
\par
that they can be subsequently assigned some market value. Again, some validation process is
\par
appropriate for the outputs to increase the internal validity of the measure. Both methods dis-
\par
cussed here also lack sensitivity to the strategic role which IT can play in the firm. More
\par
generally, Myers [24] has commented that DCF methods suffer in their ability to capture the es-
\par
sence of value in strategic plans.
\par
Recently, Nolan [25] suggested developing a "new economics of computing" which stresses the
\par
need "to reflect the strategic linkage between the IT investment and the business strategies of the
