\par
value to firm. Along these lines, Cash and Iconsynski \textbf{161, }and Clemons and Kimbrough \textbf{191 }have
\par
suggested the need for frameworks which explicitly suggest where firms might apply IT to create
\par
sustainable competitive advantage. Some obvious applications involve changes in channels of
\par
supply, development of barriers to entry and switching costs, IT-driven cost leadership via low
\par
cost operations, and reduction in transactions costs t o effect sales. Recently, banks have begun to
\par
investigate the viability of "electronic data interchange" by placing their order-entry systems on
\par
the factory floor. This is meant to just-in-time inventory management and link the buyer to its
\par
suppliers. It also simultaneously handles the necessary billing and payment details via computer-
\par
delivered advices and electronic funds transfers. In this context, McFarlan 1221 recognized that the
\par
potential of IT lies in the extent to which it influences the structure of competition in the
\par
marketplace and changes the way a firm can compete.
