While the "marginal analysis" of the value-added chain approach does much to focus manage-
\par
ment attention on strategic opportunities for IT, it does relatively less to provide management
\par
with business value measures for the strategic gains a firm makes by investing in IT. Value-added
\par
approaches offer a natural point of departure for business value linkage descriptions and make
\par
sense in the framework of the theory of the firm. Moreover, they may suggest higher order impact
\par
validation tests. But, since value-added chain approaches do not actually constitute a theory for
\par
the workings of production processes creating strategic IT impacts, we suggest the need to look
\par
elsewhere for descriptive measures of performance in a value-added chain setting.
\par
Strassman \textbf{[33] }and van Nievelt \textbf{1341 }are also often-quoted proponents of a related "value-added"
\par
assessment approach. These authors split the firm into two realms: one invoiving direct produc-
\par
tion operations, and another involving the overhead of the firm management, computing, etc..
\par
Their approach seeks first to identify the impact of management on firm performance. They
\par
recommend use of measures called "return on management" ROM and "management
\par
productivity". RObf is defined a s gross profit before tax profit divided by the costs of employed
\par
and purchased management services and management capital. Tliis ratio is roughly "management
\par
value added" divided by "management cost." IT investments initially are lumped into the non-
\par
operating side and subsequeatly backed out in order to explain inter-division and inter-firm per-
\par
formance variations. Their treatment of business value linkages for IT are only implicit in the
\par
cost accounting arithmetic they perform; i.e., the business value outputs for IT, along with the
