\par
Just as the purpose or function to be served by productivity measures will influence which
\par
measures are relevant, so will management's purpose in conducting an IT business value assess-
\par
ment influence the kind of business value linkage it seeks to define. If the firm is in a cost-cutting
\par
mode, management may wish to justify investment in an IT based on the costs it will enable the
\par
firm to save. This would require a different business value linkage than if management wished to
\par
demonstrate that the IT will lead to increased market share in a product area.
\par
Stating that management must spend considerable time up front in the definition of a business
\par
value linkage does not imply that we believe, once management has defined the business value
\par
linkage, that it is immutable. Indeed, the business value linkage could be defined differently for
\par
an initial IT investment than for a later performance review. This is consistent with Ginzburg's
\par
view:
\par
\emph{It i s only once we understand how the new information will be used that its value}
\par
\emph{can be estimated. Thus, efforts to quantify benefits should focus on the changes i n}
\par
\emph{organizational process which will result from changes to information systems. }\textbf{[14!}
\par
It makes sense to reconsider the business value linkage defined a t the time an IT investment was
\par
initially evaluated, because it is very likely that unintended uses and benefits may have arisen
\par
which promote cost reduction or revenue enhancement. This is a contingency approach similar to
\par
Nolan's suggestion to track the "strategic interlock" of IT investments.
\par
\textbf{3.2. Indirect Output Validation: A 'Business Value Linkage' Procedure}
\par
The philosophy behind the approach which we will describe below is that it is possible to at-
\par
tribute business value to information technology investments, provided there is evidence of a busi-
\par
ness value linkage. In this section we discuss a means to provide such evidence using tests of cor-
\par
relation for IT as input variables into, or design variables affecting, an economic production
\par
process, and that results in higher order firm outputs. If it can be shown that the model tested
\par
has explanatory power and the I T variables are significant, then this provides evidence to suggest
\par
a business value attribution to the IT is warranted. Assuming a regression model were used, for
\par
instance, an estimate for the actual amount of business value is given by the sum of the values
\par
of the coefficients of the IT design variables in the regression model multiplied by the value of
\par
the higher order output, provided the model has reasonable explanatory power and the IT vari-
\par
ables are significant.
\par
The basic procedure for creating testable BVL correlation models can be described as follows:
