We close our discussion o i the caveats of using BVL regression for IT-influenced production
\par
processes by again recommending that the reader keep in mind the technical interpretation of
\par
'significant variables' in regression models. The presence of a significant explanatory variable re-
\par
lated to IT with a positive sign on its coefficient provides evidence that it is worthwhile for
\par
management to attribute some business value from a higher order output of the firm to the IT. It
\par
improves on arbitrary decisions about which outputs to include and which to exclude in cost
\par
benefit analysis, but users of BVL regression should recognize that regression rarely provides
\par
"conclusive" evidence. Thus, although an IT variable may be significant in a BVL regression
\par
model with a high multiple correlation coefficient and a low standard error of regression, it is still
\par
possible that a business valxe attribution can be made in error.
