In this section we present an example involving treasury workstations to illustrate how our BVL
\par
correlation procedure can be used to provide evidence for the indirect outputs of IT. Many I T
\par
applications in the financiai services industry present very similar assessment difficulties; their
\par
direct outputs may not possess any "economic" or "business value" content while their higher
\par
order, indirect outputs may be difficult to definitively link to the I T and measure. Direct outputs
\par
constitute focused impacts while indirect outputs result from more diffuse impacts. An added fea-
\par
ture of the IT we discuss is it has revenue and cost impacts. This enables the explicit considera-
\par
tion of primal and dual side output validation.
\par
\textbf{4.1. An Application of BVL Correlation to Treasury Workstation Assessment}
\par
Treasury management is the management of a firm's cash and debt positions. Good treasury
\par
management reduces financing costs by mobilizing f \textasciitilde{} n d s within the firm and improves
\par
profitability by leaving no idle balances uninvested. Increasingly unstable economic conditions,
\par
rising interest rates, and the shift to floating currency exchange rates in international markets in
\par
the early to mid-1970s has made more careful treasury management a necessity. About the same
\par
time, however, advances in data processing and time-sharing computer-based services by banks
\par
have made improved management of day-to-day treasury operations possible. In today's environ-
\par
ment, treasury management has become even more complex, calling for on-line, up-to-the-moment
\par
balance positions, and the ability to move money around the globe to where it is needed most in
\par
an instant.
