\par
The first two are direct outputs and could be evaluated in a straightforward manner: a first
\par
order approximation by the bank of the business value of these would be given by the costs of
\par
having bank employees perform these activities, in the absence of the TMIS. The third could also
\par
be approached from this perspective, but there may be a more diffuse benefit as well.
\par
One possible approach to evaluating the third is to construct a statistical test to determine
\par
whether there is evidence that supports the assertion that TMIS use delivers cost savings via
\par
lower funds transfer error rates. Since most banks capture information about funds transfer er-
\par
rors, it would be sufficient for the test to evaluate whether there are significant differences be-
\par
tween customer error rates in pre-TMIS and post-TMIS implementation settings. Once the bank
\par
has built up a significant TMIS customer base, error statistics would be tested in the pre/post
\par
TMIS conditions. Assuming the bank can identify explicit costs for its funds transfer errors, this
\par
cost can then be applied \textbf{as }a surrogate for the value of the incremental reduction in transfer
\par
errors made by the corporate treasurer's staff. If there is no significant difference in the pre-TMIS
\par
and post-TMIS groups, then management would not include error reduction as a quantifiable
\par
TMIS benefit. The focus on \textbf{\emph{ex post }}evaluation is justified by management's need to develop a
\par
baseline for the technology's contributions. It can also guide future value assessments \textbf{as }manage-
\par
ment decides to add new capabilities.
\par
From results of Lucas and Krcmar, management may believe that the presence of TMIS en-
\par
