\textbf{\emph{5. }Conclusion}
\par
Developing and testing a business value linkage for an IT should be a useful thought process for
\par
senior management. In a sense, "positive" results will be guaranteed: management can obtain
\par
guidance to include or exclude outputs which might justify their investments in IT. Knowing
\par
what to exclude is often just as important as ascertaining that all the appropriate impacts have
\par
been recognized. This can also boost senior management's confidence about making decisions
\par
based on the results of previous IT performance assessments. In addition, our approach provides a
\par
solid basis on which to begin to build new cost-benefit models for information systems. For ex-
\par
ample, performance evaluation for decision support systems, executive information systems, and
\par
expert systems is just becoming an issue with management, as firms begin to gain experience with
\par
their development and use.
\par
X related use of business value assessment is t o enable management to operationalize the quality
\par
gains which IT can provide. Our example involving the deployment of a treasury management
\par
system provides an appropriate context in which to test for quality gains in the payments process.
\par
BVL correlation tests could be run on alternate configurations of the same basic system a t beta-
\par
test sites, as product managers finalize the design of a treasury product involving information
\par
April 1988 , Center for Digital Economy Research
\par
Stem School of Business
\par
IVorking Paper IS-88-47Identifying Business Value Linkages Page 27
\par
technology. Since quality in funds transfer operations involves minimizing the occurrence and
\par
speeding the repair of errors, business value assessment can be used to identify opportunities to
\par
boost "back-office profit. \textbf{'I}
\par
Business value linkage assessment may also provide a means to test whether 'user satisfaction' is
\par
a useful surrogate for system value in specific corporate contexts. Currently, there is a con-
\par
troversy in the IS literature over whether user satisfaction with an information system is a
\par
relevant dimension in tagging its value. While our purpose in this article is not to make a judg-
\par
ment about the viability of measures of user satisfaction, the method we propose might be used to
\par
identify circumstances where user satisfaction is associated with hard, economic gains for the
\par
firm, and thus, is an appropriate surrogate.
\par
We believe the biggest benefits from BVL, correlation are likely to accrue in situations where
\par
careful retrospective study of IT investments is required to assist the evaluation of proposed IT
\par
investments. BVL assessment methods are strengthened when substantial data from ongoing
\par
operations are available. And, their results are more likely to influence senior managers if they
\par
provide enhanced insights beyond those of the trusted methods of managerial finance. Their
\par
primary usefulness will be i3 providing the chief executive officer and the chief financial officer
\par
with scientifically developed evidence about the impact of new information technologies at any
\par
level of the firm deemed to be relevant. Management decision, as ever, involves triangulating with
\par
multiple methods to an acceptable outcome; so much the better that the tools supporting this
\par
process provide sharp insights along the way.
