John Page, Fritz Lechleuthner and Loretta Hervey
\par
The impact of the current diffusion of information technologies has
\par
been compared to the societal transformation accompanying the indus-
\par
trial revolution. In the view of observers like Daniel Bell, however, a cru-
\par
cial difference lies in man's current awareness that such a transforma-
\par
tion is occurring, in his attempts to predict its long- and short-term
\par
consequences, and in the formulation of national policy responses.
\par
I t is a rare moment in cultural history when we can self-
\par
consciously witness a large-scale social transformation. Few
\par
persons realized, when the industrial revolution was beginning,
\par
the impact of what was taking place ... Today with our greater
\par
sensitivity to social consequences and the future ... we are more
\par
alert to the possible imports of this technological and social
\par
change; and this is all to the good, for to the extent to which we
\par
are that sensitive, we can try [to] estimate the consequences
\par
and decide which policies we should choose, consonant with the
\par
values we have, in order to shape, accept, or even reject the
\par
alternative futures that are available to us (Bell 1960).\textbf{The "Information Society" of the Future}
\par
The complex of social and economic changes resulting from
\par
widespread application of new information technologies is often referred
\par
to as the "Information Society". This phrase, however, is just one way of
\par
labeling the series of rapidly developing economic and social changes
\par
which have emerged in varying degrees in both market-economy and
\par
socialist countries since the 1950s. Other labels which have been coined
\par
include "the post industrial society" and the "third wave". I t seems rea-
\par
sonable to suggest that all these phrases refer to the same series of
\par
phenomena, starting perhaps from different points of entry, and
\par
emphasizing one aspect over others. The "information society" label
\par
focuses attention on what many believe to be a root cause of the
\par
phenomena we observe: the growth of electronics, computers and
\par
telecommunications, in a word, the microelectronics revolution.
\par
If we were mainly concerned with the economic phenomena, we
\par
might interpret the same phenomena in terms of cycles of innovation or
\par
"long waves". If we were particularly interested in the changing life-
\par
styles of the "third wave", we would take the microelectronics revolution
\par
as given, and discuss the new freedom it granted from the total work syn-
\par
chronization of industrial civilization. In this paper, the chosen point of
\par
entry is the effects of new information technology, but as will become
\par
clear when we discuss issues and impacts, the choice of entry does not
\par
seem to determine the shape of the landscape.
\par
There is a large and growing volume of literature on the information
\par
society. Futurologists. social scientists, and technologists have focused
\par
on the forms a completely computerized society could take by the early
\par
twenly-first century. They have looked as well a t short,er-term issues
\par
associated with the transition to this future--including employment, re-
\par
structuring of the educational system, and the maintenance of national
\par
economic competitiveness. The scale of analysis ranges from the global
\par
to t.he individual enterprise, and methods are as varied as trend extrapo-
\par
lation, case studies, anci econometric analysis. The diversity of
\par
approaches reflects the pervasiveness and diversity of the new informa-
\par
Lion t.echnologies thernselves.\textbf{\emph{V k w s }}of \textbf{\emph{the future. }}Masuda (1980) has traced the evolution of infor-
\par
mation technologies from post-World War 11 applications in national
\par
defense and space exploration programs to business and public service
\par
applications in the 1960s and 1970s, and finally to increasing use of per-
\par
sonal computers hooked up to computer networks in the 1980s and
\par
beyond. Visions of the final product of this evolution--a completely com-
\par
puterized society--vary from the utopian to the deeply pessimistic.
\par
Masuda looks forward to an age of "computopia" beginning in the
\par
early twenty-first century. In his "universal society of plenty", informa-
\par
tion and knowledge replace goods and services as primary societal pro-
\par
ducts. Masuda predicts that the center of production will shift from the
\par
factory to the "global information utilityv--a computer-based public
\par
infrastructure consisting of information networks and data banks, to
\par
which all citizens will have access. Freed from the need to labor for sub-
\par
sistence. man will have time for leisure, self-improvement and commun-
\par
ity activities; with the aid of the global information utility, education will
\par
be greatly expanded, accessible to all, without income, time and place
\par
restrictions.
\par
Although Masuda points to the danger that computerized manage-
\par
ment systems and centralized data banks kould become powerful instru-
\par
ments of social control, h e is optimistic that changes in human values
\par
will curb this tendency. He believes that as information values replace
\par
material values, emphasis will be laid on the expansion of individual cog-
\par
nitive capabilities rather than material consumpt,ion, and competition
\par
will be replaced by cooperation.
\par
In contrast, analysts such as Nora and Minc (1980) survey the future
\par
computerized society with less confidence. They agree that it will be
\par
highly productive and much less labor-intensive, but are unsure if
\par
society can adjust to the new work situation without conflict. They ask
\par
what will happen when work is no longer a central value underlying
\par
social organization, and whether the problem of distributing the tasks
\par
and r \textasciitilde{} w n r d s of t.he remaining work will produce confrontation between
\par
competing groups. They wonder as well if the varying ability of individu-
\par
als to adjust to "processed" computerized language could exacerbatesocial inequality. The title of the final chapter of Nora and Minc's book
\par
\textbf{\emph{The C o m p u t e r i z a t i o n of Sbciety, }}"Will a Computerized Society Be a
\par
Society of Cultural Conflicts?", reflects these questions.
\par
\textbf{\emph{S o r t e r - t e r m p e r s p e c t i v e s . }}Despite their differing views, both
\par
Mzsuda and Nora and Minc emphasize the element of choice in the forrn
\par
that the computerized information society will take. Japan's 1972 "Plan
\par
for the Information Society" is designed to move the nation toward
\par
Masuda's "computopia" by testing some of its components (including
\par
community communications information systems, regional remote con-
\par
trol medical systems, computer-oriented education, and regional pollu-
\par
tion prevention systems) (Masuda 1980). Nora and Minc's assessment of
\par
the impacts of information technology is explicitly presented as a spur
\par
to Government decision-makers to devise policies to avoid potentially
\par
negative socio-economic consequences.
\par
Thus forward-looking perspectives on the information society should
\par
not, i n our view, be regarded as deterministic models of the future. Pol-
\par
icy issues now confronting decision makers are not concerned with how
\par
to get from a known present to a (more or less) defined future state.
\par
Instead we suggest that the shorter-term impacts and issues (to be
\par
reviewed in Section 111) should be considered in the light of a transition
\par
towards a continuously evolving information society. Still, not all
\par
scenarios for the future are equally probable, and the longer term
\par
scenarios already discussed will provide guidelines for the analysis of
\par
shorter-term issues and policies, which in Daniel Bell's words, allow us to
\par
"shape, accept, or even reject the alternative futures available to us"
\par
(Bell 1980).
\par
\textbf{Today's Information Society}
\par
Whether or not "computopia" is cast as a faraway vision or a realiz-
\par
able future, there is a body of research describing, in quantitative terms,
\par
what may be regarded as the existing information society. Parker (1976)
\par
provided the key paper in this area, as he examined trends in the distri-
\par
bution of the US labor force, in the composition of the national income,
\par
and in personal consumption. The best-known section of his analysisconcerns changes in labor force composition. Following Porat's scheme
\par
(1974), Parker adds a forth category to the traditional division of employ-
\par
ment into the agriculture, industrial, and service sectors--namely the
\par
"information sector", consisting of all employees in the original three
\par
sectors whose main activity is associated with information processing.
\par
This new aggregation showed that in the mid-70s, the predominance of
\par
the service sector was declining; in turn, the information sector was
\par
rapidly taking over as the main constituent of employment. These find-
\par
ings were relatively insensitive to the use of conservative or liberal
\par
assumptions in the classification of occupations.
\par
The conclusion that perhaps more than half the total labor force in
\par
the United States is employed in the information sector has since been
\par
confirmed and extended by other authors--for example, Porat and Rubin
\par
(1977) and Rubin and Sapp (1981). In their analyses it is important to
\par
distinguish between the "information sector" and the "service sector".
\par
Previous work clearly demonstrated simultaneous growth in the service
\par
sector and decline in the agricultural sector and, later, in the industrial
\par
sector. When the information sector was separated out as a new com-
\par
ponent, it became clear t h a t t h e service sector had in fact begun to
\par
decline as a result of t h e replacehent of personal services by
\par
information-related services.
\par
These trends in labor distribution are paralleled by similar trends in
\par
distribution of US national income and personal consumption. Parker
\par
therefore seems justified in concluding that if a society may be charac-
\par
terized by its predominant areas of work, economic activity and personal
\par
consumption, then indeed we are already living in an information
\par
society. The continued rapid growth in the information sector was evi-
\par
denced by Strassmann (1982), in his examination of the US market for
\par
such products as office automation equipment and personal computers.
\par
He states that in 1981 the total market amounted to 9,850,000 units, pro-
\par
jected to rise to 40,700,000 by 1986.Parker (1976) points out that the information society has important
\par
qualitative, as well as quantitative characteristics. Machines which sig-
\par
nificantly enhance and extend man's information processing capability
\par
could potentially offer new possibilities of economic growth independent
\par
O F finite physical and energy resources. But in order for the information
\par
society to develop in directions favoring human values, structural and
\par
institutional adaptation may be required. For this reason implemen ta-
\par
tion of efFective policies in the next few decades is crucial.
