\textbf{The Need for Research}
\par
The socio-economic impacts of inform.ation technology are a subject
\par
of concern to reseachers, practitioners, and the political world itself. In
\par
fact, such prominent personalities as the late President Andropov,
\par
President Mitterand, and Prime Minister Thatcher have all made key pol-
\par
icy statements underlining the necessity to optimize the application of
\par
information technology i.n their countries. The need to develop effective
\par
policies a t the industrial and Governmental levels to deal with the issues
\par
(and opportunities) of the emergent information society suggests a con-
\par
sequent requirement for analysis and research.. Ever1 a cursory look atsome issues policy makers face in such areas as employment and the
\par
organization of work, industrial development, education and retraining,
\par
and public investment in information technology infrastructure indi-
\par
cates a level of complexity making it difficult to identify practical policy
\par
options. Indeed, some policy issues are concerned with questions of
\par
social or cultural values, rather than more objectively perceived factors.
\par
Apart from the complexity of these matters, it appears that events--
\par
in the shape of actual impacts of new information technology--are begin-
\par
ning to move so rapidly that individual ad \emph{h o c }policies are becoming
\par
increasingly inadequate for dealing with real situations by the time such
\par
policies are ready for implementation. While research itself cannot pro-
\par
vide automatic solutions, it seems that a carefully co-ordinated effort in
\par
policy-oriented research, taking into account impacts at the enterprise,
\par
national, and international levels, could provide a much needed back-
\par
ground against which practical policies could be devised and imple-
\par
mented by concerned authorities.
