nal research. \textbf{A }summary of possible topics is provided in Section IV of
\par
this report.\textbf{11. INFllRW\textbackslash 'IlON TECHNOLOGY, APPLICATIONS AND SOCIETY}
\par
The potential threats and benefits inherent in changes resulting
\par
from the new information technologies have been articulated in count-
\par
less papers and scholarly exchanges. Study of its influence on society
\par
has been typically approached from a technological perspective. That is,
\par
the likely developments of a given technology were projected and its
\par
probable social impacts were then derived. Or, more creatively, various
\par
future forms of a technology were conceived with subsequent attempts
\par
to assess the beneficial and harmful effects of each on society.
\par
Both approaches have increasingly been rejected as too determinis-
\par
tic and, in the face of actual experience, often wrong (see Godet 1983a
\par
and 1983b). Instead, scholars have concluded that "the focus should not
\par
be on the technology itself, but on the social policy options and the prob-
\par
able consequences of those policy choices ... We should focus our atten-
\par
tion on social problems and institutional analysis in order to keep the
\par
technology in appropriate perspective" (Parker 1976). However, since we
\par
shall later be discussing issues which policy makers and the public a r e
\par
facing in the evolution towards the future information society, it is a s
\par
well to indicate broadly the technologies with which we are concerned
\par
and to relate these to applications having or likely to have socio-
\par
economic impacts.
