\textbf{The Technologies}
\par
According t o Sugiyama (198Z), the technological basis of the infor-
\par
mation society may be viewed as having three main elements:
\par
-- \emph{Communication technology }(for transmitting and receiving
\par
inform ation)
\par
-- \emph{Computer technology }(for processing, storing and retrieving
\par
information)
\par
-- \emph{Control technology }(for guiding operational processes through
\par
the use of information)The continued rapid growth in all three technologies does not need
\par
stressing, but it may be noted that an equally important factor is that
\par
many novel, highly significant applications are offspring of the marriage
\par
of two or more of the above technologies. For example, the development
\par
of computer time sharing on the one hand and computer control of
\par
telecommunications (packet swikching) techniques on the other, made
\par
possible such applications as electronic fund transfer and airline reser-
\par
vation systems. Another combination of computer and communications
\par
technology (in the shape of \textbf{TV) }resulted in videotex. In the immediate
\par
future, broadcast satellite systems, combined with computerized text
\par
and image processing, will find immediate application in the direct
\par
transfer of broadband digital data, information and pictorial images from
\par
source to individual user. This will add a challenging new dimension to
\par
the transborder data flow problem.
\par
\textbf{The Applications}
\par
Masuda (1980) suggested that applications of computerization could
\par
