\par
regional scale, and finally a global scale. For any particular application
\par
the three different levels could also be considered sequentially--local
\par
applications would normally precede national applications, and the latter
\par
would precede global applications. Thus different applications would not
\par
necessarily be at the same stage at any particular point in time. Masuda
\par
suggests, for example, that at the second level, communications features
\par
are added to the products of computer technology to create "telematics",
\par
while satellile communication is added to telematics to reach the third
\par
level. He also groups applications into four classes: those directed
\par
towards science, business, the public, and the individual. This seems a
\par
convenient framework in which to list applications in a perspective
\par
relevant to societ.al impacts and implications.\textbf{m L I }- \textbf{Stand Alone Systems}
