duce applications of information technology not listed above. For exam-
\par
ple, robotized factories (already far advanced), practical applications of
\par
artificial intelligence, and the successful development of a "thinking
\par
machine" in the shape of the fifth generation computer would have far-
\par
reaching consequences worth close observation.
\par
\textbf{Society and Technology}
\par
Returning to a sociological, rather than technological perspective.
\par
one must note the absence of successful attempts to provide a sys-
\par
tematic methodology describing the complex relations between technol-
\par
ogy, particularly information technology, and society. At IIASA, one such
\par
effort resulted in a "Framework for Information Technology-based Inno-
\par
vation" (Sugiyama 1982). Sugiyama's paper points to the importance of
\par
information as a resource, comparable to material and energy. I t then
\par
describes technological innovations, arriving at four broad categories of
\par
"impacts on society": economic, social, political, and cultural.Apart from the problem of semantics, groupings of this kind a r e
\par
largely a product of the author's imagination and conventional thinking.
\par
There is no way t o prove exhaustive coverage, mutual exclusivity, or uni-
\par
form levels of conceptualization. Unfortunately, similar deficiencies
\par
occur in other, often excellent, materials. For example, Parker (1976)
\par
provides his own particular "list'' of economic questions, social issues,
\par
application areas, and (shorter-term) policy questions. Also, in their
\par
report about t h e 1982 Conference on t h e "Information Society", t h e FAST
\par
editors encountered similar problems in their a t t e m p t t o provide sys-
\par
tematic coverage (Bjorn-Anderson 1982).
\par
The FAST editors explain t h a t they are unable to offer a comprehen-
\par
sive, systematic model of t h e society/information technology interplay
\par
in all its facets. Nevertheless, t h e information society develops cease-
\par
lessly. Bold experiments a r e underway in many countries, rules a n d
\par
regulations a r e being devised, a n d policy matters a r e dealt with on t h e
\par
basis of whatever advice, insight, o r instinct is available.
\par
\textbf{m. IMPACTS AND ISSUES}
\par
I t has been stated "that even scientists render their work coherent,
\par
progressive and manageable by embodying shared social/intellectual
\par
filters in their disciplines. These socially buttressed frameworks deter-
\par
mine what are relevant a n d important goals and questions, what a r e
\par
valid techniques and explanatory systems, and what counts a s relevant
\par
a n d meaningful information" (Wynn and Otway 1982). This recognition
\par
underlies our attempt in this section to break down and organize various
\par
articulations of concern into packages suitable for descriptive t r e a t m e n t
\par
in this survey and for subsequent evaluation in future research.
\par
Our choice has been a grouping of seven broadly defined issue a r e a s
\par
for which we have assembled relevant ideas concerning the implications
\par
of the new information technologies. \textbf{A }first glance a t t h e list will make
\par
it obvious t h a t the issue a r e a s a r e not mutually exclusive; r a t h e r each
\par
represents a point of departure from which several phenomena a.nd
\par
changes in society could be s e e n and analyzed.The seven issue areas are t h e following:
\par
1. Work and Employment
\par
2. Business and Management
\par
3. Developing Countries: Challenges and Opportunities
\par
4. Education and Learning
\par
5. Culture and Values
\par
6. International Regulation for Social Benefit
\par
\textbf{7. }The Economic Value of Information
\par
\textbf{I. Work and Fmployment}
\par
That the penetration of information technologies in the industrial
\par
and service sectors will have an enormous impact on the numbers and
\par
types of jobs available is generally accepted. According to Rada (1980), a
\par
consultant t o the International Labour Organisation, "the argument is
\par
not whether jobs will be lost, but whether other jobs will be created else-
\par
where. This question is debatable and and the answers are largely a
\par
metter of speculation".
\par
In the industrial sector, the large-scale introduction of robots is
\par
expected to rapidly eliminate jobs in machine shops and assembly lines.
\par
Though national level projections are lacking in the literature surveyed,
\par
quantitative estimates of job losses have been made a t the enterprise
\par
level in several cases. For example, General Electric's plans to introduce
\par
up t o 1000 robots in its appliance plants between 1980 and 1990 are
\par
expected to result i n the replacement of 50\% of its 37,000 assembly line
\par
workers (Norman 1980). In Norman's view, "jobless growth" increasingly
\par
characterizes many manufacturing industries, as productivity per hour
\par
improves while staffing is reduced. He cites a study undertaken in the
\par
Federal Republic of Germany which indicates that employment among
\par
printers decreased by 21.3\% be tween 1970 and 1977, while productivity per
\par
hour increased by 43.5\%.Although declining employment opportunities in industry have been
\par
offset until now by expansion of the service sector, many observers doubt
\par
that this compensation will continue. Norman refers to predictions that
\par
"the most far-reaching impacts of micro-processors will be felt in offices
\par
and in such service activities as retailing and maintenance work." He
\par
suggests that "in most areas of the tertiary sector, micro-electronics is
\par
likely to lead to slower rates of employment growth and even to job
\par
losses". Rada concurs that "in the short run, in developed countries, the
\par
information revolution will have much the greatest socio-economic
\par
effect through the automation of offices". He cites a study which predicts
\par
that automation could eliminate as many as 2 million of the 5 million
\par
existing secretarial jobs in the Federal Republic of Germany. Similarly,
\par
Norman points to French study findings indicating that as many as 30\%
\par
of jobs in banks and insurance companies could be eliminated by com-
\par
puters by 1990.
\par
But both investigators emphasize the difficulty of accurately fore-
\par
casting the dimensions of computer-induced job displacement in the ser-
\par
vice sector a t this point in time. According to Rada, "the full conse-
\par
quences on employment will be felt only on completion of the new invest-
\par
ment cycle in manufacturing and servi'ces ... full-scale development of
\par
office automation, for instance, is expected to take place in the 1980s".
\par
Norman agrees that "the full impact of the flood of new office technology
\par
will only be felt when the machines are linked in far-flung networks
\par
through which information is transported, stored and processed".
\par
In parallel to their discussion of information technology-induced job
\par
losses, labor market analysts have identified a number of strong growth
\par
sectors, both in manufacturing and services. Expanded production of
\par
computers and electronic equipment of other types (including games) is
\par
expected to create substantial numbers of new jobs in industry. At the
\par
same time, increased interest in tourism and entertainment as well as
\par
growing needs for health, education and social services are predicted to
\par
spur new job opportunities in the tertiary sector.In the face of these diverse trends, and given the paucity of hard
\par
data, the net impact of new information technologies on the availability
\par
of jobs remains unclear. In Rada's words, "a comprehensive framework
\par
and explanatory variables which would assist in an assessment of partic-
\par
ular t2echnological developments is not available." Like other investiga-
\par
tors, he identifies a need for cooperative research and open debate.
\par
Despite uncertainties about the ultimate impact of information
\par
technologies on the labor market, there is widespread agreement that
\par
action policies are needed urgently to deal with short- and medium-term
\par
labor displacement. In the opinion of Norman, "the transition to the
\par
electronic age will ... require policies to deal with technological unem-
\par
ployment in addition to those that support high-technology industries.
\par
Simply hoping that the unemployment problem will disappear in the
\par
white heat of technological revolution does not constitute a viable
\par
employment policy in a period of relatively show growth and rapid tech-
\par
nological change".
\par
The literature surveyed suggested t h r e e types of employment poli-
\par
cies under discussion or implemented to varying degrees in industrial-
\par
ized countries: reduction of working time and work sharing, government
\par
support for expansion of high-technology businesses, and creation of new
\par
employment opportunities in the service sector. A fourth policy, execu-
\par
tion of large-scale retraining programs, is discussed in another section
\par
of this paper.
\par
Reduced working hours and job-sharing. As formulated by Norman,
\par
"the time may have come to consider how to share work in a high-
\par
productivity society", to permit "lighter rvorkloads and higher living
\par
standards for many", rather than "higher profits and earnings for a few".
\par
Mechanisms suggested for achieving more equitable sharing of available
\par
work include a shorter working week, longer vacations, sabbaticals, and
\par
reduced opportunities for overtime.
\par
The advantages of such strategies are that they would reduce unem-
\par
ployment a t least on the short.-run, and would avoid sudden extreme
\par
reductions in work availability for individuals. Arguments against the
\par
shortening of working hours include increases in labor costs withassociated inflationary effects, damaging of international competitive-
\par
ness, the uncertain practicality of enforcing statutory provisions and
\par
international agreements on standardized working hours, and possibly
\par
detrimental effects on Third World development.
\par
But despite uncertainty about its effectiveness as an ernployment
\par
policy, many observers regard the progressive shortening of working
\par
hours as inevitable. \textbf{As }Rada h a s noted, "the evidence seems to suggest
\par
the beginning (or for some t h e continuation) of a shift from a society
\par
with unemployment to one in which increased leisure is regarded as nor-
\par
mal". Mitterand, in his 1982 speech before the Versailles summit, agreed
\par
t h a t "the rapid substitution of new jobs for old ... will be accompanied by
\par
a profound evolution in the content of work and its organization. It will
\par
give another meaning to t h e shortening of work-time".
\par
The prospect of increased sharing of available work itself raises a
\par
host of questions concerning organization and personal adjustment.
\par
Rathenau (1982), a Swedish expert in information technology policy,
\par
states "how to distribute work among people, but also how to distribute
\par
work, study, and recreation over the duration of a person's life, how to
\par
find t h e link-up between wages and needs ... these are so many problems
\par
demanding radical solutions in t h e foreseeable future". Mitterand (1982)
\par
emphasizes similarly the "need for research on the organization of
\par
work ... the effects of new technologies on the length of work and its pos-
\par
sible reduction".
\par
Government support for expansion of high-technolog y businesses.
\par
Although deferring t h e introduction of new technology is sometimes sug-
\par
gested as a means for circumventing unemployment, most analysts con-
\par
c u r that such a policy would seriously h a r m a nation's competitive
\par
strength in the world market, would result in t h e loss of certain
\par
manufacturing activities to other countries, and would prevent the labor
\par
force from benefiting from job creation in the micro-electronics sector.
\par
For these reasons, world leaders have soundly rejected this option, call-
\par
ing instead for government investments in micro-eiectronic technology
\par
and for the support of demand to promote the development of markets
\par
for new goods and services. Mitterand stated a t the Versailles summit,"We must respond to the technological revolution by encouraging indus-
\par
trial investments in the private and public sectors". Johannson (1982), a
\par
Swedish analyst, has noted that OECD countries have shown general
\par
agreement on t h e need to subsidize R\&D and initiate public procurement
\par
policies favorable to the diffusion of micro-electronic products.
\par
Norman noted in 1980 that the government of the Federal Republic
\par
of Germany is investing about 25 million dollars per year into R\&D pro-
\par
jects in support of the computer industry. Similar investment strategies
\par
have been documented for France, Japan and the USA. Prime Minister
\par
Thatcher of the United Kingdom has stressed the need t o promote com-
\par
petition between firms in the micro-electronics sector, noting that Brit-
\par
ish Government policy is t o put nationalized industries into private
\par
hands, and t o break u p monopolies (Thatcher 1982).
\par
\textbf{\emph{Creation of e m p l o y m e n t in t h e s e r v i c e s e c t o r . }}As noted above,
\par
several analysts feel that creation of new employment opportunities in
\par
the service sector can compensate for job losses in other areas caused
\par
by the diffusion of information technologies. Trends which could lead to
\par
an expansion of jobs in the service sector include (1) t h e aging of popula-
\par
tions, associated with increased need for labor-intensive health and
\par
social services; (2) increasing amounts of leisure time and rapid turn-
\par
over in technologies, creating a desire and need for continuing educa-
\par
tion; (3) rapid social change, including increasing unemployment and
\par
divorce, associated with a need for counselling services of all kinds; (4)
\par
the possibility of a backlash reaction to the widespread diffusion of infor-
\par
mation technologies, producing a demand for more human contact in
\par
activities of daily living; and (5) the dernand for specialized personnel
\par
such as programmers arid electronic engineers in the information tech-
\par
nology branch itself. As well, Rostow (1983) has noted t h a t a great back-
\par
log has evolved in recent years in regard to t.he renovation of public ser-
\par
vice infrastructure. The broadest view of the potential of the service
\par
sector as a source of jobs is provided by Strassrnan (1982). H e describes
\par
a Framework For a new social economy based on new services, pointing to
\par
the opportunity for expansion of operating personnel who can deliver
\par
services tailored to t h e needs of clients.\textbf{2. Business and Management}
\par
Business enterprises and prevailing management practices have
\par
been praised and blamed--by varying constituencies--as both the cause
\par
of, and the cure for, many of the "problems" and "benefits" associated
\par
with the new information technology. Millions of jobs and billions in
\par
\textbf{4}
\par
equipment have been or will be rendered obsolete and uncompetitive by
\par
the new technology, at a rate and on a scale never before encountered.
\par
A t the same time, technical breakthroughs and marketing successes in
\par
the information technology sphere have created millions of new jobs and
\par
hope for increasing levels of societal wealth in the future (Masuda 1980,
\par
Strassman 1982).
\par
Traditional management practices have themselves come under
\par
scrutiny and are changing as new "info-technical" possibilities become
\par
more widely available. Thus, as a cause, a cure, and a case in point, the
\par
issues surrounding the impacts of new information technologies on the
\par
"business community" are pervasive--as numerable as the variables
\par
which can be used to describe business institutions and managerial
\par
actions and as varied as existing views about the roles of businesses,
\par
entrepreneurs, and managers in an economy.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Growth o f informatics }us \emph{an industry. }}One question which must be
\par
raised a t the outset is whether the development of the informatics indus-
\par
try is following patterns set earlier by other industries. If this seems to
\par
be the case, retrospective study of the behavior of emerging industries
\par
could provide insights about strategies for supporting and guiding the
\par
information technology industry. If the development of this industry is
\par
an unprecedented phenomenon, however, policy formulation becomes
\par
the more uncertain.
\par
One may identify a number of intriguing features characterizing the
\par
evolution of the informatics industry. First, there is the dramatic shift
\par
t
\par
in the importance and value of hardware relative to software, with some
\par
software products costing nearly as much as the computers on which
\par
they are run. Secondly. a tremendous range of labor and capital intensi-
\par
ties are found in subsectors of the informatics industry; the capitalinvestments needed to develop microchips may be contrasted with the
\par
demand for labor to execute such tasks as data input. This range is
\par
reflected in the varied composion of the labor force working in the infor-
\par
matics industry: highly skilled persons are required for software pro-
\par
gramming, while minimally trained persons can handle data entry tasks.
\par
One must also take note of the range in the scale of activities under-
\par
taken in the informatics branch. The capital intensity of hardware tech-
\par
nology has produced the "bigger is better" approach taken by mainframe
\par
producers. In the case of production of communications equipment,
\par
aggregation into large industrial units has been a national practice for
\par
many years, due in part to the preference of national telecorn adminis-
\par
trators for single source equipment supply arrangements. However,
\par
analysts have noted that a "bigger is better" philosophy acts t o curb
\par
innovation in the communications equipment sector. In recent years,
\par
new products in the areas of peripherals, personal computers, games,
\par
a n d other devices have shown t h e market potential for innovative small-
\par
scale ventures. Mitterand (1982) has concluded that "technical innova-
\par
tion essentially remains t h e achievement of small and medium-sized
\par
firms".
\par
Attention must also be focused on increasing trends toward techni-
\par
cal convergence of hardware products. The emergence of "telematics"
\par
has begun to blur the distinction between the communications equip-
\par
m e n t industry and the computer industry. It will also become increas-
\par
ingly difficult to draw t h e line between the production of machine tools
\par
a n d robotics. In some cases such structural changes are spurring com-
\par
petition between subsectors; for instance, following t h e break-up of AT\&T,
\par
t h e residual company is joining forces with Olivetti in an attempt to
\par
compete with IBM and the Japanese.
\par
Another feature or the informatics industry is the variation in indus-
\par
trial norms a t t h e enterprise, national, and international levels. Rada
\par
\emph{(1980) }stresses the multi-national, even global, effort needed to ensure
\par
coherent guiding principles for ttie emergent global information society,
\par
both in terms of infrastructure and operations. Development of a joint
\par
industrial policy among Western European countries--to permitsuccessful competition with Japanese an d American
\par
electronics/communications industries and to reverse their position as
\par
net importers of technology--is the \textbf{\emph{raison d'entre }}of such efforts as the
\par
FAST program. Yet, some authorities believe that achievement of indus-
\par
trial convergence on a continental scale will prove insufficient in the
\par
future; in their opinion, even larger units will have to cooperate to carry
\par
out the research and development necessary to operationalize t h e fifth
\par
generation computer concept. A salient issue in this context is to iden-
\par
tify the level of aggregation a t which policies should be formulated (glo-
\par
bal, regional, international) and to determine how high-level policy
\par
measures can be implemented while ensuring fair corporate behavior by
\par
the participants in a global industrial effort, as well as to provide a n ade-
\par
quate level of public control.
\par
The friction between the perceived need for international coopera-
\par
tion and the desire for national self-sufficiency also emerges from the
\par
current discussion about the informatics industry. Many governments
\par
consider it essential to maintain a national capability in every sector of
\par
the economy, and thus support domestic informatiori technology indus-
\par
tries to assure that they remain competitive in the international
\par
market. In this context, self-sufficiency in information products is
\par
treated as a matter of national security. Some decision makers, how-
\par
ever, consider that isolationist approaches create a wasteful duplication
\par
of effort. They strive to develop workable international agreements to
\par
reduce international confrontation in the sphere of informatics systems.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Effects of the penetration of informu,tion technology a t the enter-}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{prise level. }}Investigators such a s \textbf{R.M. }Lee (1983) and Wynne and Otway
\par
(1982) have examined new forms for and limits of applying information
\par
technology in bureaucracies and other managerial set-tings. One argu-
\par
ment is that there will be more "power" for middle management in a
\par
decentralized, yet electronically interconnected mode of operation. A
\par
countervailing view is that use of new informatiori technologies will
\par
strengthen the posilion of managers at the center.Clearly, employees' access to a central source of information will
\par
change the corporate environment, in terms of power and hierarchy. It
\par
is possible that a greater amount of teamwork will emerge, with a hor-
\par
izontal (rather than hierarchical) structure organized around the access
\par
to a common source of information.
\par
Strassman foresees significant improvements in administrative pro-
\par
ductivity through task simplification (or elimination) in a quantitative
\par
sense and enhancement of value added of enterprise activities in a quali-
\par
tative sense. If new office technology is used to transform organizational
\par
processes rather than just to mechanize existing office procedures,
\par
employees' work will take on new significance; departments' missions
\par
will change, with more value being placed on customer service and
\par
greater responsiveness being introduced into smaller units of service
\par
delivery. In Strassman's view, introduction of information technology
\par
could permit the lagging service sector of the economy to achieve new
\par
levels of productivity and wealth creation.
\par
According to Strassman, however, new conventions on cost account-
\par
ing and investment evaluation will be required for such redirection of
\par
managerial priorities. Increased attention will also have to be given to
\par
individual learning and t o adaptation to changes in power and the organi-
\par
zation of information. The receptivity of management and workers to
\par
new information technologies and the creation of management struc-
\par
tures responsive to the new technologies thus become major issues at
\par
the enterprise level.
\par
\textbf{3. Developing Countries: Challenges and Opportunities}
\par
We can consider the impacts of irlformation technology on develop-
\par
ing countries from two points of view, the strategic and the tactical. The
\par
strategic is mainly concerned with changes in the North-South situation
\par
likely to be induced by the spread of information technology, particu-
\par
larly its concentration in the industrialized countries. On the tactical
\par
level, it is necessary to consider how information technology can
\par
actively assist the development process.\emph{Strategic impacts. }Certain developing countries have hitherto found
\par
it profitable t o undertake the manufacture of electronic components for
\par
export, t h u s providing new employment possibilities for semi-skilled
\par
labor a t costs which cannot be matched in the industrialized countries.
\par
This is perhaps a special case of a more general pattern of trade between
\par
North a n d South: the North tends to export certain types of jobs t o the
\par
South, a n d to import finished o r semi-finished produc:ts; this supplies the
\par
South with foreign exchange t o pay for needed imports of capital goods,
\par
equipment, a n d other goods. As t h e possibilities and extent of produc-
\par
tion automation develop in industrialized countries, however, t h e com-
\par
petitive advantage of developing countries' lower labor costs could be
\par
reduced. In one sense, therefore, factory automation could result in a
\par
loss of jobs in both industrialized and developing countries, and a t t h e
\par
same t i m e push the balance of trade against developing countries, rein-
\par
forcing a t r e n d which may h a r m both. Both Mitterand (1982) and Rostow
\par
(1983), from their different standpoints, come t o t h e same conclusion:
\par
t h e application of information technology a s an innovative force in
\par
industrialized societies requires an increase in t h e funding of develop-
\par
m e n t in t h e developing countries, both internationally a n d bilaterally.
\par
Rada (19R0) stresses t h a t the concentration of the electronics
\par
industry in t h e industrialized countries skrves only t o reinforce t h e
\par
existing imbalance of trade and the industrialized countries' lead in the
\par
application of science and technology. Electronics apparently account
\par
for over 30\% of manufactured goods exported from t h e developing coun-
\par
tries, but it is often claimed that, a s such countries a r e merely one link
\par
in a chain of a process of distributed manufacture controlled by multina-
\par
tional corporations, they have no influence on the operation as a whole;
\par
their contribution t o t h e finished product does little t o assist develop-
\par
m e n t of their own industry. Indeed, as already noted, once the cost of
\par
process automation falls to levels matching the labor costs in the pro-
\par
cessing country, automation is applied in the industrial-country point of
\par
t.he production chain. nut a t the developing country end. Thus the
\par
developing countries lose not only their export earniugs but also the pos-
\par
sibility of applying any new know-how.These impacts suggest that policies and strategies for broad-based
\par
technical assistance and development-aid programs could be formulated
\par
a t both the bilateral and international levels. However, there seems to
\par
be little sign of agreement on the form, scope, and content of such
\par
North-South programs.
\par
Rada (1980) notes policy trends in the developing countries them-
\par
selves in respect to computer technology. The countries mentioned
\par
(Argentina, Chile, Brazil, and India) are a t the richer end of the develop-
\par
ment spectrum and the policies mainly concern the relaxation or impo-
\par
sition of import controls either to stimulate certain types of computeri-
\par
zation, or to favor the development of a home EDP industry. For
\par
instance, India has developed elaborate policies and regulations govern-
\par
ing the purchase and use of computers: there is a home industry and
\par
foreign investment in it is regulated. Rada considers that other develop-
\par
ing countries will wish to adopt t h e Indian pattern of regulating both
\par
markets and use.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Tactical issues. }}From a tactical viewpoint, a key question is how to
\par
apply existing information resources to t h e development process itself
\par
and how to identify and develop t h e necessary kinds of information, if
\par
they do not already exist. Page (1982) has reviewed some existing infor-
\par
mation transfer technologies in the light of their applicability to the gen-
\par
eral requirements for professional information in developing countries.
\par
While access to scientific, technical, medical, economic, and other data-
\par
bases and databanks offered by on-line information services is important
\par
in industrialized countries, much of t.he material is irrelevant t o
\par
developing countries' problems. Existing mini- and micro-computer
\par
techniques would, however, facilitate the development and operation of
\par
simple, cheap, and easy-to-use information systems. This would often
\par
include local information, not readily accessible by ot,her means but
\par
highly relevant to development needs. New mass storage systems
\par
developed for electronic office applications in indus triaiized countries
\par
are very suitable for providing acc:ess to full text information. \textbf{A }main-
\par
frame computer is not needed to process the material contained by the
\par
storage systems and they are therefore suitable as vehicles for thedistribution of full texts t o local information systems for local retrieval,
\par
a convenient way of improving South-South cooperation.
\par
In this respect, the need for better and cheaper telecommunications
\par
as a basis for cooperation and information exchange, both on a North-
\par
South and a South-South basis has often been sLressed. Regional com-
\par
munication satellites, working with smaller local research stations are
\par
now state-of-the-art: these make it unnecessary for developing countries
\par
and regions to repeat the development of a complex, costly and difficult-
\par
to-maintain terrestrial telecommunications infrastructure such has
\par
been built up over the last century in industrialized regions.
\par
There a r e economic, political, and structural obst.acles to the appli-
\par
cation of these technologies. Their identification and further study to
