\textbf{\emph{7. }The Economic Value of Information}
\par
Many analysts argue that if the iriforrnation society is "different", a
\par
fundamental part of this difference is concerned with the society's
\par
economic behavior. .Masuda (1980) characterizes the economic struc-
\par
ture of his idealized cornputopia as a synergistic economy, with
\par
increased self-production of information by users and shared utilization
\par
of information. Although he does not provide a detailed discussion of
\par
the concept, it seems clear that in his view such an economy will bebased on t h e production and exchange of information. This will be the
\par
dominating force, rather than the production and distribution of
\par
material goods either through a market or a planned economy. He does
\par
not discuss the relationship between production and exchange of infor-
\par
mation and that of material goods; presumably some sort of linkage will
\par
have to evolve as the information economy gets under way.
\par
Other authors see a fundamental contradiction between a western-
\par
style industrial economy and t h e evolving information society, if that
\par
society is t o serve human beings rather than develop into a more effec-
\par
tive means of enslaving them. Engberg (1979), for example, suggests
\par
that the principle of competition for material goods embodied in
\par
economic behavior in the industrial society is inconsistent with the free
\par
exchange of information principles fundamental to the information
\par
society: he equates the idea of competitiveness with the development of
\par
t h e work ethic, and (in the information society context) an unnecessary
\par
distinction between work and free time. Other analysts have considered
\par
that the evolving information society has much in common with the
\par
non-money economy of the informal (and non-taxable) exchange of goods
\par
and services which is becoming an unofficial adjunct to the formal
\par
economic behavior of industrial societies.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Some }}\emph{characteristics of the information economy. }Parker in his
\par
1976 seminar paper discusses some of t h e contradictions and difficulties
\par
inherent in applying converitional econornics to the information society
\par
in its present manifestation. In his view, information is not a commo-
\par
dity, a commodity being a product of "matter-energy"; however, informa-
\par
tion is always associated with some minimum arnount of matler or
\par
energy as t h e information carrier. In practice it is difficult to separate
\par
the two forms frorn an economic point of view, since it is the combina-
\par
tion of the two in the form of information goods and services which are
\par
bought and soltl in the market place in t h e same way as physical goods.
\par
But, as Parker says, "information a s such is not a commodity that
\par
can be readily bought and sold. We do not have satisfactory units for
\par
measuring quantities of iriforrnation or for establishing a price per unit".
\par
Echoing this thought Thornpsori (1979) has comment.ed on the concept ofinformation as an economic good, and how it might be handled and
\par
exchanged. He advances the hypothesis that "the new transformative
\par
wealth-creating class of innovative applications of this new technology
\par
appears inhibited by some constraining factors ... stemming from two
\par
sources: (1) our perceptions of utility and wealth, or in a word, econom-
\par
ics; and \textbf{(2) }our use of language". He views the optimization of the bene-
\par
fit from the use of information technology as involving the establish-
\par
ment of an information market place, requiring a new type of mass
\par
medium for the exchange process (perhaps a parallel concept with
\par
Masuda's information utility), linked by user-friendly networks which
\par
would be necessary in designing and organizing the market place. Its
\par
infrastructure must be such that it encourages the suppliers and users
\par
to interact synergistically. In a later paper (1982) the same author
\par
returns to the theme of the information market place, adding that it is
\par
necessary to find ways of operating it that have the appearance of treat-
\par
ing wealth: but by wealth it seems clear that something different is
\par
meant than a value which can be expressed in monetary terms.
\par
Both Parker and Thompson emphasize the point that, in terms of
\par
"value", information is radically different from physical commodities. In
\par
general, the original possessor of inkormation does not lose value when
\par
he passes it on to someone else. In fact, it'could be said that (aside from
\par
secret information) a piece of information gains (social) value each time
\par
it is used, unlike the tangible goods of the industrial society (which, if
\par
they are usable, usually have less monetary value at each reuse). Not
\par
only does information in general gain in value wit11 each acquisition, but
\par
the marginal cost of distributing it to the (N+l)th user is minimal, once
\par
the original cost of production and distribution to N users has been met..
\par
Parker points out that this property makes it hfficult to create
\par
incentives for the original production of informat.ion. However, he also
\par
stresses the difficulties encountered when society attempts to legally
\par
enforce property rights in information, as though it were a material
\par
good. Thompson underlines an inherent economic contradiction: the
\par
more a piece of information is copied, for example, by taping a song
\par
broadcast on the radio, the more it can be said to gain in social value;from the point of view of the money economy, however, value is lost
\par
since the manufacturer of the medium (the original record) and the
\par
owner of the copyright lose a sale each time a copy is made.
\par
The central problem of Thompson's new information market place is
\par
therefore how value is to be determined, if not in terms of money. Obvi-
\par
ously something more complex than an audience-rating system is
\par
required, but the question is still open: i.s it necessary to derive a new,
\par
non-monetary measure of value for the economics of the information
\par
society, and if so, how should this be done with the needs of the transi-
\par
tion phase in mind?
\par
\textbf{\emph{The present price }}of \textbf{\emph{information. }}If Masuda, Engberg, and Thompson
\par
are right in suggesting that monetary values must be superseded in the
\par
new information economics, there would seem to be no sign of a trend in
\par
this direction in respect of information in our present society. In the
\par
more traditional areas of information provision, such as local or national
\par
public libraries, the original concept that information is a basic resource
\par
like water, for which the user does not have to pay directly, still survives.
\par
However, the application of informatics and telematics to the problems
\par
of retrieving and selecting relevant items of scientific, technical, medi-
\par
cal, economic, and other informat,ion for the professional user has, if
\par
anything, resulted in a much closer association of monetary value in the
\par
rigorous application of complex price scales per unit of information or
\par
per unit of time the information system is occupied.
\par
Before the advent of computerized information retrieval systems,
\par
retrieval of relevant literature items was a function performed without
\par
specific charge by the reference librarian: on-line information retrieval
\par
using continental or worldwide computer networks has now become a
\par
major international business with significant user charges. It is
\par
noteworthy that the user pays not only the added value of the use of
\par
communications and the host compuler facility enabling the relevant
\par
references to be sorted and delivered to his screen, but also for the
\par
information itself.In some countries, the practice of photocopying for private study
\par
a n d research purposes is still allowed, but in others copyright legislation
\par
is becoming increasingly invoked to provide a legal means for charging
\par
royalties on photocopies. Now that the automatic ordering and elec-
\par
tronic delivery of full texts is becoming a practical proposition, publish-
\par
ers propose to use this technology to regain control over copying by
\par
licensing and royalty arrangements, whether the copy is electronic or a
\par
photocopy.
\par
In summary, therefore, the computerization process in the informa-
\par
tion field itself has so far led to the generation of an information market
\par
place based entirely on cash value, and whose characteristics are in no
\par
way dissimilar to any other manifestation of a market economy. If the
\par
authors previously cited are correct in their concept it seems clear that
\par
the transition between the monetary market place and non-monetary
\par
market place for information is by no means straightforward.
\par
For the present, the investments made by information producers
\par
and suppliers have t o be recovered with a sufficient return to meet the
\par
cost of supplying technologically-advanced information products. The
\par
issues therefore reduce t o the following: assuming that a new non-
\par
monetary information economy is broadly attainable at some future
\par
point in time, what a r e the characteristics'of the transitional system as
\par
\textbf{I}
\par
distinct from the information market place as we know it today? Can
\par
policies be identified, both a t the level of the information industry itself
\par
and a t the governmental level, which should be followed to ease this
\par
transition?
\par
\textbf{\emph{Investment. }}If the new information society with its new economic
\par
concepts is regarded as both desirable and attainable, policy makers
\par
must also satisfactorily deal with the problem of how investment is to be
\par
directed into providing new information technology facilities which will
\par
form the infrastructure for that society itself. Under present cir-
\par
cumstances it is quite clear that investment for computers, communica-
\par
tion networks and new iriformation systems, data banks, etc. must be
\par
made on the basis of an expected monetary return, at least in the
\par
private sector, or as a direct result of government subsidies or centralplanning. In any case, the funds required must come from earnings,
\par
whether Rom the information sector or from other parts of the econ-
\par
omy. It would therefore seem that an important issue for the transition
\par
period is how the necessary investment capital can be provided, the
\par
degree to which (in market econorr,ies) private capital needs to be sup-
\par
plemented, and whether or not machinery is required to steer invest-
\par
ments into priority channels. How are priorities to be determined?
\par
\textbf{\emph{A ualue added }}approach. Strassmann (1982) has shown that, in deci-
\par
sions relating to the introduction of information technology at the enter-
\par
prise level, standard economic doctrine as represented by accounting
\par
systems results in policies dominated by cost saving, the main
\par
ingredient in productivity gains in industrial economics. He points out
\par
that the net gain to the enterprise is much greater if a value added
\par
approach is taken, and that this leads to quite different policies centered
\par
around maximizing productivity by reorganizing the work (and the
\par
organizations carrying out the work), and by redeploying and retraining
\par
surplus labor. He gives examples of how a value added analysis provides
\par
a means for optimizing enterprise investment in information technology
\par
since it can indicate the overall importance of different component,\textasciicircum,
\par
rather than merely the degree to which they save costs.
\par
Does this type of approach offer the possibility of bridging t h e gap
\par
between matter-energy economics and the view of information per se as
\par
an economic good? Does the value added concept provide a system of
\par
measurement which potentially makes for better policy analysis in
\par
implementing information technology a t the national or supranational
\par
level than the existing economic tools? Strassmann's answer seenis to
\par
be a qualified "Yes": the qualification lies in the need to develop new
\par
organization and management structures in the public sector, in adrli-
\par
tion to economic tools, to direct the large amounts of capital investment
\par
in information technology required to maximize productivity in t h e ser-
\par
vice sector of the economy as a whole. To quote: "the greatest challenge
\par
ahead lies in transforming the information-intensive ant1 extremely inef-
\par
ficient service sectors of our economy into organizations that a r e niuch
\par
more responsive to unique customer needs a t a low management cost".Parker in his 1976 paper made the same point from a different angle: a t
\par
a time when the information sector is the largest in our society, and
\par
when the application of information technology is the means to obtain
\par
productivity gains, it could also be counterproductive to pour more
\par
money into labor-intensive information ac tivitles: instead, governments
\par
should devote resources t o improving productivity in the iriformation
\par
sector itself. It may be observed that Parker, too, stresses the need for
\par
structural and institutional changes to make optimum use of informa-
\par
tion technology.
\par
Returning to the question of new economic tools as the other major
\par
component of policy decisions for applications of information technol-
\par
ogy, both Strassmann and Parker stress the need for new insights. The
\par
former points out that new information network planning is almost
\par
entirely concerned with the cost of equipment, microelectronics and
\par
associated services. Under a complete accounting system, the major
\par
cost i s found t o be not hardware (probably reducing to negligible propor-
\par
tion of the total), and operation, but non-technological costs, such as
\par
training, start-up inefficiency, and consultancy. These organizational
\par
costs apparently only decrease slowly as experience is doubled: "the gap
\par
between the very rapid decline in cost of technology and the slow
\par
\textbf{I}
\par
decrease in social learning costs is not adequately understood nowadays
\par
... The technology application choices may have to be dictated much
\par
more by the training and usability expenses than by any other considera-
\par
tion. The cultural acceptability of information technology then emerges
\par
as one of the primary influences if we want to elevate our analysis to the
\par
national level" (Strassman 1982).
\par
It seems therefore that those who have written on this subject
\par
regard t h e development of new economic insights as essential if we are
\par
t o understand how to manage the information society that exists now,
\par
and if we are t o be able to exercise some control over its future develop-
\par
ment. Whether or not Masuda's new information society with its dif-
\par
ferent economic values is regarded as a realizable objective or not, most
\par
would agree that there are urgent economic issues in the present stage
\par
which are imperfectly understood. Moreover, investment decisioris oninformation technology and its applications at the enterprise and
\par
national level have to be made now and in the future, and it seems that
\par
the analytic procedures and measurement systems developed in
\par
industrial-age economics do not seem to work very well in this respect.
\par
The arguments advanced by Parker, Thompson and Strassmann would
\par
suggest that a different approach is required--one which embodies a
\par
much more flexible and interdisciplinary view; the economic issues are
\par
seen to be intimately connected with social issues such as employment,
\par
education and retraining and, of course, with organizational and struc-
\par
tural change.
\par
The research questions which emerge from the discussion of the
\par
effects of information technologies (IT) on work and employment, busi-
\par
ness and management, developing countries, education and learning,
\par
culture and values, international regulation for social benefit, and the
\par
economic value of information may be summarized as follows:
\par
\textbf{\emph{Work and Employment:}}
\par
- How does IT affect job content?
\par
- Will new jobs be created to replace those lost through IT?
\par
- How can labor utilization be planned to adapt t o structural
\par
changes in industry and service sectors due to IT? For
\par
instance, How can IT be applied to increase productivity
\par
without decreasing the labor force? or How can labor be
\par
redeployed or retrained to improve quality of output?
\par
- How effective are policies to shorten the work week or to
\par
encourage job-sharing in averting unemployment associ-
\par
ated with IT?\emph{Business a n d Management:}
\par
- What would constitute an optimum industrial structure in
\par
terms of type and size of enterprises in the IT field? What
\par
differences a r e created by the type of industry under dis-
\par
cussion (i.e., computer industries vs auto industries)?
\par
- At what level of aggregation should policies be formulated
\par
(global, international, national)? How can high-level policy
\par
measures be implemented while mantaining public control
\par
and fair corporate behavior (taking into consideration
\par
inter-industry variation)?
\par
- What are the implications of IT for improving adrninistra-
\par
tive productivity in the service sector? What are its impli-
\par
cations for managerial priorities, employee behavior, and
\par
organizational effectiveness?
\par
\emph{Developing Countries:}
\par
- How can IT resources be applied to the development pro-
\par
cess itself? (That is, to improve Third World possibilities
\par
for getting a foothold in wealth-producing technology; or
\par
for building up their infrastructure to permit entry into
\par
world markets).
\par
- What are implications of IT for technical assistance and
\par
development aid programs?
\par
- What kind of effect will IT have on employment opportuni-
\par
ties in the Third World? Will IT widen or narrow the gap in
\par
wealth between developed and developing regions?
\par
- What kind of effect could IT have on the Third World as a
\par
market for developed countries' products?
\par
\emph{Education and Learning }:
\par
- How will information technology affect the quality of edu-
\par
cation?- How can t h e democratic access of all sectors of the popula-
\par
tion to training in IT be assured?
\par
- How can the needed volume and types of future education
\par
and vocational training facilities be forecast? How can
\par
such planning be related to the employment market?
\par
- How can the content of educational curricula be made
\par
more quickly responsive to the requirements of the labor
\par
market? Is i t possible to make the school system as sensi-
\par
tive to market needs as the computer industry is to its
\par
market?
\par
\textbf{\emph{Culture and Values:}}
\par
- To what extent will IT result in a trend to accelerate decen-
\par
tralization of the working and social environment?
\par
- Should such a trend be maintained?
\par
- What would be its secondary effects, for exmple in urban
\par
planning. transportation, and telecommunications?
\par
- Is it probable t h a t information technology will contribute
\par
significantly to t h e development of new forms of social ine-
\par
quality by creating a new have-not, computer-illiterate
\par
class?
\par
\textbf{\emph{h t e r n a t i o n a l Regulation for }}\emph{Soccial }\textbf{\emph{Benefit:}}
\par
- To what extent is it necessary and desirable to regulate the
\par
transborder flow of data and electronic information pro-
\par
ducts to minimize social disbenefits, especially in the
\par
fields of privacy and security of data relating to third par-
\par
ties?
\par
- What are t h e modalities for any desirable level of control of
\par
transborder data flow?\textbf{\emph{R e }}\emph{E c o n o m i c }\textbf{\emph{Value }}\emph{o f E n f o r n a t i o n :}
\par
The social issues summarized above are closely bound u p with
\par
economic aspects: new insights into the economics of informa-
\par
tion, particularly in value measurement, appear to be an
\par
inseparable part uf their analysis. Attention should therefore
\par
be paid to the following:
\par
- Monitoring studies quantifying the development of the
\par
information sector of t h e economy.
\par
- If information does not behave as a material commodity,
\par
identification of other possibilities for value measurement.
\par
- Examination of hypotheses such as that many kinds of
\par
information gain in value with each acquisition, while the
\par
marginal cost of distributing i t to the (n+l)th user is
\par
minimal.
\par
- The use of a value-added approach rather than simple
\par
cost-saving criteria t o make decisions on introducing new
\par
information technology a t the enterprise level; and the
\par
implications of criteria based on value-added on organiza-
\par
tion and management structures.
\par
- If such economic criteria t o be of value in decision
\par
makjng a t the enterprise level, could they be adapted as
\par
criteria for information-intensive activities in the public
\par
sector?
\par
Beyond identification of impacts and issues from a survey of current
\par
thinking, the authors also felt a need to survey the state of policy \textbf{mak-}
\par
ing itself. However, with the resources and time available in 1983, it was
\par
not possible t o make an in-depth evaluation of policies now being pur-
\par
sued by governments and industry to deal with the many issues associ-
\par
ated with the emerging information society. We did Find, flowever, t h a t
\par
in several countries (both market-economy and socialist), policies have
\par
been announced t o expand information technology and microelect.ronics
\par
industries. Driving forces appear to be a perceived need to improvenational self-sufficiency, to increase automation and robotization, and to
\par
compete for export markets. In our preliminary survey it was more diffi-
\par
cult to find coherent national policies directly related to the social
\par
issues, though piecemeal approaches have been suggested and some
\par
small-scale pilot programs have been set up. There does not seem to be
\par
an awareness, however, that more comprehensive policies, in general,
\par
are or will be needed.
\par
In his paper on the "Economic and Social Impacts of Modern Elec-
\par
tronic Technology" provided in Appendix I1 by permission of the author,
\par
Vasko has noted policy initiatives in several countries, including the USA,
\par
Japan, the Netherlands, Sweden, the United Kingdom, France, Austria,
\par
the Federal Republic of Germany, and the socialist countries of Eastern
\par
Europe. On a regional level, activities have been undertaken in such pro-
\par
grams as FAST (Commission of the European Communities \textbf{1982), }and
\par
other initiatives have been designed to improve the position of the EEC
\par
countries in micro-electronics and information technology genera.11\textasciitilde.
\par
Priorities for future research would be to further identify policy options
\par
developed a t the national and international levels, and to initiate the
\par
process of policy evaluation.
\par
The list of research questions identified in this report constitutes a
\par
first step in an attempt to delineate the content of a possible IIASA-
\par
coordinated, cooperative, multi-cultural, and multi-disciplinary research
\par
program focusing on the socio-economic impacts of new information
\par
technologies. A next step would be to narrow the research options by
\par
carrying out a broad-ranging survey of decision makers or by hol.d.ing a
\par
workshop to determine levels of interest in particular topics and their
\par
national and international relevance. A workshop could also serve as a
\par
forum to discuss whether IIASA's most productive role would be to serve:
\par
(1) as a clearinghouse, keeping national researchers informed about the
\par
projects of colleagues in obher countries, (2) as a coordinator of a
\par
research project of which components are undertaken in national insti-
\par
tutes, or (3) as a focus for in-house research on a topic of manageable
\par
size.\textbf{REFERENCES}
