o Indonesia's rich tropical rainforests harbor large numbers of plant
\par
and animal species, many of which remain undiscovered since vast
\par
areas have not yet been thorough\textasciitilde y inventoried. These genetic
\par
resources may yield valuable products including timber, food,
\par
medicines, industrial inputs such as resins, rattan, pharmaceuticals,
\par
and other non-timber forest products. A large proportion of these
\par
biological resources are already important to local residents and the
\par
Indonesian economy. Others may prove valuable in the future as
\par
sources of new products or for their genetic material which can be
\par
used to improve existing breeds of cultivated crops.
\par
o In addition to their commercially harvestable products, Indonesia's
\par
forests are important for watershed protection, animal habitat,
\par
recreation and tourism, education, research, as well as the
\par
maintenance of ecological processes and climatic stability. The
\par
value of these functions are often overlooked, since they are not
\par
exchanged in the market and do not appear in national incom\textasciitilde{}
\par
accounts. Conversion or over-exploitation of forests will result in
\par
the loss of these benefits, adverse impacts to nearby residents, and
\par
may threaten the long-term sustainable capacity of Indonesia's
\par
resources to provide for its residents.
\par
o To protect these benefits provided by forests, Indonesia has set
\par
aside almost 16 percent of its land as protection forest and plans to
\par
establish parks and protected areas on another 10 percent. However,
\par
most of these areas are not protected effectively and suffer from the
\par
same problems affectin\textasciitilde other types of forest.
\par
o Effective protection will require designing and implementing programs
\par
which deal with the needs of nearby residents. In addition, lack of
\par
funding and skilled personnel severely constrain current protection
\par
efforts. Behind these constraints lies a lack of political will at
\par
the central government level which argues for a stronger
\par
consideration of economic benefits and costs associated with the
\par
management of Indonesia's biological resources.
\par
o Economic studies need to be undertaken to estimate the value of
\par
benefits provided by natural forests, parks and protected areas. It
\par
is hoped that by showing that these areas provide significant
\par
benefits to Indonesia, the government will be more willing to take
\par
the steps necess\textasciitilde ry to effectively protect these resources.
\par
- 11 -CONSERVATION OF BIOLOGICAL RESERVES IN INDONESIA:
\par
ECONOMIC ISSUES, BENEFITS AND COSTS
\par
1. INTRODUCTION
\par
Many of the world's remaining natural areas are located within the
\par
borders of developing countries. Increasingly, citizens and governments
\par
in developed countries and concerned citizens within the developing
\par
countries are pressuring to have some of these natural areas set aside as
\par
parks or other types of protected areas such as wildlife sanctuaries or
\par
nature reserves. This is especially true of cour.tries in the tropics
\par
where the remaining rainforests are believed to hold up to half of the
\par
world's species of plants and animals (Myers 1983). Indonesia's
\par
rainforests are considered by experts to be especially valuable due to
\par
the large numbers of species they contain along with their high degree of
\par
endemism.
\par
Though the biological resources housed in aquatic and marine
\par
ecosystems as well as Indonesia's vast array of agroecosystems represent
\par
a wide diversity of species, this paper will focus on the conservation of
\par
forest resources. With 10 percent of the world's tropical rainforests
\par
and 60 percent of those in SE Asia, Indonesia's forest resources are of
\par
domestic, regional and global importance. These forests serve a number
\par
of functions: commercial production of timber and other non-timber forest
\par
products, watershed protection, habitat for a wide range of plant and
\par
animal species, recreation, education, research and maintenance of
\par
ecological processes. Of all these uses, however, only the marketed
\par
outputs from forests can be easily valued in economic terms. Exports of
\par
timber from Indonesia were the largest producer of foreign exchange after
\par
{$\cdot$}oi1 and brought in approximately \$2.5 billion in 1987.
\par
In addition to their value in producing both marketed and
\par
non-marketed goods, lands designated for forest areas also have
\par
alternative uses. In Indonesia, the principal demands for such lands
\par
arise from government sponsored transmigration settlements, smallholder
\par
agriculture (both sedentary and slash-and-burn), plantation agriculture
\par
and smaller areas for mining, aquaculture and other uses. Since
\par
approximately three-fourths of Indonesia's total area designated as state
\par
forest land (though the actual percentage forested is much lower),
\par
demands for these other uses are often met by converting these lands.
\par
World Bank and FAO estimates place the rate of deforestation (defined as
\par
conversion to other uses or loss of productive forest) at as much as one
\par
million hectares per year. .
\par
The increasing demands put on Indonesia's forest resources have
\par
profound implications for the other benefits which forests provide {$\bullet$}
\par
. Because the value of these uses often do not appear directly in the
\par
marketplace, they-=are frequently overlooked. As a result, the continued
\par
, .- 2 -
\par
exploitation and conversion of Indonesia's forest resources will entail a
\par
number of costs which have important implications for the future, though
\par
most will not appear in statistics measuring economic growth and
\par
welfare. Because of this, many people believe that too few natural areas
\par
are being protected.
\par
This is a classic example of what economists refer to as market
\par
failure. People do not perceive the full range of benefits from parks
\par
and protected areas (PPAs), so that the associated costs of
\par
protection--in terms of direct management costs and development benefits
\par
forgone--loom large. As a result, a smaller area is protected than would
\par
be the case if there were a full accounting of all benefits and costs
\par
associated with protection.
\par
This paper addresses the benefits and costs of designating forest
\par
lands\_as parks and protected areas and biological reserves (these terms
\par
are used interchangably in this report). A number of issues are
\par
considered including the justifications for managing lands as PPAs, how
\par
to measure the benefits and costs associated with different degrees of
\par
protection and strategies for the management of these areas to benefit
\par
both current and future generations. These issues underly a number of
\par
difficult analytical questions including decisions on how much and which
\par
land to protect and how such lands can be managed in the most beneficial
\par
and cost-effective manner.
\par
The report has two main components. Sections 2 and 3 discuss a
\par
variety of economic issues related to parks and protected areas in
\par
general with some specific examples from Indonesia. Sections 4 and 5
\par
look at PPAs in Indonesia--emphasizing forest lands--and options for
\par
improving the effectiveness of protection efforts.- 3 -
\par
2. POTENTIAL BENEFITS AND COSTS ASSOCIATED WITH
\par
PARKS AND PROTECTED AREAS
\par
2.1. Benefits
\par
PPAs can provide a wide range of benefits to society. One possible
\par
categorization of benefits is shown in Table 1. Though most of the
\par
categories are relatively straightforward, they are briefly described
\par
below.
\par
Biodiversity. The maintenance of biodiversity (short for
\par
biological diversity which includes all species, genetic variation within
\par
species, and all varieties of habitats and ecosystems) is currently
\par
considered to be one of the most important benefits to be derived from
\par
the protection of natural areas. Biological resources form the basis of
\par
large numbers of industries and are important sources of food, medicines,
\par
chemicals, and other products. By protecting habitats, the variety of
\par
species they contain also can be protected.-
\par
Watershed values. Maintaining the natural vegetative cover helps
\par
control erosion and reduces sedimentation and the risk of downstream
\par
flooding brought about by the shallowing of riverbeds. Dense vegetation
\par
such as that characteristic of many protected areas can also help to
\par
regulate streamflows. The extent of the benefits depends on the type of
\par
soil, topography and natural cover in the protected area, the alternative
\par
uses available, and the types of investment and land use downstream.
\par
Ecological processes. In their natural state, protected areas
\par
provide a number of environmental services in addition to watershed
\par
protection. These services often benefit people downslope and downstream
\par
by maintaining the productive capacity of nearby areas and acting as
\par
natural filters to reduce air and water pollution. In forested areas,
\par
the vegetative cover contributes to the overall global life support of
\par
the planet. In wetland areas, many types of aquatic species are
\par
dependent on the existence of such areas during some porti6n of their
\par
life cycle. Areas with tropical forest cover may support a diverse
\par
community through a natural recycling of nutrients though, once the
\par
forest cover is removed, the area may quickly become infertile.
\par
Consumptive benefits (in addition to tourism). Generally, if an
\par
area is to be considered protected, it is assumed that all consumptive
\par
outputs will only be harvested on a sustainable basis. Protected areas
\par
can provide a number of such outputs including timber, forage, food,
\par
wildlife products, fish, fiber, herbs and medicines. In Indonesia, for
\par
example, the collection of rattans and resins from forests is an
\par
important income generating activity. Depending on the category and
\par
objectiv\textasciitilde of the protected area, the allowance for consumptive uses of
\par
the resources may range from being totally forbidden (such as in strict
\par
nature reserves and most areas in national parks) to serving as principal
\par
functions (such as in multiple use areas).- 4 -
