\par
Recreation/tourism. Recreation and tourism are normally primary
\par
objectives in national parks and important secondary objectives in many
\par
other types of protected areas {$\bullet$}... Except in areas whose principal
\par
objective is either the strict protection of ,natural conditions or
\par
research, some level of tourism and recreational use is normally
\par
allowed. The provision of such services by PPAs is a primary means of
\par
receiving direct financial benefits from these areas and stimulating
\par
employment and rural development in surrounding areas. Even in areas
\par
where the current level of tourism is low due to a lack of accessibility,
\par
use levels may increase substantially over time as incomes rise and
\par
promotional programs are established.
\par
Education and research. Research in protected areas may focus on a
\par
wide variety of topics from animal behavior to changes in species
\par
diversity to the measurement of environmental status and trends. By
\par
examining ecological processes in free from human interventions
\par
conditions, it is possible to better understand the workings of the
\par
natural environment. This can provide knowledge which can be used to
\par
improve management of other undeveloped areas as well as regions
\par
converted to other land uses. Research can either be manipUlative (i.e.,
\par
change the underlying conditions of the study area in some manner) or can
\par
monitor natural conditions affecting these areas.
\par
Research is often integrated with education, and protected areas
\par
provide fertile ground for field study by students of all levels and many
\par
disciplines. Protected areas also provide an arena in which to instill
\par
people with an understanding and appreciation of the natural
\par
environment. This can have beneficial effects on the protected area
\par
itself by making people more aware of the adverse consequences of
\par
behavior which threatens its integrity.
\par
Non-consumptive benefits. This category of benefits includes those
\par
values people currently derive from protected areas which are not related
\par
to direct use. Aesthetic benefits may accrue when one passes nearby. the
\par
area or views it from a distance. Cultural values for certain lands IDay
\par
be especially important in some rural societies, while urban societies
\par
may associate in spiritual values with having a nearby oasis from life in
\par
a man-made environment. Such values may currently be small in many
\par
developing countries but can be expected to grow over time. \textasciitilde ertain
\par
protected areas may also house important and valuable sites of historical
\par
significance.
\par
Future values. In addition to the values people derive from both
\par
current consumptive and non-consumptive use, the protection of certain
\par
areas provides a variety of benefits from their continued existence.
\par
Some people derive benefits from simply knowing that an area is
\par
protected, even though they will never see or use it--referred to as
\par
existence value. - Similarly, they may want to protect- an area so .their,
\par
'l!hildren can appreciate it--giving rise to bequest value. Others may , -;:::.
\par
hope to see or use an area, even if they are unsure whether they-will
\par
ever have the opportunity. If such people are risk averse, they may be
\par
willing to pay some positive amount--which economists refer to as option
\par
value--to retain the option of using this area sometime in the future.",
\par
- 6 -
\par
In most protected areas, undertaking-development will have
\par
irreversible consequences. A value can be assigned to delaying such
\par
development when uncertainty exists about the benefits of the
\par
alternatives available. If information might be gained by delaying such
\par
an irreversible decision, the expected value of that information has been
\par
termed quasi-option value (Conrad 1980).
\par
2.2. Costs
\par
There are three main types of costs associated with PPAs: direct
\par
costs, indirect costs and opportunity costs. Direct costs include those
\par
which are directly related to the establishment and management of
\par
protected areas. Indirect costs refer to adverse off-site impacts caused
\par
by establishing PPAs, such as damage by wildlife. Opportunity costs
\par
represent the forgone benefits associated with using an areas a PPA
\par
rather than harvesting the resources it contains.
\par
Direct costs. The first category of direct costs are those
\par
associated with establishing an area as a protected area. If the land to
\par
be used is not already owned by the government, there may be costs to
\par
acquire title to the land. When people are already living within the
\par
area, they may require relocation depending on the management strategy
\par
and objectives for the protected area. There will also be costs
\par
associated with developing roads and facilities, if needed, and costs to
\par
develop a management plan for the area.
\par
In addition to the costs of establishment, there are a numper of
\par
ongoing costs to maintain and manage a protected area. Administrative
\par
and staff costs must be considered as well as maintenance costs for roads
\par
and facilities. Protected areas should also have an ongoing monitoring
\par
and research program to keep track of changes in status and trends in the
\par
park. If tourists will be using the park, an educational program is also
\par
often deemed to be valuable.
\par
A critical need, especially in developing countries, is for
\par
adequate enforcement to protect the area. As will be discuss\textasciitilde d in the
\par
following section, poaching of wildlife and timber and "clearing of
\par
protected areas for agriculture are often critical problems. An
\par
effective protection program will include the enforcement of regulations
\par
combined with other strategies such as education, incentive systems and a
\par
rural development program for nearby residents.
\par
Indirect costs. Another type of cost of protected areas is
\par
illustrated by damages caused by wildlife to areas outside the protected
\par
area itself. These damages can include crops trampled or eaten by
\par
wildlife (such as has occurred with elephants in Sumatra), as well as any
\par
harm to people,livestock, or materials. It should be obvious that the
\par
attit-udes of those effected by such indirect costs towards protected
\par
areas amrthe wildlife and other resources they contain will be much more positive if they are compensated for such damages.-
\par
"\textasciitilde - 7 -
\par
Opportunity costs. Th\textasciitilde\_opportunity costs of a protected area will
\par
depend on the potential alternative uses of the area as well as the
\par
restrictions on allowable uses within the area. They will include
\par
forgone outputs from the protected area, both resources currently on the ..
\par
site as well as benefits which could have been derived through more
\par
intensive exploitation or conversion to some alternative use (net of
\par
investment costs). Again, if it is local communities who are asked to
\par
forgo outputs which they were previously accustomed to receiving,
\par
compensation or development of alternative sources of these products will
\par
be called for.
\par
2.3. Comparing Benefits and Costs
\par
The proceeding pages have briefly discussed the range of potential
\par
private and social benefits and the main costs associated with PPAs. A
\par
comparison of these benefits and costs forms the basis for informed
\par
decision-making regarding PPAs. However, such comparisons and decisions
\par
are brought with difficulties. In considering the economic arguments
\par
associated with management alternatives fo\textasciitilde any given biological reserve,
\par
the area in question will fall into one of three categories.
\par
Privately beneficial. There are examples of areas where the
\par
benefits from PPAs directly obtainable by individuals or firms are
\par
clearly larger than the associated costs or the benefits of alternative
\par
uses. Unless these are large social costs not incurred by the private
\par
investor, it is most efficient in these cases to allow the private sector
\par
to provide the "service"-protection of a natural area--without
\par
government intervention.
\par
Examples of such cases are not uncommon, but the areas involved
\par
teud to be small and the nature of the services provided rather
\par
specific. Privately run recreation areas such as campgrounds, ski
\par
resorts or game reserves may fall into this category if they result in
\par
limited portions of an area being kept in its natural state. It is,
\par
unlikely that there are any important protected areas of this type in
\par
Indonesia, though, especially with more tourism development, it is
\par
possible that some areas may move into this category.
\par
Socially beneficial. While the management of a PPA can be both
\par
privately and socially beneficial, a more common case is where the
\par
provision of a PPA is socially beneficial (the net benefits to society
\par
are positive) though an individual or firm could not effectively capture
\par
enough of the benefits to willingly provide protection or preservation on
\par
a commercial basis without subsidization. Protection of upper watershed
\par
areas, for example, may be justified for preserving high quality water
\par
supply for a downstream area. Government support of game parks in East
\par
Africa may be socially efficient in terms of attracting tourists and the
\par
wide range of associated services and tourist expenditur,esboth within
\par
and outside the{$\cdot$}PPAs.- 8 -
\par
Undefined benefits. In many cases it is more-difficult to
\par
determine whether the net benefits to society of protecting a natural
\par
area are positive or negative. The costs of protection may be known, but
\par
the benefits may be diffuse or very hard to measure. While governments
\par
may well decide to protect some of these areas, the costs and extent of
\par
these actions are issues which need to be addressed.
\par
Decision makers have several options in applying an economic
\par
approach to comparing the benefits and costs of PPAs. If estimates of
\par
both the benefits of a PPA and the associated costs are known, some form
\par
of a benefit-cost analysis (BCA) can be carried out. Since this is the
\par
most common framework for evaluating the trade-offs in project decisions,
\par
the techniques for BCA are well known. They involve the evaluation of a
\par
stream of benefits and costs over some chosen period of time. A BCA can
\par
result in the calculation of a net present value (NPV) figure, a
\par
benefit-cost ratio (B/C Ratio) or an internal rate of return (IRR) for
\par
the proposed PPA. For details on the mechanics involved in BCA,
\par
especially for environmental -decisions, see Hufschmidt et ale (1983),
\par
USAID (1987) or Dixon et ale (1988).
\par
In almost all cases involving PPAs, the decision makers will be
\par
faced with a situation in which quantifiable benefits are less than the
\par
total benefits. When the quantifiable benefits alone are greater than
\par
the costs, the decision is relatively easy. These areas will fall into
\par
the "socially benefiCial" category and warrant public and/or private
\par
investment. However, when this is not the case, policymakers must base
\par
their judgements on qualitative considerations relating to the value of
\par
the unquantified benefits. It must be decided if these benefits are
\par
sufficient to compensate for the gap between the quantified benefits and
\par
costs.
\par
There are no firm rules for such decisions. However, given the
\par
uncertainty of the true magnitude of future values from benefits derived
\par
from genetic resources, species protection, option value and existence
\par
value, caution should be taken in dismissing unquantified benefits. If
\par
the area is not established as a PPA, some of these benefits will be
\par
irreversibly lost. Also, protected areas will-become relatively more
\par
valuable-in the future, since they are a finite resource which will
\par
become increasingly scarce.
\par
One possible rule for policy makers faced with such decisions is
\par
known as the safe minimum standard\textasciitilde (SM\pounds ) approach, originally developed
\par
by Ciriacy-Wantrup (1952) and later advocated by Bishop (1978). In
\par
essence, the SMS approach uses a modified version of the "minimax"
\par
criterion which involves choosing the alternative which minimizes the
\par
maximum possible loss which could result from making the wrong decision.
\par
The alternative meeting this criterion is chosen unless the direct,
\par
indirect and/or opportunity costs to society-are deemed "unacceptably
\par
large" (Bishop 1978). -\textasciitilde '.
\par
- 9 -
\par
In cases where the known costs of establishing a PPA exceed the
\par
quantifiable benefits, the existence of some unquantifiable benefits
\par
means that the maximum loss associated with establishing a PPA will be
\par
some amount less than the-difference between the quantified benefits and
\par
costs. However, while the cost of not establishing the PPA is unknown,
\par
it is potentially very large. If, for example, some species are lost
\par
because the area is not protected, the potential uses for these species
\par
will never be known. This means that failure to protect the resource
\par
incurs some unknown probability that a serious future social or economic
\par
loss will occur. Following the SMS rule would call for avoiding such a
\par
potential loss, unless it would involve an unacceptably large known costs
\par
to society. Essentially, such decisions become a question of
\par
intergenerational distribution, since they involve accepting some known
\par
cost today to prevent a potentially larger cost in the future. While
\par
economics can add crucial information to such a dialogue, the decision
\par
must be made on political grounds.
\par
An alternative in evaluating these types of choices is to use
\par
cost-effectiveness analysis (eEA). This relatively common method of
\par
project evaluation even does not attempt to value benefits; instead, it
\par
focuses on identifying the least-cost method of reaching a desired goal
\par
(e.g., protection of a certain number of hectares of a specific habitat).
\par
An opportunity cost approach can also be used in situations where
\par
other techniques do not appear as useful. In this method, the forgone
\par
benefits from some alternative use are qualitatively compared to the
\par
benefits of establishing a reserve. Both of these approaches are
\par
described in more detail in the section of this paper dealing with
\par
Cost-Based Approaches.
\par
Various other analytical techniques exist which attempt to
\par
establish efficiency conditions for dealing with multiple goals. These
\par
approaches may be useful when the decision maker has both quantitative
\par
and qualitative goals--not an uncommon situation for PPAs. However,
\par
detailed discussion of approaches such as multi-objective analysis is
\par
beyond the scope of this paper.
\par
A\textasciitilde{} should.be clear from the previous sections, there are major
\par
challenges to adequately assessing economic values of PPAs, and the
\par
remainder of this report deals with these problems. Also addressed are a
\par
number of management and policy issues associated with PPAs and the issue
\par
of how governments can appropriate (capture) many benefits of PPAs which,
\par
until now, have resisted appropriation. Following a generalized
\par
discussion of these issues, the paper turns to Indonesia. It looks at
\par
how the Government of Indonesia has dealt with these issues up to now,
\par
and then examines options for improving the management of PPAs through
\par
donor activities.- 10 -
\par
3. ECONOMIC ISSUES ASSOCIATED WITH PARKS AND PROTECTED AREAS
\par
3.1. Obstacles to Valuing the Benefits of PPAs
\par
Few would dispute the assertion that preservation of PPAs is a
\par
noble idea. However, in developing countries where natural resources
\par
often provide the base for a large part of the economy, the costs
\par
associated with establishing PPAs are often thought to be so high as to
\par
preclude setting aside large areas for this purpose. The need for
\par
additional agricultural land and for the foreign exchange which can be
\par
earned by exploiting forests and mineral resources appears to overwhelm
\par
the possible benefits which can be received by protecting natural areas.
\par
Establishing PPAs, therefore, is often characterized as a lUXUry that
\par
developing nations cannot afford.
\par
As noted above, because many benefits which result from
\par
establishing PPAs are not easily valued, they are often overlooked when
\par
decisions are made on how to best use these "areas. The following section
\par
discusses in more detail the problems behind the quantification of many
\par
benefits from PPAs.
\par
Most of these problems stem from the inability or failure of the
\par
free market to establish socially efficient prices for PPA outputs. For
\par
most goods and services, prices are established in the marketplace based
\par
on a variety of factors which affect their supply and demand. However,
\par
there are some goods and services which the market cannot accurately
\par
value due to characteristics which are not accounted for in the pricing
\par
system. These factors are referred to as "market imperfections" or
\par
{$\cdot$}'market failures". If not adjusted for, they result in market prices
\par
which do not accurately reflect the true value to society of the good or
\par
service in question. Many of the benefits of PPAs are subject to these
\par
market imperfections as discussed below. Table 2 lists the major
\par
benefits of PPAs along with the characteristics aSSOCiated with each.
\par
The main divisions relate to: (1) whether or not use of the benefit is
\par
competitive, (2) to the location and ease of valuation of benefits and
\par
(3) to whether change in or loss of the benefit will be irreversible.
\par
More detailed discussion of these characteristics follows.
\par
Non-rivalry. With most goods, each person's consumption of the
\par
good reduces the amount available for everyone else by the amount
\par
consumed. For example, when someone consumes an apple or purchases a
\par
house, the amount of apples or houses available to anyone else is reduced
\par
by that amount. For goods which exhibit non-rivalry, this phenomenon
\par
does not occur; one person's consumption does not affect the amount
\par
available to others. The total amount of the good available can be
\par
enjoyed by anyone without diminishing its supply. A beautifuL view or
\par
-\textasciitilde " air to breathe are examples of non-rival goods. Such goods are also
\par
commonly referred to as public goods. In some casesj,--b1=nefits accrue
\par
locally, while in other cases they can be distributed globally.
\par
. ."
\par
- 11 -
\par
Some goods are only non-rival up to a certain point, after which
\par
further use begins to incur rivalry (or competition). Recreation is a
\par
clear example of such a good--an area can be enjoyed many people Without
\par
one person's enjoyment being diminished by the actions of another.
\par
Beyond a certain point, however, congestion sets in, and each
\par
individual's enjoyment is reduced as additional people use the area.
\par
These limited, non-rival goods are known as congestible goods.
\par
The problem with valuing non-rival goods is that the market cannot
\par
set an efficient price for them. Since one person's consumption does not
\par
affect the enjoyment of others, the economically efficient price is
\par
zero. This implies that unlimited use should be allowed, since there are
\par
no benefits from excluding some users. There will be certain consumers,
\par
however, who will be willing to pay a positive price for such goods, so
\par
that they will be undervalued by the market. If some people are not
\par
excluded and/or no price is charged for using the good, the true value of
\par
the good will not be established.
\par
As illustrated in Table 2, almost all.the benefits of PPAs are
\par
non-rival in nature. When an area is established as a PPA, many of the
\par
benefits provided become available to all, and one person's use does not
\par
detract from the values obtained by any other users.
\par
Non-excludability. For some goods or services, it is not feasible
\par
to exclude anyone from consuming the good because the costs entailed
\par
would be greater than the benefits received from excluding them. This
\par
characteristic can apply to both rival or non-rival goods; exclusion is a
\par
separate issue.
\par
Non-excludability is closely related to what economists refer to as
\par
"extern\textasciitilde l effects" or "externalities". An externality occurs when the
\par
production or consumption of a good or service by one person or group
\par
involuntarily affects others. In the absence of regulations or property
\par
rights to "internalize" these impacts, those producing the externality
\par
will have no incentive to take into account its effect on others. If
\par
this external effect is negative, too much of the good will be produced
\par
(or consumed); if the effect is positive, less than the socially
\par
efficient amount will be produced (or consumed) {$\bullet$}
\par
.
\par
Many of the benefits associated with PPAs exhibit this property.
\par
In Table 2, it can be seen that many benefits, such as downstream flood
\par
risk reduction or improved/maintained water quality, share the
\par
non-excludability characteristic to some degree. Many of those who
\par
benefit from the establishment of a PPA often cannot be forced to pay for
\par
such benefits. These users who benefit from the existence of such a good
\par
but do not pay for it are known as free-riders. Since it is too costly
\par
to exclude them from enjoying these benefits, there is no incentive for
\par
them to reveal the value they place on these benefits. 'As a result,
\par
governments which establish PPAs cannot appropriate all the benefits
\par
which these areas provide. Under such conditions, a PPA may be "socially
\par
beneficial" even though no individual or firm would be willing to provide
\par
the required protection by themselves (the "privately beneficial"- 12 -
