\par
category described earlier) .\textasciitilde If this is the case, normal market.
\par
mechanisms will not supply the socially desireable level of PPAs. The
\par
presence of non-excludable benefits which are felt on an international-
\par
level, such as existence value (the value of knowing your children could
\par
see a rhinoceros in Kalimantan) or global life support (such as the
\par
effect of rainforests on atmospheric circulation and climatic stability),
\par
implies that even a national government cannot appropriate all the
\par
benefits of PPAs.
\par
Off-site benefits. \textasciitilde fany of the benefits provided by PPAs extend
\par
beyond the boundaries of the PPA. There may be benefits to nearby
\par
farmers, for example, from protecting important watershed land to
\par
regulate flood peaks. Other benefits may even be global in scope, for
\par
example, the maintenance of genetic stocks.
\par
Many off-site benefits are also non-excludable; however, not all
\par
non-excludable benefits occur off-site (an example of the latter case
\par
might be recreation in some PPAs, where excluding people may cost more
\par
than it is worth). Though these two proper\textasciitilde ies often overlap, they are
\par
separate issues. However, the same valuation problem occurs with
\par
both--since individuals do not request these services and do not control
\par
their provision, they are not required to pay for them. This does not
\par
make these benefits any less real. However, if the social benefits of
\par
PPAs are to be accurately valued, all benefits should be included,
\par
whether someone pays for them or not. If they are not included, less
\par
than the optimum amount of PPAs will be established.
\par
Uncertainty. Incomplete or inadequate information can also make it
\par
difficult to place values on many of.the benefits of PPAs. In most
\par
cases, there is incomplete knowledge of what resources the area. contains,
\par
let alone what they may be worth. In addition, it is difficult to
\par
predict what would happen if an area were developed--the effects of
\par
development, both on-site and off-site, are difficult to predict or
\par
quantify.
\par
The market failures described previously add to the information
\par
problems as their presence makes it difficult to accurately value many of
\par
the beneiits of PPAs. Predicting the future value of these benefits
\par
further adds to the uncertainty problem.
\par
Irreversibility. Natural areas are complex systems whose
\par
constituent biological resources have adapted to their individual
\par
environment and the overall system interactions over long periods of
\par
time. If a natural area is disrupted by harvesting some resources or
\par
converting the land to other uses, centuries may pass before it will
\par
return to its former state, if ever. As a result of the uncertain
\par
consequences of development and the possible losses entailed, a more
\par
{$\bullet$} cautious approach is warranted when the results of a decision may be
\par
irreversible (Krut1lla and Fisher 1975).."
\par
- 14 -
\par
When an irreversible development is undertaken, future alternatives
\par
become limited or are foreclosed. - As a result, there is a value in
\par
delaying a decision which involves irreversible effects. As mentioned,
\par
this value arising from the delay of such an irreversible decision has
\par
been termed quasi-option value (Arrow and Fisher 1974), and it can be
\par
characterized as the expected value of information which might be gained
\par
by delaying an irreversible decision (Conrad 1980). It should be noted,
\par
however, that this definition implies that development may also generate
\par
quasi-option value if it leads to better information for future decisions
\par
(Freeman 1984; Miller and Lad 1984).
\par
As a result of all these problems, most of the benefits of
\par
protected areas are often simply considered intangible. As such, they
\par
are either ignored or undervalued during the decision making process, and
\par
the values of protected areas are lumped into the "undefined benefits"
\par
category discussed earlier. However, many of these benefits can be
\par
estimated to provide a reasonable indication of their true value to
\par
society. By more accurately estimating benefits, decision makers can
\par
make better informed decisions on the key q\textasciitilde estions they face. The
\par
following section briefly describes methods which can be used to value
\par
the benefits provided by PPAs. For a more complete background of these
\par
methods, see Hufschmidt et al. (1983), Dixon and Hufschmidt (1986) and
\par
Dixon et al. (1988).
\par
3.2. Methods for Valuing the Benefits of PPAs
\par
3.2.1. Valuation of Benefits
\par
Use of market prices. The valuation techniques in this category
\par
can be used to value effects which change the quality or quantity of
\par
outputs which are eventually exchanged on the market. For example,
\par
deforestation may adversely affect farmers downstream as a result of
\par
increased erosion which deposits s"ed:1ment on farmland and damages crop
\par
production. Alternatively, the sediment may enter a reservoir downstream
\par
and reduce storage capacity. Both of these effects can be valued by
\par
looking at the change in value of production of the good or service that
\par
occurs as a result of the change in land use. This specific technique is
\par
known as\_the change in productivity approach {$\bullet$}
\par
{$\bullet$}
\par
It is important that the effects be evaluated in a with-and-without
\par
context, that is, effects should be measured compared to some baseline
\par
situation. For example, in valuing the off-site productivity effects of
\par
a PPA as compared with converting the land to agriculture, only the
\par
additional external effects from the conversion should be included, not
\par
the total effect. An example might be damage from sedimentation
\par
downstream from a PPA. If sedimentation damages are worth \$X when the
\par
upstream area is a PPA and damages would increase to \$Y if the upstream
\par
area were converted to agriculture, only the additional damage (equal to"
\par
Y minus X) would be attributable to the conversion of the PPA.'.
\par
- 15 -
\par
Another technique in this category is the loss of earnings
\par
approach. In this case,rather\textasciitilde thanevaluating a change in output of
\par
some product, the change in productivity of human beings is used to
\par
measure the effect of a change in environmental conditions. Another
\par
possibility is to measure the medical costs associated with some
\par
increased (or decreased) level of environmental damage. This technique
\par
is most appropriate when applied to minor illnesses caused by changes in
\par
environmental quality; when more acute illnesses or death is involved,
\par
there are strong objections to the use of this technique. Its
\par
applicability for analysis of benefits of PPAs is limited to the
\par
evaluation of casas with possible health implications.
\par
Use of surrogate market prices. Because of the market shortcomings
\par
described earlier in this chapter, many environmental effects have no
\par
established market price. However, in some cases it may be possible to
\par
estimate the value of an environmental good or service by examining
\par
prices paid for another marketed good. In essence, this approach uses
\par
observable market prices for one good to estimate the value of an
\par
environmental good which does not have its 9WD price.
\par
The most widely used technique in this category is the property
\par
value approach. Property values (i.e., prices for housing or other real
\par
estate) are affected by a number of variables including size, construcion
\par
materials, location and environmental quality attributes. A study using
\par
this approach would gather data on price and characteristics of a number
\par
of houses. Theoretically, after controlling for all other variables
\par
except environmental quality, the residual price difference could then be
\par
ascribed to differences in environmental quality. A related technique,
\par
the land value approach," is based on the same principles. These .
\par
techniques are discussed in detail in Hufschmidt et al. (1983), but due
\par
to their limited applicability in developing countries, they will not be
\par
discussed further here.
\par
Another technique in this category which is potentially useful for
\par
the evaluation of PPA benefits is the travel cost approach--widely used
\par
to value recreation. The fees for some forms of recreation such as park
\par
use are often much lower than an individual would actually be willing to
\par
pay\textasciitilde{} Th\textasciitilde{} difference between what a person actually pays and the maximum
\par
amount he would be willing to pay is known as consumer's surplus. The
\par
travel cost approach looks at the pattern of recreational use of a park
\par
and attempts to derive a demand curve (expressing the relationship
\par
between price and quantity of recreational use demanded) to esti\textasciitilde ate the
\par
total amount of consumer's surplus.
\par
To do this, visistors are divided into a number of origin zones of
\par
increasing distance from the park. A survey is used to determine the
\par
time and monetary cost involved in getting to the park (normally, per
\par
capita use of .the park will decrease as distance to the park increases).
\par
The technique assume-a that people react to increased travel costs as they
\par
would to increased admission fees at a park, and that at some level of
\par
cost, demand is reduced to zero. What the technique does is trace out a
\par
demand curve based on all this information. The value of the park is notequal to the amount of travel costs; this information is simply used to
\par
derive the demand curve. In essence, travel costs are used asa
\par
surrogate for admission fees to determine consumer's surplus.
\par
This technique, though somewhat difficult, is an excellent way to
\par
estimate the recreational-value- of an area such as a national park. The
\par
discussion here has been kept brief since few protected areas in
\par
Indonesia currently have a substantial tourist component; interested
\par
readers should refer to the references given earlier for more details.
\par
Contingent valuation methods (CVM). Lack of market prices or
\par
unavailability of adequate information may make it impossible to use the
\par
market and surrogate market techniques described in the previous
\par
sections. This will often be the case where markets are not well
\par
developed, when dealing with goods which are traded rather than bought or
\par
sold, or with goods which never enter the market process such as those
\par
which are used only by subsistence villagers. Instead, surveys are used
\par
to directly question people on how they would react to a given
\par
situation. Based on their answers, one can. determine the value of a good
\par
or service to each individual and then extrapolate to determine the
\par
aggregate effect on all affected individuals.
\par
As discussed previously, we are interested in the total value of an
\par
effect, not just the price. This total value includes the price paid
\par
plus any consumer's surplus which may accrue to the individual. CVM uses
\par
two measures of consumer's surplus. The first is "compensating variation"
\par
(CV), which estimates how much payment would be required to keep an
\par
individual at a certain initial level of satisfaction if he were forced
\par
to face new circumstances which were not as favorable. That is, there
\par
will be a certain payment which, combinedw-fth{$\cdot$} a-new situation, would
\par
leave the individual equally well off as he was in the initial situation;
\par
this payment would be the compensating variation.
\par
The second measure is "equivalent variation" (EV). This measure
\par
looks at the satisfaction level of the individual after a change is made
\par
(rather than before as CV does). It measures how much an individual
\par
would be willing to pay to avoid returning to the initial situation
\par
(assuming he is better off after the change is made) or how much he would
\par
have to receive in compensation to voluntarily not return to the initial
\par
situation (assuming the initial situation is preferable). (These
\par
consumer's surplus measures are explained in detail in most
\par
microeconomics textbooks.)
\par
One common type of CVM, is the bidding game. Here, a hypothetical
\par
situation is described to the individual and then he is asked the maximum
\par
amount he would pay for the item in question. For example, urban
\par
residents might be asked how much they would pay to have a certain area
\par
protected as a national park. Alternatively, people could be asked about
\par
the minimum amount they would accept to avoid a hypothetical situation.
\par
An example...\_of: this might be..surveying villagers on how much they would
\par
have to be paid to give up the right of using a PPA to collect plants,
\par
hunt animals or gather firewood. In many cases, however, obtaining
\par
.'
\par
- 16 -"
\par
- 17 -
\par
realistic answers to such questions may \_be-difficult.\_ .... Another example
\par
would be to ask park users'howmuch they would have to. be .. paid to give up
\par
the opportunity of using a park for recreation.
\par
There are two major types of bidding games--single-bid games and
\par
iterative-bidding games. In the first type, the surveyor describes a
\par
situation and asks the individual for a single bid which would make the
\par
respondent indifferent between having the good described at that bid
\par
price or not having the good (assuming the{$\cdot$} good is something he would
\par
want). In an iterative bid game, rather than asking for an open-ended
\par
bid, the individual is asked if he would pay \$X for the situation or good{$\cdot$}
\par
described. The amount is then varied iteratively until the maximum .
\par
willingness to pay (or minimum willingness to accept compensation) is
\par
. reached.
\par
It should be noted that very different answers may occur depending
\par
on which measure of consumer's surplus \emph{is }used (CV or EV). Though the
\par
differences should be small theoretically (differing only by the income
\par
effect of whether payment is made or received, and by the fact that
\par
willinness to pay is constrained by income): in reality, they are often
\par
quite large. The "correct" measure depends on the distribution of
\par
property rights, i.e., who is given the initial rights to use the
\par
resource; this determines whether the appropriate question refers to
\par
compensation (for giving up the right) or willingness to pay (to get the
\par
right).
\par
A variation of the bidding game is the take-it-or-leave it
\par
experiment. In this variation, rather than being asked for a bid, the
\par
respondent is given a situation and offered a certain amount to accept
\par
this situation which can be accepted or rejected (take-it-or-leave it).
\par
Different respondents are given different dollar amounts, and the final
\par
result is a series of sub-samples, each of which have been given a
\par
different amount yielding some percentage of people who have accepted the
\par
offer and another percentage who have rejected it. These aliswers can
\par
then be analyzed using a logit model to yield an average willingness to
\par
pay (expressed in probabilistic terms).
\par
Ye\textasciitilde{} another variation is the trade-off game, in which individuals
\par
are asked to choose between two different bundles of goods. An example
\par
might be to ask respondents to choose between: 1) a certain sum of money
\par
plus a certain level of an environmental good, and 2) a different sum of
\par
money plus a different level of the environmental good. The answer can
\par
then-be interpreted as the-marginal willingness to pay for the additional
\par
level of the environmental good. This technique could be used to
\par
identify valuations of individuals for different sized PPAs, different
\par
total amounts of PPAs or even different types of PPAs.
\par
One problem with all these CVM approaches described thus far is
\par
that money is involved in all of them. In rural villages or areas with
\par
migrating tribes, many things may be exchanged rather than bought and
\par
sold. One way to overcome this dependence on a monetary alternative is
\par
the costless choice-method. Here, individuals can be given the choice of- 18 -
\par
a certain environmental good and several alternative,' well-known goods.
\par
For example, villagers might be asked what they would be willing to
\par
accept in exchange for not hunting in a wildlife sanctuary. The choice
\par
could be given in terms of amounts of rice, firewood or-even land. This
\par
method could be very valuable in determining the value of different uses
\par
of PPAs to villagers, though obtaining realistic answers is often
\par
difficult.
\par
Finally, one last survey{$\cdot$}approach that is potentially valuable is
\par
the Delphi technique. In this approach, "experts" are interviewed rather
\par
than consumers. These experts attempt to place a value.on a certain good
\par
through an iterative process where they are first independently asked to
\par
place a value on a good and then these values are discussed as a group.
\par
After considering all the opinions, each expert re-evaluates his decision
\par
and makes a new estimate. Ideally, the values will become less divergent
\par
in each round, eventually clustering around a mean value. Care must be
\par
taken to avoid confrontations or allowing a single member of the group to
\par
dominate.
\par
Since surveys rely on hypothetical situations rather than observed
\par
behavior (as in the market-based approaches), it is important that the
\par
situations are described clearly and completely. There can also be
\par
"information bias" if surveys are not undertaken in an unbiased manner.
\par
Another problem is that people may give answers to influence the results
\par
in a manner favorable to them--this is known as "strategic bias". If
\par
people think they may actually be charged for the good in question, they
\par
may give an answer that is less than the amount they would actually be
\par
willing to pay. Alternatively, they may exaggerate their answer if they
\par
believe they will not actually have to pay the amount but are in favor of
\par
the proposal under consideration, .or if they believe their answer will
\par
affect the compensation they will receive.
\par
Other problems with surveys are discussed in Hufschmidt et al.
\par
(1983) and Freeman (1979). Despite all these potential shortcomings,
\par
however, properly conducted surveys can provide estimates of value which
\par
cannot be obtained by other means.
\par
3.2.2 Cost-Based Approaches
\par
.
\par
In cases when valuing benefits proves intractable, the analysis can
\par
focus instead on costs. Here, we are not focusing on the direct and
\par
indirect costs of establishing and maintaining a PPA, because valuing
\par
these costs \textasciitilde s relatively straightforward. Instead, the focus is on the
\par
forgone costs which would be imposed if an area were to become protected
\par
or, alternatively, the costs which would be incurred if an area were to
\par
be no longer protected. In the latter case, the costs which would be
\par
avoided by protecting an area can also be viewed as benefits--there is no
\par
difference between a cost avoided and.a benefit except the perspective
\par
from which the impact is vi!twed. Certain impacts from converting an area
\par
from a PPA to, for example, agricultural land can be viewed as either a
\par
reduction in benefits or an imposition of costs.",
\par
",
\par
- 19 -
\par
The first approach describedhere,the opportunity cost approach,
\par
measures what would have to given up to receive some (presumably
\par
unquantifiable) level of benefits. The second, the cost-effectiveness
\par
approach attempts to find the least cost means of achieving some
\par
predetermined level of benefits. The final two approaches measure the
\par
value of certain adverse environmental impacts which would occur if a
\par
protected area were converted to some other use.
\par
Opportunity cost approach. The concept of opportunity costs and
\par
the opportunity cost approach were already introduced in sections 2.2 and
\par
2.3. In this approach, rather than trying to measure the benefits of
\par
some action (such as the establishment of a PPA), the forgone income from
\par
some alternative use is measured instead. In the PPA case, one might
\par
attempt to value the benefits of the best alternative use of the land
\par
rather than the benefits of the PPA. If this forgone value is low, then
\par
it may be wise to protect the area since the benefits of protection are
\par
more uncertain.
\par
A corollary of this approach could be used to look at the benefits
\par
of a proposed development project in a valuable natural area. In this
\par
case, one would examine other alternatives to the project or perhaps
\par
other sites where the project could be located and evaluate the
\par
difference in the benefits of the alternatives. For example, we might
\par
examine the net benefits of building a coal-fired electrical plant on
\par
degraded or abandoned land rather than building a dam and hydroelectric
\par
plant in a potential PPA. Another example might be to examine moving a
\par
forestry concession to another area to protect some unique resource. The
\par
difference in net benefits between the two alternatives would then
\par
indicate the true opportunity cost of protecting the PPA. If this cost
\par
were low, it may be prudent to develop the alternative project.
\par
This approach is especially useful for areas with unique resources
\par
which might be irreversibly lost if the area were not protected. It
\par
indicates what will have to be given up to protect some unique resource.
\par
The final decision is a political one-it has to weigh the benefits which
\par
would be received in the short-run against the losses to future
\par
generations who might have wished to preserve this resource.
\par
Cost-effectiveness analysis (CEA). CEA was briefly discussed in
\par
section 2.3. When the value of reaching some policy objective cannot be
\par
valued, but the objective is believed to be important, attention can be
\par
focused on how to achieve the objective in the most cost efficient manner
\par
rather than estimating benefits. CEA involves setting a goal and then
\par
analyzing different means of achieving it. It can also be used to
\par
analyze how to allocate the funds available.so that they are used most
\par
effectively. A variant of this technique, incremental cost-effectiveness
\par
analysis, examines the level of funds needed to reach different targets
\par
and then evaluates whether the additional funds needed to meet more
\par
stringent goals appear warranted.- 20 -
\par
For example, CEA could be used to determine how to protect a
\par
certain species or habitat type in a least-cost manner. The cost of
\par
establishing, managing and effectively protecting the resource in a
\par
number of different areas could be compared, and the least-cost
\par
alternative would then be chosen.
\par
The first step of a CEA is to decide upon a target to achieve. In
\par
addition to the examples given above, other targets might be maximum
\par
acceptable rates of soil erosion, a certain level of water quality, a
\par
minimum population of a certain species or a desired output of timber.
\par
It is important that the target be set at an appropriate level. Even
\par
though benefits presumably cannot be valued precisely (which is why this
\par
technique would be used), trade-offs among different targets and the
\par
costs involved in achieving them must be considered.
\par
Once the target is established, the CEA is performed by examining
\par
various-ways of reaching the target. The costs of these alternatives are
\par
then evaluated, and the least-cost alternative is then chosen.
\par
Cost-effectiveness analysis can also be use\textasciitilde{} in guiding national policies
\par
and decisions as discussed later in this report.
\par
Techniques using market expenditures. One method of analyzing the
\par
value of adverse environment impacts on those affected is to examine
\par
responses to these impacts. If costs are voluntarily incurred to
\par
alleviate damage, these costs indicate the victim's minimum valuation of
\par
the damage. The amount is only a minimum for two reasons: the response
\par
may be constrained by the ability to pay for the defensive measures, and
\par
the benefits of the measures taken may be far greater than costs
\par
involved. The approach which examines these reactions is known as the
\par
preventive expenditures method.
\par
This procedure examines actual expenditures made to alleviate some
\par
environmental problem. It is based on the idea that an individual would
\par
only incur these costs if it was felt that the reduction in damage was at
\par
least equal to the amount spent. In addition, the individual would '
\par
continue to incur more costs up to the point where the additional
\par
expenditure is just equal to the value of the additional reduction in
\par
damages. (In economic terms, additional costs would be incurred until
\par
marginal -benefi ts of the reduction in damages were equal to the marginal
\par
costs of further reduction).
\par
A good example of this technique is described in Kim and Dixon
\par
(1986). This study looks at the responses of lowland paddy farmers in
\par
Korea who construct water diversion dikes to avoid damages to crops from
\par
silt-laden water which runs off from upland areas. The perceived
\par
benefits by these farmers must be at least as great as the costs they
\par
voluntarily incur. This and other similar practices could be used to
\par
place a minimum value on the impacts of what would occur if upland
\par
forests were destroyed in other areas. Actions taken to alleviate
\par
flooding are another example.- 21 -
\par
Techniques using potential expenditures. The techniques. described
\par
in this section examine investments which would be needed to offset or
\par
mitigate environmental damages. In some cases, use of these techniques
\par
can determine whether it is more efUcientto prevent damage from
\par
occuring or whether such damages can be repaired at reasonable cost.
\par
The first technique in this category, the replacement cost
\par
approach, looks at how much it would cost to replace productive assets
\par
which are damaged by some project or development. These costs must then
\par
be compared with the cost of preventing the damage from occurring; if the
\par
replacement costs are greater than the cost of prevention, then the '
\par
damage should be avoided. This assumes, of course, than the benefits of
\par
repairing the damage are greater than the costs.
\par
For example, deforestation may result in increased peak streamflows
\par
during the rainy season. If these increased flows would result in
\par
damages downstream such as washing out roads or bridges, the costs of
\par
replacing these structures should be compared with the costs of
\par
preventing the damage from occurring. A le\textasciitilde s obvious application of the
\par
technique might be the cost of physically replacing soil and nutrients
\par
which would be lost if land use practices which increased erosion levels
\par
were allowed. Kim and Dixon (1986) examine such a case. Another example
\par
might be to analyze the cost of providing alternative wildlife habitat if
\par
a wildlife-rich area is proposed for development.
\par
A variation of this approach is the shadow project approach. If a
\par
development or change in policy would cause the loss of some
\par
environmental good or service, the cost could be approximated by
\par
examining the cost of a supplementary project which would provide a
\par
substitute for the good or service. For e\textasciitilde ample, if villagers depend on
\par
a nearby forest for fuelwood and consideration is being given to
\par
restricting this use, the cost of providing the fuelwood by developing a
\par
nearby woodlot could be examined.
\par
Yet another variation of this technique is the relocation cost'
\par
approach. Here, the costs of relocating a physical facility which would
\par
be damaged by a change in environmental quality would be used to estimate
\par
the value of the damage. For example, if a development project would
\par
result in a decrease in water quality such that a water supply intake
\par
would no longer be able to use this water, the cost of relocating the
\par
intake upstream of the project would also be a project cost. Some of the
\par
examples given pr2viously could also be evaluated by this technique if
\par
they could be relocated rather than-replaced.
\par
Cost-side approaches can be valuable in many situations, especially
\par
when benefits are difficult to estimate. In addition, they provide a
\par
means of evaluating the benefits and costs of preventing or mitigating
\par
adverse impacts before the damage is dQne.- 22 -
\par
3.2.3. Applying Valuation Techniques to PPA Benefits
\par
The variety of benefits outlined in Table 1 range from very
\par
tangible, directly usable benefits centered on production of goods and
\par
services to such intangibles as "global life support" and evolutionary
\par
processes. Table 3 indicates how some of the valuation techniques just
\par
described could be applicable for valuing certain categories of
\par
benefits. This listing is not exhaustive--it is merely indicative of
\par
where one can begin to determine values.
\par
The actual valuation technique to use in each case will dependo\textasciitilde{}
\par
the particular circumstance. The value of a PPA for preserving certain
\par
ecological processes, for example, can be estimated by various means: a
\par
CVM approach estimates wi11ingness-to-pay to retain this function; the
\par
opportunity-cost approach examines what net benefits must be given up to
\par
protect an area; and a loss of earning approach may be useful if the
\par
ecological process has clearly defined health benefits.
\par
3.3. Increasing the Returns from PPAs
\par
Better estimation of the benefits provided by PPAs and the costs
\par
incurred from converting them to other uses is an important step in
\par
getting policy makers to recognize the importance of PPAs. However, many
\par
of these benefits are difficult to capture. Even if governments are
\par
convinced of their existence, budget limitations will continue to
\par
restrict the establishment, management and protection of PPAs. If PPAs
\par
were able to provide greater returns to the government to offset their
\par
costs, it is likely that they would be looked on more favorably.
\par
3.3.1. Difficulties in Appropriating Benefits
\par
The first section in this chapter discussed some of the economic
\par
factors which pose difficulties in the estimation of benefits from PPAs.
\par
Many of these same factors are also respoI],sib1e for the difficulties
\par
governments have in appropriating such benefits.
\par
Perha\textasciitilde s the most important of these factors is non-excludability.
\par
Once a P\textasciitilde A is established, many of the benefits are enjoyed by everyone,
\par
not only visitors to the PPA. For example, nearby residents are all able
\par
to enjoy scenic views of the area. Farmers and villagers downstream
\par
benefit from animals which breed in the park but wander beyond the
\par
boundaries to areas where they can be legally killed.
\par
All of these effects are social benefits. The problem for
\par
governments, however, is that they are forced to bear the cost of
\par
providing them without receiving any monetary returns. The benefits
\par
described thus far mostly remain within national borders which, at least,
\par
means that the social welfare within the country is improved, even if at
\par
government expense. These benefits justify the use of general tax
\par
revenues to support PPAs.- 23 -
