\par
Many of the most significant benefits, however, are not confined
\par
within national borders--if a species is protected through the creation
\par
of a PPA, all mankind benefits. If a tropical forest is protected, this
\par
adds to the entire global life support system. In these cases,
\par
developing country governments are providing benefits to everyone,
\par
including residents of countries far richer than their own, without
\par
compensation.
\par
The central problem with non-excludable benefits is that people
\par
have no incentive to pay for them. Since they cannot be excluded from
\par
enjoying the benefits once they are provided, they are free to enjoy the
\par
benefits at no cost. The only means of encouraging people to pay for the
\par
benefits they receive is to make them aware that, without outside help,
\par
some of these benefits may no longer be provided. (This issue is
\par
discussed further in the following section).
\par
In Table 2, some of the benefit categories are characterized as
\par
"possibly non-excludable" such as recreation, genetic resources,
\par
education and research. Fees for these benefits are often not charged
\par
because of existing laws such as patent rights or because common practice
\par
has been to provide such benefits freely due to the difficulty in
\par
obtaining payment. In some cases, however, governments can change their
\par
practices and require payment for access to these benefits.
\par
Benefits which occur off-site are also difficult to charge for,
\par
since people have no choice in the decisions that result in such
\par
benefits. However, if the off-site benefits occur within the same
\par
jurisdiction as the area from which these benefits originate, the
\par
governing authority may be in a position to require payment (e.g.,
\par
through taxation) for benefits received. Even though people may not have
\par
any alternative but to receive these benefits, charging them a portion of
\par
the value of the benefits they receive would still leave them better off
\par
than if the area from which these benefits are derived were no longer
\par
protected.
\par
3.3.2. Obtaining Financial Returns from PPAs
\par
ThQugh it may not be possible to appropriate all the benefits which
\par
PPAs provide, it'is possible to increase the returns above the levels
\par
currently received. Some of the methods can be implemented by the
\par
country where the PPA is located, while others would involve
\par
international cooperation.
\par
User fees. The easiest method of appropriating benefits is by
\par
charging on-site users a fee based on the benefits they receive from
\par
using the area. Many countries already charge entry fees for recreation,
\par
but often the charges are minimal.
\par
Developing countries should institute two...,tiered fee systems, with
\par
a low charge for domestic residents and a higher charg\textasciitilde for international
\par
visitors. Given the high costs of international travel, the additional
\par
cost of an entrance fee to a PPA, even a relatively high one of US\$IO or
\par
more, will only have a negligible effect on the total number of visitors.
\par
. .- 25 -
\par
Fees for accomodations within PPAs should be priced at levels
\par
comparable to privately run accomodations. Camping fees could also be
\par
set on a two-tier system as with entrance fees.
\par
A less common type of user fee is a fee charged to foreign
\par
researchers who wish to enter PPAs. Though some areas of research will
\par
benefit the host country also, many other research topics generate
\par
benefits which accrue outside of the country. As with recreation, an
\par
additional fee charged for research would be relatively low compared with
\par
the cost of international travel and thus would probably not
\par
significantly affect the demand for research. This option is discussed
\par
further in the following section. .
\par
A more controversial type of user fee would be to charge people who
\par
are allowed to collect firewood or other minor forest products in areas
\par
where such uses are compatible with the objectives of the PPA. In areas
\par
where access is difficult except for a few places, permits could be
\par
required to enter the area. Areas where access is relatively easy may
\par
require penalties for not having a permit. \_
\par
Caution is required for this type of policy, however. In many
\par
PPAs, poaching and encroachment are the single largest danger to the
\par
PPA. Allowing villagers to make limited use of the area without charge
\par
may be more beneficial overall by reducing poaching as compared with
\par
imposing a fee for use. In such cases, nearby villagers should be
\par
allowed to use the area at no cost, and permits should only be required
\par
for people living outside a given radius of the park or for certain uses
\par
which need to be regulated.
\par
Patents and royalties. Maintenance of biodiversity is one of the
\par
most important benefits of preserving PPAs. Improvements in plant and
\par
animal food sources through biotechnology and development of many drugs
\par
and other products often can be traced back to newly discovered species
\par
or new varieties found in a developing country. However, the direct
\par
benefits to the country where the source material was found are no '
\par
greater than for any other country, i.e., they can purchase the new or
\par
improved seed or product on tha open market just like anyone else. No
\par
additio\textasciitilde a1 benefits accrue to the country which protected the source
\par
material.
\par
Historically, naturally occurring plant germp1asm has been a public
\par
good and exchanged freely between countries. Naturally occurring species
\par
and varieties are considered the "common heritage of mankind." However,
\par
given the costs currently necessary to protect as yet undiscovered
\par
species, this policy denies countries the opportunity to benefit from
\par
their sacrifice. Clearly, changes are needed.
\par
One change which would create clear incentive to protect
\par
species-rich areas is to extend patent rights to new1rdiscovered species
\par
or varieties. Under this arrangement, the benefits from any plant (or
\par
animal) which is found to have commercial applications would be split
\par
between the country where the source material was discovered and the- 26 -
\par
individual or organization who discovered the new product using this
\par
source material. Such a policy would encourage developing countries to
\par
maintain biodiversity and would also promote expenditures to discover new
\par
species. It would also be consistent with the United Nations'
\par
recognition of the right to national sovereignty over natural resources
\par
(United Nations 1974).
\par
Currently, patent law distinguishes between "invention" and
\par
"discovery." Discovery of something which exists naturally is not given
\par
any exclusive rights; only invention, i.e., the development of some new
\par
creation is given exclusive rights (see Sedjo 1988 for a discussion of
\par
this topic). Such a distinction does not recognize that discovery may
\par
not be possible if incentives are not created to protect the resource.
\par
Overhauling the international code of law is not something that can
\par
be done by one country. It may be possible, however, to restrict entry
\par
to PPAs by scientists unless written agreement is given in advance that
\par
rights to any inventions based on materials found in the area will be
\par
shared between the host country and the res\textasciitilde archers. Many scientists may
\par
initially refuse to participate in such a system, but if adopted by a
\par
number of countries, they may eventually accept it. A related option
\par
would be to auction the exclusive rights to do research (especially
\par
collection of genetic material) in a specific area. This could be
\par
considered to be a "research concession" analogous to a timber or
\par
minerals concession.
\par
These type of policies are not without cost. Initially, it may
\par
inhibit research in countries adopting such a policy and thereby delay or
\par
prevent important discoveries which might be made. There is also a loss
\par
in economic efficiency involved, since freely distributing genetic
\par
resources does not diminish their availabi1ity--they are non-rival
\par
resources as discussed earlier. In addition, scientists in the developed
\par
countries are sure to complain bitterly; this has already occurred where
\par
this idea has been advanced (for example, see Wilkes 1987). Given the
\par
potential value of unknown genetic resources, however, some means of'
\par
providing incentives for protection are urgently needed.
\par
Development of plant breeding and improvement programs. If current
\par
international laws cannot be restructured as discussed above, another
\par
alternative is for developing countries themselves, or with the help of
\par
the developed countries, to establish their own research institutions to
\par
develop new products from naturally occurring species. In Indonesia, for
\par
example, this could be done under the auspices of the Indonesian
\par
Institute of Sciences (LIPI) or the Agency for Technology Application and
\par
Assessment (BPPT). By developing new products on their own, countries
\par
like Indonesia would be able to capture more benefits from protecting
\par
their genetic endowments.
\par
International compensation schemes. There are a variety of ways in
\par
which developed country governments or NGOs could promote improved
\par
protection of PPAs and the genetic resources they contain. International
\par
schemes are necessary, since many of the benefits are not confined within
\par
the country while costs are fully borne domestically.- 27 -
\par
Sedjo (1988) discusses the possibility of designating genetically
\par
rich areas as global assets. Countries would retain sovereignty over
\par
land within their national borders but would be paid "interest" based on
\par
the asset value of the area. Payments would also reflect how well the
\par
area is maintained and thus would create a financial incentive for proper
\par
management. Potential problems with the system, however, include
\par
decisions on who should pay what share and how the asset value of an area
\par
would be determined.
\par
A potentially less complicated arrangement would be to have
\par
individual developed country governments or NGOs make individual
\par
arrangements on particular areas or{$\cdot$} with specific countries. This would
\par
reduce the problems cited above and would simplify the transaction. An
\par
initial payment would be made to help establish the area, and annual
\par
payments would be dependent on effective management being maintained.
\par
A variation on this idea is the "debt-for-nature swap" which has
\par
already been used to protect valuable areas in Costa Rica and Bolivia.
\par
Such programs may have only marginal impacts on the debt problems of
\par
developing countries but could be effective-in protecting individual
\par
areas. Basically, an organization buys debt at a discounted rate on the
\par
open market and then exchanges it for an agreement by the government to
\par
protect a certain area. In the Bolivian case, the government of Bolivia
\par
also established a fund in local currency to manage the area. For a
\par
recent review of the issues involved, see Hanson (1988).
\par
The potential scope for additional arrangements like these is
\par
extremely large. Environmental awareness in the developed countries
\par
concerning the problems of deforestation and species loss is growing.
\par
rapidly. More and more people are recognizing that, without outside
\par
help, developing countries will not be able to adequately protect their
\par
PPAs. Many individuals are now showing a,willingness to pay as evidenced
\par
by the increasing level of donations to NGOs working in this area.
\par
All of these measures have the potential to help developing
\par
countries increase the benefits they receive from PPAs. The more that
\par
these countries can benefit from h\&ving PPAs, the more incentive there is
\par
to estab\textasciitilde ish and maintain such areas.
\par
3.4. Protecting the Resources in PPAs
\par
3.4.1. Threats to PPAs
\par
Machlis and Tichnell (1985) define threats as "those activities of
\par
either human or natural origin that cause significant damage to park
\par
resources, or are in serious conflict with the objectives of park
\par
administration and management". If we widen this definition to include
\par
protected areas other than parks, this definition will serve our
\par
purposes. It should be noted that actions and occurrences which are
\par
considered to be threats depend on human ideas. of which characteristics
\par
of PPAs are important.{$\cdot$}"
\par
- 28 -
\par
While preparing the list of the world's most threatened protected
\par
areas, IUCN found that the most commonly reported threats were inadequate
\par
management resources and human encroachment (IUCN 1984). These are
\par
consistent with what are considered to be the three basic issues which
\par
-underly many problems in developing countries, including the threats to
\par
protected areas (Malik 1984):
\par
o poverty;
\par
o ever-increasing need for land; and
\par
o processes of development.
\par
All three of these issues are interrelated and are compounded by another
\par
problem facing many developing countries--population growth.
\par
Threats to protected areas can either be of human or natural
\par
origin. Sometimes, the source is a combination of the two; fire can have
\par
either human or natural causes and be affected by both natural and
\par
man-induced conditions; floods can be exacerbated by human activities
\par
both inside and outside protected area boungaries; and overgrazing by
\par
wildlife can sometimes be traced back to human activities, e.g., removal
\par
of predators.
\par
Some occurrences may be considered threats in one type of protected
\par
area yet not in another. Natural occurrences such as droughts, floods,
\par
fires. and insect outbreaks may be tolerated in strict nature reserves
\par
but not in national parks. When natural conditions threaten a species
\par
considered important by humans, it may be mitigated through human
\par
intervention while another, less obvious species is left to its own
\par
devices.
\par
Human-induced threats to PPAs are numerous and widespread. These
\par
threats. often change as the development process proceeds--PPAs in
\par
developing countries face different threats than those in the developed
\par
world (Machlis and Tichnell 1987). These authors report that threats
\par
more often cited in developing countries include unlawful entry, fire.
\par
and illegal removal of animal life and vegetation. The threats reported
\par
more often by developed countries were invasion by exotic plants,
\par
chemical\_pollution, legal removal of animals, noise pollution, and mining {$\bullet$}
\par
{$\bullet$}
\par
In many developing countries, illegal settlements and clearing of
\par
protected areas to make room for agriculture are major problems. In
\par
Indonesia, commercial logging is often done near, or even inside of
\par
PPAs. Indonesia faces the additional problem of relocating residents
\par
from the crowded Inner Islands to the sparsely settled Outer Islands.
\par
Often, such relocation places people close to PPAs which are then
\par
exploited to fulfill the needs of these new residents and their children.
\par
One of the most important aspects of management is protecting the
\par
resources contained in PPAs. The following section concentrates on
\par
dealing with human actions which constitute a threat to a protected area.'.
\par
- 29 -
\par
3.4.2. Determining Allowable Uses
\par
The first issue to be considered is, ttWhat constitutes a threat?"
\par
Actions can be divided into four categories:
\par
o uses consistent with the objectives of the PPA which do not
\par
require restrictions within the foreseeable future;
\par
o uses which do not currently require restrictions but may
\par
become threats in the future;
\par
o uses which could be allowed in restricted amounts but whose
\par
current level is damaging some resource; and
\par
o uses inconsistent with the objectives of the PPA in any
\par
amount.
\par
Of the four categories, the latter two types of uses already constitute
\par
threats, while the second category is a potential threat. These three
\par
categories of actions will all require some form of management.
\par
All potential uses of the PPA must first be classified into one of
\par
these categories. The category into which a certain use is placed will
\par
depend on the objectives of the PPA and the effect which that use would
\par
have on those objectives. Decisions will depend on the physical and
\par
biological impacts of the use. Some uses will be acceptable in one area
\par
but not another.
\par
Certain uses will be clearly inconsistent with the objectives, such
\par
?s commercial forestry or mining in a strict nature reserve. Other uses
\par
will be wholly consistent with the objectives of a PPA. Both of these
\par
categories of uses are relatively easy to manage--the former must be .
\par
totally banned and the latter can be allowed without restriction. The
\par
other two categories listed earlier, however, require that more difficult
\par
choices be made.
\par
Many uses will present no problems at low levels yet will
\par
constitute a threat if the level of use increases beyond a certain
\par
pOint. Collecting certain plants, gathering firewood and hunting
\par
wildlife may all be tolerable at low levels in some areas but will
\par
endanger the long-term viability of the resource if allowed to expand
\par
beyond the capacity of the resource to replenish itself. The decision
\par
which needs to be made is whether to allow the use at all, or whether to
\par
restrict the use to some acceptable level.
\par
For these types of uses, management and enforcement are usually
\par
easier if the use is totally banned. However, if some level of use would
\par
not damage the resource, banning the use will result in forgoing the
\par
benefits which would could be received by allowing limited use. These
\par
forgone benefits (which can also be vi\textasciitilde wed as a cost) must be weighed
\par
against both the potential damage which might occur if restricting the
\par
use does not work effectively (causing the resource to become damaged)- 30 -
\par
plus the cost of enforcing the regulations which limit use. Options for
\par
limiting these types of uses are discussed in the following section.
\par
3.4.3. Limiting Potentially Damaging Uses: Supply and Demand
\par
Options
\par
Most of the threats which arise from uses which may require
\par
limitations are threats to individual species such as over-hunting of a
\par
wildlife species or over-collection of a plant species. For uses such as
\par
gathering fuel wood, more than one species may be threatened. In
\par
addition to direct regulation of the use, two other types of responses
\par
can be taken. First, actions can be taken to increase the supply of the
\par
resource. Alternatively, steps can be taken to reduce the demand for the
\par
resource.
\par
Increasing the supply will normally involve development of an
\par
alternative supply, usually outside of the PPA boundaries or in a buffer
\par
zone. Woodlots for fuelwood, captive breeding for wildlife and farms or
\par
plantations for plant species are all examp\textasciitilde es of alternative supply
\par
sources. Universities, government research organizations and
\par
non-governmental organizations (NGOs) can help develop these supplemental
\par
supplies. If alternative sources of supply are made available, there is
\par
less incentive for poaching of these resources inside the PPA.
\par
There are a number of alternatives for reducing demand for a
\par
resource. When the motivation behind a use is to supplement income, any
\par
type of alternative which increases income will reduce the demand for the
\par
resource in question. Rural development schemes, either involving the
\par
PPA or unrelated to it, which raise the incomes of these exploiting
\par
resources in reserve areas will reduce the need to encroach on a PPA.
\par
Another means of reducing demand is to develop supplies of another
\par
resource which can substitute for the endangered resource. If the
\par
re80urce is used for food, developing alternative food crops which can be
\par
grown intensively in buffer zones or outside the park will reduce
\par
demand. Again, universiti\textasciitilde s, government research organizations and NGOs
\par
can help with this task.
\par
If-the endangered resource is poached for commercial sale, actions
\par
can be directed to regulate the sale of the product in the marketplace.
\par
If the existing penalties or the level of enforcement are increased,
\par
dealers will be less apt to participate in the sale of the resource.
\par
This will reduce the demand for the resource, thus limiting poaching.
\par
Increasing enforcement of existing laws and eliminating loopholes in
\par
these laws will be necessary for this option to succeed.
\par
Another option for reducing demand is to relocate people who are
\par
using the resource. However, forced relocation should only be used as a
\par
last resort; providing incentives which get people to voluntarily
\par
relocate is far preferable. Incentives can. include guarantees of
\par
employment, better access to government services such as education and
\par
health or providing allotments of land. In cases where populations:
\par
- 31 -
\par
cannot be sustained where they are currently located, this may benefit
\par
both the PPA and the people being-relocated.
\par
Education can also be used to reduce demand in some cases. If
\par
local residents are made aware of the damage being done, they may be
\par
convinced to reduce or stop their use of the resource. However, if
\par
residents consider the use to be vital to their survival, no amount of
\par
educaion will be effective.
\par
3.4.4. Direct Regulation and Enforcement of Potentially Damaging
\par
Uses
\par
In addition to the supply and demand options discussed above,
\par
regulating use may be necessary. This is also a demand option but works
\par
as a disincentive rather than providing a positive incentive for changing
\par
use. A number of types of regulations may be implemented.
\par
Perhaps the best form of regulation is to provide guidance and
\par
allow a village or community to regulate use of the resource on its own.
\par
This option is best suited to situations where a single, cohesive
\par
community can be given sole access to the resource. If the individuals
\par
in the community are all willing to act in the best interest of the
\par
community, they can be given control of the resource subject to outside
\par
monitoring to ensure that use stays at an acceptable level. One
\par
advantage of this option is that enforcement needs will be greatly
\par
reduced; the community can police itself and simultaneously prevent
\par
outsiders from using the resource.
\par
When this is not possible, other regulatory schemes will be
\par
needed. Where a limited amount of a particular use is not detrimental to
\par
the resource, a permit system can be used to regulate the number of
\par
users. Implementing such a system is often problematic, however.
\par
Decisions need to be made on who gets permits, and penalties need to be
\par
established for users who do not hold permits. The cost of effectively
\par
enforcing the permit system may be large.
\par
A similar system is to allow all people to use the resource but
\par
restrict the amount of the resource which can be harvested. This scheme
\par
also has difficu\textasciitilde ties--effectively enforcing the quotas may be difficult
\par
and expensive.
\par
Certain uses can be restricted to particular areas or to certain
\par
times of the'7ear. This will involve determining where Or when such uses
\par
will not be detrimental. Buffer zones can be established, for example,
\par
where certain uses will be allowed. Currently in Indonesia, buffer zones
\par
are only provided for in national parks. However, to effectively protect
\par
any reserve where local populations are nearby and accustomed to using
\par
the land and related resources, alternative areas including buffer zones
\par
will have to be establrBhed. To effectively implement this type of
\par
policy, penalties will need to be imposed on those who disregard the
\par
regulations.- 32 -
\par
In general, penalties and enforcement will always be needed to
\par
enforce regulations (except in some cases where resources are regulated
\par
by the community). The degree to which regulations will be effective
\par
depends on three factors:
\par
o the benefits to be gained by disregarding the regulation;
\par
o the chance of being caught; and
\par
o the penalty for being caught.
\par
All of these factors can be affected by management policies. The
\par
first factor can be handled through the supply and demand options
\par
discussed in the previous section. The chance of being caught will
\par
depend on the effectiveness of enforcement measures--this is usually
\par
dependent on the budget available for enforcement. Penalties should be
\par
set to reflect the damage caused by the action in question. For actions
\par
which require regulation, all three factors must be considered when
\par
regulatory policies are being designed.
\par
Though penalties and enforcement are \textasciitilde ecessary tools, they are the
\par
least desirable way of regulating activities. They create hostility
\par
between local residents and guards which can end up in violent
\par
confrontations. Dealing with the first factor--e1iminating the need for
\par
villagers to illegally use PPAs to survive--is far more desirable.
\par
All of these issues apply to PPAs, no matter where they are
\par
located. However, they are especially important in developing countries
\par
whose economies depend more heavily on exploiting their natural resource
\par
base and have less funds available to spend on protection. We now turn
\par
to the specific case of Indonesia, a country rich in natural resources
\par
but without the financial resources needed to ad\textasciitilde quate1y protect them.;
\par
- 33 -
\par
4. PPAS AND INDONESIA
\par
Interest in nature conservation in Indonesia began during the Dutch
\par
Colonial Era .-Laws protecting wildlife were passed early in the
\par
century. In 1937, a special government institution was established and
\par
put in charge of nature conservation. The Indonesian Basic Forestry Law
\par
of 1967 forms the basis for the current reserve system. In 1971,
\par
responsibility for establishing and protecting reserves was given to the
\par
Directorate of Nature Conservation and Wildlife in the Directorate
\par
General of Forestry. In 1983, forestry was elevated to ministerial
\par
status, and nature reserves were put under the Directorate General of
\par
Forest Protection and Nature Conservation in the new Ministry of Forestry.
\par
In 1977, the reserve areas system stood at about 4 million
\par
hectares. Major expansions were planned for Repe1ita 3 and 4 (1979-89),
\par
but implementation has been much slower than planned. In March 1980, the
\par
first five national parks were named.
\par
Roughly 10 percent of Indonesia is to"be put aside as PPAs--an area
\par
equal to almost 19 million hectares. Of this amount, 8 million hectares
\par
have already been gazetted, another 4.7 million hectares have been
\par
proposed and 7 million hectares are scheduled to be proposed during the
\par
next five year plan (Repe1ita V). In addition, another 30 million
\par
hectares of land have been designated as protection forest due to their
\par
importance in protecting watersheds and their fragile nature. The
\par
current protected area system attempts to fulfill three objectives
\par
adopted by the Government of Indonesia as part of the Indonesian
\par
Conservation Strategy:
\par
1. to maintain essential ecological processes and life support
\par
systems;
\par
2. to preserve genetic diversity; and
\par
3. to ensure the sustainable utilization of species and ecosystems.
\par
Figures 1 and 2 show the different types of PPAs in Indonesia along
\par
with the uses which are allowed in each. A Strict Nature Reserves (Cagar
\par
Alam) is\_the most restrictive category with virtually no uses allowed
\par
except for certaln non-manipulative research activities. Game Reserves
\par
(Suaka Margasatwa) are managed to provide habitat for wildlife as their
\par
chief priority, and National Parks (Taman Nasiona1) and Recreation Parks
\par
(Taman Wisata) have recreational use as an important component. Hunting'
\par
Reserves (Taman Buru) and Protection Forests (Hutan Lindung) protect many
\par
of the ecological functions of the forest but are not considered to be
\par
important for biological diversity.
\par
In theory and on paper, Indonesia has put together a fairly
\par
well-balanced national system of PPAs. However, most of these areas
\par
remain protected only on paper - there is little or no government
\par
presence in many places, and many of the areas are already degraded. In
\par
1983, a land use study done in connection with the government's
\par
transmigration program estimated that 13 percent of these protected areas- 34 -
