\par
were also deforested (REPPPROT 1983). \textasciitilde{} The deforested area has certainly
\par
increased since then, but it is difficult to estimate to what extent this
\par
has occurred. In addition, the boundaries for many PPAs are
\par
inappropriate--degraded land of little conservation value is included in
\par
some PPAs, while areas with valuable biological resources remain outside
\par
of the reserve system.
\par
A key issue in protecting Indonesian reserves is enforcement.
\par
Currently, enforcement of restrictions on use is poor to non-existent.
\par
Even where a reserve has a relatively large work force, protection .
\par
activities seem to be ineffective. For example, in East Kalimantan the
\par
government unit charged with the protection of the province's reserves
\par
has a staff of apprOXimately 120 people. of this number, 100 are
\par
assigned to Kutai National Park alone, and even with this large work
\par
force, Kutai is not being effectively protected. Many of the staff have
\par
little or no training and few incentives to perform their assigned
\par
tasks. Clearly, effective management of PPA systems such as East
\par
Kalimantan's will require more than assigning workers to a park.
\par
Another major problem in Indonesian reserves is a lack of
\par
commitment tq protecting these areas. Though adequate regulations are in
\par
place, these are often overridden or ignored at both the national and
\par
local level. Logging concessions often overlap with reserve boundaries
\par
or abut them. This problem is compounded by the fact that boundaries of
\par
reserves have rarely been demarcated on the ground. Mining concessions
\par
are also a prob1em--permits for exploration and exploitation are often
\par
granted within reserves by the Ministry of Mines and Energy without any
\par
consultation with the Ministry of Forestry. Though inter-ministeria1
\par
committees exist to reconcile such conflicts, commercial interests are
\par
the winner in the majority of cases.
\par
Currently, only a few selected national parks receive any
\par
significant attention. Even i.n these areas, protection has been
\par
ineffective. Outside these areas, the situation is even
\par
worse--protection is non-existent for all intents and purposes.
\par
Agricultural eccroachment is also a problem. Transmigration areas
\par
have been established near many reserves. When these lands fail to
\par
provide adequate yields after a few years (as is often the case),
\par
settlers move into nearby reserves. In addition, villagers who
\par
previously used these areas are displaced and also are forced into
\par
exploiting reserve areas.
\par
If reserves are to be adequately protected, methods must be found
\par
to provide nearby residents with alternatives to exploiting the resources
\par
contained in the reserves. Currently, few uses are officially allowed in
\par
most of the types of reserves set up by the Indonesian government.
\par
Finding ways to include local residents in reserve management and
\par
providing alternative aources of incomes will be key factors in improving
\par
reserve protection while acknowledging the aspirations of local residents.- 37 -
\par
This issue was supposed to be addressed by enacting legislation
\par
establishing buffer zones in or adjacent to national parks. However,
\par
this legislation has still not been officially approved eight years after
\par
it was first proposed. Indeed, the national parks legislation itself has
\par
not yet been approved. Though Indonesia has 16 National Parks, the
\par
legislation created to officially establish them has never been formally
\par
adopted into law.
\par
As a result of the importance of protecting reserves in Indonesia,
\par
USAID and other international organizations are seeking ways of helping
\par
the Government of Indonesia improve its capability for protecting
\par
valuable biological resources. These efforts could become part of a
\par
larger program to promote improved natural resource management in
\par
Indonesia. The remainder of this report focuses on ways in which
\par
economic analysis could be used to improve the effectiveness of
\par
conservation efforts in Indonesia.- 38 -
\par
5. IMPROVING CONSERVATION OF BIOLOGICAL RESERVES IN INDONESIA
\par
Because of the large size of the Indonesian archipelago and the
\par
wide diversity among its islands, it seems reasonable to devote initial
\par
efforts to improve biological conservation to a single area. For the
\par
purposes of this paper, it is assumed that a valuable region for study
\par
would be Kalimantan. With 82 percent of its land under the jurisdiction
\par
of the Ministry of Forestry, Kalimantan has over 30 percent of
\par
Indonesia's forest lands. Despite heavy logging and conversion to other
\par
uses over the past 15 years, these lands still appear to contain over
\par
half of "Indonesia's commercially marketable timber.
\par
In addition to its importance for the timber industry, Kalimantan
\par
is also an extremely valuable area for biological resources. Forest
\par
types include tropical rainforest, mangrove forest, large areas of
\par
freshwater and peat swamp forest, heath forest, lowland and hill
\par
dipterocarp forest, ironwood forest, limestone forest and various types
\par
of montane forests. Logging, agriculture and fire have all destroyed
\par
large areas of forest. Most severely affected have been the lowland
\par
forests below 200m elevation; these areas are also the least well-
\par
represented type in the current protected area system.
\par
In terms of biological diversity, Kalimantan ranks as one of the
\par
richest areas in the world. It also has a high rate of floral endemism
\par
and a moderately high rate of faunal endemism (the proportion of species
\par
which are restricted to particular area or region). If protection of
\par
these resources is not improved, many species will be lost.
\par
Table 4 shows the area under the Ministry of Forestry's
\par
jurisdiction by category for all the major islands. Though Kalimantan
\par
has the largest amount of forest area, it has the smallest proportion of
\par
this area in reserves of all the major islands. Furthermore, as shown in
\par
Table 5, a significant portion of the reserves are no longer under closed
\par
canopy forest cover.
\par
To help improve Indonesia's capability to protect its biological
\par
resource\textasciitilde, it is proposed that activities and studies be initiated to
\par
focus on biological reserves in Kalimantan. Three or four existing or
\par
proposed reserve sites would be chosen to undertake a number of studies
\par
and develop and implement management techniques to protect the benefits
\par
these resources provide.
\par
5.1. Rationale for Proposed Studies and Activities
\par
As discussed earlier in this paper, many of the benefits from
\par
biological reserves cannot easily be appropriated (captured). Some of
\par
these benefits, such as watershed and\_hydrology functions accrue outside
\par
reserve boundaries and benefit nearby residents. However, many of the
\par
benefits are important globally and will continue to grow in importance
\par
over time. Developed countries benefit from protected areas in a number
\par
of ways: use of genetic resources in crop and livestock improvement,"
