resources these areas contain--fall disproportionately on the countries
\par
where these reserves exist. Thus, in addition to the benefits which
\par
Indonesia will gain from activities in .this field, the developed world as
\par
a whole benefits from biological conservation efforts in Indonesia.
\par
The lessons learned from the proposed activities would also be
\par
applicable to other important biological reserves in Indonesia. If
\par
properly designed, the institutional strengthening which would be
\par
undertaken could create a model to be reproduced in other areas to
\par
increase administrative skills and improve efficiency. Local personnel
\par
trained would be able to apply the knowledge and experience gained 1n the
\par
management of other PPAs. {$\bullet$}
\par
Activities of this type would be consistent with Indonesia's stated
\par
desire to sustainably manage its natural resource base. The benefits
\par
from both pilot sites for implementation and their usefulness for other
\par
sites would improve Indonesia's ability to protect and manage its
\par
biological resources. The proposed project activities are also
\par
consistent with u.s. Congressional mandates covering Environment and
\par
Natural Resources, Protection of Tropical Forests and Biological
\par
Diversity which encourage international cooperation in the management of
\par
these resources.
\par
Though the proposed set of activities would not generate a great
\par
deal of direct employment, it should have positive effects on long term
\par
employment and income gener.ation. A crucial part of these activities
\par
would be the development of methods in which local people can benefit
\par
from biological reserves. This includes direct employment generation in
\par
reserve protection and management, and seconda.ry employment opportunities
\par
from activities such as tourism promotion. In addition, the management
\par
component should' focus on ways of allowing nearby residents to profit
\par
from the reserve through collection and harvesting of sustainable amounts
\par
of natural products.
\par
5.2. Economic Studies Needed-
\par
5.2.1 Valuation of Benefits from Biological Reserves
\par
Calculating the value of biological diversity in economic terms is
\par
extremely difficult. Most attempts are reduced to citing anecdotal
\par
examples whose implications and usefulness are questionable. The reasons
\par
why valuing biological diversity is .. so difficult stem from the market
\par
imperfections discussed earlier in this paper. Despite these
\par
difficulties, however, the valuation methods discussed earlier can be
\par
used to at least partially value these resources.-u-
\par
If the Government of Indonesia is to be convinced to protect its
\par
biological resources, it must be shown the economic rationale for doing
\par
so. A major objective of the proposed set of activities should be to
\par
calculate, to the extent possible, the benefits which can be realized
\par
from protecting biological resources in particular areas. It will not be
\par
possible to determine the value of all the benefits these resources
\par
provide, however, the current knowledge of the value of these benefits in
\par
Kalimantan is almost non-existent. Such an analysis should concentrate
\par
on the issues discussed below.
\par
Productive use value. These values are the market values of
\par
products gathered or harvested from natural forests. Currently, only
\par
timber has received much attention from the Indonesian Government, with
\par
some attention also given to rattan, the most important forest product
\par
after timber. Studies are needed to determine the value of resources
\par
harvested from natural forests, other than timber. This includes
\par
rattans, resins, food products, construction materials, medicinal plants,
\par
herbs, flowers, wildlife products, fiber and other products.
\par
There are three major categories which should be considered:
\par
products sold for cash in markets, products traded for other goods, and
\par
products consumed by the people who gather them. Such a study will
\par
require interviews with local villagers, and observations on price and
\par
quantities of goods sold in markets.
\par
Potential for tourism. Tourism in Kalimantan is currently at very
\par
low levels. However, with its rich diversity of plants, mammals,
\par
insects, reptiles and birds, there is some potential for increasing this
\par
level of tourism. Studies need to be performed on the potential benefits
\par
and costs of tourism in particular areas. Nature-based tourism is
\par
growing at a rapid rate throughout the world and could be introduced into
\par
Kalimantan with relatively little investment. On-site investments
\par
include development of camping areas, and construction and maintenance of
\par
trails and wildlife viewing sites. Other related activities require
\par
training of guides and efforts to improve marketing and promotion of
\par
activities. This activity should include private sector tourism firms
\par
and the Ministry of Tourism. National benefits include foreign exchange
\par
earnings, while local benefits would include direct employment
\par
opportunities in construction and maintenance of facilities, service as
\par
guides for tourists through the area, provision of food, and sale of
\par
local handicrafts. This aspect of the study should probably be confined
\par
to a single area of those chosen which appears to provide the best
\par
opportunities for tourism activities. For example, Tanjung Puting Park
\par
in Central Kalimantan, a potential site, is relatively accessible by boat
\par
and offers ample opportunity to see orangutans and proboscis monkies.
\par
Watershed/hydrology functions. Since protecting biological
\par
diversity will require protecting" habitat,'the other benefits from this
\par
protection will also be provided. Of the many benefits which such
\par
protection provides, the role of forests in limiting soil erosion and
\par
maintaining hydrological functions provide the best opportunity for
\par
calculating economic values. An analysis attempting to value these- 42 -
\par
benefits would have to be done by comparing erosion and hydrological
\par
processes in an undisturbed area with the effects of a similar area
\par
converted to some alternative use, most likely either commercial logging
\par
or agriculture. The objective of such a study would be to determine the
\par
cost avoided by protecting the area.
\par
In many areas, an important unpriced cost of forest use or
\par
conversion is the impact of increased erosion on downstream irrigation
\par
facilities and reservoirs. Where such investments occur, the analysis
\par
must look at the different rates of erosion under different uses, the
\par
associated sediment delivery to various points, and the consequent
\par
effects on production. Such an evaluation can use the .
\par
change-in-productivity approach or the preventive expenditures approach.
\par
Since irrigated agriculture is limited in the regions under
\par
consideration, this issue may not be applicable to the areas chosen.
\par
However, they may be some effects on downstream wet rice cultivation.
\par
Another consideration of this type is the effect of land use
\par
change on water supply. Some areas have alFeady developed water
\par
shortages which may be related, to the effects on base flow from
\par
deforestation and/or compacting of soils during logging activities.
\par
Quantifying the value of these impacts can be done by estimating changes
\par
in water supply and looking at the increased costs of pumping groundwater
\par
or bringing in water from an alternative source.
\par
Water quality is also affected by land use activities. Costs
\par
associated with decreased water quality include adverse effects on fish
\par
production, adverse effects on downstream residents and potential effects
\par
on downstream commercial users. The value associated with a decreased
\par
level of water quality can be looked at by examining the additional costs
\par
which may be associated with this decrease in water quality including
\par
additional costs to commercial users to purify water needed for
\par
processing activities and increased costs to residents.
\par
Many or all of these issues will require substantial resea'rch
\par
efforts. Studies should be based on an initial examination of the most
\par
important potential impacts and focus on those elements and areas deemed
\par
most impQrtant.
\par
Preservation of breeding stocks and population reservoirs.
\par
Reserves are often important in providing sources of plants and animals
\par
which then disperse outside of the reserve area. One area of potential
\par
study is to determine the value of a protected area as a source of seed
\par
dispersal to nearby deforested areas. The minimum value of this would be
\par
equal to the cost of manually reforesting the area. This is only a
\par
minimum value because a manually reforested area would be limited in its
\par
species diversity as compared to an area which was naturally regenerated.
\par
Another benefit in this category is the value of fish or wildlife
\par
which depend on the reserve for some portion of their life cycle.
\par
Mangrove forests are particularly important in this respect. The value
\par
of wildlife which can also be harvested outside the reserve may also be
\par
important.- 43 -
\par
Ihough all these potential studies will require creative design,
\par
the other values presented in Table 1 are even more difficult. Directly
\par
estimating values such as option value or existence value, or the
\par
potential value of undiscovered species or unknown uses of existing
\par
species are beyond the scope of this project. For example, Kalimantan is
\par
the center of distribution for the tree family Dipterocarpacae, one of
\par
the most important groups of hardwood trees. Failure to protect adequate
\par
areas of reserves may endanger some of the species in this family, or
\par
gene pools of fast-growing or disease tolerant varieties. Though no
\par
particular number can be assigned to this value, Indonesia's long-term
\par
well being may require such resources to be available in the' future.
\par
These studies should be undertaken under the guidance of a trained
\par
resource economist in combination with a sociologist or anthropologist
\par
with prior experience in Kalimantan. Local biologists and economists
\par
from nearby university environmental study centers and NGOs should be
\par
included in the study team, which will help prepare them to apply this
\par
experience to other areas. Regional Planning Agencies (BAPPEDAs) should
\par
also be represented to increase their-aware\textasciitilde essof the benefits of PPAs
\par
and involve them directly in the planning of improved management
\par
strategies. Since BAPPEDAs play the mediating role when conflicts on
\par
land use arise, it is important that they be able to present an effective
\par
case for PPAs against other conflicting potential uses.
\par
5.2.2. The Role of Economic Incentives in Protection
\par
A number of methods can be used to reduce the damage to biological
\par
reserves which result from inappropriate activities. Essentially, the
\par
two alternatives are disincentives, such as penalties for disobeying
\par
regulations, or positive incentives which make the community better off.
\par
Programs of the latter type are often called "Conservation for Rural
\par
Development" or alternatively "Rural Development for Conservation".
\par
USAID is developing programs of this type in Thailand, which faces
\par
similar problems from deforestation and exploitation of protected areas.
\par
i
\par
Development of programs of this type involves creating incentives
\par
to change patterns of use which are detrimental to PPAs. If the cost of
\par
imp1emen\textasciitilde ing these programs results in reducing damages by an amount
\par
greater than their cost, such programs will provide net benefits. Of
\par
course, measuring the benefits of these programs involves the same
\par
difficulties discussed throughout this paper. However, the success of
\par
these programs can also be measured in qualitative terms such as
\par
reductions in the amount of land encroached or the inc\textasciitilde me generated from
\par
these activities.
\par
PPAs can be managed to provide a number of incentives for the
\par
community to help in protecting them. When management plans are
\par
designed, it is important to consider how to maximize the.benefits of the
\par
PPA to the community.
\par
One method of benefiting the community is to provide as many
\par
employment opportunities as possible for local residents. Residents can- 44 -
\par
work as guards, help in construction activities, help build trails,
\par
provide daily maintenance, work in restaurants and accommodation
\par
facilities, and act as tourist guides. Local artisans and craftsmen can
\par
be allowed to sell their handiwork to tourists who visit the PPA. People
\par
who are employed by the PPA have much more incentive to help protect it
\par
and ensure its continued existence.
\par
To the extent possible, local residents should be allowed to
\par
continue to profit from the existence of the PPA by giving them the right
\par
to continue harvesting resources from the PPA if such uses are
\par
sustainable and not detrimental to the objectives of the area. This,will
\par
require constant monitoring to ensure that the PPA is not being damaged
\par
by such uses.
\par
In many cases, however, establishment of an area as a PPA will
\par
require that some uses of the area will have to be restricted or
\par
prohibited altogether. For local residents who are forced to give up
\par
such practices, there will be a loss of income which is bound to create
\par
hostility. It is important that those peop!e who suffer direct losses
\par
from the establishment of a PPA be compensated in some manner. For
\par
example, agricultural inputs can be provided at subsidized or no cost.
\par
Inputs which can help improve crop yields will increase incomes and
\par
reduce the need to engage in activities detrimental to the PPA.
\par
Many of the programs listed in the supply and demand options
\par
section discussed previously are also forms of community development.
\par
Development of alternative sources of supply to replace now prohibited
\par
activities are often important in reducing the reliance on PPAs. Village
\par
woodlots and capti\textasciitilde e wildlife breeding programs are two good examples,
\par
since timber and wildlife poaching are often important problems.
\par
Revolving loan funds are another means of helping local residents
\par
improve their quality of life. This involves establishing an initial
\par
capital fund which is loaned to villagers to invest in agricultural ,
\par
inputs, machinery or other income producing activities which are
\par
currently restricted due to a lack of available funds. The loans are
\par
made at relatively low rates of interest and, when repaid, are returned
\par
to the lQan fund {$\bullet$}
\par
{$\bullet$}
\par
Analyses should be performed on the potential for using economic
\par
incentives to promote less damaging activities by nearby villagers. Such
\par
activities will require investments be made either in buffer zones or
\par
other areas near the villages affected. Before-incentives are designed,
\par
interviews should be conducted with village administrators to determine
\par
which needs are most urgent and which measures are most likely to promote
\par
participation.
\par
This analysis should be led by a trained'resource economist in
\par
combination with a sociologist or anthropologist familiar with the local
\par
area. Other team members should be drawn from BAPPEDAs, the local
\par
university environmental study center, NGOs and university faculties..
\par
.
\par
- 45 -
\par
There are two objectives of this type of program. First, this type
\par
of study should reduce the rate of degradation inside the reserve.
\par
Unfortunately, it will be difficult to evaluate the magnitude of the
\par
decrease in degradation, since there is little information on the current
\par
rate of degradation. Nonetheless, one can look at the changes which
\par
occur over the time during the implementation of the proposed project.
\par
Second, the project will show the potential employment and income
\par
generating capacity of these types of programs. This can then be
\par
evaluated to see whether the benefits of such programs outweigh their
\par
costs. It will also help provide information on how large buffer zones
\par
will need to be to adequately protect nearby reserves.
\par
5.2.3. Analysis of the Implementation of the Proposed Reserve
\par
System
\par
The current reserve system, along with plans for future reserves,
\par
follows a design set out in the National Conservation Plan prepared by
\par
the FAO (1982). The sites contained in thi\textasciitilde{} plan were selected according
\par
to three factors:{$\cdot$} importance in preserving genetic resources,
\par
socio-economic justification and management feasibility. The criteria
\par
for rating the sites in these categories are outlined in Volume I of the
\par
FAO plan.
\par
As indicated in Section 4, implementation of this plan and official
\par
gazettement of the sites selected has proceeded much slower than
\par
originally planned. Clearly, there has been a lack of commitment on the
\par
part of the Indonesian Government to allocate the resources necessary to
\par
fulfill their stated goals in terms of protecting reserves.
\par
It seems clear that by demonstrating that PPAs have a wide range'of
\par
economically significant benefits, one can more easily promote the case
\par
of enhanced natural area protection. Both of the previous types of
\par
studies advocated here will help overcome this. However, such an
\par
"accounting" approach still neglects a crucial factor-politics. Though
\par
economics can provide crucial input into the decision making process,
\par
most government decisions do not solely rely on economics. Since very
\par
few PPAs will fall into the category of "privately beneficial", it
\par
usually will be up to the government to establish PPAs. In many cases,
\par
PPAs can be justified as "socially beneficial". This would include most
\par
of those PPAs which are established as a result of their outstanding or
\par
unique resources. When information is lackin3 or valuation difficult,
\par
areas fall into the third case as{$\cdot$} having "undefined benefits". Whether
\par
relating to the second or third case, most decisions to support PPAs are
\par
social decisions whereby some magnitude of social benefits (frequently
\par
undefined) is used to justify a clearly defined cost (both budget outlays
\par
and development benefits forgone).
\par
Social benefits are frequently important in deciding the allocation
\par
of government resources--witness allocations for education,health, and
\par
the arts among others. The political case for PPAs, however, is harder
\par
to justify, in part because the benefits are more difficult to value and
\par
more diffuse in nature, and the forgone costs are easier to see..
\par
.
\par
- 46 -
\par
In considering the process whereby governments and countries make
\par
decisions on the allocation of resources for PPAs, therefore, it is
\par
important to understand which decisions are essentially "political" and
\par
which ones are "economic". (One should not underestimate, however, the
\par
potential contribution of economic arguments to the making of political
\par
decisions).
\par
The study recommended here is an economical/political analysis of
\par
what has happened to various sites advocated for protection in the FAO
\par
plan. Obviously, political impediments have managed to greatly delay the
\par
ambitious plans originally endorsed by the Government of Indonesia. Much{$\cdot$}
\par
could be learned by examining which areas have received attention by{$\cdot$}the
\par
government and comparing these areas with those whose proposed protection
\par
has languished. Is there an economic reason behind the delays for
\par
establishment? Are there rich timber or mineral resources which either
\par
the government or some private firm wishes to exploit?
\par
The study should focus on reserves in Kalimantan and analyze what
\par
actions have been taken in the 6 years since the FAO plan was published.
\par
It should be undertaken by a natural resource economist familiar with the
\par
economic and institutional issues associated with parks and protected
\par
areas. Familiarity with the Indonesian government system would also be
\par
helpful. The economist should work either from Jakarta or Bogor close to
\par
where the actual decisions are made.
\par
The purpose of undertaking this study is to see what factors have
\par
delayed the establishment of reserves which have been deemed to be
\par
priorities. For example, have the obstructions come at the national or
\par
provincial level? Have certain ministries acted to hold back
\par
gazettement? By examining which factors have had an impact on the
\par
establishment of PPAs in Indonesia, policies or institutional changes can
\par
be designed to overcome these obstacles and promote quicker action on
\par
reserve establishment. Long-term financing options, including "debt for
\par
nature swaps" and other mechanisms for international funding, could also
\par
be examined.
\par
5.3. Institutional Strengthening
\par
Any projects dealing with PPAs in Indonesia will necessarily have
\par
to involve the Directorate General of Forest Protection and Nature
\par
Conservation (PHPA). Currently, field level offices of this Directorate
\par
at both the Balai and Sub-Balai levels suffer from inadequate funds,
\par
manpower and training. Strengthening these regional offices would be{$\cdot$} an
\par
important component if this project is to show long term benefits.
\par
However, there is some possibility that these provincial offices will be
\par
abolished and their functions given to the Regional Forestry Office
\par
(Kanwil Kehutanan); if this is to occur, appropriate adjustments would
\par
have to be made. '
\par
Establishing an on-site presence of PHPA would. be needed for all
\par
areas selected for field studies. On-site training should be provided
\par
for lower and middle eschelon workers, and locally recruited workers- 47 -
\par
should be emphasized since these people often have the greatest knowledge
\par
of the areas. One possible constraint is that the Government of
\par
Indonesia requires at least a secondary school education in order for
\par
civil servants to be eligible for many of the field positions required in
\par
PPA management. This policy will have to be changed if local residents
\par
are to be able to participate. Overseas training for higher level
\par
employees should also be considered.
\par
As indicated, involvement of the BAPPEDAs, university environmental
\par
study centers and NGOs is important. Participation in the studies
\par
undertaken will increase their ability to provide useful information into'
\par
the decision making-process. It will also allow them to apply this
\par
knowledge elsewhere to sustain the benefits from these activities.
\par
Undertaking this set of activities will go far in improving
\par
Indonesia's motivation and ability to protect its valuable and diverse
\par
biological resources. It should also make the country's parks and
\par
protected areas more beneficial to Indonesia's people resulting in a more
\par
efficient, more valuable system of reservesl
\par
. .- 48 -
