\par
Published in: SOL, H.G., A. VERBRAECK, AND P.W.G. BOTS (ed.), \emph{Proceedings of the Fourth}
\par
\emph{International Working Conference on Dynamic Modelling of Information Systems}, Noord-
\par
wijkerhout, The Netherlands, September 28-30, 1994\textbf{'A HARD CORE FOR SOFT PROBLEMS'}
\par
\textbf{A Business Engineering Case Study}
\par
\textbf{within the Amsterdam Municipal Police Force.}
\par
J.W. van Meel / P.W.G. Bots / H.G. Sol
\par
Delft University of Technology
\par
School of Systems Engineering, Policy Analysis and Management
\par
P.O. Box 5015 NL-2600 GA Delft The Netherlands
\par
Tel: 31 15 78 7231 / 2948 / 2318 Fax: 31 15 78 4811
\par
E-mail: \textbraceleft vanmeel,bots,sol\textbraceright @sepa.tudelft.nl
\par
\textbf{Abstract}
\par
In this paper, a case study with Business Engineering (BE) is described within the Amsterdam
\par
Municipal Police Force. A short review shows that literature on BE-methods, new
\par
organizational forms, organization design and information systems design offers little
\par
methodological support for BE. However, integrating aspects from different bodies of literature
\par
seems to be a promising prospect. Integration in a purely deductive way is most difficult to
\par
realize. Therefore, an exploratory case study has been carried out instead. The methods and
\par
techniques used during the case study, and the final results have been evaluated. The result is an
\par
outline of an integrated simulation approach consisting of: a SocioTechnical, engineering way of
\par
thinking, a problem solving way of working, an adaptive way of controlling and a dynamic way
\par
of modelling. The proper fit between these different approaches is examined in detail.\textbf{Keywords:}Business Engineering, Organizational Impacts of IT, Social and Behavioral
\par
Sciences, Simulation Support Systems
\par
\textbf{1 Introduction}
\par
The Amsterdam Municipal Police Force is facing serious problems. In the Netherlands, crime is
\par
becoming a problem of major importance. Within the last fifty years, crime rates have actually
\par
been multiplied by ten (Department of Justice 85). From recent governmental research, it
\par
appears that every inhabitant of Amsterdam of 15 years or older has a 50\% chance annually to
\par
fall victim to one or more criminal offenses (Department of Home Affairs 91). Thus,
\par
Amsterdam has one of the highest crime rates amongst European cities. Note however, that in
\par
Holland 'criminal offence' covers anything from bicycle thefts to acts of violence. Regardless of
\par
this mitigation, the Amsterdam Municipal Police has an arduous task.
\par
To improve the performance of the Amsterdam Police Force, a large-scale reorganization was
\par
set in motion in the late eighties which is still ongoing today. Information technology is regarded
\par
as one of the building blocks of the reorganization process. Within the force, numerous plans
\par
have been formulated for restructuring the current business and information processes of the
\par
force. To judge their effectiveness, their impact on the performance of the force must be
\par
established. However, actually experimenting with police departments can be expensive and full
\par
of risk. Since the last reorganization was carried out only recently, the force's management is
\par
reluctant in announcing additional changes. The business processes and the information
\par
processes themselves are rather complex and unpredictable: most activities are carried out on a
\par
basis of experience and professionalism, and are coordinated through mutual adjustment. Few
\par
work procedures are formalized. Combined, these reasons make the restructuring of the
\par
Amsterdam Police Force a fuzzy, \emph{'soft' problem}: complex, difficult to tackle with both technical
\par
and social components.
\par
According to the force's management, crime is not a problem of the police alone: it is becoming
\par
a problem of major social importance. This has created a special interest in cooperation with
\par
universities and other scientific institutions. In particular, the School of Systems Engineering
\par
and Policy Analysis has been asked to investigate the possibilities and impossibilities of
\par
changing and restructuring police work.
\par
A short literature review reveals that the Amsterdam Municipal Police Force is not the only
\par
organization in the midst of a restructuring process. To survive, many organizations need to
\par
transform their business more frequently than they used to do. Business processes of these
\par
organizations are becoming more and more information intensive, and yet they are seldom
\par
designed with the possibilities of new information technology in mind. It could be fruitful to
\par
transform technology and user environment in an integral way instead of what is usually done:
\par
holding one constant and changing the other. Such \emph{integral} design of both organizational
\par
structures and processes and information systems is called 'business engineering' (BE) in this
\par
paper.
\par
The aim of this paper is to define a 'hard core' of an integrated design approach for business
\par
engineering. In section 1, the notion of BE is further elaborated and a short literature review into
\par
BE and related fields is presented. In section 2, a first research framework is described forfurther investigation into BE in a real life problem situation. In section 3, the results of the
\par
application of this research framework in a case study within the Amsterdam Police Force is
\par
described. In section 4, the case study results are evaluated and combined with the results of the
\par
literature review, resulting in an outline of an integrated design approach for BE. In section 5,
\par
conclusions are drawn and implications are given for further research.
\par
\textbf{2 Business Engineering}
\par
\emph{2.1 Why transformation?}
\par
Organizations in our society are facing serious problems (Hammer 90). Most of the structures,
\par
processes and technologies used within organizations today stem from the industrial age. The
\par
design principles of Frederick Taylor have long been the favourite guidelines of American
\par
industry (Davenport and Short 90). The functional-differentiated hierarchy has long been the
\par
preferred organization model when manufacturing was predominant and the business
\par
environment was stable. With the help of new technologies, manufacturers were able to expand
\par
by improving the productivity of their employees dramatically (Drucker 91).
\par
In our globally networked society, the assumptions and guidelines of the industrial age no longer
\par
hold. The boundaries in our environment are subject to change due to global transitions -
\par
changing in type and geography (Sol 92). The organizational environment is no longer stable,
\par
but characterized by increasing complexity, hostility and turbulence (Huber 84). 'Competitive
\par
pressures' such as global competition, risk, service and costs force major organizations to change
\par
(Rockart and Short 89). Rapid technological change, as well as shifting patterns of international
\par
trade and competition, have put intense strain on many organizations (Miles and Snow 86).
\par
Information technology in particular offers organizations new possibilities, and yet the
\par
experience with the application of this kind of technology appears to be rather paradoxical (Sol
\par
92).
\par
Manufacturing is no longer predominant in our globally networked society. In 1990, the service
\par
industry accounted for 75\% of all jobs in the private economy. Over the past decade, the U.S.
\par
service industry has created nearly 20 million new jobs (Roach 91). Unlike manufacturing,
\par
products devised by the service industry have a high information content. Hence, an important
\par
part of the business processes of a service organization involves information processing (Dur
\par
92). However, despite enormous capital investments in information technology (Davenport and
\par
Short 90), non-manufacturing productivity (a good proxy for productivity in the service sector)
\par
has come to a virtual standstill since the mid-seventies (Baily and Chakrabarti 89, Brynjolfsson
\par
93, Drucker 91, Roach 91, Thurow 89). Providing more and better information does not
\par
automatically result in better organizational performance (Sol 92). Not surprisingly, Drucker
\par
(1991) states that 'the single greatest challenge facing managers in the developed countries is to
\par
raise the productivity of knowledge and service workers in organizations'. Illustrative in this
\par
sense is that substantial gains are made especially by organizations that succeed in integrating
\par
services within an entire chain rather than in streamlining subprocesses. Typical examples can
\par
be found in transport, health care, financial and telecom services (Davidson 93, Short 92, Smith
\par
and McKeen 93, Sol 92).\emph{2.2 Organization and information: where is the rub?}
\par
Organizations can be seen as purposeful systems, justified in their existence by the function they
\par
perform in their environment (Ackoff and Emery 72). Organizations with structures, processes
\par
and technologies that are well suited to their environment are more likely to survive than poorly
\par
adapted ones (Burns and Stalker 61, Lawrence and Lorsch 67). The problems of organizations in
\par
our globally networked society indicate a mismatch between internal structure and external
\par
conditions. In other words, organizations need to transform their business in order to survive.
\par
Business literature abounds with all kinds of solutions for organizational transformation. Similar
\par
to consumer products, the concepts offered seem to have a life-cycle of their own: they grow,
\par
mature, age and fade away (Vantrappen 92). Likewise, with organizations becoming more and
\par
more dependent on information systems, the information systems literature has become manifest
\par
as a buzzwords generator in its own right (Sol 85).
\par
At first sight, the concepts of both bodies of literature seem to have some kind of relationship.
\par
An adhocracy organization, for example, seems to be best supported by advanced 'Computer
\par
Supported Cooperative Work systems', while Electronic Data Interchange' seems to fit well with
\par
'Just-In-Time-management'. Different organizational structures seem to require different
\par
technologies and vice versa. Over time, various relationships between organizations and IT have
\par
been suggested, especially in implementation literature, ranging from 'the use of IT leads to
\par
increased centralization' to 'the use of IT is dependent on existing power structures'. Empirical
\par
evidence for these views is both conflicting and confusing (Attewel and Rule 84, George and
\par
King 91, Markus 88, Miller et al. 91, Raymond et al. 93). George and King (1991) therefore
\par
suggest a more neutral relationship of mutual reinforcement.
\par
This interpretation explains the differences in empirical evidence and shows why generalizations
\par
about possible structural relationships are difficult to obtain by evaluation of the use of IT in
\par
organizations. Instead of looking \emph{ex-post}, it can be more fruitful to look at the use of IT in
\par
organizations \emph{ex-ante}. The question is not \emph{whether} there exists a synergetic relationship, but
\par
rather \emph{how} such relationship can be established (Sol 92). IT and organizational structures should
\par
be examined not from an emergent perspective, but from a design point of view. This perception
\par
of the role of IT in organizations has been labelled 'business engineering' (BE).
\par
\emph{2.3 What is business engineering?}
\par
'Business engineering' (BE) has become popular both in the business and information systems
\par
literature to denote organizational transformation. Some remarkable successes with the BE
\par
concept are reported in literature (Davenport and short 90, Grover 93, Hammer 90, Kaplan an
\par
Muirdock 91). There is no consensus yet on its definition (King 91, Smith and McKeen 90), or
\par
even its name: 'business process engineering' (Hammer 90), 'the new industrial engineering'
\par
(Davenport and Short 90), 'core process redesign' (Kaplan and Muirdock 91), and 'business
\par
process redesign' (Grover 93) seem to be as many names for what simply can be called 'working
\par
smarter' (Drucker 91). A common characteristic of BE literature is the focus on the design of
\par
information technology (IT) and organizational processes and structures \emph{in an integral way}
\par
(Davenport and Short 90, Dennis 93, Fried 91, Grover 93, Hammer 90, Kaplan and Muirdock
\par
91, Vogel et al. 93). Bots and Sol (1988) distinguish three perspectives with respect to the design
\par
of organizations and their technical information structures:{$\bullet$} \emph{the macro-perspective}, concentrating on the cooperation between and above different
\par
organizations and on supporting and enabling technical infrastructures
\par
{$\bullet$} \emph{the meso-perspective}, concentrating on coordinating activities that take place within the
\par
boundaries of an organization and on supporting and enabling technical architectures;
\par
{$\bullet$} \emph{the micro-perspective}, concentrating on the primary business processes that are
\par
performed at the work place level, typically by an individual or a small group, and on
\par
enabling and supporting work stations.
\par
\textbf{0} Positioning of Business Engineering
\par
These design perspectives can be used to better position the fuzzy concept of 'business
\par
engineering'. Depending on the perspective, three different forms of organizational
\par
transformation can be distinguished (Sol 92): infrastructure engineering (macro), process
\par
engineering (meso), and task engineering (micro), see 0. \emph{Business engineering} is the
\par
combination of these three forms. \emph{Infrastructure engineering} deals with strategic choices about
\par
partners, markets and products and has always been part of the job of executive management.
\par
\emph{Task engineering} deals with job design and screen lay-out and has been the job of industrial
\par
engineers and system analysts for years. In particular, \emph{process engineering} is truly innovative,
\par
for a number of reasons.
\par
Organizations are seldom structured with the possibilities of new information technologies in
\par
mind (Huber and McDaniel 86, Strassman 88). Most of the business structures nowadays have
\par
not been designed at all, but have emerged over time (Hammer 90). IT has mainly been used for
\par
automation of the resulting structures and processes, and seldom or not with new business
\par
structures in mind (Applegate et al. 88, Chen and Nunamaker 89, Davenport and Short 90,
\par
Robey 83). Infrastructure engineering is ineffective without process engineering (Streng 92); for
\par
successful implementation of strategic choices, business processes need to be redesigned andrestructured (Davenport 93). Therefore, business engineering hinges on the process dimension.
\par
Business engineering focuses on changing both technology and user environment in an integral
\par
way instead of holding one constant and changing the other. This makes business engineering a
\par
hard to tackle \emph{'soft' problem,} with both social and technical components.
\par
\emph{2.4 A design methodology: what to look for?}
\par
Business engineering (BE) is characterized by 'radical change' (Heygate and Brebach 92) that is
\par
'cross-functional' (Hammer 90) and 'IT-enabled' (Smith and McKeen 93). Mostly within short
\par
time frames, complex design decisions must be made about entire business and technical
\par
infrastructures. For successful implementation, personnel, financial, and information policies of
\par
an organization need to be aligned. BE can thus have a great effect on the functioning of an
\par
organization as a whole (Hammer 90, Kaplan and Muirdock 91), and it is not without risks
\par
(Smith and McKeen 93). BE must therefore be carefully managed and planned (Heygate and
\par
Brebach 92). What in fact is needed is a 'good-and-sound' design methodology for guiding a BE
\par
process.
\par
Before looking into possible design methods, concepts, tools and techniques for BE, the notion
\par
of a 'good-and-sound' design methodology must be further defined. A \emph{design methodology} can
\par
intuitively be defined as a coherent set of activities, guidelines and techniques that can structure,
\par
guide and improve complex design processes. With respect to design processes, Simon (1973)
\par
states that 'one of the most important uses of a computer is to model complex situations and to
\par
infer the consequences of alternative decisions to overcome bounded human rationality'. So,
\par
modelling and automated support is crucial for a design methodology. The analytical framework
\par
of Seligman et al. (Seligmann et al. 89) has therefore been used for sharpening the intuitive
\par
definition as it pays explicit attention to these aspects. According to this framework, a design
\par
methodology is characterized by a way of thinking, controlling, working, and modelling as
\par
presented in 0. Preferably, these "ways" are supported by a coherent set of automated tools (a
\par
designers' environment or workbench).
\par
\emph{A way of thinking} determines the underlying philosophy or 'weltanschauung' of a design
\par
methodology. It can be seen as a delineation of the problem domain the methodology addresses
\par
(here: BE), possibly supported with a design theory (here: a coherent set of concepts and
\par
principles for designing organizations). The way of thinking is often overshadowed by, or
\par
implicit in, the techniques and methods embedded in the methodology. These hidden
\par
assumptions are, however, of great influence on the ultimate appropriateness of methodologies.
\par
The \emph{way of controlling} deals with the managerial aspects of the methodology. It includes
\par
planning and feedback and determines in which ways various persons and groups should
\par
interact. The \emph{way of working} consists of the tasks of the methodology which must be performed.
\par
Synonyms for tasks in this context are steps, phases, activities or actions. The \emph{way of modelling}
\par
consists of the design products or intermediate results of the methodology. This will be mainly
\par
models of the resulting system in terms of conceptual models or specific model formalisms.
\par
The original analytical framework has successfully been used for understanding the structure of
\par
a number of IS methodologies (Verhoef 93, Wijers 91). The framework can, however, also be
\par
used in a more prescriptive way. Following the configuration hypothesis of Mintzberg (1979a),
\par
\emph{a 'good and sound' design methodology} is a methodology which explicitly addresses all five
\par
dimensions of the framework and has a 'good and consistent fit' between them.\textbf{0} The analytical framework: a design methodology and its support
\par
\emph{2.5 A brief literature review into BE and related fields}
\par
The normative definition of a 'good-and-sound' design methodology has been used for analyzing
\par
and understanding recent BE literature and literature from related fields. A brief literature review
\par
has been carried out into design methods, concepts, tools and techniques for supporting BE
\par
design processes. This literature review has consisted of four steps. First, four bodies of
\par
literature have been selected for further investigation:
\par
{$\bullet$}\emph{BE methods}:literature on how to apply BE, often with examples;
\par
{$\bullet$}\emph{new organizational forms}:literature on new IT-enabled organizational forms;
\par
{$\bullet$}\emph{organization design}:literature on division of labour and coordination;
\par
{$\bullet$}\emph{information systems design}:literature on designing IT applications.
\par
The first two bodies of literature can be seen as complementary, since literature on 'new
\par
organizational forms' actually describes possible results of the application of a 'BE method'. The
\par
last two bodies of literature can also be seen as complementary, since organization design and
\par
information systems design each cover one side of the definition of BE.
\par
Next, for the publications considered relevant, each was condensed into a one-page summary to
\par
obtain a general overview. Third, the analytical framework for understanding as described in the
\par
previous section has been applied to gain a better understanding. A matrix has been set up with
\par
the dimensions of the framework on the X-axis and the different literature categories on the Y-
\par
axis. The cells of the matrix contain summary results and key words (the result can be found in
\par
the appendix). Finally, the matrix has been used for further analysis to determine (partial)
\par
support for a 'good-and-sound' design methodology for BE. Detailed results of the literatureanalysis are given in (Meel 94), but some general conclusions about the four categories can be
\par
made here.
\par
BE methods found in literature roughly follow the pattern of general problem solving, but pay
\par
little attention to explaining why this specific method would be more beneficial than other
\par
methods. Suggested new organizational forms have often common characteristics: they are flat,
\par
project- and team-oriented, and make use of advanced information technology, especially
\par
communication technology. Both literature on BE methods and literature on new organizational
\par
forms appear to be rather fuzzy and not well elaborated, although both offer intriguing results
\par
and promising viewpoints. The literature on organization design offers a rich set of design
\par
theories and modelling concepts, but pays less attention to the procedural aspects of design. The
\par
literature on information systems design offers a rich set of methods, modelling formalisms,
\par
techniques and tools, but pays less attention to design theories. Most striking is the fact that
\par
neither organization design literature nor information systems design literature pay much
\par
attention to the possibilities of \emph{integral} organization and information systems design.
\par
It appears that BE obtains little methodological support. However, an optimist can see the field
\par
of BE as an integrating research area for organization and information systems design. By using
\par
modelling formalisms and support tools from information system design \emph{and} design theories and
\par
modelling concepts from organization design in a problem solving approach, actual business
\par
process redesign efforts can be structured, guided and supported. Since it would be hard to
\par
integrate these admittedly equivocal aspects in a strictly deductive way, an exploratory case
\par
study has been carried out. Before starting, a rough research framework has been defined.
\par
\textbf{3 Defining a research framework}
\par
Explorative research does not release the researcher from formulating an explicit conceptual
\par
framework before starting his study. A rough (not rigorous) research framework ('working
\par
frame' in Miles 79) needs always to be defined before the field can be entered (Checkland 91,
\par
Miles 79, Mintzberg 79b, Yin 89). For the exploratory case study reported in this paper, a
\par
'problem solving perspective' which relies strongly on the use of 'dynamic models' has been
\par
chosen.
\par
\emph{3.1 Problem solving}
\par
Business engineering greatly influences the functioning of organizations. Personnel, financial,
\par
and information policies need to be aligned for successful change. The effects of organizational
\par
change need to be thoroughly explored in advance to legitimate such drastic changes, regardless
\par
of the complexity of the organizations involved. Costs, loss of production and safety risk,
\par
however, generally prohibit the experimentation with alternatives in reality. According to Sol
\par
(Sol 82), it can be fruitful to use a systematic, model-based \emph{problem solving} approach for this
\par
kind of design problem. Based on the model cycle of Mitroff et al. (1974), Sol (1982) argues
\par
that problem solving can be seen as a model cycle, consisting of a number of steps and products,
\par
as shown in 0.\textbf{0} The process of problem solving
\par
A \emph{problem situation} is a situation in which a decision maker, or a group of decision makers, is
\par
dissatisfied with the current situation, has alternative courses of action available, can make
\par
choices which have a significant effect, and has some doubt as to which alternative to select
\par
(Ackoff 81, Dur 92). Note that this definition does not imply that all alternatives are known in
\par
advance (Bots 89). The first activity of the problem solving cycle is the creation of a mental
\par
model of the current problem situation, which can broadly express the actual problem situation
\par
and potential solutions. Once formulated, this model is referred to as \emph{'conceptual model'}. The
\par
second activity is the more detailed specification of the problem situation, necessary to obtain an
\par
empirical model of the problem area. This results in a \emph{descriptive empirical model} of the
\par
problem situation. The third activity is to analyze the problem situation and generate solutions
\par
for it, using the empirical model as a substitute for the real problem situation. Solutions are
\par
translated in terms of the empirical model, transforming the descriptive empirical 'as is' into a
\par
\emph{prescriptive empirical model} 'as could be' or \emph{experimental model}. Comparing the effects of the
\par
various experimental models results in a set of tentative solutions, which can be implemented in
\par
the problem situation. Provided that the empirical model 'as is' corresponds closely to the
\par
problem situation, the experimental model provides reasonably accurate information concerning
\par
the effects of a specific solution, should it be implemented. This implies that the descriptive
\par
empirical model must resemble the problem area closely and that this correspondence must be
\par
tested empirically.\emph{3.2 Dynamic models}
\par
Models of organizations resembling real problem situations can be very complex. First,
\par
organizations can be modelled from many different perspectives, depending on the viewpoints
\par
within the organization. Second, depending on the perspective, a great number of sequences of
\par
activities within the organization can be modelled. Third, most of these sequences are dependent
\par
on time or status and most of them take place concurrently. Models are needed which can have
\par
different levels of abstraction, consisting of parallel, concurrent sequences of activities and
\par
which can incorporate an explicit time dimension. Models with these characteristics are defined
\par
as \emph{'dynamic models'} (Dur and Bots 92).
\par
For these dynamic models, a number of description forms do exist, like (coloured) Petri-nets,
\par
Causal Models, Event Network Models and Action Diagrams (Sol and van Hee 91, Sol and
\par
Crosslin 92, Sol and Verbraeck 92). However, the basic building blocks of these formalisms do
\par
have a high level of abstraction. Application of these formalisms to real life problem situations
\par
results in rather abstract representations and tends to be difficult to comprehend for those who
\par
are not experts in these techniques. For complex problem situations, the resulting models tend to
\par
become very large. It can be very difficult to comprehend such a dynamic model, thus impeding
\par
its validation (Dur 92). A dynamic modelling formalism that tries to overcome these problems is
\par
task/actor modelling (Bots 89. Dur 92).
\par
\emph{Task/actor modelling} offers building blocks that do resemble actual phenomena within
\par
organizations, and yet still have the appropriate features for modelling dynamic aspects of
\par
business processes. The basic modelling constructs of task/actor modelling are 'tasks', 'actors'
\par
and 'items'. A task/actor model consists of a number of actors which can perform a number of
\par
tasks on distinct items in order to achieve a certain goal. To incorporate dynamic aspects, the
\par
notion of an 'agenda mechanism' is used. Each actor has his own agenda which tells him which
\par
tasks he needs to perform. When a task occurs, it is put on the agenda of the actor; upon
\par
finishing a task, an actor selects a new task from his agenda. Actors and items are represented by
\par
\emph{object definitions}; the sequencing and coordination between tasks is represented by \emph{task}
\par
\emph{structures}.
\par
Object definitions and task structures can be transformed quite easily into an executable \emph{discrete}
\par
\emph{event simulation model}. Animation is used to visualize the dynamic aspects of the modelled
\par
business process by showing status changes of the model over time (Verbraeck and de Vreede
\par
93, Wierda 91). Within the problem solving cycle of 0, the animated discrete event simulation
\par
model is equivalent to the empirical model. For the correspondence test between the dynamic
\par
simulation model and the problem area, structural and replicative validation can be used (Dur
\par
92). \emph{Structural validation} is performed to test whether the model behaves in special situations
\par
consistently with the expectations of the designer. \emph{Replicative validation} is performed to test the
\par
degree of similarity between the numerical output of the simulation model and the
\par
corresponding values in reality (Kleijnen 92).\emph{3.3 The experience}
\par
Dynamic modelling in a problem solving context as described in the previous sections has been
\par
used and refined in a number of research projects (Sol 92), for example in designing
\par
interorganizational information systems for international rail transport of cargo (Wierda 91), in
\par
the development of an information system for treasury management and in-house banking
\par
(Motshagen 91), in the application of fleet management information systems with mobile data
\par
communication (Schrijver and Sol 91), in a project supporting investment decisions on
\par
application of EDI in the port of Rotterdam (Streng 92), and in evaluating the effects of
\par
introducing new information technology into administrative organizations (Dur 92). Each study
\par
strongly focused on modelling of complex organizational processes to evaluate in advance the
\par
effects of introducing information technology in organizations. These findings have made clear
\par
that dynamic modelling can be of value for supporting business engineering in real life problem
\par
situations. Therefore, dynamic modelling is used as a research framework for the study as
\par
described in this paper.
\par
\textbf{4 The case of the Amsterdam Municipal Police Force}
\par
As stated in the introduction, crime is becoming a social problem of major importance in the
\par
Netherlands. To improve the performance of the Police Force, a large scale reorganization has
\par
been set in motion in the late eighties which is still going on today.
\par
\emph{4.1 The problem situation}
\par
Within the force, lots of ideas do exist for improving its performance. Strategic reports contain
\par
discussions about topics such as 'innovation', 'prevention', 'the police as an enterprise', and 'the
\par
police manager as entrepreneur'. Keywords in the current situation are 'decentralisation',
\par
'management by objectives', 'project work' and 'information technology'. At the operational
\par
level, so called \emph{'neighbourhood teams'} are created: small, decentralized units responsible for
\par
general police work in small geographical areas. This implies that the teams have to deal with all
\par
matters in their neighbourhood themselves, from the first call for service by the public to
\par
handing someone over to the public prosecutor. Within the neighbourhood teams, \emph{'management}
\par
\emph{by objectives'} has been introduced. The top management of the force annually defines 'force
\par
objectives': problem areas on which the activities of the force must be focused. Based on these
\par
objectives, the neighbourhood teams must set up a number of \emph{'project plans'}, in which the teams
\par
define how they expect to reach these objectives. Projects are meant to open up opportunities for
\par
more preventive action, and should become the main activity of each neighbourhood team.
\par
Information technology is regarded as one of the essential building blocks of the change process.
\par
The Amsterdam police force has developed a multi-user transaction processing system to
\par
facilitate the paper work within neighbourhood teams. This system is organized around a central
\par
database, which can be used at a remote level. The force's former central registration and
\par
information department is no longer needed. In the past, \emph{a management reporting tool} has been
\par
created for providing figures about incidents and crime rates of a neighbourhood as whole. This
\par
reporting tool has a lot of shortcomings. Most of the data definitions of the tool are out of date. It
\par
has no interface to the force's central transaction processing system, so it must be fed with data
\par
manually. Since it is not very motivating to input the same data twice, the reporting systemcontains a lot of inaccurate and outdated information. However, the main problem is that the
\par
system can deal with 'traditional' police data concerning incidents and accidents, but hardly with
\par
data concerning projects. No information could be generated about individual projects.
\par
According to the users, the reporting tool did not fit in the project-oriented neighbourhood teams
\par
anymore.
\par
This problem with relevant, correct and accurate management information for neighbourhood
\par
teams was the immediate cause for the Amsterdam Municipal Police Force to authorize a first
\par
study. The assignment was to investigate the possibilities of management information systems
\par
for supporting project work. To gain insight in the business process of neighbourhood teams,
\par
dynamic modelling was used.
\par
\emph{4.2 Conceptualization}
\par
The first step of the case study was the conceptualization of the problem area under study. The
\par
actual problem area has been defined as the daily activities of the neighbourhood teams of the
\par
Amsterdam Police Force. The Force has twenty-four neighbourhood teams, each consisting of
\par
sixty to eighty officers. Each team has the same organizational structure: each neighbourhood
\par
team has its own chief, assisted by a staff officer, and consists of eight crews of six to eight
\par
officers and one chief. Crews work in shifts and have their own schedule, defining starts and
\par
ends of the crew shifts. There are two types of shifts: during the 'assistance shift', a crew is
\par
responsible for car patrol and answering questions from the public at the desk of the
\par
neighbourhood team. During the 'prevention shift', a crew performs tasks like paper work and
\par
project activities; these tasks are mostly performed in the office. For the documentation of the
\par
results of the conceptual phase, object definitions as described by Bots (1989) were used.
\par
\emph{4.3 Specification}
\par
Based on the results of the conceptualization, a first dynamic simulation model has been
\par
developed for the activities of one of the neighbourhood teams. With this model, it could be
\par
shown that neighbourhood teams spent just 15\% of their total daily capacity on project work.
\par
The main activities of a neighbourhood team appeared to be car patrol and handling the resulting
\par
paper work. Given this observation, one can wonder whether a new MIS would contribute to a
\par
better functioning of the entire organizational chain. The objective of the case study was
\par
therefore reformulated from MIS design to looking for ways to increase the time spent on
\par
execution of project work1. Based on the results of this first prototype dynamic model, a more
\par
sophisticated dynamic model has been developed for three of the twenty four neighbourhood
\par
teams of the force (for a detailed description of this model, see Meel 94).
\par
A modelled neighbourhood team consists of a number of crews, each with its own schedule.
\par
During a scheduled shift, each police officer is responsible for a specific set of tasks. During car
\par
patrol the officers respond to requests for assistance; when on desk duty, an officer helps citizens
\par
at the desk of the neighbourhood team; when on office duty, an officer performs administrative
\par
tasks, project activities, and other tasks, such as investigation, maintenance and looking after the
\par
neighbourhood team dog. Tasks within the model are triggered by work supply. Calls for service
\par
are generated by citizens and received by the operator in the communication centre. The
\par
1Note that this reformulation of problem and goals during the specification phase is quite common for this
\par
approach. The empirical model serves well in avoiding type III errors (solving the wrong problem).operator asks the citizen a few questions to decide whether police action is required. If so, he
\par
assigns the call a priority ranging from 1 to 3 and sends it to the operator who communicates
\par
with the patrol cars. If no patrol car is available, the communication operator can decide, based
\par
on the call's priority, its location, and an additional set of rules, whether it is necessary to
\par
interrupt a patrol car, to let the neighbourhood team start a new car patrol team or to ignore the
\par
call. The communication operator is therefore able to change the capacity of the number of
\par
police officers in the model. A call that has been handled by a car patrol team can result in paper
\par
work for the neighbourhood team. Other work supply types in the model are project activities,
\par
citizens at the desk of the neighbourhood team and 'secondary', which is an aggregation of
\par
activities which are too insignificant to be individually incorporated in the model. A work
\par
supply generator is used for modelling that piece of the problem situation which influences the
\par
amounts of work supply, but which is not direct relevant according to the problem statement.
\par
To be able to simulate the activities of a neighbourhood team, data such as inter-arrival times,
\par
amounts of paper works, and absence figures has been analyzed. The results of these analyses
\par
have been added to the model by means of theoretical and empirical random distributions. Much
\par
attention has been paid to data with respect to car patrol as this is the main activity of the
\par
neighbourhood teams. For want of aggregated, directly usable information, raw data from the
\par
registration system of the communication centre has been analyzed. First, the numbers of
\par
incoming calls have been analyzed for the three neighbourhood teams involved. Per year, per
\par
week and per day, these numbers are rather stable, but they show strong hourly fluctuations,
\par
mainly depending on the period of the day. Within the data, seven periods have been
\par
distinguished with similar inter-arrival characteristics. The inter-arrival time per period has been
\par
modelled by an exponential random distribution. Other random distributions related to car patrol
\par
have appeared to depend mainly on the accident type of incoming calls. Accident types are
\par
represented by 120 different codes, in the registration system of the communication centre.
\par
These codes range from accidents such as 'traffic accidents' and 'noise disturbance' to more
\par
exotic ones like 'ghost driver', 'unleashed pitbull' and 'exhibitionist'. By grouping codes with
\par
similar characteristics such as service time, inter-arrival times and definition, the 120 codes were
\par
aggregated to 33 code groups. Goodness of fit between these exogenous model data and real
\par
data has been verified with the Chi-square test and the Kolmochorov-Smirnov test (Meel 94).
\par
\emph{4.4 Correspondence check}
\par
To test its correspondence with the problem area, the dynamic simulation model was validated
\par
by means of structural and replicative validation.
\par
The animation features of SIMAN/Cinema (Pegden et al. 90) were used for the structural
\par
validation. In SIMAN/Cinema, graphical animation screens can be defined, representing the
\par
internal status of the model. This results in a movie-like animation of the behaviour of the model
\par
at run time. An example screen dump of the animation is given in 0, showing the business
\par
processes of one of the modelled neighbourhood team. The structural validation was carried out
\par
with the help of the police officers of the neighbourhood teams involved. The developed model,
\par
and especially the graphical representation of the model, has turned out to be very recognizable.
\par
Samples from the time registration system of the neighbourhood teams involved were used for
\par
the replicative validation. Discrepancies between model and sample results were tested with the
\par
t-test for comparison. Both the structural and the replicative validation indicated an acceptable
\par
correspondence between the dynamic model and the problem area.\textbf{0} Business process of a modelled neighbourhood team
\par
\emph{4.5 Solution finding}
\par
Several experiments were carried out with the developed dynamic simulation model. The results
\par
were analyzed and discussed with members of the police staff.
\par
Based on conversations with police officers, our own findings, the results of a task group of the
\par
police force, and an analysis of the dynamic simulation model, twenty-five potential change
\par
alternatives were identified. Among them were alternatives such as the employment of more
\par
police officers, the development of faster computer systems, and different procedures for car
\par
patrol. After the identification phase, the alternatives were translated in terms of the dynamic
\par
simulation model of the problem situation.
\par
Contrary to police expectations, some of the obvious alternatives had no consequences for the
\par
time spent on projects. Alternatives such as the introduction of new and faster information
\par
technology for paper work, or more rigorous procedures within the communication centre, have
\par
no structural effects on the time spent on projects. The optimization of subprocesses does not
\par
seem to lead to a better functioning of the police organization. The deployment of more police
\par
officers in the model does not lead to a proportional increase in time spent on execution of
\par
projects either. This phenomenon appeared to be caused by detachments to other departments or
\par
illness. A third of the police officers of neighbourhood teams is structurally not available for
\par
daily operations. Rather than employing more police officers, alternatives should aim at
\par
decreasing this structural absence. The greatest effect is achieved by the removal of the division
\par
of labour between car patrol and office duties. This implies that car patrol officers are added to
\par
the officers doing office work while remaining available for requests for assistance. Between
\par
service requests, car patrol officers consequently work in the office. This construction does not
\par
automatically result in the expected negative effects such as an increased number of ignored
\par
calls or interrupted activities of patrol teams.
\par
Based on the above results, a mix of alternatives is suggested for the three neighbourhood teams
\par
examined:{$\bullet$}handling of requests for assistance by office based teams;
\par
{$\bullet$}reducing the percentage of agents placed in other departments from 7\% to 3\%;
\par
{$\bullet$}reducing the percentage of agents absent by illness or leave from 22\% to 17\%;
\par
{$\bullet$}reducing the percentage of time spent on 'remaining' tasks from 19\% to 14\%.
\par
These alternatives lead to a combined increase in the time spent on execution projects from 15\%
\par
to 40\%. For the total capacity of 156 police officers, this implies up to 40 extra officers available
\par
daily for projects for the three neighbourhood teams involved. Extrapolated to the total capacity
\par
of the neighbourhood teams of the force, this increase implies a saving of 8\% on the force's
\par
annual budget.
\par
\emph{4.6 Implementation}
\par
When the alternatives are to be implemented, project work will become the main activity of
\par
neighbourhood teams. However, this change in focus has severe implications. First, the tasks of
\par
the neighbourhood team chief need to change. Presently, most of the time of the team chief is
\par
taken up by day-to-day operational problems and human resource management. However, when
\par
project work becomes a major part of the daily routine, different skills are needed. The team
\par
chief must be able to plan projects in advance, to raise funds for the projects of his team within
\par
and outside the force, to manage large, time-consuming projects and to evaluate the impact of
\par
the projects on the safety in the neighbourhood.
\par
Second, the problems with the current management reporting tool will become more urgent. At
\par
the moment, the Amsterdam Municipal Police Force is developing an automated crime analysis
\par
system. This expert system can create graphical and tabular representations of the crime rates in
\par
a certain geographical area and can analyze trends. However, the management information tools
\par
developed by the teams for their own usage are not sophisticated at all. With the aid of standard
\par
software packages, small reporting systems have been developed for answering simple
\par
management questions like 'How much personnel capacity is available the coming month?',
\par
'Which projects are currently running and what is their status?', and 'How many incidents have
\par
been handled by the neighbourhood team?'. Despite technical and procedural shortcomings, the
\par
functionality of the reporting systems seems to be appropriate for the team chiefs and the
\par
information is actually being used by them. What is needed, in fact, for the near future are not
\par
sophisticated, narrow focused crime analysis systems but flexible reporting tools which can give
\par
information about the business processes as a whole.
\par
\emph{4.7 Evaluation}
\par
The results of the case study were presented to the management and two tasks forces of the
\par
Amsterdam Municipal Police Force. The low percentage of time spent on project work turned
\par
out to be an eye opener. The results showed a gap between the vision of top management and
\par
actual daily routines. The presentations formed a basis for a critical discussion about the original
\par
reorganization objectives, implementation and current business processes. The alternatives given
\par
and arguments provided proved to be acceptable. In particular, the introduction of flexible
\par
response-teams and the reduction of the structural absence were seen as valuable improvements.
\par
The fact that the results not only indicated these effective directions, but also the many
\par
ineffective directions for business engineering, was regarded as one of the major merits of the
\par
case study.\textbf{5 Reflection}
\par
Although the case study results are promising, the question whether the methods and techniques
\par
used form a 'good and sound' design methodology can not be answered right away. Therefore,
\par
the approach used in the case is analyzed in further detail and combined with the results of the
\par
literature survey as described in section 2.
\par
\emph{5.1 Way of thinking}
\par
The way of thinking consists of a delineation of the problem domain and a design theory. A
\par
delineation of the 'soft' problem of BE has been given in section 1.
\par
As a starting point in formulating a design theory, the SocioTechnical design paradigm seems to
\par
be quite appropriate for BE because it distinguishes explicitly between a social and a technical
\par
subsystem and advocates a joint optimization of them. This paradigm can be closely related to
\par
the positioning of BE between organization design and information systems design in section 1.
\par
It is also interesting to notice that the SocioTechnical approach is the only school of thought
\par
which has components \emph{both} in organization and IS design literature (cf. the matrix in the
\par
appendix). Furthermore, many SocioTechnical design concepts can be found in descriptions of
\par
new IT-enabled organizational forms, see f.i. (Lewin and Stephens 93, Ostroff and Smith 92,
\par
Stalk 92).
\par
SocioTechnics offers a wide range of design principles (Eijnatten 93). Four of them fit the case
\par
results:
\par
{$\bullet$} in complex and turbulent environments, the organization as a whole needs to be
\par
decomposed in a number of parallel operating subunits which are responsible for one
\par
complete business process in the chain from supplier to consumer;
\par
{$\bullet$} these subunits ought to operate as semi-autonomous teams and must be able to carry out
\par
their daily routines quite independently from other subunits;
\par
{$\bullet$} management structures should be designed to support general decision making, giving
\par
semi-autonomous teams self-control over the budgets, equipment, personnel and
\par
information they use;
\par
{$\bullet$} management information should not give highly specific information about a limited set
\par
of aspects, but ought to give generalized and aggregated information about a business
\par
process as a whole.
\par
Together, these four basic principles constitute a global (though limited) design theory for
\par
flexible, information intensive service organizations in complex, hostile environments.\emph{5.2 Way of controlling}
\par
The case study was carried out in close cooperation with police officers from various
\par
departments. Dynamic models were developed in an incremental way by means of prototyping.
\par
A control strategy which seems to fit most with that of the case study is an adaptive strategy
\par
(Keen 80). This strategy sees a design project as an adaptive process of learning for both
\par
consultants and stakeholders. Consultants can learn from the stakeholders about current
\par
operations, bottlenecks in current functioning and possible improvements. Stakeholders can
\par
learn from consultants about design methodologies, techniques and tools. For effective learning,
\par
a close cooperation between stakeholders and consultants is needed.
\par
Design projects must be incremental: a design project must aim at quick delivery of an initial
\par
model to give stakeholders the opportunity to respond and thus clarify what they really want.
\par
The initial model needs to be further developed by means of a number of iterations. At the start
\par
of a design project, organizational and informational problems are often fuzzy and ill-structured,
\par
and final objectives are rarely concise. The project plan should be very open-ended at the start of
\par
a design project, but after each completed step, the project plan needs to be adjusted and
\par
discussed.
\par
To reduce complexity, design projects must also be middle out: rather than trying to grasp the
\par
complete organization at once, take a single unit or process and start from there.
\par
\emph{5.3 Way of working}
\par
The way of working in the case study was driven by actual problems in a real world problem
\par
situation. As stated in section 1, this problem orientation has been found in several other articles
\par
on BE. A problem solving way of working differs from the traditional steps undertaken in IS
\par
design because it focuses not only on information but also on organizational problems.
\par
Organization design literature offers far less support for a way of working. (Laboratory)
\par
experiments as advocated in some Human Relations literature are not always possible.
\par
Diagnostic contingency studies are difficult to relate to real business needs. However, it is
\par
interesting to notice that although the way of thinking of the Classical School of Management
\par
seems to have become obsolete, their 'scientific' way of working, focusing explicitly on real
\par
business problems, is currently achieving more recognition (Davenport and Short 90).
\par
SocioTechnics advocates a problem oriented way of working as well.
\par
\emph{5.4 Way of modelling}
\par
In the case study, the neighbourhood team's business process proved to be very hard to model.
\par
Although some aspects of the force are regulated by law, police officers are professionals and
\par
develop their own procedures. Reasons for structuring at the operational level are seldom made
\par
explicit and most of them have evolved over time. Police officers use their own 'slang' to
\par
communicate with each other and it takes some time to understand their language. Most
\par
business process are not scheduled, but depend on time and status and are very dynamic.
\par
The vocabulary for describing business processes offered by organization design literature is
\par
rather abstract. Relationships between concepts are mostly linear and seldom take interactions
\par
into account, see f.i. (Schoonhoven 81). Organization design seems to lack a realistic vocabulary
\par
for describing functional, material and dynamic relationships. In information systems design,business processes are expressed by means of modelling formalisms such as entity relationship
\par
diagrams and data flow diagrams. However, these modelling techniques can only represent static
\par
aspects. A major exception is formed by the object oriented approaches which model business
\par
processes with a number of concurrent interacting objects. However, object oriented modelling
\par
techniques focus more on the creation of object oriented program structures than on analyzing
\par
and diagnosing business processes. Dynamic modelling is a modelling approach which
\par
elaborates the object oriented modelling paradigm in a form more suitable for business
\par
engineering. In the case study, a special form of dynamic modelling, task/actor modelling has
\par
been used (Dur 92).
\par
\emph{5.5 Automated support}
\par
For the realization of an executable simulation model in the case study, the simulation
\par
environment SIMAN/CINEMA was used (Pegden et al. 90). This environment offers a general
\par
purpose simulation language and very good real-time animation features. In SIMAN/Cinema,
\par
graphical animation screens can be defined, which can represent the internal status of a
\par
simulation model. At run time, this results in a movie-like animation of the behaviour of the
\par
model.
\par
Organization design literature does not pay much attention to support tools, this in contrast with
\par
IS design literature. However, Data Base Management Systems, Data Dictionaries and 4th
\par
generation languages can be very useful for implementation, but less for analysis and design.
\par
CASE-tools can be very helpful for creating and checking of graphical, static representations of
\par
business processes, but are of less help for representing dynamics aspects and experimentations
\par
with alternative designs. Discrete event simulation workbenches do not have these deficiencies
\par
with respect to analysis and design. However, they are less sophisticated in the model
\par
management area.
\par
\emph{5.6 Looking for fits}
\par
The results of the preceding sections are summarized in 0. To be able to speak of a 'good and
\par
sound' design methodology in terms of section 2, the fit between the different ways must be
\par
examined.\textbf{0} Analyzing the BE approach
\par
The way of working and the way of modelling show a good fit. The problem solving way of
\par
working emphasizes the use of models in a design process. The modelling representation
\par
techniques (objects, task structures, animated simulation models) differ in level of abstraction
\par
but can be translated into each other in a flexible way. The output of one step can be used as
\par
input for a following step. The model construction proceeds from low to high complexity, which
\par
accounts for the middle-out control strategy. Multiple iterations of (parts of) the problem solving
\par
cycle are needed to construct such models, which fits the adaptive, incremental control strategy.
\par
The way of thinking is characterized by an engineering perspective (as opposed to an emergent
\par
perspective). This fits with the adaptive and yet controlled problem solving process and the
\par
heavy usage of descriptive and prescriptive modelling. The modelling constructs used offer
\par
sufficient conceptual freedom to represent both technical and organizational aspects of the
\par
problem domain. The way of thinking is also characterized by a SocioTechnical perspective
\par
which is made explicit in a number of design principles. These principles can give guidance in
\par
the transformation of the descriptive model into prescriptive models during the solution finding
\par
phase. Given a descriptive model, the number of options for changing and combining model
\par
objects is exponential and the number of possible solutions can easily get out of hand.
\par
SocioTechnical design principles can indicate where to start looking for improvements. Besides
\par
design principles, the SocioTechnical school of thought also advocates a way of working which
\par
fits with the general problem solving process, and an adaptive way of controlling which
\par
emphasizes a close cooperation between consultants and stakeholders.
\par
Discrete event simulation workbenches and languages effectively support the way of working,
\par
modelling and controlling. The adaptive way of controlling emphasizes learning, both for
\par
consultants and stakeholders; learning implies experimentation. With respect to field
\par
experiments, simulation offers optimal control over experimental conditions. Animation offerspossibilities to present understandable models to stakeholders who are mostly not familiar with
\par
modelling techniques in general and dynamic modelling techniques in particular (Verbraeck and
\par
de Vreede 93). With an animated simulation model, the correspondence of the empirical model
\par
with the problem domain at hand can be checked both structurally and quantitatively (Dur 92).
\par
Problems can be analyzed by means of statistical techniques or by closely tracing animated
\par
model behaviour. Solutions can be translated in terms of changes in model parameters and
\par
model structure. In fact, simulation methodology itself fits well into the general problem solving
\par
cycle (Sol 82). It is also interesting to notice that simulation languages like SIMULA have given
\par
an important impulse to the current object oriented approaches. In simulation languages, lots of
\par
elegant and sophisticated modelling constructs can be found to represent complex, dynamic
\par
entities such as organizations (Bots 89, Dur 92).
\par
Finally, if looked upon from a researcher's, rather than a practioner's point of view, the
\par
combination of design theories and executable, animated discrete event simulation models can
\par
be a very beneficial one. Experimenting with design theories in actual problem settings is often
\par
impeded by the high level of abstraction of the theories at hand and seldom feasible. However, a
\par
discrete event simulation model, enhanced with animation, can give a concrete form to these
\par
design theories. Conversely, simulation makes it possible to test design theories in actual
\par
problem situations in an experimental setting.
\par
\textbf{6 Conclusions and further research}
\par
Business Engineering is becoming a popular word for integral design of \emph{both} organizational
\par
structures and processes \emph{and} information systems. Although interesting and stimulating
\par
experiences with BE are reported in literature, the concept of Business Engineering is still
\par
immature. There are lots of ideas, partial theories, and design principles, but a solid framework
\par
does not exist, let alone a \emph{methodology} for Business Engineering. Therefore, an exploratory case
\par
study has been carried out with BE within the Amsterdam Municipal Police Force. A selection
\par
from conceptual work and practical experience served well as a research framework for this case
\par
study. The results of the case study have shown that a problem solving approach to BE using
\par
dynamic modelling is not only practical, but also congruent with SocioTechnical principles for
\par
organization design, and adaptive, incremental design. In other words, the research reported in
\par
this paper indicates that partial theories from organization and information systems design
\par
literature combined with empirical findings from case studies can be forged into a hard core for
\par
a systematic approach to solving 'soft' BE problems.
\par
Dynamic modelling alone is not sufficient for such types of problems. The focus of existing
\par
literature in this area is especially on the modelling dimension. Modelling formalisms and
\par
automated support for representing complex and dynamic problem situations have been
\par
elaborated but far less attention is paid to aspects other than modelling. With respect to the way
\par
of working, the focus is primarily on the task 'problem conceptualization' and 'model
\par
specification'. Other tasks and control aspects receive far less attention. Integration of dynamic
\par
modelling with elements from fields such as sociotechnical design, soft systems methodology,
\par
and information systems design can compensate for this modelling bias. Further research will
\par
focus on elaborating the systematic approach as outlined in section 5 into in more detail. A
\par
second mcase study is currently underway at the Criminal Investigation Department of the
\par
Amsterdam Municipal Police Force to sharpen and fine tune the approach (see also Meel 94).In parallel to case study research, new support tools are being developed to facilitate the use of
\par
the design approach. Until now, the way of supporting lacks tools and techniques for
\par
experimenting with large and complex dynamic models. As business processes can be organized
\par
in many ways, the number of possible alternatives, and therefore, the number of possible
\par
experiments is almost infinite. Computers and analysts, however, are scarce resources. Tools
\par
and techniques are needed to structure and plan the experimentation phase to get a satisfactory
\par
cost/benefit ratio. Meel and Aris (1993a, 1993b) show how support tools for \emph{regression based}
\par
\emph{experimental design} can lead to an efficient and effective experimenting phase.
\par
The way of supporting also lacks tools and techniques for working with groups and group
\par
modelling. During the case study, an appeal was made for the participation of a great number of
\par
functionaries of the police force in interviews, validation sessions, and presentations. Although
\par
the application of these techniques resulted in valuable information, it also implied a heavy work
\par
load, both for researcher and the client organization. Various iterations were needed to obtain
\par
proper data, especially when the data from various sources was conflicting. De Vreede (1993)
\par
shows how \emph{a group decision room} can be used to speed up the dynamic modelling process and
\par
enhance stakeholder participation.
\par
Combination of the outcomes of all these research efforts should be aimed at creating a set of
\par
refutable protective belts around the 'hard core' as described in this paper. Hard core and
\par
protective belts form what Lakatos would call a research programme into the development of a
\par
structured, systematic approach for solving 'soft' BE-problems. The agenda for this research
\par
programme is ambitious, challenging and - ironically - open ended.
